                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">526</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">454</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Sora 2 Pro 15s high (480) + 8s re-timing</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;color:#9ca3af;">duration brackets</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">480</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">394</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">350</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">302</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Naive mix, 480p: 4× Grok 8s defaults + 4× extend (48+180)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;color:#9ca3af;">default + flat</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">228</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">187</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">166</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">144</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Same mix at 720p (96+180) + re-render lost 6s (72)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;color:#9ca3af;">default + flat</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#f472b6;">1,335</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">1,095</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">975</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">841</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;margin-top:16px;">Discount rates are the monthly Basic 82% / Pro 73% / Ultra 63% plan rates. The last two rows are the cautionary tale: a Grok-centric pipeline looks like the cheapest line on the sheet (228 list) until the 8-second default costs you six seconds of re-render and the flat extend charges 180 more — 1,335, twice the deliberate Omni path, with worse timing control throughout. Per-second models punish the audio default; flat models punish volume; defaults punish templates.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Pitfalls and One-Line Fixes</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Confirm the ceiling before writing the script.</strong> 15s for Kling/Seedance 2, 30s for Seedance 2.5, 15s with a silent 8-default for Grok. The script should never exceed the model''s window — that mismatch is discovered at delivery, not at order time.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Pin duration explicitly on every model with a default.</strong> Grok''s 8 and Omni''s 5 are both defaults; both will happily bill seconds you never asked for and withhold the ones you did.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Convert flat operations to per-second before adopting them.</strong> Veo3 at 360 for one generation is fine; the extend at 360 per four added seconds is 90/s. The conversion is the whole audit.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Bill multi-shot against Σ shots, not the prompt.</strong> Three shots of 5/5/12 is a 22-second order no matter what the description says. Set shot seconds first, then write prose that matches.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">On transformation jobs, send exact source seconds.</strong> No default catches you there, and the field double-functions as vendor submission length — the truncation is real, not cosmetic.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Never render 480p "for safety" on a 720p-capable model.</strong> Upscaling a 480 source to 1080 costs 45/s of source seconds — more than rendering 720 correctly at 12/s and re-timing. Get the resolution lane right the first time; the correction ladder on Grok is one of the worst values in the catalog.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;padding-left:24px;line-height:1.9;">
                <li>Record each model''s billing model (per-second / flat / bracket / Σ-shots) in the preset notes — timing math is meaningless without it.</li>
                <li>Check for silent defaults on any model you automate: Grok 8s, Omni 5s. Explicit beats defaulted in every audit trail.</li>
                <li>Divide every flat operation by the seconds it delivers; anything over ~40/s on a short add-on belongs in a per-second model instead.</li>
                <li>For long-form, price Seedance 2.5''s 30-second window against stitching two 15s clips plus an extend — the long window is often the cheaper continuous take.</li>
                <li>Storyboard at bracket boundaries (10/15/25), never between them; the 8/s at 25s is the best per-second number in the flat-pricing world.</li>
                <li>Re-audit templates quarterly for dropped duration fields — a "fine until it isn''t" failure class only appears after someone changes the front end.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">FAQ</h2>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Why was I charged for 8 seconds when I asked for 30?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">You almost certainly hit a default. Grok Imagine video 1.5 accepts 1–15 seconds and quietly renders 8 when the duration field is absent; Omni does the same at 5. Neither errors, both bill exactly what rendered. The fix is to pin duration explicitly in every template rather than trusting any field you have never seen fail.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Does extending a Veo video cost the same as generating one?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">At FuseAI''s price rows, yes — veo3_extend is a flat 360 credits per operation, identical to a veo3 generation, independent of how many seconds you add. Which makes extension the worst per-second buy on the flagship lane: four added seconds cost 90 each. For continuation-heavy work, a per-second model with a long window is the structural answer.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Why did my multi-shot video bill 22 seconds when I planned 30?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Because multi-shot billing is the sum of the shot durations, not the duration written in your prompt. Three shots configured at 5, 5, and 12 seconds produce a 22-second order and a 22-second audio bed. The prompt is creative text; the shot table is the contract.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: My Sora storyboard request for 20 seconds failed — why?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Sora 2 Pro storyboard is duration-bracketed: price rows exist at 10, 15, and 25 seconds and nowhere between them. A 20-second request has no row to resolve to and fails at the order gate with no charge. Plan at the brackets — and note the 25s row works out to 8 credits per second, the cheapest per-second number any flat-priced lane in the catalog offers.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Why does my transformation clip have no audio even though I enabled it?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Omni transformation never produces audio — with a video input the vendor suppresses audio generation entirely, which is why the model has no scene dimension in its price table at all. Nothing was misconfigured; the option you think you enabled does not exist on that endpoint. Budget a post-production audio track instead of an audio toggle.</p></div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:0;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Closing: Seconds Are a Contract, Not a Wish</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Every failure scene above reduces to one sentence: the clock is priced differently in four different ways, and your project only assumed one. A default is a clause. A bracket boundary is a clause. An extend priced per-operation is a clause. Before the next batch, open the timing rows for your two most-used models inside <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/3-0-omni-text-to-video" style="color:#fbbf24;text-decoration:none;">Kling 3.0 Omni text-to-video</a> and <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/video-1-5-preview" style="color:#fbbf24;text-decoration:none;">Grok video 1.5 preview</a> on FuseAI, find which clause your templates are silently relying on, and pin it — because the cheapest seconds in this catalog are the ones you never had to buy back.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- budget-ai-video-tier-shootout (category=4)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'The Budget AI Video Shootout: Grok 1.5 Preview vs Seedance 2 Mini vs Wan 480p vs Kling Turbo, Priced Per Finished Clip',
  4,
  'budget-ai-video-tier-shootout-grok-seedance-mini-wan-turbo',
  'The four cheapest AI video paths on FuseAITools — Grok 1.5 with upscale, Seedance 2 Mini, Wan 480p, Kling V3 Turbo — costed per finished 8-second clip.',
  'cheap AI video, budget AI video generator, Grok imagine video 1.5, Seedance 2 mini, Wan 480p, Kling V3 Turbo pricing, video upscale cost, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>The Budget AI Video Shootout: Grok 1.5 Preview vs Seedance 2 Mini vs Wan 480p vs Kling Turbo, Priced Per Finished Clip</title>
    <meta name="description" content="The four cheapest AI video paths on FuseAITools — Grok 1.5 with upscale, Seedance 2 Mini, Wan 480p, Kling V3 Turbo — costed per finished 8-second clip.">
</head>
<body>
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(ellipse 65% 50% at 50% 0%, rgba(251,191,36,.06), transparent),radial-gradient(ellipse 50% 40% at 100% 90%, rgba(96,165,250,.05), transparent),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318, #0b0c0f);color:#e5e7eb;padding:20px;border-radius:12px;">

        <section class="introduction">
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Premium video models price a single accepted clip like a restaurant dinner — 300 to 500 credits, and the drafts were appetizers you paid full price for. The budget tier has a different physics: generations cheap enough to fail on purpose, upscaling cheaper than re-rendering, and reference tricks that cut rates another 50%. This shootout compares the four cheapest serious paths on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com" style="color:#fbbf24;">FuseAITools</a> — not on sticker rates, but on the only number that matters: <em>total credits per finished, accepted clip</em>, drafts included.</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The brief: one 8-second, 720p social clip, realistically requiring three attempts to land. List credits (100 = $1); monthly plans meter at <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63%</strong>.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="contenders">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Four Contenders and Their Tricks</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#60a5fa;">Grok Video 1.5 Preview — the two-stage specialist.</strong> Generation is 6 credits/sec at 480p, 12 at 720p (1–15 seconds, 8 default), and that''s the whole ladder: the preview ships 480p/720p only. The trick is the pairing: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/upscale" style="color:#60a5fa;">Grok Upscale</a> bills by <em>source</em> seconds — 480p→720p at 15/sec, 720p→1080p at 30/sec, 480p→1080p at 45/sec. Draft cheap at 480p, upscale only the winner.</p>
            <p style="color:#a78bfa;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Seedance 2 Mini — the reference-clip arbitrage.</strong> The lowest meter on the shelf: 6/sec at 480p, 15 at 720p — and 3/sec and 9/sec respectively when you attach a reference video (with_video scene), the same inversion as its big siblings. Duration 4–15s. At 3/sec, an 8-second clip costs a beer''s worth of credits.</p>
            <p style="color:#60a5fa;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Wan 3.0 at 480p — the full-feature floor.</strong> 12 credits/sec with audio free, 2–30 seconds, all five input modes (up to 10 reference images) intact. It''s not the cheapest per generation; it''s the cheapest way to make a <em>controlled</em> draft that already has sound.</p>
            <p style="color:#f472b6;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Kling V3 Turbo — the respectable one.</strong> 27/sec at 720p, 36 at 1080p, 3–15 seconds, single-frame image-to-video that keeps your source aspect. The budget-tier adult: nothing clever, everything solid.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="clip-economics">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Eight Seconds, Three Attempts, One Winner</h2>
            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Path</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">The math</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">List credits</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Ultra 63%</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/video-1-5-preview" style="color:#60a5fa;">Grok</a> draft-then-upscale</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">3 × 480p drafts (6×8=48) + upscale winner to 720p (15×8=120)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">264</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~166</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Grok all-480p (platform tolerates)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">3 × 48 = 144, no upscale</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">144</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~91</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedance/v2-mini" style="color:#a78bfa;">Seedance 2 Mini</a> + reference clip</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">3 × 720p at 9/sec with_video (72 each) = 216; 480p runs at 3/sec = 72 total</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">216 / 72</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~136 / 45</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Seedance 2 Mini bare (no ref)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">3 × 8s at 15/sec (120 each) = 360</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">360</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~227</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan 3.0</a> 480p with sound</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">3 × (12×8=96) — audio included every take</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">288</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~181</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-turbo-text-to-video" style="color:#f472b6;">Kling V3 Turbo</a> 720p</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">3 × (27×8=216) = 648 — the honest adult premium</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">648</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~408</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Turbo with Wan-drafted motion</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">3 Wan 480p drafts (288) + 1 Turbo final (216), motion concept locked</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">504</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~318</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Scoring:</strong> cheapest accepted 720p clip goes to the Grok two-stage path (264 list) or Seedance Mini with a reference phone-video (216, and 72 if the platform eats 480p). Wan is the priciest of the cheap ones per attempt — and the only one handing you free audio and 10-slot references along the way. Turbo loses this bracket outright; it earns its keep when the frame must come from <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-turbo-image-to-video" style="color:#f472b6;">a specific still</a> and keep its aspect.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="what-cheap-loses">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">What You Actually Give Up at This Tier</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Resolution ceilings are real.</strong> Grok 1.5 has no native 1080p (the upscale station is the only door, at 30/sec by source seconds from 720p). Seedance Mini stops at 720p — the ladder up is <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedance/v2" style="color:#a78bfa;">Seedance 2</a> at 96–324/sec.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Duration floors bite differently:</strong> Mini starts at 4 seconds (no 2-second micro-clips), Turbo starts at 3; Wan spans 2–30 — the only contender where both directions are flexible.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Physics ambition is what the premium tiers sell.</strong> Complex fluid, cloth, and contact motion remain Seedance 2.5 / Kling 3.0 territory. The budget tier is for clips where the idea, not the simulation, carries the watch.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Upscaling is not re-rendering.</strong> A 480p draft upscaled to 720p keeps its motion exactly; a fresh 720p attempt changes everything. The two-stage path controls variance — that''s a feature, until the draft itself was the problem.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="action-checklist">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist</h2>
            <ol style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Never final-render on a first attempt at any tier.</strong> At budget rates, two drafts cost less than one lucky take''s re-shoot at premium rates.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Shoot the 10-second phone reference before writing the motion prompt</strong> — Seedance''s with_video scene halves-to-thirds the rate and anchors the movement.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Route audio-relevant drafts to Wan 480p</strong> — free sound on every draft is the evaluation tool the other cheap tiers don''t offer.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Upscale selectively, wholesale never:</strong> only the accepted clip climbs the Grok ladder (15/sec from 480p); drafts stay low-res on purpose.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Apply the plan discount to bracket math, not sticker math.</strong> Ultra (63%) makes a 264-credit Grok path ~166 — roughly the list price of one Turbo 720p attempt. Three attempts later, the plan tier is doing real work.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section class="faq">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: What is the cheapest way to get a usable 8-second AI video clip?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Seedance 2 Mini at 480p with a reference video: 3 credits/sec → 24 credits per attempt, ~72 list for three attempts (about 45 on Ultra monthly). If the platform needs 720p, Grok 1.5''s draft-at-480p-then-upscale path totals 264 list credits for a finished clip including two failed attempts.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Is upscaling cheaper than generating at high resolution directly?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Per finished second, no: 480p generation (6/sec) plus 480p→720p upscale (15/sec) totals 21 credits per delivered second versus 12 for direct 720p. But the drafts are the point — at a one-in-three keep rate, two-stage totals 264 list credits for an accepted 8-second clip (3 × 48 + 120) versus 288 for three direct 720p attempts. When failure is likely the two-stage route wins; when you routinely land first takes, render direct.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Which budget model keeps audio?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Wan 3.0 at 480p (12/sec) — native audio at no surcharge across 2–30 seconds, the only budget option with sound in every draft. Grok 1.5 Preview and Seedance 2 Mini are picture-first tiers; bolt on ElevenLabs or Suno audio (roughly 2–20 credits) if the clip needs a voice or bed.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Do subscription discounts matter more at the budget tier?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Proportionally they matter identically (Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63% of list) — but psychologically the cheap tier rewards them most: at ~45–91 credits per accepted Ultra clip, the whole shootout costs less than one premium-tier draft, making high-volume testing nearly free.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: When should I graduate from the budget tier?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">When three failed drafts no longer beat one better model: watch-and-convert drops, physics-dependent shots, or client frame-locks. The promotion ladder is explicit — Mini to <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedance/v2-5" style="color:#a78bfa;">Seedance 2.5</a>, drafts to Kling 3.0 finals, 720p ceiling to native 1080p — and per-clip economics tell you the month to move.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="closing">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Fail Cheaply, Deliver Cleanly</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The budget tier''s real product isn''t cheaper pixels — it''s permission to iterate. Two-stage upscaling, reference-clip rebates, and free-audio drafts are all the same idea: spend on the accepted clip, not on every attempt. Run your next campaign as a pyramid: Grok and Mini at the base, Wan where sound matters, Turbo at the apex — and watch the per-accepted-clip line collapse.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- course-creator-slides-narration-promo-video-cost (category=4)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'The Course Creator Month: Slides, Narration, and Promo at 2,986 List Credits — What Eight Lessons Actually Cost',
  4,
  'course-creator-slides-narration-promo-video-cost',
  'An educator''s eight-lesson month priced line by line: 48 rendered slides, byte-billed narration, per-minute captions, and a promo stack under 700 credits.',
  'online course content AI cost, slide generation dense text pricing, course narration voiceover bytes, lecture caption transcription cost, Kling omni promo video credits, Seedance draft video pricing, course launch trailer cost, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>The Course Creator Month: Slides, Narration, and Promo at 2,986 List Credits — What Eight Lessons Actually Cost</title>
    <meta name="description" content="An educator''s eight-lesson month priced line by line: 48 rendered slides, byte-billed narration, per-minute captions, and a promo stack under 700 credits.">
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/news/course-creator-slides-narration-promo-video-cost">
</head>
<body style="margin:0;padding:0;background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(circle at 15% 0%, rgba(244,114,182,0.07) 0%, transparent 45%),radial-gradient(circle at 85% 100%, rgba(96,165,250,0.05) 0%, transparent 45%),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318 0%, #0b0c0f 100%);color:#d1d5db;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="max-width:960px;margin:0 auto;padding:32px 20px 64px;">
        <header style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h1 itemprop="headline" style="font-size:2rem;line-height:1.25;color:#f9fafb;margin:0 0 16px;">The Course Creator Month: Slides, Narration, and Promo at 2,986 List Credits — What Eight Lessons Actually Cost</h1>
            <p style="color:#9ca3af;font-size:0.95rem;margin:0;">Published <time itemprop="datePublished" datetime="2026-11-12">November 12, 2026</time> · FuseAI Tools Editorial</p>
        </header>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Introduction: A Course Is a Slide Deck With a Voice, Priced Like One</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The solo-educator business looks like content and behaves like documentation: every lesson is <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">a slide deck (dense text), a read-aloud script (long bytes), and a transcript (long minutes)</strong> — and the platform prices exactly those three quantities. One module, eight lessons, shipped in a month on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/qwen" style="color:#f472b6;text-decoration:none;">FuseAI</a>: forty-eight rendered slides plus their drafts, twenty-five corrections, eight six-minute narrations, eight caption runs, a three-clip promo draft, one promo final, trailer music, and six course-card heroes — <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">2,986 list credits</strong>: 2,450 at Basic''s 82%, 2,179 at Pro''s 73%, 1,881 at Ultra''s 63%.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The shape matters more than the total: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">production is nearly three-quarters of the month and marketing is 23%</strong>. Slides and narration carry the bill because they scale per lesson; the entire promo stack — drafts, final cut, music, hero images — is 676 credits, cheap enough that re-cutting the ad weekly is a rounding error rather than a budget event. This is the inverse of the agency pattern, and it is why course businesses can afford to iterate on teaching while everyone else iterates on advertising.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Stage 1: Slides — the Draft Lane Where Resolution Can''t Cost Money</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">A teaching slide is a text-density object: term lists, code captions, arrow-annotated diagrams at readable size. The working lane is <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/qwen/v3-pro-text-to-image" style="color:#f472b6;text-decoration:none;">Qwen 3 Pro text-to-image</a> at <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">24 credits per 2K render</strong> (12 at 1K; <code>resolution</code> mandatory), and at six slides per lesson × eight lessons that is forty-eight finals = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">1,152 list credits</strong> — the biggest line of the month, 37% of everything. Before any deck renders, the twenty structurally hard slides (the ones with arrows and dense captions) get composed on plain <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/qwen/v3-text-to-image" style="color:#f472b6;text-decoration:none;">Qwen 3 text-to-image</a>, which bills <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">12 credits at both 1K and 2K</strong> — same price at every allowed resolution — so layout reviews cost 240 and the Pro lane only sees approved specs. Eighty percent of the final invoice should be paid pixels; the draft lane is how that stays true.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Post-production corrections ("the caption is wrong on slide four," "this arrow points at the wrong term") are edits, not re-renders: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/image-2-0-segment-edit" style="color:#f472b6;text-decoration:none;">Grok Imagine 2.0 segment-edit</a> bills a flat 6 to touch one region of an approved slide, so the month''s twenty-five fixes = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">150 list credits</strong>. Regenerating them at 24 would have cost 600 and re-rolled the slides that were already right.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Stage 2: Narration — Long Scripts Are Where Bytes Stop Being Cute</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">In the short-ad lanes, byte-based voice-over is a rounding error. In a course, it is a career decision. <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/turbo-2-5" style="color:#f472b6;text-decoration:none;">ElevenLabs turbo</a> bills <code>ceil(UTF-8 bytes / 1000) × 10</code>, and a six-minute lesson read is roughly 900 words ≈ 5,700 bytes = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">six whole blocks, 60 credits a lesson</strong> — not because the voice is expensive but because <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">the script is long</strong>. Eight lessons = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">480 list credits</strong>. The budgeting habit that follows is arithmetic, not taste: at a ~150-word speaking pace, a minute of narration is ~950 bytes, so <em>every minute of script you keep is one block you don''t pay</em>. A lesson that rambles from six minutes to eight doesn''t just lose viewers; it bills two extra blocks per read and two extra minutes per caption.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Captions come from <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/speech-to-text" style="color:#f472b6;text-decoration:none;">speech-to-text</a> at <code>ceil(seconds / 60) × 6</code> — the same utility that made law-firm budgets re-order, and it is why course transcripts bill 288 here: eight lessons × six minutes = six blocks each (36 credits), and the ceiling bites exactly the way it always does: a 6:01 lesson pays seven minutes. Accessibility captions, search-indexable transcripts, and the study-guide source text all come off this one line — the cheapest per-feature utility on the sheet.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Stage 3: The Promo Stack — Draft on Mini, Ship on Omni, and the Audio Switch You Don''t Need</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Launch marketing for a course is short-form video, and short-form is where the draft-then-final discipline pays its usual rent. Three promo concepts get rehearsed on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedance/v2-mini" style="color:#f472b6;text-decoration:none;">Seedance 2 mini</a> at 720p without a reference video (15/s × 8s = 120 a clip) = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">360 list credits</strong>; the winning concept ships as a 12-second cut on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/3-0-omni-text-to-video" style="color:#f472b6;text-decoration:none;">Kling 3.0 Omni text-to-video</a> at 720p with the audio switch left off — the <code>no_audio</code> scene bills 20/s against 30/s with sound, so 12 × 20 = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">240 list credits</strong>. The audio switch is worth reading twice: the promo''s sound is the trailer music and the instructor''s own voice-over, so switching the model''s sound generation on would be paying 10 more credits per second — 120 on one clip, 360 instead of 240 — for audio that gets muted in the edit. A multi-shot order bills Σ shot-seconds, so the 12-second final is three 4-second cuts priced as twelve seconds, not "three videos."</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Two small lines finish the stack: the trailer bed on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/generate" style="color:#f472b6;text-decoration:none;">Suno</a> (generate + one extend = two flat-20 operations = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">40 credits</strong>) and six course-card heroes on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/nano-banana/nano-banana-2-lite" style="color:#f472b6;text-decoration:none;">Nano Banana 2 lite</a>, the flat-6 single-price lane where resolution is not a billing dimension at all — thumbnails don''t miss 4K, and the invoice doesn''t either: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">36 credits</strong>. Whole promo stack: 676. An eight-lesson course advertising itself for the price of half a slide batch is what a solo budget looks like when the lanes are read correctly.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Month''s Invoice: Eight Lessons, One Module, No Video Camera</h2>
            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:20px;font-size:0.92rem;">
