                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">197</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">175</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">151</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Trailer music bed</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Suno flat 20 × 2 ops</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">40</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">33</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">29</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">25</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Course-card heroes, 6</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Nano Banana 2 lite, flat 6</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">36</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">30</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">26</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">23</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Month total</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">—</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#f87171;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">2,986</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">2,450</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">2,179</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#34d399;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">1,881</strong></td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;margin-top:16px;">Discounts are the monthly plan rates: Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63%, applied per line and rounded, columns summing to the total. Two receipts for the creator deciding whether a course business closes. First, the per-lesson unit: 1,152 + 480 + 288 + 150 across the deck, voice, caption, and fix lines = 2,070 of production for eight lessons ≈ <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">259 credits a lesson</strong> before drafts and marketing — a course priced against that number knows its break-even. Second, the byte-metered middle: narration (480) plus captions (288) = 768 credits, a quarter of the month, spent entirely on <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">length</strong>. The slide line can be cut by drafting better; the voice lines can only be cut by talking less — which, in course businesses, is the cheaper habit anyway.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Pitfalls: Five Ways a Course Month Goes Over</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Composing slides on the Pro lane.</strong> At 24 a render, layout experimentation pays finals prices for throwaways. The flat-12 draft lane bills 12 at any allowed resolution — compose there, render approved specs on Pro.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Paying for model sound you will mute.</strong> The promo''s audio is music plus the instructor''s voice; leaving the Omni audio switch on costs 10 more per second (120 on a 12-second cut) for invented SFX the edit deletes.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Long scripts, quietly.</strong> Byte-billed narration and minute-billed captions both meter <em>length</em>, not quality: a lesson that rambles two extra minutes pays two blocks and two minutes on every read and every transcript. Cut the script; the invoice will thank you on all eight lessons.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">The 6:01 ceiling.</strong> STT rounds every partial minute up. Editing lesson audio to just under a whole minute is a real saving; letting it drift thirty seconds over is a phantom extra minute billed forever.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Re-rendering a slide to fix one caption.</strong> A regeneration re-rolls the deck''s typography. A segment edit is flat 6 and changes only the flagged region — twenty-five fixes at 150 instead of 600.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist: Standing Up the Course Pipeline</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;padding-left:24px;line-height:1.9;">
                <li>Write every script to the minute: count words at ~150/min, check the byte estimate (×4) against block boundaries, and cut before recording, not after invoicing.</li>
                <li>Draft the hard slides on the flat-12 lane, lock composition, render finals at Pro 2K — and keep the deck spec versioned so edits reference approved files.</li>
                <li>Route every caption and typography fix through the flat-6 segment-edit lane; log parents so the course changelog is provable to students.</li>
                <li>Rehearse promo concepts on the mini draft lane at 120 a clip; ship only the winning concept on Omni, <code>audio=false</code>, priced as Σ seconds.</li>
                <li>Trim lesson audio to whole minutes before transcription; the ceiling function is the least forgiving line in the stack.</li>
                <li>Treat marketing as a 676-credit stack, not a budget line: re-cut the promo weekly while the module earns — iteration is cheap when the lanes are read correctly.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">FAQ</h2>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: What does one AI-produced course lesson actually cost?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Around 259 list credits of core production for a six-slide lesson with narration and captions: six rendered slides (144), one six-minute narration (60), one caption run (36), and a share of corrections. Eight lessons price at 2,986 for the month including drafts and the whole promo stack — 1,881 on Ultra at 63%.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Why does voice-over cost so much more per course than per ad?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Because it bills by the thousand UTF-8 bytes and courses are long. A 30-second ad script is one block (10 credits); a six-minute lesson read is ~5,700 bytes = six blocks (60), and eight lessons is 480 credits. The lever is script length: at ~150 words per minute, every minute you keep is ~950 bytes you don''t pay, on narration and on captions.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Which lane renders text-dense slides best?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">The dense-text tier of the Pro image lane: 24 credits at 2K (12 at 1K, resolution mandatory) on Qwen 3 Pro, with the flat-12 standard lane as the composition draft tier where resolution cannot change the price. Run one canary of your most caption-heavy slide at 2K before committing a forty-eight-slide batch; slides are the biggest course line and the cheapest to rehearse.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: How should a solo creator budget the launch promo?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Under 700 credits, all in. Three 8-second concepts drafted on Seedance 2 mini (120 each), one 12-second final on Kling Omni 720p with the audio switch off (240 at the no-audio rate; turning sound on is 10 more per second you will not use), trailer music on Suno (two flat-20 operations), and six flat-6 course-card heroes: 676 total. That is what a weekly-recoverable marketing stack costs beside a 2,070-credit production line.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Do captions and transcripts have a cliff to plan around?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Yes — round-up at every minute boundary. STT bills <code>ceil(seconds/60) × 6</code>, so a 6:01 lesson pays seven minutes and a module of them pays seven or eight minutes per lesson forever. Edit lesson audio to sit just under whole minutes, and re-check after every re-cut; it is the cheapest line to defend on the sheet.</p></div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:0;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Closing: Teach More, Bill Per Byte — the Ratio Is the Business</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The course ships because two numbers were tracked from the first lesson: bytes per script and finals per slide. Production priced that way keeps its shape — 2,070 credits of decks and voice for eight lessons, marketing that never outgrows its 676-credit envelope, drafts always on the lane where they cannot cost finals prices. The invoice is that ratio held — 2,986 list credits a month, 1,881 at Ultra''s 63%. The lanes run inside <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/qwen/v3-pro-text-to-image" style="color:#f472b6;text-decoration:none;">Qwen 3 Pro</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/3-0-omni-text-to-video" style="color:#f472b6;text-decoration:none;">Kling 3.0 Omni</a>, and their neighbors on FuseAI — run the script-length arithmetic on one real lesson before recording the module; six minutes of tight teaching is cheaper than eight minutes of good teaching, and the students cannot tell. Try <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/turbo-2-5" style="color:#f472b6;text-decoration:none;">FuseAITools</a> for course narration and caption pipelines.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

