    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="max-width:960px;margin:0 auto;padding:32px 20px 64px;">
        <header style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h1 itemprop="headline" style="font-size:2rem;line-height:1.25;color:#f9fafb;margin:0 0 16px;">Twelve SKUs, Four Marketplaces: A Cross-Border Listing Image-Set and Main-Video Month at 3,624 List Credits</h1>
            <p style="color:#9ca3af;font-size:0.95rem;margin:0;">Published <time itemprop="datePublished" datetime="2026-11-12">November 12, 2026</time> · FuseAI Tools Editorial</p>
        </header>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Introduction: The Listing Is an Image Set With a Compliance Envelope</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">A marketplace listing is not one photo — it is a <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">seven-to-nine-image set with a hard ruleset bolted on</strong>: a main image on a pure-white background with the product filling most of the frame and zero text or logos on it, infographic images that carry the feature callouts, a size or spec chart, lifestyle context shots, and increasingly one main video. A twelve-SKU launch rolling onto four marketplaces used to mean shipping samples to four studios. On <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/qwen" style="color:#60a5fa;text-decoration:none;">FuseAI</a> the whole catalog month — twelve full image sets, localized callout overlays for three extra markets, and a main video on the five hero SKUs with bilingual narration — prices to <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">3,624 list credits</strong>: 2,971 at Basic''s 82%, 2,645 at Pro''s 73%, 2,282 at Ultra''s 63%.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The cost shape is the argument. This is <em>not</em> a translation job wearing an image budget — <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">the images are the volume and localization is the hidden line</strong>. The infographic set and its per-marketplace edits together outweigh every video on the sheet. Walk it with the rate card open, because the marketplace rules decide the lanes: pure white forces the transparency clause, dense callout text forces the resolution floor, and "translate the overlay" is genuinely an image-editing problem, not a re-generation one.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Stage 1: The Main Image — the Pure-White Rule Decides Your Resolution</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Every marketplace enforces the same main-image spec from a different direction: background must be pure white, the product must fill the frame, and no text, watermark, or logo may appear on it. That makes the hero image a <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">cutout problem before it is a photography problem</strong> — and on this platform the parameter that produces a clean alpha is <code>background=transparent</code> on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image/v2-5-flare-text-to-image" style="color:#60a5fa;text-decoration:none;">GPT Image 2.5 Flare</a>, which is a <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">1K-only capability</strong> at 6 credits. Request transparent at 2K and the order can pass every gate and still return an opaque file billed at the 18-credit tier — so the twelve hero shots run at 1K transparent and get composited onto a pure-white canvas as their own step: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">72 list credits</strong> for the entire main-image line. The white background is not a pricing failure to work around; it is the reason the working resolution is 1K.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Stage 2: Infographics and Spec Charts — Dense Text Is a Resolution Floor</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The feature-callout images and the size chart are the densest workload in a listing: labels, leader lines, a comparison table, unit callouts, all readable at zoom. That is why they run on the <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/qwen/v3-pro-text-to-image" style="color:#60a5fa;text-decoration:none;">Qwen 3 Pro text-to-image</a> lane — the family this platform''s information-dense generation testing put ahead for labeled layouts — at its <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">24-credit 2K tier</strong> (<code>resolution</code> is mandatory; 12 at 1K, 24 at 2K). Two infographic images per SKU across twelve = 24 finals = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">576 list credits</strong>, plus one spec/size chart per SKU at 2K = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">288</strong>. Rendering these at 1K to save twelve credits each is a false economy: the callouts go unreadable at thumbnail-plus-zoom, the listing under-performs, and you re-render at 2K anyway. Dense text earns its resolution floor.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Lifestyle and in-context shots — the product on a desk, in a hand, in use — are the one lane where the cheaper pyramid wins. Two per SKU on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/nano-banana/nano-banana-2-1" style="color:#60a5fa;text-decoration:none;">Nano Banana 2.1</a> at 2K (9 credits, resolution mandatory) = 24 images = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">216 list credits</strong>. No fine text to hold, so 2K is plenty and the 4K tier would be pixels the product page never displays.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Stage 3: Localization Is an Edit, Not a Re-Generation</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Here is where a four-marketplace launch either stays cheap or quietly triples. The naive move is to re-prompt every infographic with the German, French, and Japanese callouts — three fresh generations per image, each a full 24-credit Pro render that re-rolls the product photo, the layout, and the compliance-critical units that were already approved. The disciplined move treats "swap the overlay text" as a <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">localized edit to an approved file</strong>: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/image-2-0-image-edit" style="color:#60a5fa;text-decoration:none;">Grok Imagine 2.0 image-edit</a> runs a flat 6 credits to change the callout regions and leave the product pixels provably untouched. Twenty-four infographics × three extra marketplaces = 72 localization passes = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">432 list credits</strong>. The same 72 done as re-generations on the Pro 2K lane would be <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">1,728</strong> — and would restart visual review on every one. Edits are cheaper four times over, and the number is the whole case for the edit lane.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Stage 4: The Main Video — Draft on the Mini Lane, Ship on Kling, Narrate in Bytes</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">A main product video lifts conversion, but it is the premium layer of the listing, so you rehearse it before you render it. Draft: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedance/v2-mini" style="color:#60a5fa;text-decoration:none;">Seedance 2 mini</a> at 720p without a reference video is <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">15 credits per second</strong> — one 8-second turntable or in-use clip is 120 credits to learn the reveal doesn''t land. Final: the hero shots are images, so the listing video is a <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-turbo-image-to-video" style="color:#60a5fa;text-decoration:none;">Kling V3 Turbo image-to-video</a> job at 720p (27/s), where the same 8-second cut is 216 and five hero-SKU finals = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">1,080 list credits</strong>. Video bills <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">per second, not per clip</strong>, so eight seconds is the discipline: the marketplace caps useful listing-video length well below what a cinematic run would cost, and every extra second is 27 credits you did not need.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Narration closes it. Each hero video gets localized voice-over on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/multilingual-v2" style="color:#60a5fa;text-decoration:none;">ElevenLabs Multilingual v2</a>, billed by <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">UTF-8 byte-thousandths at 20 credits</strong>. Eighteen short scripts (five videos × the market mix), each landing under a thousand bytes, round to one block = 20 apiece = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">360 list credits</strong>. Because it is bytes and not words, keep the read to a single tight sentence per video and the whole audio line stays a fifth of the video line — the cliff is a script that drifts past a thousand bytes and quietly doubles its own block count.</p>
        </section>

