INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'The Five-Station E-commerce Video Pipeline: One Product Photo In, Ad Kit Out, 368 Credits on the Invoice',
  4,
  'ecommerce-video-pipeline-five-station-cost-guide',
  'One product photo runs through five FuseAITools stations — GPT Image 2.5, Seedream 5, Kling V3 Turbo, ElevenLabs, Suno — out as a 368-credit complete ad kit.',
  'AI video pipeline, e-commerce product video, GPT Image 2.5, Seedream 5 layer decomposition, Kling V3 Turbo, ElevenLabs voiceover, Suno, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>The Five-Station E-commerce Video Pipeline: One Product Photo In, Ad Kit Out, 368 Credits on the Invoice</title>
    <meta name="description" content="One product photo runs through five FuseAITools stations — GPT Image 2.5, Seedream 5, Kling V3 Turbo, ElevenLabs, Suno — out as a 368-credit complete ad kit.">
</head>
<body>
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(ellipse 65% 50% at 50% 0%, rgba(192,132,252,.06), transparent),radial-gradient(ellipse 50% 40% at 100% 90%, rgba(129,140,248,.06), transparent),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318, #0b0c0f);color:#e5e7eb;padding:20px;border-radius:12px;">

        <section class="introduction">
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Most AI content advice still treats each tool as a vending machine: insert prompt, receive artifact, leave. But an e-commerce listing needs a <em>kit</em> — hero shots, cutouts, a motion clip, a voiceover, a music bed — and kits are built by pipelines, not vending machines. This walkthrough runs one real brief through five stations on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com" style="color:#c084fc;">FuseAITools</a>, and because every station bills in the same credit currency, the entire job is quotable before the first render.</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The brief: a matte-black travel mug, one supplier photo on white, delivered as a full ad kit for a marketplace listing. Station prices are list credits (100 credits = $1); monthly plans meter credits at <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63%</strong>, so the final invoice is shown for all three.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="station-1">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Station 1 — Scene Shots: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image/v2-5-flare-text-to-image" style="color:#c084fc;">GPT Image 2.5 Flare</a></h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The supplier photo is raw material, not art. Flare turns it into lifestyle scenes: the mug on a hostel windowsill at dawn, condensation and all. Use the <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image/v2-5-flare-image-to-image" style="color:#c084fc;">image-to-image mode</a> and pass the product shot as an input URL (up to 16 accepted, so you can fold in brand references). The long-prompt ceiling — 20,000 characters — means the scene direction can be a paragraph, not a keyword soup.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Cost:</strong> 6 credits at 1K, 18 at 2K, 24 at 4K per image, flat across the Flare/Sunburst pair. Four scene candidates at 2K = <strong style="color:#c084fc;">72 credits</strong>. Planning transparent-background cutouts in this station? Transparency is only offered at 1K — decide now or route around it at Station 2.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="station-2">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Station 2 — Clean Assets: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedream/5-pro-layer-decomposition" style="color:#818cf8;">Seedream 5 Pro Layer Decomposition</a></h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">This is the station most people skip and shouldn''t. Layer decomposition takes a finished image and splits it into individually editable layers — product separated from background, shadows kept with the product — as fixed PNG output. Every banner variant, every seasonal recolor downstream starts here.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The mechanics reward precision: coordinates are a 0–1000 bounding box, the input window runs from 262,144 to 36,000,000 pixels, aspect ratios from 1:16 to 16:1, and the size field is mandatory (no <code style="color:#a5b4fc;">auto</code>). The budget route is <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedream/5-flash-layer-decomposition" style="color:#818cf8;">Seedream 5 Flash</a> at a flat 6 credits on all tiers, versus Pro at 12 (1K/1.5K) and 24 (2K).</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Cost:</strong> four scene images decomposed at Pro 1.5K = <strong style="color:#818cf8;">48 credits</strong>, or 24 on Flash if you trust the drafts. Note the output contract: layers are always PNG; the base image can be png or jpeg and defaults to jpeg — flip that default if the layers feed a compositor.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="station-3">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Station 3 — Motion: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-turbo-image-to-video" style="color:#f472b6;">Kling V3 Turbo Image-to-Video</a></h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Pick the winning scene from Stations 1–2 and give it 5 seconds of life — a slow push-in, steam rising, light walking across the matte finish. Turbo''s image-to-video takes exactly one reference image (≤10MB) and keeps the source aspect ratio, so your 1:1 marketplace crop becomes a 1:1 clip with no reframing tax. Duration is yours to set from 3 to 15 seconds; prompts up to 2,500 characters.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Cost:</strong> per-second billing — 27 credits/sec at 720p, 36 at 1080p. The kit needs one 5-second 1080p hero clip: <strong style="color:#f472b6;">180 credits</strong>. Draft the camera move at 720p first (135) or bounce the idea through <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan 3.0</a>''s 480p tier at 12/sec (60 for the same five seconds) before committing.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="station-4">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Station 4 — Voice and Room: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/turbo-2-5" style="color:#a78bfa;">ElevenLabs Turbo 2.5</a></h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">A 30-word voiceover ("Six hours of heat. One hand. Zero spills.") is roughly 150 characters. Turbo 2.5 bills 10 credits per 1,000 characters — that line is <strong style="color:#a78bfa;">2 credits</strong>, a rounding error with a microphone. Use <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/multilingual-v2" style="color:#a78bfa;">Multilingual v2</a> (20/1K) when the listing needs the same read in German and Japanese. A product-placement sound effect — the lid click, the pour — runs through <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/sound-effect-v2" style="color:#a78bfa;">Sound Effect v2</a> at 0.36 credits per second — a five-second pour is about 2 more.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="station-5">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Station 5 — Music Bed: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/generate" style="color:#34d399;">Suno</a></h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">One loopable 15-second bed — warm, minimal, no vocals fighting the voiceover. Every Suno operation in the studio (generate, extend, cover, vocals) is a flat <strong style="color:#34d399;">20 credits per attempt</strong>, so budget three attempts and you still have a licensed-to-platform mood track for 60 credits total. The extension station matters when the marketplace wants a 30-second cut: generate short, extend once, edit never.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="invoice">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Invoice</h2>
            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Station</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Job</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">List credits</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Basic 82%</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Pro 73%</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Ultra 63%</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">GPT Image 2.5 Flare</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">4 scene shots @ 2K</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">72</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">59</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">53</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">45</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Seedream 5 Pro</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">4 decompositions @ 1.5K</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">48</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">39</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">35</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">30</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Kling V3 Turbo</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">5s hero clip @ 1080p</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">180</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">148</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">131</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">113</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">ElevenLabs ×2</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Voiceover + lid-click SFX</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~8</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">7</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">6</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">5</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Suno</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">3 music attempts</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">60</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">49</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">44</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">38</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Full kit</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Images + layers + video + audio</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">368</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~302</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~269</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~232</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">A complete, layered, motion-plus-audio ad kit for roughly what a stock-photo subscription charges for eight JPEGs a month. Multiply by 40 SKUs and Ultra billing lands at ~9,300 credits — still under a hundred dollars.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="pitfalls">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Pipeline Failure Modes</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Contract mismatches between stations.</strong> Flare only offers transparent backgrounds at 1K, but decomposition wants a definite size (no auto) within its pixel window — a 4K scene must be down-planned before Station 2, not after a rejection.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Aspect ratio is decided at Station 1, forever.</strong> Turbo''s image-to-video keeps the source frame; a square hero image begets a square clip no matter what the marketplace prefers later.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Drafting in the wrong currency.</strong> The per-second stations make iteration expensive — that''s why Wan 3.0''s 480p tier (12/sec) exists in this pipeline even though it ships nothing final.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Counting generations, not accepts.</strong> This invoice assumes one take per station. Real acceptance rates are 2–3× on faces and reflective surfaces; matte metal on windowsills was chosen for the demo partly because it''s forgiving.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="action-checklist">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist</h2>
            <ol style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Fix the canvas first.</strong> Decide the marketplace aspect ratio before Station 1; every downstream station inherits it.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Batch the scene pass, thin the decomposition pass.</strong> 4 scenes at 2K, decompose only the 2 you''ll actually animate — saves 24 credits on Pro.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Draft motion cheap, render once at 1080p.</strong> A 720p Turbo pre-viz (135) or Wan 480p bounce (60) before the 180-credit final.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Write the voiceover before the music.</strong> Voice is 2 credits; music beds are 20 per attempt — arrange the cheap station around the script, not the reverse.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Bill the whole kit to your plan rate.</strong> 368 list credits is 232 on Ultra monthly (63%) — quote jobs at your real cost basis, not list.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section class="faq">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: How much does one product''s full ad kit cost in credits?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">About 368 credits at list price: four 2K scene shots (72), four Pro layer decompositions (48), a 5-second 1080p hero clip (180), voiceover plus one sound effect (~8), and three Suno music attempts (60). Monthly plan holders pay roughly 302 / 269 / 232 credits at Basic 82%, Pro 73%, or Ultra 63%.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: What does layer decomposition add over just generating clean images?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Editable structure. Seedream 5''s decomposition outputs fixed PNG layers with product separated from background, so recoloring a scene or swapping the backdrop for a seasonal banner is a layer edit — you never re-roll the whole image and lose the take that passed QA.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Can I skip stations on a tight budget?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Yes, in cost order: decomposition has a Flash twin at flat 6 credits (vs Pro''s 12–24), motion can draft on Wan 3.0 at 480p (12/sec vs Turbo 1080p''s 36/sec), and voiceover is already trivial (10 credits per 1,000 characters on Turbo 2.5). The station nobody should skip is voice — it''s 2 credits and lifts click-through more than a fancier music bed.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Which file formats cross between stations?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">JPEG or PNG into GPT Image 2.5 and Seedream (≤30MB per image on the Seedream window; ≤10MB into Kling Turbo''s single reference frame). Decomposition layers always leave as PNG; base images default to JPEG unless you flip the format field. Everything audio leaves ElevenLabs and Suno ready for a marketplace uploader.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: How do I scale this pipeline to a catalog?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Template Station 1''s prompt as a brand block (scene, light, lens) and vary only the product; keep the aspect ratio locked across the catalog so decomposition and motion parameters never change; and set a per-SKU credit budget (e.g., 400 list) so acceptance-rate variance at Station 3 is absorbed by drafting, not by upgrading plans.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="closing">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Run the Pipeline</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Five stations, one brief, one invoice — that''s the difference between collecting AI tools and operating an AI production line. Every station above is one click away: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image/v2-5-flare-text-to-image" style="color:#c084fc;">Flare</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedream/5-pro-layer-decomposition" style="color:#818cf8;">Seedream 5 Pro</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-turbo-image-to-video" style="color:#f472b6;">Kling V3 Turbo</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/turbo-2-5" style="color:#a78bfa;">ElevenLabs</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/generate" style="color:#34d399;">Suno</a>.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Start with your hardest SKU — the one with the most variants to justify the layer economy — and let the invoice prove the model before you point the line at the catalog.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- elevenlabs-vs-suno-voice-boundary-cost-showdown (category=3)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'ElevenLabs vs Suno on Voice: Where Spoken Audio Ends and Sung Audio Begins — The Crossover Math Nobody Shows You',
  3,
  'elevenlabs-vs-suno-voice-boundary-cost-showdown',
  'ElevenLabs bills voice by length, Suno bills flat 20 per operation. The real speech-vs-song boundary on FuseAITools, crossover math, and a 160-credit audio kit.',
  'ElevenLabs pricing, Suno credits, AI voiceover vs AI music, TTS per character billing, AI singing generator cost, audio identity kit, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>ElevenLabs vs Suno on Voice: Where Spoken Audio Ends and Sung Audio Begins — The Crossover Math Nobody Shows You</title>
    <meta name="description" content="ElevenLabs bills voice by length, Suno bills flat 20 per operation. The real speech-vs-song boundary on FuseAITools, crossover math, and a 160-credit audio kit.">
</head>
<body>
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(ellipse 65% 50% at 50% 0%, rgba(167,139,250,.07), transparent),radial-gradient(ellipse 50% 40% at 100% 90%, rgba(52,211,153,.05), transparent),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318, #0b0c0f);color:#e5e7eb;padding:20px;border-radius:12px;">

        <section class="introduction">
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Two studios on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com" style="color:#a78bfa;">FuseAITools</a> sell you the human voice, and almost nobody compares them — because on paper they don''t compete. <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/turbo-2-5" style="color:#a78bfa;">ElevenLabs</a> meters speech by length; <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/generate" style="color:#34d399;">Suno</a> charges one flat 20 credits for everything it does, voice included. But every brand, course creator, and podcaster eventually faces the same commission — "we need a person saying our thing" — and the answer changes the invoice completely. This is the boundary map: which studio owns which job, where the pricing shapes actually cross over, and the traps hiding in both meters.</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">List credits (100 = $1); monthly plans meter at <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63%</strong>.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="pricing-shapes">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Two Pricing Shapes, Two Definitions of "Voice"</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">ElevenLabs treats voice as <em>information</em>: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/turbo-2-5" style="color:#a78bfa;">Turbo 2.5</a> bills 10 credits per 1,000-character block, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/multilingual-v2" style="color:#a78bfa;">Multilingual v2</a> bills 20 per block, and the length of your script is the entire bill. Duration side: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/speech-to-text" style="color:#a78bfa;">Speech to Text</a> at 6 per minute, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/audio-isolation" style="color:#a78bfa;">Audio Isolation</a> at 0.30 per second, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/sound-effect-v2" style="color:#a78bfa;">Sound Effect v2</a> at 0.36 per second. Voice as data, metered like bandwidth.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Suno treats voice as <em>performance</em>: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/generate" style="color:#34d399;">generate</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/extend" style="color:#34d399;">extend</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/add-vocals" style="color:#34d399;">add vocals</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/add-instrumental" style="color:#34d399;">add instrumental</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/upload-cover" style="color:#34d399;">cover</a> — every operation exactly 20, regardless of whether the result runs 15 seconds or three minutes. Voice as a record, priced like a studio session.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The structural insight: ElevenLabs punishes long text and rewards short; Suno punishes many attempts and rewards long. Your script''s shape picks the studio before its content does.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="crossover">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Crossover Point: Where Flat 20 Beats the Meter</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Do the arithmetic the menus don''t show you. A sung jingle is 20 credits flat no matter how many words it carries. The same words through Turbo 2.5 hit 20 credits at the <strong style="color:#a78bfa;">2,000-character mark</strong> (two billing blocks) and through Multilingual v2 at <strong style="color:#a78bfa;">1,000 characters</strong> (one block). Below those lengths — a 150-character tagline is one block, 10 or 20 credits — speech is cheaper or equal. Above them, only a reason to sing, not a reason to save:</p>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#a78bfa;">Choose ElevenLabs when the voice carries facts:</strong> narration, dubbing, course modules, IVR lines, audiobooks. Melody would damage the message, and per-block billing rewards the tight scripts these formats already demand.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#34d399;">Choose Suno when melody is the message:</strong> brand themes, ads that need a hook, contests that need karaoke beds (<a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/add-instrumental" style="color:#34d399;">instrumental in 20</a>), UGC prompts that need a vocal layer over someone else''s demo (add-vocals, 20).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">The genuine gray zone is short-form emotional copy</strong> — a brand line that could be read or sung. At 150 characters both answers cost 10–20 credits; the decision is creative, and pricing should not pretend to vote.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="cjk-trap">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Billing Footnote CJK Creators Discover the Hard Way</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">ElevenLabs "per 1,000 characters" is metered on <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">UTF-8 byte length</strong>, and Chinese, Japanese, and Korean characters occupy three bytes each. A 950-character Chinese script is 2,850 bytes — three billing blocks, 30 credits on Turbo, 60 on Multilingual — against the 10–20 that the identical character count in English would cost. The per-language bill genuinely triples. An English 950-character narration and a Mandarin one are the same creative deliverable at different prices; quote clients accordingly. Suno''s flat-per-operation has no such dimension at all — a Mandarin theme track costs the same 20 as an English one. (Duration blocks carry the same rounding in reverse: STT bills whole minutes, so a 61-second clip is two blocks, 12 credits.)</p>
        </section>

        <section class="kit">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Audio Identity Kit: Both Studios on One Invoice</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">A real launch commission — coffee brand, one campaign, everything the channels demand — spends both studios in their own lanes:</p>
            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Station</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Job</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">List</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Basic 82%</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Pro 73%</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Ultra 63%</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Spoken taglines ×3 (Turbo 2.5, ~150 chars each)</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">read versions</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">30</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">25</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">22</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">19</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Sung jingle: generate + extend + add-vocals (Suno)</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">three ops</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">60</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">49</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">44</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">38</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Podcast intro bed (Suno generate)</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">one op</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">20</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">16</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">15</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">13</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Stem split of live event recording (Isolation, 90s × 0.30)</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">voice from room</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">27</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">22</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">20</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">17</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Transcripts for captions (STT, 3 min total)</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">timed text</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">18</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">15</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">13</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">11</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Pour/steam/grinder SFX (3 × 5s × 0.36)</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">texture</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">5</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">4</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">4</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">3</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Complete audio identity</strong></td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">spoken + sung + post</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">160</strong></td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">131</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">117</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">101</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Notice the split: Suno''s three operations (80 list) outweigh everything ElevenLabs does except its own single flat op. The studio that <em>defines</em> the brand voice — the sung theme — is also the one with the least billing dimension, which is exactly why it gets re-rolled the most. Cap attempts; bank winners.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="pitfalls">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Four Boundary Myths, Debilled</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">"Suno can do my voiceover."</strong> Suno''s add-vocals adds a <em>sung vocal layer to music</em>; it is not a narration engine and no prompt turns it into one. Short-form spoken copy belongs to ElevenLabs full stop — the boundary is a capability wall before it''s a price line.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">"1,000 characters is 1,000 characters."</strong> Not in a UTF-8 byte meter. Asian-language scripts bill at up to triple their Latin character count, and the block function (ceil) rounds you up from the first block.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">"Flat 20 means flat risk."</strong> Suno operations are flat whether the take works or not; ElevenLabs is flat whether the take <em>flatters you</em> or not. Both studios charge for failure — attempt caps per station are the only shared control.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">"Isolation is a licensing tool."</strong> Audio Isolation splits stems from anything you upload; capability is not clearance. Use it on your own recordings and cleared uploads, exactly as you''d treat a cover.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="action-checklist">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist</h2>
            <ol style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Classify the commission first: facts or melody?</strong> Information-transferring voice goes to ElevenLabs; anything whose job is to be hummed goes to Suno at its flat 20.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Byte-count scripts before quoting</strong> in any 3-byte language — measure UTF-8 length, divide by 1,000, round up, multiply by the studio''s block rate.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Bank the winning Suno track as a template</strong> and extend it (same 20) instead of re-rolling the campaign theme from scratch.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Round your duration assets up in the estimate, not the invoice:</strong> plan STT in whole minutes and isolation/SFX in whole seconds — 61 seconds is two minutes, 5.4 seconds is six.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Bill the full kit at your plan rate before client quotes</strong> — 160 list becomes 131 / 117 / 101 on Basic / Pro / Ultra monthly, and voice work is one of the cheapest line items in a campaign when priced knowingly.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section class="faq">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Is AI voiceover or AI music cheaper for a brand campaign?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">They''re different products on different meters: a spoken tagline on Turbo 2.5 is 10–20 credits by length, a sung theme on Suno is exactly 20 by operation. For one tagline plus one theme plus post (stems, transcripts, effects), the full audio identity above lands at 160 list — roughly 101 on Ultra monthly.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Can Suno generate spoken narration instead of singing?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">No. Its vocal operations (add-vocals, cover, extend) all build sung performances inside musical tracks. Spoken deliverables — narration, dubbing, ads read straight — are ElevenLabs territory, at 10 (Turbo 2.5) or 20 (Multilingual v2) credits per 1,000 bytes.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Why does a Chinese script cost three times an English one of the same length?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Because ElevenLabs billing meters UTF-8 bytes, not visual characters: CJK glyphs take three bytes each, so 950 Chinese characters is 2,850 bytes — three billing blocks where 950 English characters is one. Budget non-Latin narration at 3× the naive character math.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Which ElevenLabs tool removes background noise from a recording?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Audio Isolation, at 0.30 credits per second — a 90-second live recording splits into voice and ambience for 27 credits, cheaper than re-recording almost anything. It works on uploaded material you own or have cleared; the capability says nothing about the rights.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Where should a course creator start — narration or intro song?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Narration first: course modules are the byte-meter''s home turf (tight scripts, one voice, Multilingual v2 for international versions at 20 per block), and the intro song is one flat Suno operation you can defer to any afternoon. A 12-module course at 3k characters per module runs ~360 list on Turbo; the theme costs 20 forever.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="closing">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Boundary Is Creative — the Invoice Is Arithmetic</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">ElevenLabs and Suno don''t really compete; they split the human voice into data and performance and price each honestly once you read the meter. Facts get bytes, melodies get operations, and the 2,000-character crossover is the only place the two invoices briefly agree. When the finished spot needs motion too, the sung hook and the read tagline both drop straight onto a per-second shelf like <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan 3.0</a>, whose native audio accepts a reference of the voice you just made. <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/turbo-2-5" style="color:#a78bfa;">Metered speech</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/generate" style="color:#34d399;">flat-rate song</a> — one 160-credit kit, 101 of them on Ultra, and a boundary you can finally quote.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- etsy-seller-product-photos-seasonal-reshoot-cost (category=2)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'Thirty SKUs, One Person, No Re-Shoots: An Etsy Maker''s Holiday Content Month at 1,522 List Credits',
  2,
  'etsy-seller-product-photos-seasonal-reshoot-cost',
  'An Etsy maker''s 30-SKU month priced line by line: 1K-only transparent cutouts, colorways as 6-credit edits, and seasonal re-scenes cheaper than re-shoots.',
  'Etsy product photo AI cost, transparent background cutout pricing, product colorway variation editing, seasonal listing refresh cost, handmade craft marketing video, Etsy thumbnail generation credits, process reel video pricing, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Thirty SKUs, One Person, No Re-Shoots: An Etsy Maker''s Holiday Content Month at 1,522 List Credits</title>
    <meta name="description" content="An Etsy maker''s 30-SKU month priced line by line: 1K-only transparent cutouts, colorways as 6-credit edits, and seasonal re-scenes cheaper than re-shoots.">
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/news/etsy-seller-product-photos-seasonal-reshoot-cost">
</head>
<body style="margin:0;padding:0;background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(circle at 15% 0%, rgba(248,113,113,0.07) 0%, transparent 45%),radial-gradient(circle at 85% 100%, rgba(251,191,36,0.05) 0%, transparent 45%),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318 0%, #0b0c0f 100%);color:#d1d5db;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="max-width:960px;margin:0 auto;padding:32px 20px 64px;">
        <header style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h1 itemprop="headline" style="font-size:2rem;line-height:1.25;color:#f9fafb;margin:0 0 16px;">Thirty SKUs, One Person, No Re-Shoots: An Etsy Maker''s Holiday Content Month at 1,522 List Credits</h1>
            <p style="color:#9ca3af;font-size:0.95rem;margin:0;">Published <time itemprop="datePublished" datetime="2026-11-12">November 12, 2026</time> · FuseAI Tools Editorial</p>
        </header>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Introduction: A One-Person Shop Competes on Merchandising, Not Studio Time</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The handmade marketplace rewards shops that <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">look like brands</strong>: clean cutouts on every listing, color options shown as actual options, seasonal scenes swapped in without touching a camera. The old cost of that was a light box, a weekend, and either a studio bill or a craft-hour deficit. The holiday-quarter merchandising month on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/nano-banana" style="color:#f87171;text-decoration:none;">FuseAI</a> prices to <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">1,522 list credits</strong>: 1,247 at Basic''s 82%, 1,111 at Pro''s 73%, 958 at Ultra''s 63% — roughly <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">51 credits per SKU</strong> across thirty products, every variant, scene, badge, and reel included.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">This is the small-scale sibling of the agency and chain sheets in this series, and the economics reward exactly what the bigger shops have to engineer: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">variation is an edit, and seasonality is a composite</strong>. The one clause that bites is the transparency rule — the flag that produces alpha PNGs only exists at 1K — and the entire pipeline is designed around it, because thumbnails at 600 pixels do not miss 4K, but a re-shot product photo misses the sale window.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Stage 1: The Cutout Clause — Alpha Exists at 1K, So the Shop Works at 1K</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Every listing starts with the product on nothing: no backdrop, ready to composite onto any scene, banner, or holiday card. The lane is <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image/v2-5-flare-text-to-image" style="color:#f87171;text-decoration:none;">GPT Image 2.5 Flare</a> with <code>background=transparent</code> — and the clause that the failure-post-mortem generation learned the hard way still governs here: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">transparency is a 1K-only capability</strong> at 6 credits. Ask for alpha at 2K and the order can clear validation, bill the 18-credit tier, and hand back an opaque PNG. So all thirty hero cutouts run at 1K: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">180 list credits</strong>, full alpha, shop-wide reuse. The resolution ceiling is not a limitation once the pipeline admits the truth about handmade marketing: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">the product photo is never displayed full-bleed</strong> — it lives in a thumbnail, a search-result tile, or a composite scene, all of which start from pixels you already have at 1K.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Stage 2: Colorways and Seasons Are Edits — the Two Lines That Used to Be Re-Shoots</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">A candle in four scents and a knit in five yarn colors used to mean one photo per variant — or a listing that hid options behind text. On the edit lane, a colorway is a <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">regional change to an approved product photo</strong>: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/image-2-0-image-edit" style="color:#f87171;text-decoration:none;">Grok Imagine 2.0 image-edit</a> bills a flat 6, shape and label and scale stay identical because they were never re-rolled. Forty variant edits = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">240 list credits</strong>, and every color option on the shop front starts from the same physical object. Regenerating "the same candle in blue" is not cheaper at 6-vs-6; it is the listing where the jar got taller, and the review photo stopped matching the product.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Seasonality rides the same logic one tier down. Twelve holiday re-scenes — the evergreen product thumbnails for the gift search — run on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/nano-banana/nano-banana-2-lite" style="color:#f87171;text-decoration:none;">Nano Banana 2 lite</a>, the flat-6 lane where resolution is not a billing dimension at all: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">72 list credits</strong> to swap pine-cone backdrops and warm light onto the seasonal set, and the same 72 again in January when the shop re-scene''s to spring. The alternative — treating each season as a new shoot — is the pricing mistake that turns a two-week merchandising beat into a weekend in the studio. Season one, composite one; the cutouts from Stage 1 make it possible.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The tasteful middle of the stills sheet is in-context finals: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/nano-banana/nano-banana-2-1" style="color:#f87171;text-decoration:none;">Nano Banana 2.1</a> at its 2K tier, <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">9 credits</strong> a render (1K 6, 4K 12) — twenty-four lifestyle scenes, the candle on the mantel and the knit on the park bench, for <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">216 list credits</strong>. These are the images that sell the size and the warmth a cutout cannot, and 2K is their floor because lifestyle tiles get clicked on zoom.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Stage 3: Badges and Canary Rows — Where Text Density Earns Its 24</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Gift guides need words on images: "Gifts for makers," "Under $25," the sale banner with three price points and a date. Dense text is the one workload where the cheap lanes stop being cheap-by-accident, so eight shop-category covers with real typographic load run on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/qwen/v3-pro-text-to-image" style="color:#f87171;text-decoration:none;">Qwen 3 Pro text-to-image</a> at 2K — <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">24 credits</strong> a render, <code>resolution</code> mandatory — for <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">192 list credits</strong>. That is 13% of the month spent on the highest text density the shop publishes, and it is exactly the right shape: a misspelled gift-guide banner is a screenshot that markets the shop for free, in the worst direction.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">And before any multi-render batch — scene sets, badge series, variant runs — one idea gets probed on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/qwen/z-image" style="color:#f87171;text-decoration:none;">z-image</a> at a flat 3: twenty composition probes = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">60 list credits</strong>, throwaways by design. The canary row scales down but does not disappear at solo volume; if anything, a one-person shop survives bad batches longer when the experiment costs three.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Stage 4: Process Reels — the Feed Wants Video, the Draft Lane Says 48</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Handmade sells process: the pour, the stitch, the lathe. Four draft reels rehearse concepts on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedance/v2-mini" style="color:#f87171;text-decoration:none;">Seedance 2 mini</a> at 480p without a reference video — 6/s × 8s = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">48 a draft</strong>, <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">192 for the month</strong>. The winning concept ships as a 10-second <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-turbo-image-to-video" style="color:#f87171;text-decoration:none;">Kling V3 Turbo image-to-video</a> final at 720p (27/s) = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">270 list credits</strong>, started from an actual shop photo so the process video shows the real product. Two 5-second <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/video-1-5-preview" style="color:#f87171;text-decoration:none;">Grok video 1.5 preview</a> cutdowns for Pinterest tiles bill 6/s at 480p (1–15s durations, default 8): <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">60 credits</strong>. Music under it all: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/generate" style="color:#f87171;text-decoration:none;">Suno</a>, generate plus one extend, two flat-20 operations = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">40 credits</strong>. The whole video stack — 562 credits — is 37% of the month: real, but a third, not a takeover.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Month''s Invoice: Thirty SKUs, Fourteen Lines of Arithmetic, One Person</h2>
            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:20px;font-size:0.92rem;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:#1f2937;">
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:left;">Line</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:left;">Priced as</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">List</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">Basic 82%</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">Pro 73%</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">Ultra 63%</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Product cutouts, 30 SKUs</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">GPT 2.5 Flare 1K transparent × 6</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#f87171;">180</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">148</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">131</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">113</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Colorway variants, 40 edits</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Grok 2.0 image-edit, flat 6</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">240</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">197</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">175</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">151</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Lifestyle scene finals, 24 at 2K</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Nano Banana 2.1 2K × 9</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">216</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">177</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">158</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">136</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Composition probes, 20 drafts</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">z-image, flat 3</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">60</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">49</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">44</td>
