                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Process-reel drafts, 4 × 8s</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Seedance 2 mini 480p 6/s</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">192</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">157</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">140</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">121</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Process-reel final, 1 × 10s</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Kling V3 Turbo i2v 720p 27/s</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">270</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">221</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">197</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">170</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Pinterest cutdowns, 2 × 5s</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Grok video 1.5 preview 480p 6/s</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">60</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">49</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">44</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">38</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Reel music bed</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Suno flat 20 × 2 ops</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">40</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">33</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">29</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">25</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Month total</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">—</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#f87171;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">1,522</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">1,247</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">1,111</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#34d399;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">958</strong></td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;margin-top:16px;">Discounts are the monthly plan rates: Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63%, applied per line and rounded, and the columns sum to the total. Read the sheet by question, not by lane. "Should we offer this in more colors?" is answered by a flat 6 — the variant line is 240 credits for forty options, cheaper per unit than the materials. "Should the shop look seasonal?" is answered by 72. "Should we show the process?" is the only question that asks for real money — 562 credits of video, 37% of the month, and the reason one draft lane and one final lane is the entire social strategy at this scale. Stills answer every merchandising question for under 30 credits a decision; the video stack answers the discovery question, and the whole shop''s marketing costs less than a single commissioned product-photography weekend.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Pitfalls: Five Ways a Small Shop Balloons a Small Month</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Requesting transparent backgrounds at 2K.</strong> The alpha flag is a 1K capability; higher requests can bill 18 and still deliver an opaque file. If the pipeline needs compositing, the working resolution is 1K — plan every cutout there and upsell the scene, not the sprite.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">"Regenerating the same candle in blue."</strong> A fresh generation of a variant is a new object: the jar drifts, the label re-wraps, the review photo stops matching. The edit lane is flat 6 and shape-preserving; the re-roll is six credits and a refund dispute.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Treating each season as a new shoot.</strong> Holiday thumbnails generated from scratch are slower and pricier than re-scening evergreen cutouts. Twelve seasonal covers at flat-6 lite is a 72-credit decision; a "reshoot everything" habit is a lost December.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Badge text at thumbnail prices.</strong> Gift-guide covers carry real typographic load; the flat lanes will misspell at scale. Badges belong at 24 on the dense-text lane — 13% of the month, and the only line where a five-credit save costs a screenshot.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Shipping reels without drafts.</strong> The 48-credit draft is the insurance policy: a bad process angle discovered on the mini lane costs 48, the same discovery on the Turbo final costs 270. One draft per concept is not optional overhead.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist: Running the Shop''s Merchandising Month</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;padding-left:24px;line-height:1.9;">
                <li>Generate every product once, at 1K transparent, and reuse the alpha sprites across the entire season''s composites — the cutout is the asset, scenes are disposable.</li>
                <li>Put every colorway and size variation through the flat-6 edit lane with parent-file logging so variants provably share one object.</li>
                <li>Schedule seasonal re-scenes as 6-credit covers on the flat lite lane, and re-scene both directions — holiday in October, spring in January.</li>
                <li>Keep the canary row at solo scale: probe compositions at 3 before any batch that costs 6-to-24 apiece.</li>
                <li>Send badge and gift-guide text to the Pro 2K dense lane only, proofed like copy — small shops get screenshotted for typos.</li>
                <li>Reel policy: draft everything at 48, ship one final at 270, cut Pinterest tiles from the same concept at 30, and let the Suno bed be the flattest line on the sheet.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">FAQ</h2>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: What does it cost to do product photos for an entire Etsy shop with AI?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">About 51 list credits per SKU for a full merchandising month: thirty transparent cutouts, forty colorway edits, twenty-four lifestyle finals, twelve seasonal covers, eight badge covers, probes, and a complete process-reel stack — 1,522 credits total, 958 on Ultra at 63%. One commissioned product-photography weekend for the same shop quoted more before retouching.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Can AI generate transparent-background product images reliably?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Yes, with one clause that sets your workflow: <code>background=transparent</code> on GPT Image 2.5 Flare is a 1K-only capability at 6 credits — request it at 2K and the order can bill the higher tier and still return an opaque file. For a handmade shop that is not a compromise: cutouts get composited into scenes and thumbnails, all of which start from resolution you already have at 1K.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: How should product color options be produced?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">As edits of the approved product photo, never fresh generations. A regional color swap on the flat-6 image-edit lane keeps shape, label position, and scale identical by construction — forty variants for 240 credits — while regenerating "the same item in blue" re-rolls the object itself and is how listings end up not matching the review photos.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Are seasonal listing refreshes worth the cost for a small shop?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">At 6 credits a cover they are the cheapest visible marketing change a shop can make: twelve holiday re-scenes on the flat lite lane totaled 72 credits this month — 4.7% of the sheet — and the same 72 buys the January spring swap. The unaffordable version was ever the studio re-shoot, not the decision itself.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: How much of a one-person shop''s budget should go to video?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">This month, 562 of 1,522 — 37% — and it is the right ceiling for solo scale: four 8-second drafts on the mini lane (48 each), one 10-second Turbo final from a real shop photo (270), two 5-second Pinterest cutdowns (30 each), and a flat-20 Suno bed. Process content is what handmade feeds reward, but it is the only lane here that bills per second, so drafts decide what the one final gets to be.</p></div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:0;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Closing: The Shop Looks Like a Brand Because the Pipeline Behaves Like One</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Thirty SKUs merchandised by one person because two rules were set at the first listing: the product is generated once and everything after that is an edit, and seasons are composites rather than shoots. The invoice is those habits priced — 1,522 list credits a holiday month, a 51-credit per-SKU unit price the agency sheets in this series would envy, and a canary row that costs 60 and saves batches. The lanes run inside <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image/v2-5-flare-text-to-image" style="color:#f87171;text-decoration:none;">GPT Image 2.5 Flare</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/image-2-0-image-edit" style="color:#f87171;text-decoration:none;">Grok Imagine 2.0 edit</a>, and their neighbors on FuseAI — cut out your best-seller once at 1K transparent first; the season''s worth of covers that composite off that one sprite is the cheapest merchandising a handmade shop will ever do. Try <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/nano-banana/nano-banana-2-lite" style="color:#f87171;text-decoration:none;">FuseAITools</a> for Etsy product photos and seasonal listing covers.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- faceless-video-channel-assembly-line-six-station-cost-guide (category=4)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'The Faceless Channel Assembly Line: Six AI Stations, One 60-Second Video, About 200 Credits on the Invoice',
  4,
  'faceless-video-channel-assembly-line-six-station-cost-guide',
  'Six-station faceless video pipeline on FuseAITools: Kimi scripts, Seedance Mini visuals, ElevenLabs voice, Suno music — every episode priced in credits.',
  'faceless YouTube channel AI, AI video pipeline, Kimi k3 script, Seedance 2 mini cost, ElevenLabs narration pricing, Suno background music, automated video workflow, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>The Faceless Channel Assembly Line: Six AI Stations, One 60-Second Video, About 200 Credits on the Invoice</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Six-station faceless video pipeline on FuseAITools: Kimi scripts, Seedance Mini visuals, ElevenLabs voice, Suno music — every episode priced in credits.">
</head>
<body>
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(ellipse 65% 50% at 50% 0%, rgba(167,139,250,.07), transparent),radial-gradient(ellipse 50% 40% at 100% 90%, rgba(129,140,248,.05), transparent),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318, #0b0c0f);color:#e5e7eb;padding:20px;border-radius:12px;">

        <section class="introduction">
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The faceless channel is the closest thing AI content has to a real factory: the product is identical every episode, the stations are specialized, and the entire operation lives or dies on unit cost. This walkthrough builds one 60-second episode — script, visuals, narration, music, effects, subtitles — on six <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com" style="color:#a78bfa;">FuseAITools</a> stations and ends, as every serious factory review must, with the per-unit invoice and the monthly extrapolation.</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The episode: a 60-second explainer ("Why airplane windows have holes in them"), six visual beats, English narration, subtitled. List credits (100 = $1); monthly plans meter credits at <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63%</strong>.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="station-1">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Station 1 — Research and Script: Kimi &amp; Grok, the Two-Credit Newsroom</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">A 160-word narration script plus six shot descriptions is roughly 800–1,000 output tokens. Through <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kimi/generate" style="color:#22d3ee;">Kimi K3</a> (741 credits per million input, 3,705 per million output), a full pass with 3k tokens of source material lands around 5–6 credits. Its reasoning-summary output is handy here: you can see the model''s fact-checking path without paying to store the chain-of-thought. For the research pass — pulling the boring mechanism facts so the script can be punchy — <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok-chat/generate" style="color:#60a5fa;">Grok 4.x chat</a> bills the same rate both directions (249 in / 741 out per million), making the combined newsroom cost of an episode roughly the price of a stick of gum.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Factory habit worth stealing: keep one canonical script prompt (hook rule, read-aloud cadence, six-beat shot list) and only swap the topic line per episode. Variation comes from the topic, not the template.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="station-2">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Station 2 — Visuals: Seedance 2 Mini Is the Volume Engine</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Six beats × 8 seconds = 48 seconds of motion. On <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedance/v2-mini" style="color:#a78bfa;">Seedance 2 Mini</a> with a reference clip attached (phone pans, screen recordings, stock motion), the with_video scene prices at 3 credits/sec at 480p and 9 at 720p: the whole episode''s visuals cost <strong style="color:#a78bfa;">144 credits list at 480p</strong> — or 432 if the channel has graduated to 720p. This is the station where faceless channels live or die — no other configuration gets you 48 seconds of motion for 144 credits.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Upgrade two beats per episode to <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan 3.0</a> at 480p (12/sec — 96 each for two 8-second beats) when a beat needs free native audio (the sound of the jet engine IS the beat) or 10-image reference lock for a recurring host object. Budget beats stay on Mini; signature beats graduate.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="station-3">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Station 3 — Narration: ElevenLabs Turbo, the Cheapest Quality on the Line</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">160 words ≈ 950 characters. <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/turbo-2-5" style="color:#a78bfa;">Turbo 2.5</a> bills 10 credits per 1,000 characters — one take is 10 credits, two takes are 20, and the channel voice stays identical across every episode because the voice ID is the brand. Character-count billing means script length is your only throttle: discipline the hook-to-body ratio and this station stays under a quarter of the episode budget even at premium resolution elsewhere.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="station-4">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Station 4 — Music and Room Tone: Suno, Flat 20 a Swing</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">One bed per episode from <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/generate" style="color:#34d399;">Suno</a> at 20 credits flat, banked as a channel-intro template so future episodes reuse the winning generation instead of re-rolling. When the platform wants 30 extra seconds, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/extend" style="color:#34d399;">extend</a> costs the same 20. A <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/sound-effect-v2" style="color:#a78bfa;">Sound Effect v2</a> pressurization-pop or seatbelt-click runs 0.36/sec — three 4-second effects ≈ 4 credits.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="station-5">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Station 5 — Subtitles: Transcript Your Own Narration for Six Credits</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Feed the finished narration to <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/speech-to-text" style="color:#a78bfa;">ElevenLabs Speech to Text</a> — 6 credits per minute — and you get word-timed captions from the exact audio the viewer hears, no drift from your script''s pre-recording version. A 60-second episode bills one minute: 6 credits. Burn-in timing from the transcript beats auto-captions for retention on mute-autoplay feeds.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="station-6">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Station 6 — Assembly: The Free Station (and the One That Sets Your Ceiling)</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">NLE assembly, thumbnail, upload: zero credits, and the actual bottleneck. At unit costs this low, the factory''s constraint is your review time — which is exactly why the budget tier shootout logic applies: draft visuals on Mini, promote only the beats that carry the story to pricier models, and keep the human in the loop only where the loop is worth human hours.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="invoice">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Episode Invoice, Then the Month</h2>
            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Station</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Job</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">List</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Basic 82%</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Pro 73%</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Ultra 63%</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Script + research (Kimi / Grok chat)</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~4k tokens total</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">8</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">7</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">6</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">5</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Visuals (6 × 8s, Seedance Mini 480p with refs)</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">48s of motion</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">144</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">118</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">105</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">91</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Signature beats (2 × 8s Wan 480p, audio free)</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">promotion option</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">192</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">157</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">140</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">121</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Narration (Turbo 2.5, 2 takes)</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~1,900 chars billed</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">20</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">16</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">15</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">13</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Music (Suno ×1) + effects</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">bed + 3 SFX</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">24</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">20</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">18</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">15</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Subtitles (STT, 1 min)</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">timed transcript</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">6</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">5</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">4</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">4</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Episode (lean / with signature beats)</strong></td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">60s finished video</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">202 / 394</strong></td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">166 / 323</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">148 / 288</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">127 / 248</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Twenty lean episodes a month: <strong style="color:#a78bfa;">4,040 list credits — about 2,545 on Ultra monthly, roughly $25 of video production for a channel that used to need a freelance editor per week</strong>. The invoice''s shape is the lesson: visuals are 70% of spend, everything else is rounding error, so quality improvements should be bought in the visuals station only.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="pitfalls">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Assembly Line Failure Modes</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Script-visual misalignment.</strong> Generating six beats from a loose outline guarantees re-rolls; lock the shot list at Station 1 with exact durations — on per-second billing, "about 8 seconds" is a budget line you didn''t approve.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Voice drift across sessions.</strong> The channel voice is branding; regenerate narration with the same ElevenLabs voice settings every episode, never "whichever sounds good today."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Reference hoarding.</strong> Mini''s with_video scene makes references <em>cheaper</em> (3/sec at 480p) — channels that still render without_video at 6/sec are paying double for worse motion fidelity.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Optimizing the wrong station.</strong> Cutting 20% off narration saves 4 credits; cutting one wasted 8-second visual saves 24–96. The visuals meter is the only number worth watching weekly.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="action-checklist">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist</h2>
            <ol style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Template Station 1</strong> — canonical script prompt, topic slot, six-beat shot list with seconds attached.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Bank a reference-clip library</strong> for Mini''s with_video pricing tier and motion consistency at once.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Promote exactly two beats per episode</strong> to Wan 480p (free audio, reference packs) — budget discipline as a quality tool.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Freeze the channel voice and music bed</strong> after episode 3; reuse generations, don''t re-roll them.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Track credits-per-published-episode weekly</strong> and bill it at your plan rate — monthly Ultra (63%) turns the lean line into ~127 credits an episode, which is where faceless stops being a side project and starts being arithmetic.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section class="faq">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: How much does one AI-generated faceless video cost in credits?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">About 202 credits at list for a lean 60-second episode (script 8, visuals 144, narration 20, music and effects 24, subtitles 6), or 394 with two promoted signature beats. Plan holders pay roughly 166 / 148 / 127 lean — Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63% monthly.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Which station costs the most and which is the best value?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Visuals dominate at ~70% of spend — and are also where discounts go furthest: Seedance 2 Mini''s with_video scene (3/sec at 480p) is 48 seconds of motion for 144 credits. The best value on the line is script: a full research-and-write pass on Kimi K3 plus Grok chat runs under 10 credits.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Can the narration and subtitles use the same audio?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Yes — that''s the trick behind station 5. Feed the finished ElevenLabs narration into Speech to Text (6 credits/minute) instead of auto-transcribing the mixed video; captions match the exact delivery, and you pay once for a transcript of audio you already own.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: How do I keep costs flat as the channel scales to daily uploads?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Reuse assets instead of re-rolling them: one Suno bed extended per episode (20 credits flat per operation, same as generate), a growing Mini reference library that keeps visuals in the 3/sec with_video scene, and the frozen script/narration templates. Thirty episodes a month lands near 6,000 list credits — about 3,800 on Ultra, under two hours of a human editor''s old rate.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: What''s the first quality upgrade when revenue appears?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Move the visuals station up the ladder, not sideways: promote the two signature beats per episode from Mini''s with_video 480p tier (48 credits the pair) to Wan 480p with free audio (192 the pair) — about +144 credits an episode — then test watch-time. The line''s structure means one station can be upgraded without rebuilding anything.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="closing">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Build the Line, Then Feed It</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Faceless channels don''t fail for lack of ideas — they fail on unit economics. At ~200 credits per episode list, ~127 on Ultra, the line is cheap enough to run twenty ideas a month and keep only what the analytics crown. The stations are all one bookmark away: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kimi/generate" style="color:#22d3ee;">script</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedance/v2-mini" style="color:#a78bfa;">visuals</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/turbo-2-5" style="color:#a78bfa;">voice</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/generate" style="color:#34d399;">music</a>.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- flat-rate-vs-per-second-ai-video-billing-showdown (category=4)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'Ten Credits or Forty-Eight a Second: The Flat-Rate vs Per-Second Billing War Running Inside One AI Video Shelf',
  4,
  'flat-rate-vs-per-second-ai-video-billing-showdown',
  'Sora 2 bills 10 credits per clip; Wan 3.0 bills 48 per second: a flat-rate vs per-second AI video cost showdown with the crossover math per workflow.',
  'AI video pricing model, flat rate video, per second billing, Sora 2 credits, Veo pricing, Wan 3.0 pricing, Hailuo cost, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Ten Credits or Forty-Eight a Second: The Flat-Rate vs Per-Second Billing War Running Inside One AI Video Shelf</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Sora 2 bills 10 credits per clip; Wan 3.0 bills 48 per second: a flat-rate vs per-second AI video cost showdown with the crossover math per workflow.">
</head>
<body>
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(ellipse 65% 50% at 50% 0%, rgba(129,140,248,.07), transparent),radial-gradient(ellipse 50% 40% at 100% 90%, rgba(96,165,250,.05), transparent),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318, #0b0c0f);color:#e5e7eb;padding:20px;border-radius:12px;">

        <section class="introduction">
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">There are two economies on the <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com" style="color:#818cf8;">FuseAITools video shelf</a>, and most users shop in only one of them without ever noticing the other exists. The per-second economy meters everything: resolution, seconds, scenes. The flat economy charges one price per generation and lets you walk away. A <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/sora/text-to-video" style="color:#60a5fa;">Sora 2</a> text-to-video render costs 10 credits — roughly a fifth of a second of <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan 3.0</a> at 1080p. And yet teams pay Wan''s meter all day, every day. The answer is not that one camp is cheaper; it''s that they price different <em>workflows</em>, and the crossover is pure arithmetic.</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Prices below are list credits (100 credits = $1). Monthly plans meter credits at <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63%</strong> — discounts scale both economies equally, so they never change the crossover, only its units.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="the-two-camps">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Tale of the Tape</h2>
            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Flat-rate camp (per generation)</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Price</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Per-second camp (metered)</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Rate</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/sora/text-to-video" style="color:#60a5fa;">Sora 2 text/image-to-video</a></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">10 / clip</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedance/v2-mini" style="color:#a78bfa;">Seedance 2 Mini</a></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">3–15 / sec</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/veo3/text-to-video" style="color:#818cf8;">Veo 3.1 Lite / Fast</a></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">24 / 48 / clip</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Grok Video 1.5 Preview</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">6–12 / sec</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/hailuo/image-to-video-standard" style="color:#fbbf24;">Hailuo 2.3 Standard / Pro</a></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">40–120 / clip</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-turbo-text-to-video" style="color:#f472b6;">Kling V3 Turbo</a></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">27–36 / sec</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/sora/pro-text-to-video" style="color:#60a5fa;">Sora 2 Pro</a> (by duration × size)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">120–480 / clip</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Wan 3.0</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">12–48 / sec</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/text-to-video" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan 2.6</a> / <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v2-5-turbo-text-to-video-pro" style="color:#f472b6;">Kling 2.5 Turbo Pro</a></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">60–480 / clip by duration</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedance/v2-5" style="color:#a78bfa;">Seedance 2.5</a> (premium physics)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">27–250 / sec</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/veo3/extend" style="color:#818cf8;">Veo 3 / Extend</a></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">360 / clip</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Kling 3.0 (std → 4K)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">20–100 / sec</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">One honest caveat up front: the flat camp is not uniformly "dumber." Sora 2 Pro is flat but tiered by duration and size (10-second high: 250; 15-second high: 480). What makes it flat-camp is that seconds are a menu, not a meter — you can''t render one second more.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="crossover">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Crossover Arithmetic</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Variable 1 — duration.</strong> Flat prices cap your exposure: a Sora 2 Pro 15-second high render is 480 credits whether it works or not. On the meter, the same ambition (Wan 1080p × 15s) is 720, and the per-second camp''s real ceiling play (30-second Wan clips at 1,440) has no flat-camp equivalent at all. Under ~8 seconds the flat camp wins nearly every matchup; over ~12, the meter''s granularity starts paying for itself — because on a meter you can also <em>shrink</em> the deliverable.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Variable 2 — iterations per accepted clip.</strong> This is where the structures truly diverge. Flat: every draft costs the full price whether you use 2 seconds of it or delete it at submission review. Meter: drafts at the cheap tier are the camp''s superpower — 5 seconds of Wan 480p is 60 credits, less than a single Sora Pro attempt at 120. A workflow that drafts cheap and finals once lives in the meter; a workflow that rolls full-length candidates lives in flat.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Variable 3 — variance tolerance.</strong> If the platform accepts "whatever the model decided" (ambient loops, B-roll, test cards), flat''s 10–48-credit generations are the cheapest video on the shelf, period. If the client signed off on 1080p, 12 seconds, exact aspect ratio — the meter is the only camp selling that, and the price of admission is the per-second rate.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="worked-example">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Two Invoices, Two Different Products</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Goal: 20 usable 7-second social clips in a month, 1-in-3 keep rate. <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Flat invoice:</strong> 60 Sora 2 generations at 10 = 600, plus 20 Veo Lite quality spikes at 24 = 480 → <strong style="color:#818cf8;">1,080 credits list</strong> — for 20 clips you <em>select from variance</em>, at whatever length and look the models decided. <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Meter invoice for 20 clips to an exact brief</strong> (7s, 1080p, Wan at 48/sec): finals alone are 6,720, plus ~720 if you pre-viz every shot at 480p first. That''s the honest framing: the camps aren''t selling the same clip. A 6× price gap for locked resolution, locked duration, locked framing, and revision-to-spec is a bargain to a client with a spec — and nonsense to a meme page.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">What discipline actually changes is the overhead ratio inside the meter camp: drafting once at 480p (12/sec) instead of re-rolling 1080p finals adds ~10% to the invoice and typically halves the discarded-final count. And at Ultra monthly (63%), the flat invoice lands near ~680 credits versus the undisciplined meter''s ~4,690 — a gap no plan tier closes, which is precisely why the crossover deserves a spreadsheet instead of a habit.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="pitfalls">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Where Each Camp Bites Back</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Flat camp: silent waste.</strong> A 10-credit generation you never watch still billed 10; a 360-credit Veo 3 take parked in "maybe later" is a $3.60 paperweight. Flat billing hides abandonment cost because each unit feels cheap.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Meter camp: duration creep.</strong> Every extra second is a line item nobody approved. 5s → 10s doubles the invoice; 10s → 30s on Wan 1080p turns a 480-credit clip into 1,440 without any decision being made — just a slider.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Scene multipliers surprise people.</strong> The meter''s pricing is per-second <em>and</em> per-scene-state: Kling with sound runs +33–50%, Seedance with a reference video drops −40%. The flat camp just… doesn''t have this layer.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Comparing list to list across camps is a category error.</strong> 10 credits (Sora 2) vs 48/sec (Wan 1080p) isn''t a 4.8× decision; it''s 10 vs 240-to-1,440 depending on what you''re actually shipping.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="action-checklist">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist</h2>
            <ol style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Bill per accepted clip, not per generation.</strong> Both camps'' prices are meaningless until divided by your keep rate.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Split the shelf by job, not by taste.</strong> B-roll and loops → Sora 2 / Veo Lite / Hailuo Standard. Controlled deliverables → Wan / Kling / Seedance meters.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Draft in the meter''s cheap tiers (480p, 3/sec Seedance Mini) and final once.</strong> That single habit is the difference between the two invoices above.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Use <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/sora/pro-storyboard" style="color:#60a5fa;">flat-rate storyboarding (120–200 credits)</a> as the pre-meter gate.</strong> Approve structure cheaply, then spend meter seconds only on approved shots.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Apply your plan rate to the crossover, not just the sticker.</strong> Ultra''s 63% makes a 360-credit Veo 3 take cost 227 — occasionally the "expensive flat" option is the disciplined one.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section class="faq">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: What is the cheapest AI video generation on FuseAITools?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Per generation: Sora 2 at 10 credits flat (Basic 8, Pro 7, Ultra ~6). On the meter, the cheapest reachable clip is Seedance 2 Mini with a reference video at 3 credits/sec — a 4-second clip at ~12 credits list. Quality tolerance decides which "cheapest" matters.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: When does per-second billing beat flat pricing?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Three cases: long clips (beyond flat camp ceilings — 20–30s at your chosen resolution), heavy iteration (drafting at 480p meter rates of 6–12/sec instead of re-rolling full flat clips), and precise durations (a 7-second cut bills 7 seconds, not a fixed tier). Below those, flat usually wins on accepted-clip cost.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Why does Sora 2 Pro cost 120–480 if it''s "flat"?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Flat means one price per generation for a chosen configuration, not one price overall: Sora 2 Pro tiers by duration × size — 10s standard 120, 10s high 250, 15s standard 200, 15s high 480. You pre-select the tier; the meter camp lets you select any second count.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Do subscription discounts favor one camp?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">They apply identically (monthly Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63% of list credits), so no structural advantage — but high-volume flat-camp users hit plan ceilings sooner per dollar of output, because their accepted work is spread across more generations; meters concentrate spend into fewer, deliberate finals.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Can I mix camps on one project?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">That''s the correct answer, not a compromise: flat generations for exploration (Sora 2 at 10, Veo Lite at 24), flat storyboards for structure, and meter finals only for the shot list that survived review. The two economies are complements — one buys variance, the other buys precision.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="closing">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Run Your Own Crossover</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The flat-vs-meter question has an answer per team, and it''s computable in an afternoon: take last month''s accepted clips, price them twice — once on the flat shelf (<a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/sora/text-to-video" style="color:#60a5fa;">Sora</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/veo3/text-to-video" style="color:#818cf8;">Veo</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/hailuo/image-to-video-standard" style="color:#fbbf24;">Hailuo</a>), once on the disciplined meter (<a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-turbo-text-to-video" style="color:#f472b6;">Kling Turbo</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedance/v2-mini" style="color:#a78bfa;">Seedance Mini</a>) — and keep the invoice with your name on it.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- flux-3-klein-batch-image-production-workflow (category=2)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'Flux 3 Klein and the Economics of Cheap Batch Image Generation: A Production Workflow Guide',
  2,
  'flux-3-klein-batch-image-production-workflow',
  'Flux 3 Klein bills 3/6/12 credits per image at 1K/2K/4K, up to 4 images per request — a workflow guide to batch text-to-image and multi-reference editing.',
  'Flux 3 Klein, batch image generation, cheap AI image, per image pricing, text to image, image editing, black forest lab, Flux family, num_images, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Flux 3 Klein and the Economics of Cheap Batch Image Generation: A Production Workflow Guide</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Flux 3 Klein bills 3/6/12 credits per image at 1K/2K/4K, up to 4 images per request — a workflow guide to batch text-to-image and multi-reference editing.">
</head>
<body>
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(ellipse 65% 50% at 50% 0%, rgba(52,211,153,.07), transparent),radial-gradient(ellipse 50% 40% at 100% 90%, rgba(16,185,129,.05), transparent),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318, #0b0c0f);color:#e5e7eb;padding:20px;border-radius:12px;">

        <section class="introduction">
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Every image family eventually grows a budget tier — the model that isn''t trying to win benchmarks, it''s trying to win volume. The <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext" style="color:#34d399;">Flux</a> line just got one. <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/flux-3-klein-text-to-image" style="color:#34d399;">Flux 3 Klein Text-to-Image</a> and its sibling <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/flux-3-klein-edit-image" style="color:#34d399;">Flux 3 Klein Edit Image</a> price as low as 3 credits per image at 1K — the cheapest per-image rate in the family — and they let you request up to four images in a single call.</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">That combination — low unit cost times multi-image batching — reframes what "an idea worth testing" costs. When a four-way variant set at 1K runs 12 credits list (about 8 on an Ultra plan), exploration stops being a budget line and becomes the default first move.</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">This guide is about that arithmetic: how Klein''s per-image pricing works, the split between its text and edit modes, and how it sits alongside the premium <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/flux-2-pro-text-to-image" style="color:#34d399;">Flux 2 Pro</a> models rather than replacing them.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="pricing-architecture">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">How Per-Image, Per-Resolution Pricing Works</h2>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Klein uses a pricing mode that multiplies two things the vendor controls — resolution and image count — so the whole cost model reduces to <code>credits per image × number of images</code>. There is no duration, no scene, no batch surcharge hiding elsewhere.</p>

            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Resolution</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Credits per image (list)</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">4-image batch (list)</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">4-image batch at Ultra 63%</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">1K</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">3</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">12</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~7.6</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">2K</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">6</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">24</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~15.1</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">4K</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">12</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">48</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~30.2</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Two constraints shape how you use it: <code>num_images</code> is capped at 1–4 and defaults to 1, and resolution defaults to 1K — but both are required fields on the request, so the front end must always send them explicitly. The billing count is taken from the images you asked for, not the images returned, so a run that yields fewer stills isn''t refunded. That is precisely why the four-up batch is the sweet spot: one request, four candidates, one predictable line item.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="workflow">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Two Models, Two Job Types</h2>

            <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Klein Text-to-Image — Volume Concepting and Variant Sets</h3>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Text mode is the concepting engine: a prompt up to 5,000 characters, nine aspect ratios (1:1 default, through 16:9, 9:16, 21:9 and friends), and a four-image batch that turns one prompt into a selectable set. An <code>enhance_prompt</code> switch (default on) lets the model expand terse prompts, and a seed field supports reproduction when a take is worth locking.</p>

            <div style="background:rgba(31,41,55,0.3);padding:16px;border-radius:8px;margin:16px 0;">
                <p style="color:#34d399;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:8px;">Variant set in one call: "flat-lay of a minimalist skincare brand, cream and sage palette, soft daylight" — resolution 1K, num_images 4. Twelve list credits buys four compositions to choose from.</p>
                <p style="color:#9ca3af;">Note: text mode has no "auto" aspect — it always renders at a stated ratio, so pick the frame before you batch.</p>
            </div>

            <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Klein Edit Image — Multi-Reference Editing on a Budget</h3>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Edit mode accepts 1–10 input images (jpg/png/webp, up to 30 MB each) plus an instruction prompt, and unlike text mode it does support an <code>auto</code> aspect that follows the first image''s proportions. Same per-image pricing, same batch cap — which makes it an affordable way to run edit experiments across a product set without reaching for the premium tier.</p>

            <div style="background:rgba(31,41,55,0.3);padding:16px;border-radius:8px;margin:16px 0;">
                <p style="color:#34d399;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:8px;">Recolor a chair across a room mock: pass the interior photo as reference, prompt "reupholster the accent chair in terracotta bouclé, keep everything else identical." aspect auto, resolution 1K.</p>
                <p style="color:#9ca3af;">For final, maximum-fidelity edits, re-run the chosen composition through <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/flux-2-pro-image-to-image" style="color:#34d399;">Flux 2 Pro Image-to-Image</a>; Klein earns its keep on the exploration pass.</p>
            </div>

            <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Klein vs Flux 2 / 2 Pro — Where Each Belongs</h3>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Klein is the volume tier, not the ceiling tier. The right mental model is a funnel: brainstorm and variant at 1K on Klein, reserve <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/flux-2-image-to-image" style="color:#34d399;">Flux 2</a> and Pro for the hero images and high-resolution finals where peak quality justifies the cost. Cheapest exploration, premium commitment — the family now spans the whole range instead of forcing one budget decision.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="pitfalls">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Batch-Cost Traps to Design Around</h2>

            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Risk</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">What happens</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Countermeasure</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Billed by requested count</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Cost is <code>per-image × num_images</code> even if the vendor returns fewer usable images — a partial batch isn''t a discount.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Batch only prompts you''d genuinely want 4 cuts of; use num_images=1 for precise one-offs.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">4K batch as the default</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">A 4K four-up is 48 credits list — 16× the 1K equivalent — for pixel counts most screens never show.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Explore at 1K, upres winners to 2K/4K only for the finals pass.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Missing the required fields</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Resolution and num_images are billing keys and must be sent; relying on vendor defaults breaks the price match.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Always set both explicitly in the form before generating.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Expecting "auto" in text mode</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Auto aspect exists only for edit mode; text mode rejects it and needs a concrete ratio.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Choose the target ratio up front for text-to-image batches.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Choosing the wrong sibling</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Klein is a budget model; forcing it to be the hero-image tier costs quality on work that needed Flux 2 Pro.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Route by intent: Klein for volume, Flux 2 / Pro for the flagship frames.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </section>

        <section class="action-checklist">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist</h2>
            <ol style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#34d399;">Adopt the four-up default.</strong> num_images=4 at 1K (12 list credits) turns every prompt into a selectable set for pennies.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#34d399;">Keep a strict resolution ladder.</strong> Explore 1K → shortlist at 2K → upres only winners to 4K.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#34d399;">Always set resolution + num_images.</strong> They are required billing keys, not optional hints.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#34d399;">Use edit mode for reference-driven work.</strong> Up to 10 input images at ≤30 MB each, with auto-aspect for edits that must preserve framing.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#34d399;">Pin winners with a seed.</strong> When a take is right, lock the seed for controlled re-runs.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#34d399;">Hand off finals upstream.</strong> Graduate the selected frames to <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/flux-2-pro-text-to-image" style="color:#34d399;">Flux 2 Pro Text-to-Image</a> for hero quality.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section class="faq">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: How much does a Flux 3 Klein image cost?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Per image: 3 credits at 1K, 6 at 2K, 12 at 4K — billed as that rate times the number of images you request (up to four). A full 4-image 4K batch is 48 credits list, dropping to about 30 on an Ultra monthly plan (63% discount), 35 on Pro (73%), and 39 on Basic (82%).</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: What is the difference between Klein Text-to-Image and Edit Image?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Text-to-Image generates from a prompt alone and renders at an explicitly chosen aspect ratio (no auto). Edit Image accepts 1–10 reference images plus an instruction, and adds an "auto" aspect option that follows the first image''s proportions. Pricing is identical between them.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Is Klein the cheapest way to generate images on FuseAITools?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Within the Flux family, yes — 3 credits at 1K is the family''s lowest per-image rate, and the four-image batch keeps per-candidate cost tiny even as exploration volume grows. It trades peak fidelity for that price, which is why it''s positioned for drafts and variants rather than hero finals.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Why do I get billed for four images if only some are usable?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Billing is based on the count you request, not the count returned, so a partial batch is charged in full and there''s no automatic refund for rejected stills. Set num_images to the number you actually want candidates for, and use 1 for precision single shots.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Should I use Klein instead of Flux 2 Pro?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Use both for different stages. Klein is the volume tier for concepting, variant sets, and edit exploration at the lowest cost per image; <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/flux-2-pro-image-to-image" style="color:#34d399;">Flux 2 Pro</a> remains the choice for final, highest-fidelity deliverables. The mature workflow explores on Klein and commits on Pro.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="closing">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Real Shift</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Flux 3 Klein''s importance isn''t that it''s a cheaper model — it''s that cheap batching changes behavior. When a four-way variant set costs about a coffee''s worth of credits, teams stop committing to one guess and start generating options by default. The win isn''t a better single image; it''s a hundred inexpensive ones that make the eventual hero image smarter.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Try <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/flux-3-klein-text-to-image" style="color:#34d399;">Flux 3 Klein on FuseAITools</a> for budget batch image generation.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- flux-image-price-curve-klein-batch-generation (category=2)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  '20 → 8 → 3 Credits: The Flux Image Price Curve, and the "Pro" Lane That Actually Means Cheaper',
  2,
  'flux-image-price-curve-klein-batch-generation',
  'The Flux image price curve across three generations: flux-2''s flat 20/36 menu, the pro lane that means cheaper, and Klein''s 3-credit batch floor.',
  'Flux image AI pricing history, flux-2 vs flux-3 klein cost, per-image generation credits, batch image pricing numImages, AI image resolution cost curve, flux kontext pro pricing, image model rate card, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>20 → 8 → 3 Credits: The Flux Image Price Curve, and the "Pro" Lane That Actually Means Cheaper</title>
    <meta name="description" content="The Flux image price curve across three generations: flux-2''s flat 20/36 menu, the pro lane that means cheaper, and Klein''s 3-credit batch floor.">
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/news/flux-image-price-curve-klein-batch-generation">
</head>
<body style="margin:0;padding:0;background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(circle at 15% 0%, rgba(232,121,249,0.07) 0%, transparent 45%),radial-gradient(circle at 85% 100%, rgba(251,191,36,0.05) 0%, transparent 45%),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318 0%, #0b0c0f 100%);color:#d1d5db;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="max-width:960px;margin:0 auto;padding:32px 20px 64px;">
        <header style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h1 itemprop="headline" style="font-size:2rem;line-height:1.25;color:#f9fafb;margin:0 0 16px;">20 → 8 → 3 Credits: The Flux Image Price Curve, and the "Pro" Lane That Actually Means Cheaper</h1>
            <p style="color:#9ca3af;font-size:0.95rem;margin:0;">Published <time itemprop="datePublished" datetime="2026-11-12">November 12, 2026</time> · FuseAI Tools Editorial</p>
        </header>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Introduction: An Image Rate Card That Reads Like a Descending Staircase</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Video pricing history is a story about units getting finer. Image pricing history is a story about one unit getting <em>cheaper</em>, fast — and the <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext" style="color:#e879f9;text-decoration:none;">Flux family</a> on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/generate" style="color:#e879f9;text-decoration:none;">FuseAI</a> is the cleanest descending staircase on the whole platform. Three generations, three per-file prices for the same job at the same 1K resolution: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">20 credits, then 8, then 3</strong>. A render that cost a twenty-credit menu row in the first generation costs a <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">three-credit batch unit</strong> in the last, and the newest lane is the only one that offers 4K. Somewhere in that collapse, a lane named "Pro" quietly became the budget option — which is exactly the kind of thing a rate card''s fossil layers will tell you if you read them bottom-up instead of trusting the labels.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">This is the image-side companion to the Kling and Wan histories: same method (divide the menu, follow the unit), opposite direction. Where per-second video kept its price and moved the discounts, per-file images dropped the price through the floor and made <em>resolution</em> the only lever left to pull. All figures are list credits; the standing monthly discounts — Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63% — apply per lane, so the reference order used throughout, a four-file 1K batch on Klein at 12 list, nets out to just under 10, 9, and 8 credits by plan.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Generation 1: The Flat 20/36 Menu — Absolute Pricing With a Resolution Penalty</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The founding artifact is <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/flux-2-text-to-image" style="color:#e879f9;text-decoration:none;">flux-2 text-to-image</a> (and its <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/flux-2-image-to-image" style="color:#e879f9;text-decoration:none;">image-to-image</a> twin): absolute per-order pricing on a quality dimension of just two rungs — <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">1K = 20 credits, 2K = 36</strong>. Note the shape: 2K is not double 1K, it is 1.8×, a small built-in nudge up the resolution ladder, but the base rate is high enough that the whole menu reads as premium. Twenty credits for one 1K image was the reference point every later generation would be measured against — and, as it turns out, was one of two prices hiding behind a confusing name.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Generation 2: The "Pro" Lane That Was Actually the Discount Tier</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Here is the rate card''s most instructive trap. <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/flux-2-pro-text-to-image" style="color:#e879f9;text-decoration:none;">flux-2-pro text-to-image</a> and <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/flux-2-pro-image-to-image" style="color:#e879f9;text-decoration:none;">its image-to-image twin</a> — the lane whose name is literally the word for "more" — bill <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">1K = 8, 2K = 10</strong>. Pro is cheaper than the plain flux-2 lane at both rungs, and dramatically cheaper at 2K: 10 against 36, because the pro menu''s 2K rung barely prices above its 1K. Read the ladder the wrong way and you pay 36 for what the "pro" name was quietly offering at 10. It is the image-world version of the audio-surcharge collapse in the Kling history: a dimension the first generation priced aggressively, the second generation repriced almost flat — and the naming gave no signal which way the curve went.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The lesson generalizes past this family: on a multi-generation rate card, <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">tier names are not a price ordering</strong>. "Pro" marks a model lineage, not a more expensive column. Anyone who sorts a cost sheet alphabetically instead of arithmetically will pick the 36-credit option believing the word "pro" meant worth it.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Generation 3: Klein and the Batch Floor — 3 Credits, Plus a Free 4K Rung</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Added 2026-10-20, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/flux-3-klein-text-to-image" style="color:#e879f9;text-decoration:none;">flux-3-klein text-to-image</a> breaks the staircase through the floor with a structural change: it is a <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">batch-priced</strong> lane (PER_BATCH_SIZE_QUALITY), where the stored number is a <em>per-file</em> rate and the bill is that rate × a mandatory <code>numImages</code> of 1–4. The per-file menu is <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">1K = 3, 2K = 6, 4K = 12</strong> — a clean doubling ladder, the first Flux lane with a 4K rung at all, and a base rate under one-sixth of the founding 20. The <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/flux-3-klein-edit-image" style="color:#e879f9;text-decoration:none;">edit-image twin</a> is same-price, same-batch, with an added <code>auto</code> aspect option. Two ideas arrive together here that the earlier menus kept separate: <em>resolution finally costs what it costs</em> (perfect 2× per rung, no penalty, no subsidy) and <em>volume is a knob you choose inside one order</em> rather than a reason to re-quote.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Because Klein''s ladder is linear at both axes, the arithmetic is unusually honest: a 4-file 1K batch is 4 × 3 = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">12 credits</strong> — the exact same 12 that a single 4K file costs, which is a nice way to remember the whole menu. The 20-credit founding render now buys you either six 1K files, three 2K files, or one 4K image that the first generation couldn''t even order.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Curve as a Table: One Job, Three Generations</h2>
            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:20px;font-size:0.92rem;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:#1f2937;">
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:left;">Lane (t2i / i2i)</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:left;">Pricing shape</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">1K</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">2K</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">4K</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">flux-2</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">absolute per order</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">20</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">36</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">—</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">flux-2-pro</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">absolute per order</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">8</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">10</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">—</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">flux-3-klein</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">batch, per file × numImages</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">3</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">6</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">12</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;margin-top:16px;">Read down the 1K column and the whole argument is there: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">20 → 8 → 3</strong>, a 6.7× collapse across three generations for the identical job. Read across the Klein row and you see what replaced price as the decision: a perfectly linear resolution ladder (3/6/12) where the only question left is which rung you need — and a volume knob (1–4 per order) that lets you buy variants without touching the per-file rate. The founding 20-credit render is now a rounding error; the interesting question has moved entirely to resolution and count.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Pitfalls: Five Ways Old Image Habits Misprice the Klein Era</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Sorting Flux lanes by name instead of number.</strong> "Pro" is the cheaper column, not the premium one — 8/10 against plain flux-2''s 20/36. Choosing the 36-credit 2K render because the word "pro" sounded like more is a self-inflicted 3.6× on the most basic decision on the card.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Forgetting numImages on a batch lane.</strong> Klein''s stored 3/6/12 are <em>per-file</em> rates; the bill is rate × numImages, and numImages is mandatory. A four-file 4K batch is 48, not 12 — the batch knob is cheap only when you remember it multiplies.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Ordering 2K on flux-2 out of habit.</strong> The plain lane''s 2K rung is 36; the pro lane''s 2K is 10 for what the menu treats as the same quality dimension. If you must use a flat-absolute lane, the 2K row you want is the one that barely costs more than 1K, not the one that costs 3.6× more.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Assuming resolution always meant a penalty.</strong> On Klein, 4K is a normal 2× rung, not a premium tier — the first Flux family that lets you go top-resolution for 12. Teams that flinch off 2K because flux-2 priced it at 36 are overpaying the flinch, not the pixels.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Treating t2i and i2i as different prices.</strong> Across all three generations, text-to-image and image-to-image bill identical menus. The input type is a workflow choice, never a line item — routing an edit through the "more expensive-sounding" lane is pure superstition.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist: Pricing an Image Rate Card That Keeps Falling</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;padding-left:24px;line-height:1.9;">
                <li>Divide every lane''s menu by its rungs before choosing by name — on this card the cheapest column is called "pro," and the newest one prices per file.</li>
                <li>Default new image volume to the Klein batch lane: the 3-credit floor and linear 3/6/12 ladder make it the correct first quote until a contract reason says otherwise.</li>
                <li>Set numImages deliberately, 1–4 — the batch knob multiplies a per-file rate, so decide the variant count as a budget choice, not a form default.</li>
                <li>Treat 4K as affordable on Klein (12) rather than a premium — the resolution decision is now about the asset, not the invoice.</li>
                <li>Re-quote legacy flux-2 renders against the pro and Klein lanes on sight; a 20-credit habit is a 3-credit decision mispriced by 6.7×.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">FAQ</h2>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Why is "flux-2-pro" cheaper than plain "flux-2"?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Because on this card the name marks a model lineage, not a price tier — and the counterintuitive fact is real on the rate card: flux-2-pro bills 8 at 1K and 10 at 2K, while plain flux-2 bills 20 and 36. The "pro" lane happens to be the value menu, its 2K rung priced barely above its 1K. Reading the numbers instead of the label saves up to 3.6× on a 2K render.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: What does the price curve look like across the three Flux generations?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">A descending staircase at 1K: 20 credits on flux-2, 8 on flux-2-pro, 3 on flux-3-klein — 6.7× cheaper at the base resolution across the family''s life. The newest lane is also the most capable (its only 4K menu, at 12) and the most flexible (batch ×numImages). Price fell while capability rose, which is exactly the direction an image rate card moves when compute gets cheap and the platform competes on volume.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: How does Klein''s batch pricing actually bill?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">The stored rate is per file — 3 at 1K, 6 at 2K, 12 at 4K — and the charge is that rate times a mandatory numImages of 1 to 4, so one order producing four 1K variants bills 4 × 3 = 12 credits. Both dimensions are billing keys: resolution selects the rung, count multiplies it. Get the per-file number and the count right and the arithmetic is linear on both axes — the friendliest structure on the card once you remember the multiplication.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Is 4K worth it when Klein offers it for 12 credits?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">On Klein, 4K is a clean 2× step over 2K and 4× over 1K — a normal rung, not the penalty the flux-2 era attached to resolution. Twelve credits for a top-resolution file that the founding menu could not even order makes 4K the default when the asset will be zoomed or printed; drop to 1K (3) or 2K (6) for thumbnails and in-feed tiles. The decision is now about the deliverable, not the discount.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Which Flux lane should a new image project price against?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Klein''s 1K rate — 3 credits per file — is the defensible baseline: it is the cheapest render on the family and the only one that scales volume inside a single order via numImages. Quote the older flat lanes only when a specific model behavior you depend on exists there and not on Klein; for pure cost-per-image, the 3-credit floor is where every new sheet should start.</p></div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:0;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Closing: Read the Numbers, Not the Names</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The Flux image history compresses into two sentences a rate-card reader should tattoo somewhere visible: the cheaper column is called "pro," and the newest lane is six times cheaper than the first one. Pricing this family correctly was never about decoding the names — it was about dividing the menu, following the per-file rate through the batch multiplier, and treating resolution as the only real lever left once the price collapsed. Every lane above bills live on FuseAI, from the founding <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/flux-2-text-to-image" style="color:#e879f9;text-decoration:none;">flux-2 menu</a> through the value-priced <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/flux-2-pro-image-to-image" style="color:#e879f9;text-decoration:none;">pro lane</a> to the <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/flux-3-klein-edit-image" style="color:#e879f9;text-decoration:none;">Klein batch edit</a> floor. Try <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/flux-3-klein-text-to-image" style="color:#e879f9;text-decoration:none;">FuseAITools</a> for Flux image generation with transparent per-file batch pricing.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- game-art-studio-concept-to-launch-pv-cost-pipeline (category=4)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'Game Art Without the Outsourcing Quote: Concept Sketch to Launch PV at 1,666 List Credits, Stage by Stage',
  4,
  'game-art-studio-concept-to-launch-pv-cost-pipeline',
  'A game-studio art month priced stage by stage: 360-credit concept drafts, 1K-only sprite cutouts, and a 15-second PV that outscores every still line.',
  'game concept art AI cost, character sprite transparent background, Kling omni image-to-video pricing, game launch trailer credits, Flux 3 Klein batch pricing, Suno game theme music, ElevenLabs character voice, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Game Art Without the Outsourcing Quote: Concept Sketch to Launch PV at 1,666 List Credits, Stage by Stage</title>
    <meta name="description" content="A game-studio art month priced stage by stage: 360-credit concept drafts, 1K-only sprite cutouts, and a 15-second PV that outscores every still line.">
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/news/game-art-studio-concept-to-launch-pv-cost-pipeline">
</head>
<body style="margin:0;padding:0;background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(circle at 15% 0%, rgba(129,140,248,0.07) 0%, transparent 45%),radial-gradient(circle at 85% 100%, rgba(34,211,238,0.05) 0%, transparent 45%),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318 0%, #0b0c0f 100%);color:#d1d5db;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="max-width:960px;margin:0 auto;padding:32px 20px 64px;">
        <header style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h1 itemprop="headline" style="font-size:2rem;line-height:1.25;color:#f9fafb;margin:0 0 16px;">Game Art Without the Outsourcing Quote: Concept Sketch to Launch PV at 1,666 List Credits, Stage by Stage</h1>
            <p style="color:#9ca3af;font-size:0.95rem;margin:0;">Published <time itemprop="datePublished" datetime="2026-11-12">November 12, 2026</time> · FuseAI Tools Editorial</p>
        </header>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Introduction: The Art Budget Is Three Budgets Wearing One Vest</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">A four-person studio prepping a launch quarter asked for "game art." What they actually needed was three separate production lines with three different billing shapes: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">still images priced per file</strong> (concept, standing art, sprites, key art), <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">video priced per second</strong> (the launch PV), and <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">audio priced per operation and per byte</strong> (theme track and character VO). On <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext" style="color:#818cf8;text-decoration:none;">FuseAI</a>, the whole quarter''s art package — concept exploration, six locked characters, 40 sprite cutouts, hero key art, a 15-second multi-shot PV, a theme track, and bilingual VO — lands at <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">1,666 list credits</strong>: 1,366 at Basic''s 82%, 1,216 at Pro''s 73%, 1,049 at Ultra''s 63%. The outsourcing quote for the same spec started at four figures in dollars per <em>character</em>.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">This article walks each station with the live rate card open, because the surprises are structural, not artistic: the PV costs more than every still image combined, the sprite sheet''s transparent backgrounds only exist at one resolution, and the cheapest draft lane is a different vendor family than the final. Character consistency is handled as an editing problem, not a prompting prayer.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Stage 1: Concept Exploration — Batch Pricing Is a Dial, Not a Tax</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Concept work is the cheapest stage per decision because <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/flux-3-klein-text-to-image" style="color:#818cf8;text-decoration:none;">Flux 3 Klein</a> prices per image by resolution tier — 3 credits at 1K, 6 at 2K, 12 at 4K — and the real charge is that unit <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">multiplied by numImages (mandatory, 1–4)</strong>. Read correctly, that multiplier is a feature: exploration wants options. Thirty concept orders at 1K with numImages=4 is <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">120 images for 360 list credits</strong> — three full silhouette boards per character before anything is "chosen." Both <code>resolution</code> and <code>numImages</code> are billing keys, so the form cannot submit without them; the budget cannot drift by omission, only by decision.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The mistake pattern inverts the dial: teams that lock finals at 4K and draft at 4K too pay 4× for pixels they will throw away. Draft at 1K (3), iterate at 2K (6), ship finals at 4K (12) — the pyramid is the same one the cost-engineering series measured on video, and game concept art is its most natural home because exploration volume is high and discard rate is ~90%.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Stage 2: Character Lock-In and Sprites — Consistency Is an Edit, Transparency Is a Resolution</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Lock-in runs on the edit endpoint.</strong> <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/flux-3-klein-edit-image" style="color:#818cf8;text-decoration:none;">Klein edit-image</a> is priced identically to text-to-image (3/6/12 per image × numImages) and adds <code>auto</code> to the aspect list, so pose variations, expression sets, and outfit swaps on a chosen silhouette are edits of a known file — not fresh generations hoping the seed behaves. Six characters × four iteration orders each × 2K × numImages=2 = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">288 list credits</strong>, and every one of them starts from the same face.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Sprites collide with the transparency clause.</strong> Engine-ready sprites need alpha, and on this platform the parameter that produces it — <code>background=transparent</code> on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image/v2-5-flare-text-to-image" style="color:#818cf8;text-decoration:none;">GPT Image 2.5 Flare</a> — is a <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">1K-only capability</strong> at the vendor level: request transparent at 2K and the order may pass every gate and still deliver an opaque file that bills 18 credits instead of 6. So the 40-sprite batch runs at 1K transparent: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">240 list credits</strong>, full alpha, engine-ready. The sprite-to-4K wish is not a pricing failure but a pipeline fact: upscale the locked sprite as its own pass, or let the engine scale — either way, transparency decides the working resolution, not the other way around.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Key art is the one place 4K earns its price.</strong> The store page banner is the only deliverable that renders on a 4K monitor at full width: three Klein 4K finals plus one hero variant, four files at 12 credits each = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">48 list credits</strong>. Spending more list than that on key art is a taste argument; spending less is a pixel-density argument the storefront will lose.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Stage 3: The Launch PV — Draft on the Mini Lane, Final on Omni, and the Σ-Shot Clock</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The 15-second trailer is the most expensive single line in the package <em>and</em> the cheapest to rehearse, because the draft and final lanes belong to different families. Draft: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedance/v2-mini" style="color:#818cf8;text-decoration:none;">Seedance 2 mini</a> runs 6 credits per second at 480p without a reference video — one full 15-second assembly is <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">90 list credits</strong> to learn that shot two is boring. Final: the locked characters are images, so the PV is an <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/3-0-omni-image-to-video" style="color:#818cf8;text-decoration:none;">Omni image-to-video</a> job — same rate card as text-to-video: 1080p with audio is 36/s, without audio 30/s.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Two billing behaviors decide the PV invoice. First, <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">multi-shot orders bill Σ shot-seconds</strong>: a three-shot trailer of 5-second cuts is charged on 15 total seconds, not on "one video" — 15 × 36 = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">540 list credits</strong> for the 1080p with-audio final. Second, audio is a scene switch priced per second: if the PV''s sound comes from the Suno track (it does), generate the visual with <code>audio=false</code> and the same cut drops to 30/s — <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">180 credits of difference on one checkbox</strong>. The trailer''s native SFX lane (whooshes the model invents) is exactly what the edit replaces; paying 6/s for sound you will mute is the most avoidable line on the sheet.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Music and VO close the package. <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/generate" style="color:#818cf8;text-decoration:none;">Suno</a> bills a flat 20 per operation: one generate plus two extends to reach a 3-minute loop = 60. <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/turbo-2-5" style="color:#818cf8;text-decoration:none;">ElevenLabs turbo</a> bills UTF-8 byte-thousandths at 10: the 900-byte English launcher VO is 10, and its Chinese sibling — same words, ~3 bytes per glyph — lands at 3 blocks = 30. Bilingual is not double; for CJK it is <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">triple</strong>, and the byte is why.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Package Invoice: Eight Lines, One Quarter of Art</h2>
            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:20px;font-size:0.92rem;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:#1f2937;">
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:left;">Line</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:left;">Priced as</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">List</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">Basic 82%</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">Pro 73%</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">Ultra 63%</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Concept exploration, 30 orders</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Klein 1K × numImages 4</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">360</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">295</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">263</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">227</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Character lock-in edits</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Klein edit 2K × 2 per order</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">288</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">236</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">210</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">181</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Sprite cutouts, 40 files</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">GPT 2.5 Flare 1K transparent</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#fbbf24;">240</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">197</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">175</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">151</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Key art finals</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Klein 4K finals ×3 + hero ×1</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">48</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">39</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">35</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">30</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">PV draft, 15s assembly</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Seedance 2 mini 480p 6/s</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">90</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">74</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">66</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">57</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">PV final, 3 shots × 5s</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Omni i2v 1080p audio-on 36/s</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#f472b6;">540</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">443</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">394</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">340</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Theme track (1 gen + 2 extend)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Suno flat 20 per operation</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">60</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">49</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">44</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">38</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Launcher VO, EN + CN</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Turbo byte-blocks 10 / 30</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">40</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">33</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">29</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">25</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Package total</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">—</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#f87171;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">1,666</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">1,366</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">1,216</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#34d399;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">1,049</strong></td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;margin-top:16px;">Discounts are the monthly plan rates: Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63%, applied to list. Sanity checks the table invites: the PV final (540) alone is 32% of the invoice and outweighs all eight still-image lines combined (936 of 1,666, but split across 160 files). Flip the PV''s audio off and hand sound to Suno: the same cut bills 30/s, saving 90 list — the single biggest checkbox in the package. And the draft lane earns its keep: one 90-credit rehearsal that catches a bad shot two is cheaper than one re-rendered 540-credit final.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Pitfalls: Five Ways the Art Budget Leaks Anyway</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Drafting at 4K.</strong> The resolution tier is the price tier: 12 credits of final-quality pixels per throwaway concept is 4× the draft budget on pixels with a 90% discard rate. The pyramid — 1K explore, 2K iterate, 4K ship — is the whole cost story of Stage 1.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Asking a sprite for transparency at 2K.</strong> The 1K-only clause on <code>background=transparent</code> has no enforcement popup; a 2K request can deliver a billed, opaque, useless file. Transparency picks the resolution. Plan the upscale separately or let the engine do it.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Re-generation instead of editing for consistency.</strong> A fresh text-to-image roll for "the same character, new pose" restarts the identity lottery. Klein edit is the same price per image; use the file you already approved as the input, and the sixth character variation finally has the same face as the first.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Counting the PV as "one video" instead of Σ seconds.</strong> Three 5-second shots are 15 billable seconds at the scene rate — 540 with audio, 450 without. Know which clock the order runs on before the storyboard expands from 2 shots to 4.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Pricing bilingual VO as 2×.</strong> ElevenLabs bills UTF-8 bytes; a Chinese script triples the blocks of its English twin. Budget the CJK lane in bytes, not in sentence counts — the 10-becomes-30 line in the invoice is structural.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist: Standing Up the Art Pipeline This Sprint</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;padding-left:24px;line-height:1.9;">
                <li>Set the three-tier habit in every template: numImages=4 at 1K for exploration, edits at 2K, finals at 4K — write the tiers into the brief, not into memory.</li>
                <li>Build characters as edit chains from one approved file; log the parent file id per character so variation rounds stay traceable.</li>
                <li>Run the sprite batch at 1K with <code>background=transparent</code> stated, and inspect one file''s alpha channel before the other 39 render.</li>
                <li>Storyboard the PV as shot-seconds before ordering: shots × duration × scene rate, with audio off if the track comes from Suno.</li>
                <li>Draft the PV once on the mini lane at 480p before committing the final assembly; one 90-credit rehearsal is the cheapest note you will ever get.</li>
                <li>Budget audio in its own units: Suno counts operations, ElevenLabs counts bytes. A flat per-track and a per-byte line never divide the same way.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">FAQ</h2>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: How much does a full indie game art package cost in credits?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">This package — 120 concept drafts, 6 characters iterated through edits, 40 transparent sprites, key art, a 15-second PV with a draft rehearsal, a theme track, and bilingual VO — totals 1,666 list credits: 1,366 on Basic at 82%, 1,216 on Pro at 73%, 1,049 on Ultra at 63%. The video line is a third of it, and every still-image line is cheaper than the one outsourced email you would have sent instead.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: How do you keep character art consistent across dozens of generations?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">By editing instead of re-rolling. Flux 3 Klein''s edit endpoint costs the same per image as text-to-image (3/6/12 by resolution, ×numImages), and taking the approved silhouette as input means pose and expression rounds inherit the face. Consistency is a property of the chain of files, not of prompt wording.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Can AI generate engine-ready sprites with transparent backgrounds?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Yes, at 1K. GPT Image 2.5''s <code>background=transparent</code> is a 1K-only vendor capability at 6 credits a sprite; the 2K tier bills 18 and may silently deliver an opaque file. Generate sprites at 1K with alpha, then upscale deliberately or let the engine scale — transparency decides the working resolution.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Why does a 15-second trailer cost more than 160 images?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Because video bills per second and images bill per file at draft tiers. The PV final is 15 Σ-seconds at 36/s (1080p with audio) = 540 credits, while the entire still pipeline — batches, edits, sprites, key art — is 936 for 160+ files. Cut the PV''s unused audio to 30/s (−90), rehearse the assembly on the mini lane (+90 before, not after), and the video line shrinks to the size of a decision instead of a surprise.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Is a Suno theme track licensed for a commercial game launch?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">The platform prices Suno operations (generate, extend, add-instrumental, add-vocals, upload-cover, upload-extend) at a flat 20 credits each regardless of output length; commercial usage rights depend on the plan terms at export time, which you should verify against the vendor''s current license before the store page goes live. Budget-wise, a full 3-minute loop assembled in three operations costs 60 credits — cheaper than any stock-license negotiation.</p></div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:0;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Closing: The Art Director Is Now Also the Invoice</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The old economics said indie art was either outsourcing quotes or personal burnout. The new economics says a launch-art package is 1,666 list credits if the pyramid holds — drafts at 1K, consistency by editing, transparency at its legal resolution, video on the Σ clock with the audio checkbox read, and one rehearsal before every final. The pipeline lives inside <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/flux-3-klein-text-to-image" style="color:#818cf8;text-decoration:none;">Flux 3 Klein</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/3-0-omni-image-to-video" style="color:#818cf8;text-decoration:none;">Kling 3.0 Omni image-to-video</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/generate" style="color:#818cf8;text-decoration:none;">Suno</a>, and their neighbors on FuseAI — and the studio''s remaining budget goes where it always wanted to: the next character.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- gpt-image-2-5-flare-sunburst-long-prompt-production-guide (category=2)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'GPT Image 2.5 Flare and Sunburst: What 20,000-Character Prompts Change for Image Production',
  2,
  'gpt-image-2-5-flare-sunburst-long-prompt-production-guide',
  'GPT Image 2.5 Flare and Sunburst: four models, identical 6/18/24 credit pricing, 20,000-character prompts, 4K output — the long-prompt workflow explained.',
  'GPT Image 2.5, Flare, Sunburst, long prompt, transparent background, image to image, 4K image generation, OpenAI image, prompt engineering, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>GPT Image 2.5 Flare and Sunburst: What 20,000-Character Prompts Change for Image Production</title>
    <meta name="description" content="GPT Image 2.5 Flare and Sunburst: four models, identical 6/18/24 credit pricing, 20,000-character prompts, 4K output — the long-prompt workflow explained.">
</head>
<body>
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(ellipse 65% 50% at 50% 0%, rgba(192,132,252,.07), transparent),radial-gradient(ellipse 50% 40% at 100% 90%, rgba(168,85,247,.05), transparent),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318, #0b0c0f);color:#e5e7eb;padding:20px;border-radius:12px;">

        <section class="introduction">
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Prompt length has quietly become a capability ceiling. Short instructions force you to compress a brand guide, a composition plan, and a lighting spec into three lines — and the image inherits every omission. The <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image" style="color:#c084fc;">GPT Image</a> family''s newest release attacks that constraint from two directions at once.</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong>GPT Image 2.5</strong> ships as four models in one breath: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image/v2-5-flare-text-to-image" style="color:#c084fc;">Flare Text-to-Image</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image/v2-5-flare-image-to-image" style="color:#c084fc;">Flare Image-to-Image</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image/v2-5-sunburst-text-to-image" style="color:#c084fc;">Sunburst Text-to-Image</a>, and <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image/v2-5-sunburst-image-to-image" style="color:#c084fc;">Sunburst Image-to-Image</a>. All four accept prompts up to 20,000 characters, render up to 4K, and — unusually — carry identical pricing: 6 credits at 1K, 18 at 2K, 24 at 4K. Flare and Sunburst are twin engines, not price tiers.</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">That design leaves real decisions on the table: what to do with a prompt budget three orders of magnitude larger than most models allow, why transparent backgrounds are a 1K-only privilege, and how to choose between twins that cost the same.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="market-opportunity">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The 2.5 Line-Up, Priced and Discounted</h2>

            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Resolution</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">2.5 Flare / Sunburst (list)</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Basic 82%</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Pro 73%</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Ultra 63%</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Old GPT Image 2 for reference</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">1K</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">6 credits</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~4.9</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~4.4</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~3.8</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">6 credits</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">2K</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">18 credits</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~14.8</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~13.1</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~11.3</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">9 credits</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">4K</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">24 credits</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~19.7</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~17.5</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~15.1</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">12 credits</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The pattern against the previous generation is worth reading carefully: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image/v2-text-to-image" style="color:#c084fc;">GPT Image 2</a> and 2.5 meet at the 1K price of 6 credits, then diverge hard above it — 2.5 doubles the 2K cost and doubles 4K. Translation for production: 2.5''s premium is concentrated in resolution, so the smart default is drafting at 1K where both generations are the same price, and paying 2.5''s 4K rate only for finals that will be printed, upsized, or zoomed.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="workflow">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Three Workflows the 2.5 Contract Enables</h2>

            <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Workflow 1 — Write the Brief, Not the Haiku</h3>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">A 20,000-character prompt budget — the same ceiling Wan 3.0 gives its video model — means the spec sheet <em>is</em> the prompt. Subject, composition, lens, lighting, palette, typography rules, and "what must not change" can all be stated literally instead of being improvised by the model.</p>

            <div style="background:rgba(31,41,55,0.3);padding:16px;border-radius:8px;margin:16px 0;">
                <p style="color:#c084fc;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:8px;">Structure long prompts as blocks: [Objective] → [Subject & action] → [Scene & era] → [Composition & camera] → [Light & palette] → [Brand constraints, verbatim] → [Negative list]. The model stops guessing in the exact proportion you stopped specifying.</p>
                <p style="color:#9ca3af;">Keep one master brief per campaign and diff it per placement — long-prompt discipline is version control for images.</p>
            </div>

            <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Workflow 2 — Cut-Out Assets: Transparent Backgrounds at 1K</h3>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">2.5 accepts a <code>background</code> parameter (transparent, opaque, or auto), which turns generated subjects into drop-in compositing assets — stickers, packaging mockups, presentation cut-outs — without a separate background-removal pass. The catch is the vendor''s own constraint: transparency is only supported at 1K. Since 1K costs 6 credits either way, treat transparent 1K output as the workhorse asset format and reserve 2K/4K for when the subject ships inside a full scene.</p>

            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#c084fc;">Ratio quirks:</strong> thirteen aspect ratios are offered, but the four unusual ones (27:16, 16:27, 9:8, 8:9) are also 1K-only — the exotic frames and transparency share the same resolution gate.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#c084fc;">Composition chain:</strong> generate a transparent subject here, then place it into scenes via <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image/v2-5-sunburst-image-to-image" style="color:#c084fc;">Sunburst Image-to-Image</a> or the older <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image/v2-image-to-image" style="color:#c084fc;">GPT Image v2 Image-to-Image</a> when budget decides.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Workflow 3 — Twin Testing: Flare vs Sunburst Is a Style Choice</h3>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Because Flare and Sunburst share the identical parameter contract and identical prices, choosing between them is purely empirical — run the same 1K prompt through both and let output quality decide, with zero cost confusion. A practical protocol: render every campaign keyframe once in each twin at 1K (12 credits list total), pick the winner per brand, and standardize. Image-to-image jobs are equally symmetric, with one notable twist: the 2.5 i2i models accept up to 16 input images and treat them as optional, so a prompt-only call is legal — reference material augments the instruction instead of gating it.</p>

            <div style="background:rgba(31,41,55,0.3);padding:16px;border-radius:8px;margin:16px 0;">
                <p style="color:#c084fc;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:8px;">Editing with references: attach the product photo plus six style shots as inputs, then instruct "render the product in the visual language of the references — same lighting temperature, same depth of field." The optional-input design is what lets one call carry both subject and style context.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="pitfalls">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">What Trips People Up on 2.5</h2>

            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Risk</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">What happens</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Countermeasure</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Assuming resolution is optional</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">2.5 requires an explicit 1K/2K/4K choice — the billing key has no null fallback, and relying on "it''ll default" fails at submission.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Set resolution as a deliberate first step in every request.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Asking for transparency at 4K</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Transparent backgrounds are a 1K capability; pairing them with 2K/4K requests gets rejected downstream.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Cut assets at 1K; if you need scale, composite the 1K cut-out into a higher-res scene.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Defaulting to 4K habitually</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">2.5 charges 24 for 4K where GPT Image 2 charged 12 — the doubling only bites if you keep the old reflex.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Draft and iterate at 1K (unchanged at 6), graduate winners upward.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Long-prompt soup</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">20,000 characters is headroom, not a target; rambling prompts dilute the constraints that matter.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Block-structured briefs; move repeatable constraints into a template, keep each ask tight.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Exotic ratios at the wrong size</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">27:16, 16:27, 9:8 and 8:9 exist only at 1K — planning a 2K banner in 27:16 breaks at submission.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Check the ratio-resolution compatibility before locking layout specs.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </section>

        <section class="action-checklist">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist</h2>
            <ol style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#c084fc;">Run the twin bake-off once.</strong> Same 1K prompt through Flare and Sunburst; record per-brand winners so the choice is made, not re-litigated.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#c084fc;">Convert one brief to block structure.</strong> Objective / subject / scene / camera / light / constraints / negatives — feel what the 20k budget is for.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#c084fc;">Move cut-out assets to 1K transparent.</strong> 6 credits list (~3.8 on Ultra monthly) replaces the generate-then-remove-background two-step.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#c084fc;">Re-baseline the 4K budget.</strong> Anything that used GPT Image 2 at 4K now costs double — re-quote recurring deliverables before renewing them blindly.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#c084fc;">Use multi-reference i2i for style transfer.</strong> Up to 16 inputs let subject and style travel in one request.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#c084fc;">Keep a legacy escape hatch.</strong> For pure-1K high-volume jobs, older members like the <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image/generate" style="color:#c084fc;">original GPT Image line</a> still compete on price per call.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section class="faq">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: What''s the difference between Flare and Sunburst?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Interface and price: none. Both accept the same parameters and bill 6/18/24 credits at 1K/2K/4K, in both text-to-image and image-to-image flavors. They are twin rendering engines, so the selection method is empirical — generate the same keyframe in both and keep whichever suits the brand''s visual language.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: How long can a GPT Image 2.5 prompt actually be?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Up to 20,000 characters — enough to embed an entire campaign brief, brand constraints, and negative list in one instruction. The length is optional headroom, not overhead: short prompts work exactly as before.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Can I get transparent backgrounds from generated images?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Yes, at 1K only. The background parameter offers transparent, opaque, or auto, and the four narrow-form factor ratios (27:16, 16:27, 9:8, 8:9) share the same 1K restriction. At 6 credits list — roughly 3.8 after the Ultra monthly discount — transparent cut-outs are the cheapest compositing asset on the model.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Why does 2.5 cost more than GPT Image 2 at high resolution?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">2.5 reprices the resolution curve: 1K stays at 6 credits while 2K moves from 9 to 18 and 4K from 12 to 24, reflecting the higher-output tier''s real rendering cost. The workaround is structural, not financial: iterate at 1K where generations cost the same, and reserve 2K/4K for approved finals.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Do the image-to-image models require input images?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">No — unlike earlier image-editing models in the family, the 2.5 i2i inputs are optional and accept up to 16 images when present. A prompt-only call is valid, which makes the i2i endpoints usable as reference-augmented generations rather than strictly edits.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="closing">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Take</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">GPT Image 2.5''s headline isn''t quality-per-pixel — it''s instruction bandwidth. A 20,000-character ceiling, optional 16-image reference context, and native transparency move AI image generation closer to how design briefs actually get communicated: specified, not hinted. The repriced 2K/4K tiers are the honest trade, and they''re avoidable by teams who draft where the price didn''t move.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Try <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image/v2-5-flare-text-to-image" style="color:#c084fc;">GPT Image 2.5 Flare</a> or <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image/v2-5-sunburst-text-to-image" style="color:#c084fc;">Sunburst on FuseAITools</a> for long-instruction image production.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- grok-chat-flat-rate-reasoning-ladder-guide (category=1)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'Grok Chat''s Flat-Rate Ladder: grok-4-5, grok-4-6 and grok-4-7, and Why Thinking Level Is Your Only Budget Dial',
  1,
  'grok-chat-flat-rate-reasoning-ladder-guide',
  'Grok Chat''s grok-4-5/4-6/4-7 share identical $2.49/$7.41 per-million pricing across four thinking levels — why the flat ladder changes upgrade logic.',
  'Grok Chat, grok-4-5, grok-4-6, grok-4-7, xAI, reasoning model, thinking level, token pricing, flat rate LLM, real-time analysis, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Grok Chat''s Flat-Rate Ladder: grok-4-5, grok-4-6 and grok-4-7, and Why Thinking Level Is Your Only Budget Dial</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Grok Chat''s grok-4-5/4-6/4-7 share identical $2.49/$7.41 per-million pricing across four thinking levels — why the flat ladder changes upgrade logic.">
</head>
<body>
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(ellipse 65% 50% at 50% 0%, rgba(96,165,250,.07), transparent),radial-gradient(ellipse 50% 40% at 100% 90%, rgba(59,130,246,.05), transparent),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318, #0b0c0f);color:#e5e7eb;padding:20px;border-radius:12px;">

        <section class="introduction">
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">LLM pricing usually works like an airline: newer seat, higher fare. The newly live <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok-chat" style="color:#60a5fa;">Grok Chat</a> models on FuseAITools break that pattern. <strong style="color:#60a5fa;">grok-4-5, grok-4-6 and grok-4-7</strong> are all billed at exactly the same rate — 249 credits per million input tokens and 741 credits per million output tokens, i.e. $2.49 / $7.41 at the platform''s 100-credits-per-dollar convention.</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">When the price column doesn''t move, the entire decision structure shifts. Upgrading across the three generations costs nothing. The variables that remain are the four thinking levels (low, medium, high, xhigh) — and one consequential trade baked into the protocol: on these reasoning models, tool use and web search are disabled when extended thinking is on.</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">This piece reads those two facts as an operations manual: what a flat-rate model ladder does to an analysis workflow, and how to spend on reasoning deliberately instead of accidentally.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="market-opportunity">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">A Price Sheet With No Trade-Offs — On Purpose</h2>

            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Model</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Input (credits / 1M tokens)</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Output (credits / 1M tokens)</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Thinking levels</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Search / tools while thinking</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">grok-4-5</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">249</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">741</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">low / medium / high / xhigh</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Disabled</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">grok-4-6</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">249</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">741</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">low / medium / high / xhigh</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Disabled</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">grok-4-7</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">249</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">741</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">low / medium / high / xhigh</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Disabled</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Three practical consequences of identical pricing across a version ladder:</p>

            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#60a5fa;">Default to newest, always.</strong> There is no budget argument for an older generation; grok-4-7 becomes the starting point and the others become control groups for A/B verification, not cost save-offs.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#60a5fa;">Cost predictability across upgrades.</strong> A pipeline quoted on grok-4-5 economics keeps its quote when you migrate to 4-7 — version bumping becomes a quality decision with zero finance review.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#60a5fa;">Output is the bill, not input.</strong> At 741 vs 249 credits per million, generated tokens cost roughly 3× consumed ones; on a reasoning model that "thinks before answering," the thinking budget is what actually moves your invoice. Subscription plan discounts still stack on top of token billing (monthly Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63%).</li>
            </ul>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">For context against the rest of the chat lineup, the same $/M-token table applies to the <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt" style="color:#60a5fa;">GPT</a> and <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/claude" style="color:#60a5fa;">Claude</a> endpoints — but Grok''s three-deep flat ladder is the cleanest "newest by default" structure in the set.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="workflow">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Two Workflows Built on the Flat Ladder</h2>

            <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Workflow 1 — The Version-Cycling Regression Harness</h3>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">When every model on a ladder costs the same, running your eval suite on all three stops being a luxury. Trend-analysis and social-monitoring teams can send the identical brief to grok-4-5, 4-6, and 4-7 at the same budget and diff the answers — measuring what a generation actually improved, in their domain, on their data.</p>

            <div style="background:rgba(31,41,55,0.3);padding:16px;border-radius:8px;margin:16px 0;">
                <p style="color:#60a5fa;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:8px;">Cost anchor: a regression run of 20 briefs × 1,500 input tokens + 800 output tokens per model ≈ 30k in + 16k out per generation — about 20 credits per model pass at list rates, all three combined for roughly the price of a coffee.</p>
                <p style="color:#9ca3af;">Pin the thinking level when comparing versions, or you''re measuring the dial, not the model.</p>
            </div>

            <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Workflow 2 — Split the Pipeline: Search Off the Thinking Path</h3>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The one constraint that reorganizes everything: these Grok models disable tools and web search while extended thinking is enabled. The sound architecture is therefore two-stage rather than one mega-call:</p>

            <ol style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#60a5fa;">Stage 1 — Gather at low/no thinking.</strong> Fresh-signal collection (current events, live sentiment pulls) runs with thinking dialled down so search stays available, keeping retrieval cheap at 249 credits/M input.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#60a5fa;">Stage 2 — Reason over the snapshot at high/xhigh.</strong> Paste the collected material into a thinking-enabled call and let the model do adversarial reading — causal chains, contrarian takes, scenario trees — with no retrieval needed because the evidence is already in the prompt.</li>
            </ol>

            <div style="background:rgba(31,41,55,0.3);padding:16px;border-radius:8px;margin:16px 0;">
                <p style="color:#60a5fa;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:8px;">Sizing note: reasoning output is billed output. A high-thinking analytical pass generating 2,000 tokens costs ~1.5 credits at 741/M — trivial alone, but multiplied across a 200-request daily queue it''s 300 credits/day list, so set the level per job class instead of globally maxing xhigh.</p>
                <p style="color:#9ca3af;">The model''s reasoning trace is not returned as visible text on these Grok endpoints — you get the answer, not the scratchpad. Bake that into expectations: verification comes from cross-version checks (Workflow 1), not chain-of-thought inspection. Where visible reasoning output matters, the <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kimi" style="color:#60a5fa;">Kimi</a> endpoint exposes reasoning summaries instead.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="pitfalls">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Failure Modes of a Flat Price List</h2>

            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Risk</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">What happens</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Countermeasure</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Assuming "newer = pricier" and underusing 4-7</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Teams keep legacy-model habits and route work to grok-4-5 for imagined savings that are exactly zero.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Make the newest model the org default; older tiers only for benchmarking.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Expecting live search inside deep thinking</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">With extended thinking on, tools/search are unavailable — requests silently lose the retrieval channel users assume they still have.</td>
                        <td style="padding:12px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Run the two-stage pipeline: collect first, reason second, never both at once.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">xhigh as a global default</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Thinking tokens bill as output at 741/M; volume queues inflate spend invisibly because input looks cheap.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Map levels to job classes: low for extraction, medium for drafting, high/xhigh reserved for adjudication tasks.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Wanting the reasoning trace</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">These endpoints don''t emit visible chain-of-thought; audit expectations built on reading the scratchpad collapse.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Audit via multi-version consensus instead of trace inspection.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Small per-request amounts hiding real spend</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">A typical chat turn costs cents; teams that never see a "big bill" can still bleed thousands of credits monthly at agent-scale call counts.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Track credits per 1,000 requests by thinking level — that''s the honest unit price of an automated workflow.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </section>

        <section class="action-checklist">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist</h2>
            <ol style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#60a5fa;">Set grok-4-7 as default, 4-5/4-6 as auditors.</strong> Flat pricing removes the upgrade cost argument entirely.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#60a5fa;">Publish a thinking-level map.</strong> One level per job class; ban global xhigh.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#60a5fa;">Rebuild retrieval as stage one.</strong> Anything needing live search runs with thinking reduced, feeding snapshots to the reasoning pass.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#60a5fa;">Meter by output tokens.</strong> 741 credits/M makes generated volume — including hidden thinking — the real invoice driver.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#60a5fa;">Run monthly version diffs.</strong> Re-run the eval trio; flat pricing means progress audits are effectively free.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#60a5fa;">Route trace-dependent audits elsewhere.</strong> When reviewers need visible reasoning, send those jobs to endpoints that surface summaries rather than hiding the scratchpad.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section class="faq">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Are grok-4-5, grok-4-6 and grok-4-7 really the same price?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Yes, identically: 249 credits per million input tokens and 741 per million output on all three — $2.49 / $7.41 at platform rates. Subscription plan discounts (monthly Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63%) apply uniformly, so moving up the generation ladder never changes your bill.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Can Grok Chat search the web while deep thinking is on?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">No. On these models, enabling extended thinking disables tools and web search. The workable pattern is a two-stage call chain: retrieve with thinking at low while search is active, then reason over the retrieved text with thinking raised — the analysis stage no longer needs the search stage''s tools.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: What do the four thinking levels actually change?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">They set how much reasoning effort the model spends before answering — low through xhigh — and since hidden thinking bills as output at 741 credits/M, the level is the primary cost dial on a flat-priced model. The deep-thinking toggle maps to the high level by default.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Will I see the model''s reasoning steps?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Not on these Grok endpoints — the reasoning trace stays internal and only the final answer is returned. If your workflow requires inspecting the model''s working, use cross-version agreement checks or pick an endpoint family that publishes reasoning summaries.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Which jobs is Grok Chat best suited for?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Analytical reads over provided material: trend interpretation, contrarian stress-testing of a thesis, summarizing contested events with the model''s characteristically direct style. Pair it with retrieval-stage tooling when facts must be current, and keep the high-thinking tiers for judgment-heavy steps.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="closing">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Take</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">A flat price list across three model generations is a quiet design decision with loud implications: quality progression becomes free, evaluation becomes routine, and the only remaining budget conversation is how hard you ask the model to think. Grok Chat''s real discipline is that on a ladder with no rungs priced differently, the thinking dial is the whole bill.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Try <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok-chat" style="color:#60a5fa;">Grok Chat on FuseAITools</a> for flat-rate reasoning workflows across grok-4-5, 4-6 and 4-7.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- grok-imagine-video-low-price-tier-upgrade-history (category=4)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  '6 / 12 / 24 a Second, Unchanged: The History of Grok Imagine''s Low-Price Tier and Its Upgrade-First Economics',
  4,
  'grok-imagine-video-low-price-tier-upgrade-history',
  'Grok Imagine video pricing history: the 6-credit per-second floor, extend''s flat menu, the upscale ladder, and 1.5 preview''s one-second entry point.',
  'Grok Imagine video pricing history, per-second AI video credits, video upscale cost vs render, grok imagine extend pricing, low price tier one second video, AI video rate card evolution, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>6 / 12 / 24 a Second, Unchanged: The History of Grok Imagine''s Low-Price Tier and Its Upgrade-First Economics</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Grok Imagine video pricing history: the 6-credit per-second floor, extend''s flat menu, the upscale ladder, and 1.5 preview''s one-second entry point.">
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/news/grok-imagine-video-low-price-tier-upgrade-history">
</head>
<body style="margin:0;padding:0;background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(circle at 15% 0%, rgba(251,113,133,0.07) 0%, transparent 45%),radial-gradient(circle at 85% 100%, rgba(129,140,248,0.05) 0%, transparent 45%),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318 0%, #0b0c0f 100%);color:#d1d5db;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="max-width:960px;margin:0 auto;padding:32px 20px 64px;">
        <header style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h1 itemprop="headline" style="font-size:2rem;line-height:1.25;color:#f9fafb;margin:0 0 16px;">6 / 12 / 24 a Second, Unchanged: The History of Grok Imagine''s Low-Price Tier and Its Upgrade-First Economics</h1>
            <p style="color:#9ca3af;font-size:0.95rem;margin:0;">Published <time itemprop="datePublished" datetime="2026-11-12">November 12, 2026</time> · FuseAI Tools Editorial</p>
        </header>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Introduction: A Price Curve That Went Sideways — on Purpose</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Most model-family histories on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok" style="color:#fb7185;text-decoration:none;">FuseAI</a>''s rate card are stories of numbers moving: Kling''s ladder collapsing, Wan''s unit changing underneath the same price. The <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/text-to-video" style="color:#fb7185;text-decoration:none;">Grok Imagine video</a> family is the counter-example, and in its own way the more instructive fossil layer. Its per-second ladder — <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">6 at 480p, 12 at 720p, 24 at 1080p</strong> — has sat on the card unchanged since the lane launched. What changed is everything around it: the <em>minimum commitment</em> fell from six seconds to one, a <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/extend" style="color:#fb7185;text-decoration:none;">continuation lane</a> appeared with flat menu rows that quietly undercut fresh renders, and an <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/upscale" style="color:#fb7185;text-decoration:none;">upscale trio</a> established that on this card, resolution is cheaper to buy at generation than to buy afterward — and you pay the premium anyway, for reasons that have nothing to do with saving.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">This is the low-price-tier formation story: not a race to the bottom on unit rate, but a systematic shrinking of the <em>smallest thing you can buy</em>. All figures are list credits; the standing monthly-plan discounts — Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63% — apply per lane, so the reference render used throughout, an 8-second 720p clip on the mainline at 96 list, lands at roughly 79/70/60 credits by plan.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Founding Ladder: Cheap Per Second, Expensive Per Order</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The founding pair — <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/text-to-video" style="color:#fb7185;text-decoration:none;">text-to-video</a> and its <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/image-to-video" style="color:#fb7185;text-decoration:none;">image-to-video</a> twin — bill a per-second rate against a duration window of <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">6 to 30 seconds</strong>. The rates are among the lowest on the platform: 6 credits a second at 480p is half of Wan 3.0''s floor rate for the same rung. But read the window, not the rate, and the original economics invert: the shortest legal video was a <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">36-credit minimum order</strong> (6 s × 6), and a serious 20-second 720p render ran 240. The founding card was already a clean doubling ladder — 6/12/24, exactly 2× per resolution rung, with resolution doing all the pricing work and duration a pure meter — but it asked for a real commitment before you could discover whether the model could deliver the shot.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Flat-Menu Fossil: Extend Retained Old-Era Prices After the Conversion</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Then came the family''s one structural inconsistency — and its most useful arbitrage. <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/extend" style="color:#fb7185;text-decoration:none;">grok-imagine-extend</a> does not bill per second. It bills an <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">absolute duration × quality menu</strong> with exactly four rows: 6 seconds at <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">24</strong> (480p) or 45 (720p), 10 seconds at 42 or 72. This is the last flat-menu shape in a per-second family, and rate-card archaeology caught it being made: as late as 2026-10-12 the lane had <em>no pricing rows at all</em> — the four were retrofitted in a single fill-in. Do the division the menu refuses to advertise and the rows hide a discount: 24/6 = 4.0 credits a second against the mainline''s 6, 45/6 = 7.5 against 12, 42/10 = 4.2, 72/10 = 7.2. Extending an existing clip runs <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">30–40% below the fresh-render rate</strong> at the same resolution. Continuation is the cheapest incremental video on the card — the rate card''s way of paying you to build on footage it already billed for.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Upgrade Ladder: Three Legal Rungs, Priced as Insurance</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/upscale" style="color:#fb7185;text-decoration:none;">upscale lane</a> is where the family''s design opinions show most clearly. It bills per second <em>of the source video''s actual duration</em>, across exactly three legal combinations: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">480p→720p at 15/s, 720p→1080p at 30/s, 480p→1080p at 45/s</strong>. Anything else — including any attempt to upscale a 1080p source — finds no pricing row and is refused. Two facts fall out of those three numbers. First, the ladder is perfectly additive: 15 + 30 = 45, so taking the stairs through a 720p intermediate costs <em>exactly</em> what the single jump does — you are never double-taxed for stopping on the landing, which also means the intermediate step is never wasted money if you wanted the 720p file too. Second, and this is the insurance part: every upgrade rung costs <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">more per second than just rendering at the target</strong> — 15 against 12 for 720p, 30 and 45 against 24 for 1080p. Upscaling on this card is not a cost optimization. It is what you buy when the composition is already approved and re-rendering it at higher resolution risks getting a <em>different video</em>.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Run the canonical example: you need 8 seconds at 1080p. Rendering native on the mainline bills 8 × 24 = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">192</strong>. Rendering it at 480p (48) and upgrading bills 8 × 45 = 360 on top, <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">408 total — more than double</strong>. Nobody takes that route for the savings. They take it when the 480p file already won the room and the client wants it big.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Low-Price Tier Arrives: 1.5 Preview Keeps the Rate, Drops the Floor</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The generation that answers the founding lane''s 36-credit minimum is <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/video-1-5-preview" style="color:#fb7185;text-decoration:none;">grok-imagine-video-1-5-preview</a>, and its strategy is pure floor-shaving. The per-second rates are <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">identical to the mainline</strong>: 6 at 480p, 12 at 720p. What changes is everything about the commitment: the duration window opens at <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">one second</strong> (runs 1–15, defaulting to 8), so the cheapest legal video on the family drops from 36 credits to <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">6</strong>. The prompt becomes optional — an image order with no text is legal. And the entry unifies: no image attached is text-to-video, images attached (up to seven) is image-informed generation, one priced lane either way. It is a neat coincidence of the current card that one second of 480p preview video bills <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">the same 6 credits as a single <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/text-to-image" style="color:#fb7185;text-decoration:none;">Grok image</a></strong> — the video floor has dropped to per-image pricing.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The tell is what the preview lane <em>doesn''t</em> sell. The upstream model supports 1080p; the preview''s pricing rows cover only 480p and 720p, and 1080p requests are rejected by the DTO whitelist for lack of a price. That is the low-price-tier playbook in one line: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">make the cheap lane cheap to enter, not cheap to use, and close the exit to the premium resolution</strong>. At 6/sec the preview is not discounted against the mainline — it is the mainline''s lower deck, with a six-times-smaller door and no staircase up to 1080p. Serious finals still bill on the founding ladder; the preview exists so a try costs the price of a 480p second, not the price of six of them.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Family as a Table: Five Lanes, One Unchanged Ladder</h2>
            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:20px;font-size:0.92rem;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:#1f2937;">
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:left;">Lane</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:left;">Billing shape</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:left;">Window</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">480p</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">720p</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">1080p</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">text-to-video / image-to-video</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">per second</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">6–30 s</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">6/s</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">12/s</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">24/s</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">video-1-5-preview</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">per second</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">1–15 s</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">6/s</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">12/s</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">no price</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">extend</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">flat menu (abs.)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">6 s / 10 s</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">24 / 42</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">45 / 72</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">—</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">upscale (source → target)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">per source-second</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">actual seconds</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">—</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">15/s (from 480)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">30/s (from 720) · 45/s (from 480)</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;margin-top:16px;">One honest footnote on naming: rate-card readers keep asking where the next-numbered generation''s row is. The answer this family gives is that <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">generations arrive as lanes, not as edits</strong> — the ladder above is the complete Grok video card, the 6/12/24 rates have never moved, and any price you cannot point to in a row like these is a price that was never on this card. History here is additive geometry: new floors, new side doors, same staircase.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Pitfalls: Five Ways to Misprice the Grok Video Card</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Treating upscale as the budget route to 1080p.</strong> It never is. The 8-second example is the proof: native 1080p bills 192; rendering 480p and upgrading bills 408 — more than double. Upscale buys composition lock-in, not savings, and quoting it as a cost play overprices the exact thing it exists to protect.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Multiplying extend like a per-second lane.</strong> Extend is the family''s flat-menu fossil: 6 or 10 seconds only, 480p or 720p only, prices 24/45/42/72 per order. A "12-second 480p extension at 4.2/s" is not a quote — the lane will not sell 12 seconds, and its effective rates come from dividing rows the menu never divides for you.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Ordering 1080p on the preview lane.</strong> The upstream model can do it; the card cannot. 1080p has no pricing row on video-1-5-preview and the request is refused before a job is created. Final-resolution work belongs on the mainline, which is the whole point of the tier split.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Assuming the two duration windows are interchangeable.</strong> The mainline starts at 6 seconds; the preview stops at 15. A 3-second sting cannot be bought on the mainline at any price, and a 20-second cut cannot be bought on the preview — the overlap is only 6 through 15, and outside it the lane choice is made for you.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Trying to upgrade from the top rung.</strong> Upscale has exactly three legal source→target combinations and 1080p appears in none of the source positions: a finished 1080p clip has nowhere to go up to, and a 720p source can only go to 1080p, not sideways. Design the ladder walk before you generate, because the card will not let you improvise it.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist: Buying Grok Video the Way the Card Is Shaped</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;padding-left:24px;line-height:1.9;">
                <li>Open every new concept on the preview lane at 1 second / 480p — 6 credits is the cheapest legal video on the family and the rate-per-second is no worse than the mainline''s.</li>
                <li>Cross the 6-second line into finals on the mainline only after the shot is proven; at 6/12/24 per second, the mainline''s window (6–30 s) is where durations over 15 and any 1080p output must live.</li>
                <li>Grow approved clips with extend, not re-rolls: the flat menu''s effective 4.0–7.2 per second runs 30–40% under fresh-render rates.</li>
                <li>Buy 1080p at generation (24/s), not at upgrade (30–45/s) — unless the lower-resolution file is already approved, in which case the upgrade premium is the price of not re-rolling a winner.</li>
                <li>Quote the ladder additively when you want the intermediate file anyway: 480→720→1080 costs 15+30 = exactly the 45/s of the direct jump, so the stopover is free optionality.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">FAQ</h2>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: What is the cheapest AI video you can order on Grok Imagine?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">One second of 480p on the video-1-5-preview lane: 6 list credits, just under 5 after any plan''s discount. The mainline''s identical 6-per-second rate hides behind a six-second minimum, making its cheapest legal order 36 credits — the preview lane''s whole function is selling the same staircase through a smaller door.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Is upscaling cheaper than rendering at high resolution?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">On this card, never. Every upgrade rung prices above the native rate for its target — 15 per second against 12 for 720p, 30 and 45 against 24 for 1080p. Upscaling buys the guarantee that the exact approved composition appears at higher resolution, which a fresh render cannot promise; it is insurance on footage you have already paid to lock in.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Why does the extend lane use a flat menu when everything else is per-second?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">It is the family''s surviving flat-menu shape — pricing rows retrofitted on 2026-10-12 after the lane briefly had none. Four rows cover 6 and 10 seconds at 480p and 720p (24/45/42/72 credits), and dividing them shows the rows embed a 30–40% continuation discount against fresh-render rates: the cheapest incremental seconds on the card are extensions of clips that already exist.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Can the preview lane produce 1080p output?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">No — by design, not omission. The preview carries pricing rows only for 480p and 720p, and 1080p requests are refused at the request-whitelist layer because no price exists to bill. Top-resolution finals belong on the mainline ladder; keeping the premium rung off the cheap lane is what keeps it a cheap lane.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Has Grok Imagine video pricing changed since launch?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">The core ladder has not moved a credit: 6/12/24 per second at 480/720/1080p, doubling per rung, spans the entire published history. What evolved is the geometry around the ladder — a one-second floor, an optional-prompt unified entry with up to seven reference images, four retrofitted flat extend rows, and three upscale rungs. When this family "cuts price," it cuts the minimum purchase, not the rate.</p></div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:0;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Closing: The Floor Is the Feature</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Grok Imagine''s pricing history teaches the lesson the collapsing ladders on this platform don''t: a low-price tier is not a low price. It is a small unit — one second instead of six, an optional prompt, two rungs instead of three — wrapped around a rate that never dropped, so that trying costs six credits and committing costs whatever the unchanged ladder says it does. Buy concept on the preview, finals on the mainline, continuations on the flat menu, and upgrades only when the composition is the asset. Every lane above bills live on FuseAI, from the <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/image-to-video" style="color:#fb7185;text-decoration:none;">founding per-second pair</a> through the <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/video-1-5-preview" style="color:#fb7185;text-decoration:none;">preview floor</a> to the <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/extend" style="color:#fb7185;text-decoration:none;">extend rows</a>. Try <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/text-to-video" style="color:#fb7185;text-decoration:none;">FuseAITools</a> for Grok Imagine video generation with per-second pricing from one-second orders.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- image-to-video-reference-control-shootout (category=4)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'How Many References Does Your Video Model Need? Kling 3.0 Omni vs Wan 3.0 vs Seedance 2.5 vs HappyHorse 1.1, Measured in What You Can Feed It',
  4,
  'image-to-video-reference-control-shootout-kling-wan-seedance-happyhorse',
  'Reference capacity compared across Kling 3.0 Omni, Wan 3.0, Seedance 2.5, and HappyHorse 1.1 — and how uploads change the per-second price.',
  'image to video AI, reference video conditioning, Kling 3.0 Omni, Wan 3.0 reference, Seedance 2.5 with video, HappyHorse reference, consistency AI video, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>How Many References Does Your Video Model Need? Kling 3.0 Omni vs Wan 3.0 vs Seedance 2.5 vs HappyHorse 1.1, Measured in What You Can Feed It</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Reference capacity compared across Kling 3.0 Omni, Wan 3.0, Seedance 2.5, and HappyHorse 1.1 — and how uploads change the per-second price.">
</head>
<body>
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(ellipse 65% 50% at 50% 0%, rgba(96,165,250,.07), transparent),radial-gradient(ellipse 50% 40% at 100% 90%, rgba(244,114,182,.05), transparent),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318, #0b0c0f);color:#e5e7eb;padding:20px;border-radius:12px;">

        <section class="introduction">
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Prompting a video model is bargaining. Feeding it references is a contract. And the four serious image/reference-to-video lines on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com" style="color:#60a5fa;">FuseAITools</a> accept wildly different contracts: one demands exactly a single frame and won''t negotiate; one swallows ten images, five videos, and five audio tracks at once; one gives you a 40% discount for uploading motion references; and one ships a dedicated reference model as its flagship input. This shootout measures the models by the material they''ll digest — because that''s the spec that determines whether your character, product, or camera move survives the render.</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">All rates are list credits per second (100 credits = $1); monthly plans meter at <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63%</strong>. Where references move the price, that''s the story, not a footnote.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="capacity">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Capacity Table</h2>
            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Model</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Images</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Videos</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Audio</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Price effect of references</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/3-0-omni-image-to-video" style="color:#f472b6;">Kling 3.0 Omni image-to-video</a></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">exactly 1</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">—</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">—</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">None — frame is the job</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/3-0-omni-reference-to-video" style="color:#f472b6;">Kling 3.0 Omni reference-to-video</a></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">optional pack</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">yes (scene: with_video)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">via audio scenes</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">1080p: silent 30, audio 36, ref-video 45/sec (+50%)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan 3.0 reference mode</a></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">≤10</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">≤5</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">≤5</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">None — 12/24/48 per sec regardless</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedance/v2-5" style="color:#a78bfa;">Seedance 2.5 with reference video</a></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">image-to-video ✓</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">conditioning input</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">—</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Cheaper: 1080p 250 → 150/sec (−40%)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/happy-horse/v1-1-reference-to-video" style="color:#34d399;">HappyHorse 1.1 reference-to-video</a></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">1–9</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">edit mode in family</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">native audio out</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">None — flat 36/45 per sec by resolution</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/image-to-video" style="color:#60a5fa;">Grok Video 1.5 Preview</a></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">presence flips mode</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">—</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">—</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">None — 6/12 per sec either way</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </section>

        <section class="styles">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Four Reference Philosophies</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f472b6;">Kling''s discipline (Omni image-to-video): exactly one frame, aspect ratio locked to auto.</strong> It sounds ascetic, it''s actually a workflow: the frame comes pre-composed elsewhere — from a <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-0-motion-control" style="color:#f472b6;">motion-control pass</a> or an external keyframe — and Kling animates it without reinterpretation. When the look is already decided, single-reference is the lowest-drift option. The <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/3-0-omni-transformation" style="color:#f472b6;">transformation model</a> extends the idea to video-in (plus ≤4 reference images) at 30–100/sec, no audio generated.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan''s buffet (≤10 images + 5 videos + 5 audio, at no surcharge):</strong> the widest contract on the shelf. Cast a character from three angles, lock a product from five, hand it a motion sketch and a voice timbre — one generation, one price (12/24/48 per second by resolution, 2–30 seconds). Its <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video-prime" style="color:#60a5fa;">Prime sibling (80/40/20 per sec)</a> takes identical inputs at express-line speed. The catch is not price but mutual exclusion: first/last-frame mode can''t mix with reference packs, and file vs link references are one-or-the-other. Planning the input bundle is half the craft.</p>
            <p style="color:#a78bfa;">Seedance''s inversion: references make it cheaper.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Across the line — <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedance/v2" style="color:#a78bfa;">Seedance 2</a> (18–324/sec by tier) and 2.5 — attaching a reference video moves the scene from without_video to with_video and cuts 40%+ off every rate (480p: 45 → 27; 1080p: 250 → 150; on 2-series, 480p 30 → 18). The pricing is a workflow instruction: <em>if you can shoot or sketch the motion, do — the model renders closer to intention and bills less for the privilege.</em> Duration runs 4–30 seconds.</p>
            <p style="color:#34d399;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">HappyHorse''s specialization:</strong> reference-to-video isn''t a mode, it''s a model in the family — 1–9 reference images, 9 aspect ratios, native audio, 36/45 per sec at 720p/1080p. Where Wan asks "how much can you throw at me," HappyHorse asks "how consistent should the subject be" and prices the answer flat.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="pitfalls">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Reference Workflow Traps</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Counting uploads instead of roles.</strong> Ten images of one product from ten angles ≠ ten references'' worth of information. Redundant views dilute; complementary views (front/side/detail/hands-in-use) lock. Wan''s cap is 10 slots, but craft is filling 4–6 well.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Forgetting the reference changes the price — in both directions.</strong> Seedance drops 40%; Kling Omni reference scenes rise (1080p: 30 silent → 45 with ref video). A "free" workflow on one line is a 50% surcharge on another.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Mixing incompatible modes.</strong> Wan rejects last-frame without first-frame, and frame + reference packs are mutually exclusive by design; HappyHorse 1.1 dropped seed acceptance entirely, so seed-pinned workflows from v1 don''t port.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Paying full rate for conditioning you could have drafted.</strong> A phone-cam walkaround uploaded to Seedance cuts the render price 40%; the same walkaround described in prompt text costs full without_video and lands further from intent.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="action-checklist">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist</h2>
            <ol style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Match the model to your material, then the material to the model.</strong> One perfect frame → Kling Omni. Multi-asset brand kit → Wan. Motion you can demo on your phone → Seedance. 1–9 subject views for consistency → HappyHorse.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Build a reference sheet once per character/product</strong> (4–6 complementary angles) and reuse it across every generation — consistency is an asset-management problem.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Quote Seedance renders in with_video pricing when you have footage.</strong> A 12-second 1080p conditioning pass is 1,800 credits (150/sec) not 3,000 — monthly plans take it to ~1,134 at Ultra 63%.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Test mode-mutex rules with a checklist, not a 480-credit error.</strong> First/last vs reference, file vs link, seed support per version — verify against the form before batch runs.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Let references carry what prompts can''t:</strong> camera feel, product proportions, voice timbre (Wan''s 5 audio slots). The reference pack is the brief; the prompt is only the shot.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section class="faq">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Which AI video model accepts the most reference material?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Wan 3.0: up to 10 images plus 5 videos plus 5 audio files in its reference mode, at no price surcharge (per-second rates stay 12/24/48 by resolution, Ultra plan holders effectively 7.6/15.1/30.2 per second at 63%). HappyHorse 1.1''s dedicated reference model (1–9 images) and Kling Omni''s reference-to-video follow.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Why would uploading a reference video make generation cheaper?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">On Seedance 2 / 2 Mini / 2.5, a reference video moves billing to the with_video scene — the model spends less compute inventing motion it can copy. 2.5 at 1080p: 250 → 150 credits/sec; 2 Mini at 720p: 15 → 9. It''s the rare pricing rule that rewards better inputs.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: How do I keep a character consistent across shots?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Use the same reference set every generation: 3–5 complementary angles in Wan''s or HappyHorse''s image slots, or one locked keyframe through Kling Omni image-to-video where the frame itself was produced from a consistent image model. Consistency comes from reusing material, not from describing the character twice.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Does more reference material cost more on any model?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Only through scenes, not counts: Wan, HappyHorse, and Grok price identically whether you attach one asset or their full cap. Kling Omni''s reference-to-video charges more when a video is attached (with_video scene, up to +50% at 1080p vs silent), and Seedance''s scene switch works the other direction.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Which model for animating a finished product photo?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Kling 3.0 Omni image-to-video (single frame, source aspect kept, from 20/sec at 720p silent) is built for exactly this. <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/video-1-5-preview" style="color:#60a5fa;">Grok Video 1.5 Preview</a> is the budget answer — same image-presence flip at 6/12 credits per second, with an <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/upscale" style="color:#60a5fa;">upscale station</a> (15/sec to 720p, 30/sec to 1080p by source seconds) when the clip earns it.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="closing">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Feed It, Don''t Describe It</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The reference-capacity axis is the most practical way to sort these four because it maps directly onto what you already own: frames, packs, phone footage, voice files. Single-frame discipline buys fidelity to one decision; buffet inputs buy a full production brief; conditioning-video discounts pay you for showing instead of telling.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Assemble the material, pick the model by its contract, and run the same asset kit through all four on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video" style="color:#60a5fa;">FuseAITools</a> — the render that keeps your product looking like your product wins, regardless of list price.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- kimi-k3-output-heavy-reasoning-cost-guide (category=1)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'Kimi k3 and the Economics of Output-Heavy Reasoning: A Cost-Engineering Guide for Thinking Models',
  1,
  'kimi-k3-output-heavy-reasoning-cost-guide',
  'Kimi k3 bills $7.41 input, $37.05 output per million tokens with visible reasoning summaries — cost engineering for output-heavy reasoning.',
  'Kimi k3, Moonshot AI, reasoning model, output token pricing, visible reasoning, thinking level, cost engineering, LLM budget, long document analysis, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Kimi k3 and the Economics of Output-Heavy Reasoning: A Cost-Engineering Guide for Thinking Models</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Kimi k3 bills $7.41 input, $37.05 output per million tokens with visible reasoning summaries — cost engineering for output-heavy reasoning.">
</head>
<body>
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(ellipse 65% 50% at 50% 0%, rgba(34,211,238,.07), transparent),radial-gradient(ellipse 50% 40% at 100% 90%, rgba(8,145,178,.05), transparent),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318, #0b0c0f);color:#e5e7eb;padding:20px;border-radius:12px;">

        <section class="introduction">
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Most chat models are priced like photocopiers: input and output roughly balanced, cost proportional to how much you feed them. <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kimi" style="color:#22d3ee;">Kimi</a>''s reasoning model <strong style="color:#22d3ee;">kimi-k3</strong> is priced like a consultant: cheap to brief, expensive to listen to. Input bills at 741 credits per million tokens ($7.41), output at 3,705 credits per million ($37.05) — a 5:1 ratio that is the single most important fact about this model''s economics.</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Why care? Because a reasoning model''s output includes its thinking. kimi-k3 exposes three effort levels (low, medium, high) and — unlike the hidden-scratchpad designs elsewhere in the lineup — returns a visible summary of the reasoning alongside the answer. The thinking you pay for is partly the product you get to inspect.</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">This guide treats those two facts — a 5:1 output skew and visible reasoning — as one design problem: how to build workflows where expensive generated tokens are worth generating, and how to tell when they aren''t.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="market-opportunity">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Reading a 5:1 Price Ratio Correctly</h2>

            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Work pattern</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Tokens (in / out)</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Cost (list credits)</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">At Ultra monthly 63%</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Verdict</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Long-document triage: 50k read, 500-token answer</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">50,000 / 500</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~38.5</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~24.3</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Excellent — input-heavy, output-light</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Hard problem pass: 2k brief, 8k reasoning + answer</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">2,000 / 8,000</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~31.1</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~19.6</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Good if the 8k thinking was the deliverable</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Chatty loop: 200-token turns × 30</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">6,000 / 6,000</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~266.8</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~168.1</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Poor — conversational symmetry is the worst fit</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The table inverts the usual LLM intuition. Feeding kimi-k3 a mountain of context is comparatively cheap — 741 credits per million read — while every token it writes back costs 3,705. Workflows that read a lot and answer briefly sit in the model''s price sweet spot; workflows that converse at length do not. Plan discounts apply on top of billing (monthly Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63%).</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Against the rest of the reasoning shelf, the contrast is instructive: the <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok-chat" style="color:#22d3ee;">Grok Chat</a> ladder bills output at barely 3× input with hidden thinking, while kimi-k3 charges 5× and shows its work. One is priced for volume; the other is priced for depth you can audit.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="workflow">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Three Workflows Designed for the Skew</h2>

            <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Workflow 1 — The Big Read, The Short Verdict</h3>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Contract reviews, RFP analysis, research-paper sweeps: entire document bundles go in (cheap side), tightly constrained answers come out. The prompt should fix the output shape before the model decides how much to write.</p>

            <div style="background:rgba(31,41,55,0.3);padding:16px;border-radius:8px;margin:16px 0;">
                <p style="color:#22d3ee;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:8px;">"Read the attached agreement set. Output exactly: (1) a table of clauses where termination risk exceeds indemnity coverage, (2) three numbered redlines, (3) nothing else. Reason as long as needed internally; summarize the reasoning in at most 150 words."</p>
                <p style="color:#9ca3af;">Explicit output budgets are the difference between a 15-credit verdict and a 150-credit essay on identical input.</p>
            </div>

            <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Workflow 2 — Effort Tiering Across the Thinking Levels</h3>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">kimi-k3''s low/medium/high dial maps directly to the output bill, since thinking tokens bill as output. Treat the level as a routing decision, not a global setting:</p>

            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#22d3ee;">low</strong> — extraction, formatting, first-pass filters: the bulk queue. (Enabling the deep-thinking toggle jumps the level to high, so leave it off for routine stages.)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#22d3ee;">medium</strong> — drafting and comparison steps where structure matters more than adjudication.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#22d3ee;">high</strong> — the 5% of jobs where the reasoning is the deliverable: math, security review, dispute adjudication.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Workflow 3 — The Visible Summary as an Audit Trail</h3>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Because kimi-k3 surfaces a reasoning summary (distinct from the encrypted internal trace), high-effort passes double as reviewable work product: a reviewer can see how the model weighed options before signing off. Compliance-adjacent teams can gate automation on it — act directly on high-confidence verdicts whose summaries check out, and route ambiguous summaries to a human instead of escalating the whole queue.</p>

            <div style="background:rgba(31,41,55,0.3);padding:16px;border-radius:8px;margin:16px 0;">
                <p style="color:#22d3ee;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:8px;">One protocol note from the same family of constraints: while extended thinking is enabled, tool use and web search are off. Retrieval belongs in a separate, cheaper stage — as it does on the Grok reasoning endpoints — feeding snapshots into the thinking pass.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="pitfalls">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Where Output-Heavy Pricing Burns Budgets</h2>

            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Risk</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">What happens</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Countermeasure</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Judging cost by input volume</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Teams stuff a 200k-token context fearing a big bill, then get surprised when a chatty 20k-token reply costs 5× more than the entire document.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Estimate every job as in×0.741 + out×3.705 (credits/M); the output term dominates.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">High thinking on routine queues</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">A global high setting bills thousands of hidden thinking tokens per trivial extraction.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Level-tier by job class; the deep-thinking toggle is a per-request choice, never an org default.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Open-ended prompts on a verbose engine</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">"Analyze this and tell me what you find" has no stopping condition; unbounded output at 3,705/M is the most expensive sentence in the product.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Specify sections, counts, and word ceilings; make the answer finish itself.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Conversational accumulation</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Multi-turn chats re-send history as input and add output each turn — the ratio compounds into a slow leak.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Summarize state into a compact brief periodically; start fresh calls from the brief.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Skipping the summary review step</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">The visible reasoning summary is free with the output; automating decisions without ever sampling it wastes the model''s one differentiator.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Spot-audit summaries on high-tier jobs — it''s the cheapest quality control in the pipeline.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </section>

        <section class="action-checklist">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist</h2>
            <ol style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#22d3ee;">Reframe the budget math around output.</strong> Every workflow quote starts with expected generated tokens, not document size.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#22d3ee;">Write output contracts into prompts.</strong> Sections, limits, "nothing else" clauses on all long-read jobs.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#22d3ee;">Tier the thinking level per queue.</strong> low for bulk, medium for drafting, high only where the reasoning is the deliverable.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#22d3ee;">Separate retrieval from reasoning.</strong> Search-stage calls stay cheap and levelled-down; the k3 pass receives snapshots.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#22d3ee;">Sample the reasoning summaries weekly.</strong> They''re included; using them for QA costs nothing extra.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#22d3ee;">Compare against the flat-priced alternatives.</strong> If a job is volume chat rather than deep adjudication, route it to the <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt" style="color:#22d3ee;">GPT</a> or Grok Chat tiers where output doesn''t carry a 5× premium.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section class="faq">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: What does kimi-k3 cost per token?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">741 credits per million input tokens ($7.41) and 3,705 credits per million output ($37.05) — output bills at 5× input. A 50k-token document read with a 500-token verdict runs about 38 credits at list price, roughly 24 after the Ultra monthly discount (63%); Pro and Basic land at 28 and 32 (73% / 82%).</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Why is the output priced so much higher than input?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Because on a reasoning model, "output" includes the thinking the model writes before answering — computation you''re paying for per token. The 5:1 ratio is the honest price of depth: the more you ask it to work through, the more it generates behind the answer line.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Can I see how kimi-k3 reached its answer?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Partially, yes — the endpoint returns a visible summary of the reasoning alongside the final answer (the full internal trace stays encrypted). That makes high-effort runs reviewable in ways hidden-scratchpad models aren''t, which is a large part of what the premium output price buys.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: What tasks fit kimi-k3''s price structure best?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Read-heavy, decide-small jobs: contract and RFP analysis, research synthesis with bounded deliverables, math and code adjudication at high effort. The unifying trait is that a short expensive output is worth it because input volume was cheap and the verdict is consequential.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: How do I keep a runaway response from overcharging me?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Bound the ask, not the model: specify output structure and word limits in the prompt, keep routine queues at low effort, and remember the deep-thinking toggle maps straight to the high (most expensive) level. With 150 open-ended tokens costing as much as a 30,000-token upload, prompt discipline is the price cap.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="closing">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Take</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">kimi-k3 is the clearest price signal in the reasoning shelf: thinking is priced as work product, and the visible summary makes that work product inspectable. The teams who win with it are the ones who stop treating all chat endpoints as interchangeable photocopiers — who feed the cheap side generously, hold the expensive side on a leash, and read the receipts.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Try <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kimi" style="color:#22d3ee;">Kimi on FuseAITools</a> for output-auditable deep reasoning workflows.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- kling-v3-turbo-shorts-volume-service (category=4)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'Building a Volume Short-Form Video Service with Kling V3 Turbo for Freelance Creators',
  4,
  'kling-v3-turbo-shorts-volume-service-business',
  'Kling V3 Turbo bills a flat 27–36 credits per second at 720p/1080p — per-clip quoting math, three service lines, and margin guardrails for shorts volume.',
  'Kling V3 Turbo, short-form video service, freelance video business, AI video pricing, per second billing, image to video ads, faceless channel content, Kling family, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Building a Volume Short-Form Video Service with Kling V3 Turbo for Freelance Creators</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Kling V3 Turbo bills a flat 27–36 credits per second at 720p/1080p — per-clip quoting math, three service lines, and margin guardrails for shorts volume.">
</head>
<body>
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(ellipse 65% 50% at 50% 0%, rgba(244,114,182,.07), transparent),radial-gradient(ellipse 50% 40% at 100% 90%, rgba(244,114,182,.05), transparent),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318, #0b0c0f);color:#e5e7eb;padding:20px;border-radius:12px;">

        <section class="introduction">
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Short-form video is the one freelance service with enormous demand and terrible unit economics. Clients want 15–30 clips a month, expect $30–80 per clip, and an editor''s hour is finite — so the business either scales into a studio or quietly dies of scope.</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling" style="color:#f472b6;">Kling</a> V3 Turbo changes the cost side of that equation. The <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-turbo-text-to-video" style="color:#f472b6;">Kling V3 Turbo Text-to-Video</a> and <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-turbo-image-to-video" style="color:#f472b6;">Kling V3 Turbo Image-to-Video</a> models bill a flat per-second rate — 27 credits at 720p, 36 credits at 1080p — with no scene tiers and no audio surcharge. A finished 10-second 1080p clip is 360 credits at list price, and subscription plans cut that further: roughly 295 on Basic, 263 on Pro, and 227 on Ultra (monthly plan discounts of 82% / 73% / 63%).</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">This article is a build guide, not a model review: three concrete service lines, the quoting math behind each, and the failure modes that eat margins when generation gets cheap.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="market-opportunity">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Margin Math That Makes Volume Work Possible</h2>

            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Deliverable</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Traditional cost</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Kling V3 Turbo cost (list)</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">At Ultra monthly (63%)</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Market rate to client</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">10s 720p social clip</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">$50–150 (editor, 2–6 h)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">270 credits</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~170 credits</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">$40–90</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">10s 1080p hero clip</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">$120–400</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">360 credits</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~227 credits</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">$80–200</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">15-clip monthly content pack</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">$750–2,000+</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">4,050–5,400 credits</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~2,552–3,402 credits</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">$600–1,500</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Two structural advantages hide in that table. First, the rate is per second, not per attempt with scene multipliers — you can forecast a month of output before you generate a frame. Second, drafts and finals live in the same pricing family: iterate at 720p, and only the accepted take graduates to 1080p, which is what keeps the list price from ever being what you actually pay.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="workflow">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Three Service Lines, Three Workflows</h2>

            <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Service Line 1 — Faceless Niche Channels (retainer, $300–800/mo)</h3>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Subscription channels in history, space, scary-story, or finance niches need 3–5 clips weekly and care about consistency more than novelty. Text mode carries this line: fixed prompt templates, 9:16 or 1:1 framing, 720p for feed content.</p>
            <div style="background:rgba(31,41,55,0.3);padding:16px;border-radius:8px;margin:16px 0;">
                <p style="color:#f472b6;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:8px;">Template: "[Era/setting] establishing shot, [single subject doing one action], [one lighting cue], slow [camera move], documentary tone" — then lock the seed family per series so episodes rhyme visually.</p>
                <p style="color:#9ca3af;">Unit cost per 10s 720p clip: 270 credits list, ~170 on Ultra monthly. A 12-clip month lands around 3,240 list credits — well inside one month of client retainer revenue at $500.</p>
            </div>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Client acquisition: cold-DM channels that post inconsistently; lead with a free pilot clip rendered from their last script.</p>

            <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Service Line 2 — Product Still-to-Motion Ads (per-campaign, $80–250/clip)</h3>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">E-commerce sellers have photography but no motion. Image mode takes one still (jpeg/png, up to 10 MB) and animates it while preserving the source aspect ratio — perfect for turning existing listing photos into 4–8 second ad hooks.</p>
            <div style="background:rgba(31,41,55,0.3);padding:16px;border-radius:8px;margin:16px 0;">
                <p style="color:#f472b6;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:8px;">Prompt discipline for product shots: describe only the motion, never the product ("camera drifts left, steam rises, label stays sharp and legible") — the image already carries the subject.</p>
                <p style="color:#9ca3af;">Quote structure: 3 motion variants per still for $120 — 3 × 8s × 1080p = 864 credits list, ~545 on Ultra — at a 3–4× margin versus manual editing equivalents.</p>
            </div>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">When a client needs the motion choreographed to a specific dance (pour, unbox, rotate-on-axis), hand that job to <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v2-6-motion-control" style="color:#f472b6;">Kling V2.6 Motion Control</a> and bill it as a premium add-on; Turbo covers the 80% that just needs tasteful camera life.</p>

            <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Service Line 3 — Ad-Creative Variation Batches (per-batch, $250–600)</h3>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Performance marketers A/B test hooks in batches of 6–12. Per-second flat pricing is what makes this sellable: quote the batch, not the shot. Same core prompt, vary one variable (framing, pace, or color story), deliver a grid.</p>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f472b6;">Batch mechanics:</strong> draft all variants at 720p/3–5s (81–135 credits list each), let the client pick the winner, re-render only that one at 1080p/15s (max 540 credits list).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f472b6;">Upgrade path built in:</strong> if the winner needs narrative staging or multi-shot direction, move it up-family to <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-0-video" style="color:#f472b6;">Kling V3.0</a> or <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/3-0-omni-image-to-video" style="color:#f472b6;">Kling 3.0 Omni Image-to-Video</a> as a scoped Phase 2.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f472b6;">Client acquisition:</strong> performance agencies with rotating contractor lists; position as the "variation vendor" that never misses a batch deadline.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="pitfalls">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">What Eats Freelance Margins on Cheap Generations</h2>

            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Risk</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">How it burns you</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Mitigation</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">"Unlimited-feel" pricing</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Clients scale revision requests when each take looks nearly free; 15 rounds of 15s 1080p = 8,100 credits on one $200 clip.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Contract for takes, not edits: 3 renders included, each extra priced from the per-second table.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Duration drift</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Turbo runs 3–15s; a "quick 15 instead of 8" habit nearly doubles clip cost without moving the client''s price expectation.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Default every draft to the shortest duration that proves the idea; 8s is the standard draft length.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Aspect-ratio mismatch on image jobs</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Image mode keeps the source photo''s ratio — a square listing photo silently ships a square "9:16" ad that gets rejected by the ad platform.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Pre-crop client stills to target ratio before generating; build the crop into intake.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Text-mode expectations on exact copy</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Prompts cap at 2,500 characters and Turbo is a motion model, not a typography engine; baked-in slogans risk garbled letters.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Keep text overlays in your editor; sell clean motion plates.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Selling Turbo for hero films</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">The tier that wins volume quotes is not the tier that wins brand-film competitions; one mis-sold flagship burns trust on a retainer.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Scope rules in writing: Turbo for feed/batch, flag-ups to the V3.0 family, priced per tier.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </section>

        <section class="action-checklist">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist</h2>
            <ol style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f472b6;">Price from the per-second table.</strong> Cost = seconds × 27 (720p) or 36 (1080p) credits, then apply your plan discount (82/73/63% monthly) — quote clients at 3–4× the discounted number.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f472b6;">Build one intake form per service line.</strong> Script + desired duration for channels; source still + target ratio for product; variable list for batches.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f472b6;">Standardize drafts at 720p / 8s.</strong> 216 credits list per draft keeps a 3-variant pitch under 650 credits.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f472b6;">Write the takes clause into every contract.</strong> Three renders included; extras metered at your per-second cost table.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f472b6;">Track discounted credits per client.</strong> Actual spend after plan discounts is your true COGS; review monthly and drop clients whose revision ratio buries margin.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f472b6;">Define your up-sell ladder.</strong> Turbo drafts → Turbo finals → <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-turbo-image-to-video" style="color:#f472b6;">image-mode product finals</a> → V3.0/Omni hero work, each with a stated price jump.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section class="faq">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: What does one Kling V3 Turbo clip actually cost?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Seconds times rate: 3–15 second clips bill 27 credits per second at 720p or 36 at 1080p, so a typical 10s clip runs 270–360 credits at list price. Subscription plan discounts (monthly Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63%) reduce that to roughly 170–295 credits for the same clip.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Turbo or Kling V3.0 for client work?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Use Turbo for anything measured in volume — feed clips, ad variations, product motion — because the flat rate makes batch quoting trivial. Reserve <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-0-video" style="color:#f472b6;">Kling V3.0</a> and the Omni line for hero shots where direction features, not price per second, decide the deliverable.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Can I animate a client''s existing product photo?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Yes — image mode accepts one jpeg/png up to 10 MB and keeps its aspect ratio, which is ideal for turning listing stills into motion ads. Remember the ratio is inherited, so crop the source to the platform''s target frame first.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: How do I stop revision requests from destroying margin?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Meter renders, not edits. Include three finished takes per deliverable in every quote, price extra takes directly from your per-second cost table, and draft every concept at 720p/8s so a rejected variant costs 216 list credits instead of a four-figure 1080p waste.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Is 720p acceptable for published social content?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">For feed-native shorts, frequently yes — compression erases the delta on phone screens, and 720p costs 25% less per second. Bill 1080p only for embeds, landing pages, and anything a client might pull onto larger displays.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="closing">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Business Case in One Line</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Kling V3 Turbo doesn''t win on peak quality — it wins on arithmetic. A freelance video business finally becomes quotable when every clip has a predictable price per second, discounts push unit costs under the "free to experiment" threshold, and the up-family ladder lets you say yes to hero work without leaving the ecosystem.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Try <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-turbo-text-to-video" style="color:#f472b6;">Kling V3 Turbo on FuseAITools</a> for volume short-form video services.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- kling-version-history-ten-pricing-jumps-turbo-to-omni (category=4)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'From 60-Credit Clips to 20 a Second: The Ten Pricing Jumps from Kling 2.5 Turbo to 3.0 Omni',
  4,
  'kling-version-history-ten-pricing-jumps-turbo-to-omni',
  'Kling''s pricing history in ten jumps: 60-credit flat clips, the audio-doubling scenes, the October 15 repricing, and Omni''s per-second scene matrix.',
  'Kling AI video pricing history, per-second video billing, Kling 3.0 Omni scene pricing, audio premium video credits, Kling motion control repricing, video generation cost comparison, Kling rate card explained, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>From 60-Credit Clips to 20 a Second: The Ten Pricing Jumps from Kling 2.5 Turbo to 3.0 Omni</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Kling''s pricing history in ten jumps: 60-credit flat clips, the audio-doubling scenes, the October 15 repricing, and Omni''s per-second scene matrix.">
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/news/kling-version-history-ten-pricing-jumps-turbo-to-omni">
</head>
<body style="margin:0;padding:0;background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(circle at 15% 0%, rgba(45,212,191,0.07) 0%, transparent 45%),radial-gradient(circle at 85% 100%, rgba(245,158,11,0.05) 0%, transparent 45%),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318 0%, #0b0c0f 100%);color:#d1d5db;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="max-width:960px;margin:0 auto;padding:32px 20px 64px;">
        <header style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h1 itemprop="headline" style="font-size:2rem;line-height:1.25;color:#f9fafb;margin:0 0 16px;">From 60-Credit Clips to 20 a Second: The Ten Pricing Jumps from Kling 2.5 Turbo to 3.0 Omni</h1>
            <p style="color:#9ca3af;font-size:0.95rem;margin:0;">Published <time itemprop="datePublished" datetime="2026-11-12">November 12, 2026</time> · FuseAI Tools Editorial</p>
        </header>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Introduction: A Rate Card Is a Fossil Record</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Most teams read a price list as a menu. It is actually a dig site: every generation''s assumptions are preserved in the column that prices it. On <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling" style="color:#2dd4bf;text-decoration:none;">FuseAI</a>, the Kling family now runs <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">nine live billing lanes across four generations</strong>, and reading them in order tells you exactly how video pricing learned to count: first by the clip, then by the clip <em>plus sound</em>, then by the second, then by the second times the scene times the input — with one day (October 15, 2026) doing more restructuring than every quarter before it. All figures here are list credits; the monthly plans apply the usual discounts — Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63% — so the flagship reference point of this series, a 10-second Omni 720p silent clip at 200 list, costs 164 at Basic, 146 at Pro, 126 at Ultra.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The arc has ten distinct jumps, and each one answers a question the previous generation''s pricing got wrong. Follow them and you can predict what the next video rate card will look like when no one is watching: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">finer units, cheaper silences, and surcharges that quietly collapse into the base price</strong>.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Jumps 1–2: The Flat Clip — and the Day Audio Doubled Everything</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Jump 1: duration was the only number.</strong> The starting artifact is <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v2-5-turbo-text-to-video-pro" style="color:#2dd4bf;text-decoration:none;">Kling V2.5 Turbo Pro</a>, still on the card, still instructive: absolute per-clip pricing with <em>no resolution dimension at all</em> — 5 seconds = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">60 credits</strong>, 10 seconds = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">120</strong>. Twelve credits a second by back-division, but you could not buy a cheaper resolution or a silent variant, because the menu predates both ideas. It is the rate card''s Cambrian layer: one dimension, everything included.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Jump 2: the scene dimension arrives — and it is a 2× surcharge.</strong> <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v2-6-text-to-video" style="color:#2dd4bf;text-decoration:none;">Kling 2.6 text-to-video</a> kept the flat menu but added a sound switch priced as a <em>scene</em>: 5 seconds is <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">90 without sound, 180 with</strong>; 10 seconds, <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">180 against 360</strong>. Audio was exactly double, on every duration, in both t2v and i2v. This is the moment the bill stopped being a function of your edit list and started being a function of your asset plan — and every later jump is a retreat from the premium 2.6 established.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Jumps 3–5: Seconds, a Overnight Doubling, and the Full Per-Second Conversion</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Jump 3: the first per-second lane.</strong> <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v2-6-motion-control" style="color:#2dd4bf;text-decoration:none;">2.6 motion control</a> broke from the clip menu entirely: once.credits stores a <em>per-second</em> rate (duration=1 rule × actual seconds), and at launch it was the cheapest Kling per second had ever been — <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">9/s at 720p, 15/s at 1080p</strong>. Two dimensions, clip-length freedom, no rounding to menu durations. The future, at a price designed to introduce it.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Jump 4: October 15 doubles the draft lane overnight.</strong> The changelog still carries it: on 2026-10-15, motion control''s two rates went <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">9→18 and 15→27</strong> — exact doubling, no transition note. Whatever the introduction subsidy was, that day it ended. Lesson preserved for every migration priced below cost: <em>introductory rates are a phase, and phases end on deploy days</em>. (3.0''s successor lane, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-0-motion-control" style="color:#2dd4bf;text-decoration:none;">3.0 motion control</a>, landed at 30/40 per second — repricing is permanent once the feature is load-bearing.)</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Jump 5: 3.0 Video converts the flagship line to seconds.</strong> Same day, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-0-video" style="color:#2dd4bf;text-decoration:none;">Kling 3.0 video</a> moved the main family to per-second pricing across a size × sound matrix — std <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">20/30</strong>, pro <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">30/40</strong>, and a brand-new 4K tier at <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">100 flat regardless of audio</strong> — while its four std/pro rates were adjusted <em>down</em> in the same change. The direction was now unmistakable: seconds up front, quality as a selector, and the top tier deliberately indifferent to sound.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Jumps 6–8: October 15 Also Shipped a Family — Turbo, Omni, and the Scene Matrix</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Jump 6: a two-rate simplicity lane.</strong> Also new that day: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-turbo-text-to-video" style="color:#2dd4bf;text-decoration:none;">V3 Turbo text-to-video</a> (and its <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-turbo-image-to-video" style="color:#2dd4bf;text-decoration:none;">image-to-video</a> twin) — no scene dimension at all, just <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">27/s at 720p and 36/s at 1080p</strong>, 3–15 seconds, i2v taking exactly one start frame. A flat menu inside a per-second world: the lane you quote when a client refuses to think about audio switches.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Jump 7: Omni prices silence below sound — and makes silence the default.</strong> The four <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/3-0-omni-text-to-video" style="color:#2dd4bf;text-decoration:none;">3.0 Omni</a> variants re-cut everything: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/3-0-omni-image-to-video" style="color:#2dd4bf;text-decoration:none;">text and image generation</a> bill <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">20/s at 720p silent, 30 with audio; 30 at 1080p silent, 36 with audio; 100 at 4K either way</strong>. Three collapses in one matrix: the audio surcharge fell from 2.6''s exact 2× to 1.5× at 720p and 1.2× at 1080p and zero at 4K; the silent rate undercut even V3 Turbo''s no-scene 27; and the scene key was renamed to <code>no_audio/with_audio</code> — a different spelling from 3.0-video''s <code>without_sound/with_sound</code>, so two generations of cost sheets do not port. Default duration: 5 seconds; multi-shot orders bill <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Σ shot-seconds</strong> (jump 8), not per clip — a three-cut teaser priced as its total runtime.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">For budget planning, the practical curve is: a 10-second 720p silent render cost <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">120 list</strong> in the 2.5 flat era, <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">180</strong> as a 2.6 silent scene, <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">200</strong> as a 3.0 std second, <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">270</strong> on the V3 Turbo no-scene lane — and <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">200</strong> again on Omni no-audio, which is where most production actually lands. Finer units did not raise the price; they moved the discount to the lane you can verify before you render.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Jumps 9–10: The Input Became a Price Dimension</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Jump 9: reference video is a third scene.</strong> <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/3-0-omni-reference-to-video" style="color:#2dd4bf;text-decoration:none;">Omni reference-to-video</a> prices nine cells instead of six: feeding in a reference video routes to <code>with_video</code> <em>regardless of the audio switch</em> — <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">30/s at 720p, 45/s at 1080p, 100 at 4K</strong>. Your inputs, not just your outputs, are now on the invoice: the same 10 seconds costs 200 silent, 300 voiced, or 450 if you steer it with a reference clip at 1080p.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Jump 10: duration stopped being a choice.</strong> <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/3-0-omni-transformation" style="color:#2dd4bf;text-decoration:none;">Omni transformation</a> (video-to-video, up to four reference images) bills <code>duration × quality</code> at <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">30/45/100 per second</strong> — but the duration is <em>your uploaded source video''s actual length</em>, probed and rounded up to whole seconds within 3–15. There is no scene dimension because a video-input render never generates audio; there is no duration slider because the file decides. It is the terminal shape of this decade-long trend: every knob the flat menu offered became either a priced selector or a measured fact.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Nine Lanes Today: One Comparison Table</h2>
            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:20px;font-size:0.92rem;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:#1f2937;">
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:left;">Lane</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:left;">Pricing shape</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">Rate card (list)</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">10s reference</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">V2.5 Turbo Pro (t2v/i2v)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">flat per clip, duration only</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">5s 60 / 10s 120</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">120</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">2.6 (t2v/i2v)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">flat per clip × sound scene</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">5s 90/180 · 10s 180/360</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">180 / 360</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">2.6 motion control</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">per second × quality</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">18 / 27 (was 9 / 15)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">180 (720p)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">3.0 video</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">per second × size × sound</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">std 20/30 · pro 30/40 · 4K 100</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">200 (std silent)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">3.0 motion control</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">per second × size</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">std 30 / pro 40</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">300 (std)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">V3 Turbo (t2v/i2v)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">per second × quality, no scene</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">27 / 36</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">270 (720p)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">3.0 Omni (t2v/i2v)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">per second × quality × audio</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">20/30 · 30/36 · 4K 100</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">200 / 300 (720p)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Omni reference-to-video</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">nine cells: + with_video scene</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">with video 30/45/100</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">450 (1080p)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Omni transformation</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">source-seconds × quality</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">30 / 45 / 100</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">300 (720p)</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;margin-top:16px;">Read the last column top to bottom and the whole argument of this article is on one axis: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">120 → 180 → 200 → 270 → 200</strong> for essentially "ten seconds, 720p-ish, no sound" — the flat era''s bargain, the scene era''s premium, the per-second era''s overshoot, and Omni''s silent-rate correction. The 120 lane is still buyable; it just predates your ability to know what you are paying for.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Pitfalls: Five Ways Old Kling Habits Misprice New Kling</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Budgeting the 9/s motion-control era.</strong> The introduction rate is gone — October 15 doubled it to 18/27 in a single deploy. Any sheet citing the old number is understating by half, and the changelog says so out loud.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Porting scene keys across generations.</strong> 3.0 video says <code>without_sound/with_sound</code>; Omni says <code>no_audio/with_audio</code>. Same concept, different spelling, different lookup — automation that copies a cost model between the two fails on the join, not on the price.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Paying the 2× audio premium out of habit.</strong> 2.6 priced sound at exactly double; Omni charges +10 at 720p-per-second and +6 at 1080p, and nothing at 4K. Teams that "always budget audio ×2" now overplan by up to 100 credits per second at the top tier, where the surcharge no longer exists.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Quoting transformation by your timeline.</strong> Duration is the uploaded source file''s real seconds, rounded up — not a slider. A 12.2-second source bills 13; a "we''ll trim it in post" plan is a billing decision, and the trim should happen before the upload.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Billing multi-shot as multiple clips.</strong> Omni''s multi-shot orders charge Σ shot-seconds inside the 3–15 window. Three 4-second cuts are 12 seconds of price, not three minimums — storyboards should count total runtime, and the 5-second default duration is a fallback, not a plan.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist: Reading Any Video Rate Card Like a Historian</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;padding-left:24px;line-height:1.9;">
                <li>Ask what the unit is (clip? second? source-second?) before asking what the price is — the unit encodes the generation.</li>
                <li>Find the surcharge dimensions (sound, scene, reference input) and chart how each one shrank; that curve predicts the next rate card.</li>
                <li>Check dated annotations before trusting a familiar number — introduction rates and repricings are recorded per day, and habits outlive them.</li>
                <li>Quote the silent lane (Omni no-audio 20/s) as your production baseline; treat audio and reference-video steering as priced add-ons, not defaults.</li>
                <li>Compare old flat lanes by back-division (120 ÷ 10s = 12/s) but never by assumption — they lack the dimensions your brief actually has.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">FAQ</h2>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Why does one Kling family have nine billing lanes?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Because each generation''s pricing model survived as a live lane: the flat per-clip menus (2.5 Turbo Pro, 2.6), the first per-second experiments (motion control), and the per-second scene matrices (3.0 video, V3 Turbo, the four Omni variants). Nothing was retired, so the rate card preserves four generations of assumptions — which is exactly why reading it in order teaches you where video pricing is going.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: What did ten seconds of 720p video cost across the generations?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">120 list credits on 2.5 Turbo Pro''s flat 10-second clip (no resolution or audio dimensions), 180 as a 2.6 silent scene, 180–270 on per-second lanes depending on quality, 200 at 3.0-video std silent, 270 on V3 Turbo''s no-scene rate, and 200 on Omni''s 720p no-audio lane — or 300 with audio. The unit went from clip to second; the silent price went back down to where the flat era started, this time itemized.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: What actually changed on October 15, 2026?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Four things in one deploy day, all recorded in the billing notes: 2.6 motion control''s per-second rates doubled (9→18, 15→27), 3.0 video''s four std/pro rates moved down while a flat-100 4K tier was added, and the V3 Turbo pair plus all four Omni variants arrived as new models. It is the cleanest single-day snapshot of the family''s strategy: kill the introduction pricing, re-cut the flagship, ship the scene matrix.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Is audio still a 2× premium on Kling?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Only in the legacy lanes. 2.6 priced sound at exactly double, and 3.0 video still asks 1.5× on std (20→30) and 1.33× on pro. Omni compresses the premium to +50% at 720p, +20% at 1080p, and zero at 4K — where both scenes bill 100 per second. The trend across the dig site is unambiguous: sound started as a surcharge and is becoming a free tier at the top of the card.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Which lane should a new project actually price against?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Omni''s 720p no-audio rate — 20 credits per second, 200 for a 10-second clip at list — is the defensible production baseline: it undercuts the no-scene Turbo lane and itemizes every optional add-on (audio +10/s, reference-video steering at 30/45, transformation on source-seconds). Older flat lanes are cheaper only when their missing dimensions match what you''d give up; quote them with eyes open.</p></div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:0;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Closing: The Unit Always Wins</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Ten jumps, one pattern: every generation replaced a bundled number with a measurable one — clips became seconds, sound became a scene, inputs became prices, and durations became properties of your files. The plan is not to mourn the 60-credit flat clip; it is to price the way the card now thinks — baseline at Omni''s silent 20/s, add audio and reference steering as visible choices, and check the dated notes before trusting any familiar rate, because October 15 proved habits outlive introduction pricing. Every lane above bills live on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/3-0-omni-reference-to-video" style="color:#2dd4bf;text-decoration:none;">FuseAI''s Kling family</a>, with the full scene matrix on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/3-0-omni-transformation" style="color:#2dd4bf;text-decoration:none;">transformation</a> and <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-turbo-image-to-video" style="color:#2dd4bf;text-decoration:none;">V3 Turbo</a> included. Try <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/3-0-omni-text-to-video" style="color:#2dd4bf;text-decoration:none;">FuseAITools</a> for Kling video generation with per-second, per-scene pricing.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

