                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#f87171;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">3,087</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">2,531</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">2,255</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#34d399;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">1,946</strong></td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;margin-top:16px;">Discounts are the monthly plan rates: Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63%, applied per line and rounded. Two receipts worth walking to the budget holder with. First, the narration columns: twelve English episodes bill 720 and twelve Chinese episodes bill 240 <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">on the same lane at the same per-byte rate</strong> — the Chinese content is not discounted, it is denser per sentence. A budget that assumed "CJK is 2× English" would have priced this pair at 2,160 instead of the actual 960, a 1,200-credit overstatement on the easiest line to get right. Second, the 36-credit canary row: 6–12-credit draft generations before each diagram batch. The alternative is discovering a mis-rendered label inside a 24-credit Pro batch that a clinician then has to re-review — the cheapest insurance row in any regulated vertical''s sheet.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Pitfalls: Five Ways a Compliant Channel Still Goes Off the Rails</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Regenerating instead of editing after review.</strong> Every fresh roll re-rolls the anatomy that was already approved. Masked edits (6 credits) keep the untouched pixels provably identical; regeneration keeps nothing.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Budgeting bilingual audio per language instead of per byte.</strong> Byte-based TTS pricing rewards Chinese and punishes verbose English scripts. Re-count the script in bytes before the first of twelve episodes, not after the first invoice.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">The STT one-minute cliff.</strong> Captions bill ceil(minutes) × 6: a 4:01 episode pays the 5-minute rate. Trim narration to whole minutes or accept paying for the padding.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Claim-shaped visuals.</strong> Photoreal "drug working" animations read as clinical evidence regardless of intent. The compliance-safe list is short: decorative motion, structural bumpers, illustrated (not photographic) anatomy.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Skipping the resolution mandatory field.</strong> On the Qwen image lanes, <code>resolution</code> is required and only 1K/2K are legal — but note the flat 12-credit pricing of the draft lane means resolution there is a <em>quality</em> decision, while on Pro it is a <em>money</em> decision. Confusing the two is how a department pays 24 for pixels it will redraw.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist: Standing Up the Education Channel</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;padding-left:24px;line-height:1.9;">
                <li>Write the no-photoreal, no-patient-imagery rule into the prompt templates themselves, not just the policy doc — templates are what the pipeline actually follows.</li>
                <li>Draft every diagram on the flat-12 lane, lock labels and layout, then render finals on Pro 2K. Two price rows, one review pass.</li>
                <li>Route all post-review corrections through masked edits; log parent-file ids so the review queue can see exactly which pixels changed.</li>
                <li>Count narration scripts in UTF-8 bytes per language before budgeting the month; re-cut episodes that land one byte over a thousand-block.</li>
                <li>Keep motion decorative: bumpers on the turbo per-second lane, intros on the mini lane, and never a visual that could be screenshotted as a clinical claim.</li>
                <li>Fund the 36-credit canary row every month without exception; in a regulated vertical it is the line that pays for itself the first mislabel it catches.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">FAQ</h2>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Is AI-generated medical illustration actually cheaper than licensed illustration?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">At this volume, by an order of magnitude: 40 labeled anatomical diagrams at 2K cost 960 list credits (605 on Ultra at 63%), where commissioned medical illustration typically quotes per-plate at rates that make sixty plates a quarter''s budget on its own. The AI lane also changes the economics of revisions — a corrected label is a 6-credit masked edit, not a new invoice from the illustrator.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: How do you keep diagrams compliant with medical review requirements?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">By construction: synthetic illustrated anatomy has no subject whose release is required, the disclaimer renders inside the 2K image, and post-review changes run through masked edits so only the labeled region moves. Compliance here is a pipeline property — draft lane, edit lane, and a clinician sign-off step — not a prompt keyword.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Why is Chinese narration cheaper than English on the same voice model?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Because ElevenLabs Multilingual v2 bills UTF-8 byte-blocks at 20 per thousand, and a 2.5-minute narration lands at roughly 700 bytes in Chinese versus 2,400 in English. Three bytes per CJK glyph sounds punitive until you notice Chinese says the sentence in a third of the characters; here it works out to 240 credits for the month''s Chinese episodes against 720 for the English ones.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Which model lane should diagram finals render on?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">The one where text density survives the label count. On this platform that is Qwen 3 Pro at 12 credits (1K) / 24 (2K), with the flat-12 standard Qwen 3 lane as the draft tier — same mandatory 1K/2K resolution field, lower rendering fidelity. Run one canary of your densest legend at 2K on both before committing sixty.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: What does a full month of patient-education video cost end to end?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Twelve bilingual episodes with 40 diagrams, captions, bumpers, an intro, and rehearsals: 3,087 list credits — 2,531 at Basic 82%, 2,255 at Pro 73%, 1,946 at Ultra 63%. Over 60% of it is the diagram library and the voice lane, which is the correct shape: in medical content the still frames carry the claims and the narration carries the liability.</p></div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:0;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Closing: Compliance Is a Pipeline Property, and So Is the Budget</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The education channel ships because two decisions were made at template time rather than at review time: every visual is synthetic by rule, and every correction is an edit by habit. The invoice is what those decisions look like when priced — 3,087 list credits a month, the byte-lane localization math finally pointed the right direction, and a 36-credit canary row that has never once been the most expensive line on the sheet. The lanes run inside <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/qwen/v3-pro-text-to-image" style="color:#22d3ee;text-decoration:none;">Qwen 3 Pro</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/multilingual-v2" style="color:#a78bfa;text-decoration:none;">ElevenLabs Multilingual v2</a>, and their neighbors on FuseAI — try the diagram draft lane on a real legend page first; it is the cheapest review your compliance process will ever get.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- month-end-budget-leaks-seven-holes-audit (category=4)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'Seven Holes Between Your Budget and Your Balance: The 20,032-Credit Video Month That Should Have Been 14,532',
  4,
  'month-end-budget-leaks-seven-holes-audit',
  'A month-end audit with seven quantified leaks: audio scene tax, blind rerolls, premium defaults, CJK bytes, tier overrun, and flat extends.',
  'AI credit budget overrun, reroll cost leak, audio scene premium credits, plan discount breakeven, wallet top-up audit, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Seven Holes Between Your Budget and Your Balance: The 20,032-Credit Video Month That Should Have Been 14,532</title>
    <meta name="description" content="A month-end audit with seven quantified leaks: audio scene tax, blind rerolls, premium defaults, CJK bytes, tier overrun, and flat extends.">
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/news/month-end-budget-leaks-seven-holes-audit">
</head>
<body style="margin:0;padding:0;background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(circle at 15% 0%, rgba(52,211,153,0.07) 0%, transparent 45%),radial-gradient(circle at 85% 100%, rgba(251,191,36,0.05) 0%, transparent 45%),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318 0%, #0b0c0f 100%);color:#d1d5db;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="max-width:960px;margin:0 auto;padding:32px 20px 64px;">
        <header style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h1 itemprop="headline" style="font-size:2rem;line-height:1.25;color:#f9fafb;margin:0 0 16px;">Seven Holes Between Your Budget and Your Balance: The 20,032-Credit Video Month That Should Have Been 14,532</h1>
            <p style="color:#9ca3af;font-size:0.95rem;margin:0;">Published <time itemprop="datePublished" datetime="2026-11-12">November 12, 2026</time> · FuseAI Tools Editorial</p>
        </header>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Introduction: The Balance Falls Faster Than the Plan Says It Should</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Every month-end has the same mystery. The plan said 16,000 list credits — a full workload on paper. The wallet said it covers that comfortably: a $100 top-up on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/pricing" style="color:#34d399;text-decoration:none;">FuseAI</a> is 10,000 raw credits, 12,437 at Basic''s 82% rate, 14,389 at Pro''s 73%, and <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">17,137 list-credit equivalents at Ultra''s 63%</strong>. And still the balance crosses zero around day 26, the top-up fires, and the number that arrives is never the number the plan promised. The gap is not mystery pricing. It is <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">seven specific holes</strong>, each of which has already been priced somewhere in this series — the reroll math, the scene premium, the byte trap, the bracket cliffs — and each of which survives because nobody puts them in the same column long enough to add them up.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">This is the capstone audit: one representative month, five workstreams, 22,618 list credits actually spent, 14,532 after the seven patches. If you read the capacity menu, the price ladder, and the reroll economics as theory, read this one as the reconciliation.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Seven Holes, Each With Its Already-Published Price Tag</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Hole 1 — the audio-scene tax (1,440 in the audit month).</strong> <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/3-0-omni-text-to-video" style="color:#34d399;text-decoration:none;">Omni''s audio</a> defaults to on at every tier below 4K: 30/s instead of 20/s at 720p. A month of 300 five-second clips that get their sound laid in post anyway is 4,500 delivered seconds paying a 10-credit silence premium — 1,440 list at 720p that post never used. This is the same 900-vs-1,080 line from the scene-misuse audit, multiplied by the batch count you never recheck.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Hole 2 — blind rerolls (4,704 on one production line).</strong> The reroll economics work measured the exact delta: a <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedance/v2" style="color:#34d399;text-decoration:none;">44-unit blind-pull month</a> costs 17,280 list; draft-first on the same briefs costs 7,872 — a 54% cut, anchored to the 394-credit mid-tier signature unit. Very few teams convert the whole pipeline, but converting half the volume on the flagship video line returns 9,408 × 0.5 = 4,704 credits — nearly a third of a raw wallet on its own.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Hole 3 — premium defaults above the delivery spec (336).</strong> The upgrade math showed selective premium (4K hero shots only) costs 5,176 where blanket-maxed costs 20,032 — 3.9× for pixels no platform spec requires. A month that maxes twelve hero renders where the shot list demands three is 336 list of the difference; the rest of the 3.9× lives in the re-render and reroll holes already counted below. When "max quality" is a template default rather than a shot-list decision, the whole 3.9× is a budget hole wearing a quality argument.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Hole 4 — the CJK byte multiplier (120).</strong> <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/multilingual-v2" style="color:#34d399;text-decoration:none;">ElevenLabs</a> reads UTF-8 bytes, so the same sentence in Chinese costs three times what it costs in English — invisible until you count. Three localized episodes at 270 list each, budgeted as three 90-credit English lines, is a 540-credit surprise — and the leak is not the language premium, which the brief approved; it is the 120-credit gap between the English-converted budget that got signed off and the byte-inflated invoice that arrived, one unauthorized switch''s worth.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Hole 5 — rehearsal debt (186).</strong> The cutout post-mortem and the rejection taxonomy both land on the same 6-credit canary: Thirty-one probe orders across three pipelines per month cost 186 credits and return the 882-credit rework gap — a net 186 back on the line that would otherwise re-render the finals the canary already validated. A team that skips all rehearsal does not save the canary budget; they spend it ten times over on the batch the canary would have corrected.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Hole 6 — tier overrun (400).</strong> The breakeven ladder is published: 990 list/month makes Basic worth it, 2,290 makes Pro worth it, 3,417 makes Ultra worth it. Below 990, wallet purchases are the right tool. Above them, paying raw-top-up rates for volume that qualifies for 73–82% metered billing is a pure 18–27% tax on every credit you planned honestly — the ladder priced against <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/video-1-5-preview" style="color:#34d399;text-decoration:none;">Grok video 1.5 preview</a> drafts at 6/s is the cheapest volume floor on the sheet.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Hole 7 — flat extends (900).</strong> Operations priced per-call hide their per-second cost: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/veo3/extend" style="color:#34d399;text-decoration:none;">veo3_extend</a> is 360 no matter how few seconds it adds, and <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/runway/extend" style="color:#34d399;text-decoration:none;">Runway extend</a> at 45 on a five-second clip is 9/s — fine once, ruinous as a habit. Twenty touch-up calls a month is 900 credits of flat-rate drift with no per-second column anywhere to argue against it, which is why the audit bills the whole flat-ops line as hole 7.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Reconciliation: One Month, Five Workstreams, 8,086 Returned</h2>
            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:20px;font-size:0.92rem;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:#1f2937;">
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:left;">Workstream (as run)</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">List spent</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:left;">Holes inside it</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">Returned</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">List clean</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">60-video production schedule (flagship line)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">20,032</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">1, 2, 3 (1,440 + 4,704 + 336, sums below)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#fbbf24;">6,480</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">13,552</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">150 images (draft + finals)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">640</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">5</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#fbbf24;">186</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">454</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Audio: 3 episodes localized</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">646</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">4</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#fbbf24;">120</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">526</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Flat ops: 20 Runway extend calls</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">900</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">7</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#fbbf24;">900</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">0</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Billing: raw top-ups above the ladder</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">400 (tax)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">6</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#fbbf24;">400</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">0</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Month total</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#f87171;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">22,618</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">7 of 7</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#34d399;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">8,086</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#34d399;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">14,532</strong></td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;margin-top:16px;">Every column adds, and every row balances on its own line. As-run: 20,032 + 640 + 646 + 900 + 400 = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">22,618</strong> — the flagship row is the published blanket-maxed 20,032-credit month from the upgrade audit, and the four side lines (image finals, three localized episodes, twenty extend calls, the tier tax) sit around it. Returned: 6,480 + 186 + 120 + 900 + 400 = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">8,086</strong>, which is exactly hole 1 + hole 2 + hole 3 on the video line (1,440 + 4,704 + 336 = 6,480) plus the other four holes whole. Clean: 13,552 + 454 + 526 + 0 + 0 = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">14,532</strong> — 64% of the as-run bill, and every row satisfies spent − returned = clean on its own line. At Ultra''s 63%, 14,532 list is about 9,155 metered credits — 85% of a $100 wallet''s 17,137-list capacity, so one wallet absorbs the clean month with 2,605 list to spare. The as-run month exceeds one $100 wallet by 5,481 list — the flagship schedule alone exceeds it by 2,895: precisely the shape of the "unexpected" top-up.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Pitfalls and One-Line Patches</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Patch scenes first, not rerolls.</strong> The audio default is one checkbox per template and returns 1,440 without changing a single creative decision — highest return-per-minute hole in the set. Reroll conversion (4,704) is bigger but slower; sequence it second.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Budget in list, pay in meters.</strong> Wallets and grants are denominated in credits; capacity in list. Convert once, at the plan rate (82/73/63), and every "overrun" becomes a subtraction problem with a known answer.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Re-check the ladder when volume changes.</strong> The 990 / 2,290 / 3,417 breakevens are monthly; a quarter that drifts from 800 to 1,500 list should move you from wallet to Basic without a meeting.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Count bytes for CJK scripts, per lane.</strong> One extra column in the audio budget (UTF-8 bytes, not characters) closes hole 4 permanently; the turbo-vs-multilingual 2× is hole 4''s quieter twin.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Convert flat operations to per-second before adopting them.</strong> An extend is only cheap until you divide it. Four 360-credit continuations a month is a per-second model waiting to happen.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Fund the canary line.</strong> 186 credits a month of 6-credit rehearsals is the hole whose patch is a purchase — and against the 882-credit rework gap it returned in the post-mortem, it is the cheapest insurance on the sheet.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;padding-left:24px;line-height:1.9;">
                <li>Pull last month''s three largest line items and mark which of the seven holes each contains — the audit is one pass, not a project.</li>
                <li>Flip audio-off on every clip whose sound gets replaced in post; verify against a 5s Omni 720p pair (100 vs. 150 list).</li>
                <li>Move one production line to draft-first pricing this month; the 54% delta is measured, not estimated.</li>
                <li>Set the premium tier per shot list, not per template: 4K where the delivery demands it, nothing above spec by habit.</li>
                <li>Add byte-count and per-second-converted columns to your audio and flat-op budgets; both holes are arithmetic, not taste.</li>
                <li>Compare month-end list volume to 990 / 2,290 / 3,417 before renewing anything; the ladder is the cheapest discount in the series.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">FAQ</h2>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: How many list credits does a $100 wallet really buy?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">It depends on which meter you convert it on: 10,000 list at raw rates, 12,437 at Basic''s 82%, 14,389 at Pro''s 73%, and 17,137 at Ultra''s 63%. The same $100 covers a 20,032-credit month only if that month is actually 14,532 of work plus 8,086 of holes — and even then the wallet holds the patched 14,532, never the as-run 22,618 — which is why the capacity plan and the leak audit have to be the same conversation.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Which leak should I fix first?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">The one that costs a click: the audio-scene default. Leaving Omni audio on where post replaces it anyway is 1,440 list credits on a 300-clip month — bigger than five of the other six holes individually and fixable in one template pass. Reroll conversion returns more (4,704 on half the flagship line) but requires a workflow change, so it comes second by sequence, not by size.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Are canary rehearsals a saving or a cost?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">A cost on the invoice and a saving on the month. Thirty 6-credit probes is 186 list a month; the cutout job they prevent is an 882-credit gap. It is the only hole on this list whose patch is spending more credits, which is exactly why it is the last one teams fund.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: My plan discount is already applied — where is the tier overrun leak?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">In the months that drift across a breakeven line without noticing. Under 990 list a month, wallet purchases are correct; above it, metered billing at 82% is strictly cheaper for the same work, and the difference compounds at the margin: at 1,500 list, Basic vs. raw is 300 credits a month, Pro vs. Basic another ~220. The ladder — 990, 2,290, 3,417 — only saves you if someone reruns it when volume changes.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Why do flat operations hide leaks better than per-second pricing?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Because a flat price flatters itself: 360 credits "for an extension" sounds bounded, and nothing in the line tells you the four seconds it delivered cost 90 each. Per-second rows force the division at decision time; per-operation rows let the conversion happen only at month-end, when the habit already has volume. The fix is mechanical — divide every flat fee by the seconds it actually returned — and it is the same arithmetic the price ladder did across all 17 tiers: the cliff is never where the flat number says it is.</p></div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:0;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Closing: The Budget Was Never the Problem; the Columns Were</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Seven holes, 8,086 credits, one month. None of them required a cheaper model, a smaller brief, or less ambition — they required reading the scene default, dividing the flat fee, counting the bytes, funding the canary, and rerunning the ladder when the volume moved. The cost-engineering series built the table of what everything costs; this audit is what happens when the table meets an unchecked template. Reconcile your own last month against it inside <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/3-0-omni-text-to-video" style="color:#34d399;text-decoration:none;">Kling 3.0 Omni text-to-video</a> and <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/turbo-2-5" style="color:#34d399;text-decoration:none;">ElevenLabs turbo 2.5</a> on FuseAI, and if the clean number lands closer to 14,532 than to 22,618 — the holes were yours, and so is the return.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- multilingual-ai-video-localization-pipeline-elevenlabs-seedream-cost-guide (category=4)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'The AI Video Localization Pipeline: One Master, Four Languages, About 70 Credits a Market — No Full Re-Render Required',
  4,
  'multilingual-ai-video-localization-pipeline-elevenlabs-seedream-cost-guide',
  'Four new markets from one master video on FuseAITools: ElevenLabs dubs and subs, Seedream layer swaps, GPT Image scenes — full credit invoice per market.',
  'AI video localization, multilingual AI dubbing, ElevenLabs pricing, Seedream layer decomposition, GPT Image scene swap, Japanese German Spanish market launch, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>The AI Video Localization Pipeline: One Master, Four Languages, About 70 Credits a Market — No Full Re-Render Required</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Four new markets from one master video on FuseAITools: ElevenLabs dubs and subs, Seedream layer swaps, GPT Image scenes — full credit invoice per market.">
</head>
<body>
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(ellipse 65% 50% at 50% 0%, rgba(34,211,238,.07), transparent),radial-gradient(ellipse 50% 40% at 100% 90%, rgba(167,139,250,.05), transparent),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318, #0b0c0f);color:#e5e7eb;padding:20px;border-radius:12px;">

        <section class="introduction">
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Going global with AI video has an obvious trap: the model will happily re-generate your whole 60-second spot for every market, at full price, every time. The professional answer is the opposite — the master video is rendered <em>once</em>, and localization touches only the layers that actually need to change: voice, captions, backgrounds, and the one or two shots where a Tokyo street genuinely isn''t a Berlin street. This walkthrough launches one English master into four markets (German, Japanese, Spanish, French) on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com" style="color:#22d3ee;">FuseAITools</a>, station by station, with the full invoice at the end.</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">List credits (100 = $1); monthly plans meter credits at <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63%</strong>. The master itself — call it 400–800 credits of production on any pipeline in this series — is a sunk cost this article deliberately excludes, because localization is where launch budgets are won or lost.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="station-1">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Station 1 — Translation and QA: The Chat Newsroom, Under 10 Credits a Language</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">A 950-character narration script plus on-screen-text strings translates through <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kimi/generate" style="color:#22d3ee;">Kimi K3</a> (741 credits per million input, 3,705 per million output) for roughly 5–6 credits a language, with the reasoning summary letting you see how idioms were handled without a second pass. A localization QA sweep through <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok-chat/generate" style="color:#60a5fa;">Grok 4.x chat</a> (249 in / 741 out per million, both directions) catches the thing machine translation reliably misses — register. Japanese marketing copy that reads like a legal disclaimer is a conversion bug, not a typo. Budget <strong style="color:#22d3ee;">~8 credits per language</strong> for translate-plus-QA, four languages at 32 list.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Factory habit: keep the English script, each target translation, and the string-separation table (narration vs. on-screen text vs. CTA button) in one canonical prompt template per market. Translations become a paste, not a project.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="station-2">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Station 2 — Dubbing: ElevenLabs Multilingual v2, One Character Budget Per Language</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Where the faceless-channel line uses <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/turbo-2-5" style="color:#a78bfa;">Turbo 2.5</a> for speed, market launches use <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/multilingual-v2" style="color:#a78bfa;">Multilingual v2</a> at 20 credits per 1,000 characters — the highest-fidelity multilingual voice model on the shelf, priced to be re-recorded. One 950-character track is <strong style="color:#a78bfa;">20 credits list per language</strong>; four markets, one take each, is 80. Two takes on the German CTA line is 100. Character-count billing means the throttle is translation length — a disciplined German translation that doesn''t bloat 30% is literally worth credits.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The voice identity carries over markets through the same voice profile, so brand recognition survives the language switch — which is the entire point of dubbing rather than re-generating speech per market.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="station-3">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Station 3 — Subtitles: Transcribe Your Own Dubs, Six Credits a Language</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Each finished dub goes through <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/speech-to-text" style="color:#a78bfa;">ElevenLabs Speech to Text</a> (6 credits/minute) to produce word-timed captions in the target language — matching the delivered performance, not the pre-recorded script, and giving platforms'' mute-autoplay the subtitles they surface first. Four 60-second markets: <strong style="color:#a78bfa;">24 credits</strong>. Auto-burned captions from the mixed master would be free and wrong; transcribing the dub is six credits and right.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="station-4">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Station 4 — Visual Localization: Seedream Layer Swaps and a GPT Image Landmark Plate</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">This is the station teams skip and then pay for twice. Text baked into scenes, storefront signage, posters, and UI plates need to read the market''s language — <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedream/5-flash-layer-decomposition" style="color:#22d3ee;">Seedream 5 Flash layer decomposition</a> (6 credits flat at any size) breaks a key frame into editable layers so the sign changes and the product, lighting, and composition don''t. For the flagship marketplace banner, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedream/5-pro-layer-decomposition" style="color:#22d3ee;">Pro</a> bills 12 at 1K/1.5K and 24 at 2K. Where a scene''s location itself must localize — the café outside needs to read Tokyo, not Paris — one <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image/v2-5-sunburst-image-to-image" style="color:#c084fc;">GPT Image 2.5 Sunburst image-to-image</a> pass (6 credits at 1K, 18 at 2K, 24 at 4K, up to 16 input images) regenerates the plate around the locked foreground.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Budget shape: three Flash decompositions plus one 2K Sunburst plate per market = <strong style="color:#22d3ee;">36 credits a market</strong>, 144 across four, plus 24 one-time for the Pro 2K flagship banner. Note what this is buying: the master video''s motion never gets re-rendered. Still frames get localized, then the original footage is masked, cropped, or overlaid in assembly — free.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="station-5">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Station 5 — The One Shot Worth Re-Rendering: Wan 3.0 and the Reference-Audio Trick</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Sometimes one hero shot must actually change motion — the presenter walks past a local landmark instead of a generic wall. Re-rendering exactly one shot is the exception that proves the rule: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan 3.0</a> at 480p bills 12 credits/sec with native audio included free, and its reference inputs take up to five audio clips — feed the finished <em>German dub</em> as reference audio and the new shot inherits the dub''s delivery rhythm and voice character instead of inventing a third performer. An 8-second hero shot for one marquee market: <strong style="color:#60a5fa;">96 credits list</strong>. Resist the urge to do this in every market; the pipeline''s economics depend on it being the exception.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="invoice">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Four-Market Launch Invoice</h2>
            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Station</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Job</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">List</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Basic 82%</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Pro 73%</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Ultra 63%</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Translation + QA (Kimi + Grok chat, 4 languages)</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">script + strings</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">32</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">26</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">23</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">20</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Dubbing (Multilingual v2, 4 × ~950 chars)</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">one take per market</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">80</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">66</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">58</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">50</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Subtitles (STT, 4 × 1 min)</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">timed per dub</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">24</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">20</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">18</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">15</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Visual localization (Flash ×12 + Sunburst 2K ×4)</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">signage + plates</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">144</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">118</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">105</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">91</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Flagship key art (Seedream 5 Pro, 2K, one-time)</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">marketplace banner</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">24</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">20</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">18</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">15</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Hero re-render (Wan 3.0 480p, 8s, dub as ref audio)</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">one marquee market</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">96</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">79</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">70</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">60</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Four-market launch</strong></td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">full localization</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">400</strong></td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">328</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">292</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">252</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Strip the one-time and optional lines and the marginal cost is <strong style="color:#22d3ee;">~70 list credits per market</strong> (dub 20 + subs 6 + visuals 36 + translation 8) — about 44 on Ultra. Compare the trap: re-generating the full 60-second spot per market on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-turbo-image-to-video" style="color:#f472b6;">Kling V3 Turbo</a> at 720p silent (27 credits/sec) is 1,620 credits <em>per market</em>, 6,480 list for the launch — sixteen times the pipeline, and every take drifts the product and the brand voice the master already nailed.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="pitfalls">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Localization Failure Modes</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Re-rendering is camouflage for "we didn''t plan layers."</strong> Spot re-gens per market usually mean the master was built with baked-in text that can''t be edited. Decompose at production time — Flash at 6 credits a frame makes pre-planning nearly free.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Translation bloat is a billing line.</strong> German and French narration routinely run 20–30% longer than English; that inflates dubbing (20/1k chars), forces re-timing, and can push the spot over platform length caps. Give the Station 1 prompt a character budget per language and enforce it.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Subtitling the mixed master instead of the dub.</strong> Captions burned from the English audio will drift from the Japanese performance — timestamps, breath, emphasis. Six credits per language to transcribe the actual dub is the whole fix.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Treating every market as marquee.</strong> Station 5 is one shot in one market at 96 credits. Do it in all four and the invoice more than doubles; let market revenue data, not enthusiasm, promote a second market to a hero re-render.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Capability is not clearance.</strong> Local landmark plates and voice profiles are generation capabilities, not rights to imply endorsement. Keep the CTA and trademark layers your own.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="action-checklist">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist</h2>
            <ol style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Build the master with separable layers</strong> — clean plates, decomposable text, no baked-in signage — before any market exists.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Template Station 1 per language</strong> with an explicit character budget so dubbing cost and spot length stay fixed.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Dub, then transcribe the dub</strong> — Multilingual v2 into Speech to Text, never captions off the English master.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Reserve the hero re-render for one marquee market</strong>, and use the dub as Wan 3.0 reference audio so the shot inherits the performance.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Track credits-per-market, not credits-per-launch</strong> — at ~70 list per marginal market, expansion stops being a budget conversation, especially near Ultra''s 63% metering.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section class="faq">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: How many credits does it cost to localize one AI video into a new language?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Roughly 70 list credits per market for dub, subtitles, translated strings QA, and still-frame visual localization — about 57 on Basic, 51 on Pro, 44 on Ultra monthly plans. A full re-render is not part of that number, and shouldn''t be: it would cost 1,620 credits at Turbo 720p rates.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Why Multilingual v2 instead of the cheaper Turbo for dubbing?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Turbo 2.5 is 10 credits per 1,000 characters but is the volume-narration voice; market-facing brand spots get the highest-fidelity multilingual model on the shelf at 20 per 1k. The doubling buys accent naturalness in-language — and one extra dub take per market (20 credits) still keeps the whole launch under 10% of one re-rendered spot.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Can the dubbed voice be kept consistent with the master''s narrator?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Yes — ElevenLabs voice profiles carry the same identity across languages, and when a shot genuinely must be re-rendered, Wan 3.0''s audio references (up to five clips) let the finished dub itself steer the new shot''s voice character, instead of generating an unrelated performer.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: What actually gets localized in the visuals — don''t I have to re-render the video?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Only stills. Seedream 5 Flash layer decomposition (6 credits, any size) edits the text and sign layers inside key frames while product and lighting stay untouched; GPT Image 2.5 Sunburst image-to-image regenerates location plates around locked foregrounds at 6/18/24 credits by size. The localized frames go back into the same master footage in assembly, which costs zero credits.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: When does per-market re-rendering make sense at all?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">When the shot''s motion, not its layers, is what must change — one hero shot, one marquee market, 96 credits at Wan 480p with free native audio. The honest test: if a 6-credit layer swap achieves 90% of the market fit, the 1,620-credit full re-render is a taste decision, not a business one.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="closing">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Render Once, Localize Everywhere</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The arithmetic of going global is really the arithmetic of deciding what must change. Voice: 20 credits. Captions: 6. The words inside the picture: 36. Everything else — the motion, the product, the brand — was already finished when the master rendered. The stations are bookmarked: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/multilingual-v2" style="color:#a78bfa;">dubbing</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/speech-to-text" style="color:#a78bfa;">subtitles</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedream/5-flash-layer-decomposition" style="color:#22d3ee;">layer swaps</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image/v2-5-sunburst-image-to-image" style="color:#c084fc;">local plates</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video" style="color:#60a5fa;">the one hero shot</a>. Four markets, one master, 400 list credits — under 252 on Ultra monthly, and a rounding error against doing it the expensive way.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- native-audio-ai-video-pricing-philosophies (category=4)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'Sound On, Meter Running: Kling 3.0 vs Wan 3.0 vs HappyHorse 1.1 vs Veo on the Three Pricing Philosophies of Native Audio',
  4,
  'native-audio-ai-video-pricing-philosophies-kling-wan-happyhorse',
  'Native AI video audio is priced three ways: surcharge, all-inclusive, or flat. Kling, Wan 3.0, HappyHorse and Veo on identical 10-second sound-on briefs.',
  'AI video with sound, native audio video model, Kling 3.0 audio, Wan 3.0 audio, HappyHorse 1.1, Veo 3.1 pricing, audio surcharge, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Sound On, Meter Running: Kling 3.0 vs Wan 3.0 vs HappyHorse 1.1 vs Veo on the Three Pricing Philosophies of Native Audio</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Native AI video audio is priced three ways: surcharge, all-inclusive, or flat. Kling, Wan 3.0, HappyHorse and Veo on identical 10-second sound-on briefs.">
</head>
<body>
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(ellipse 65% 50% at 50% 0%, rgba(244,114,182,.06), transparent),radial-gradient(ellipse 50% 40% at 100% 90%, rgba(52,211,153,.05), transparent),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318, #0b0c0f);color:#e5e7eb;padding:20px;border-radius:12px;">

        <section class="introduction">
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Native audio went from party trick to deliverable requirement in about eighteen months, and the pricing response split three ways. One camp treats sound as a metered upgrade. One rolled it into the base rate and told nobody. One refused to itemize it at all inside a flat per-clip price. If you generate video with sound on — footsteps, ambience, product whooshes — your vendor choice is really a choice between these three philosophies, and the difference shows up on every invoice.</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">We ran the same brief through all three camps: a 10-second 1080p clip with audio enabled, list prices in credits (100 credits = $1). Monthly plans on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com" style="color:#f472b6;">FuseAITools</a> meter credits at <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63%</strong>, so every quote below gets both numbers.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="camp-1">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Camp 1 — The Surcharge: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-0-video" style="color:#f472b6;">Kling 3.0</a> (and 3.0 Omni)</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Kling''s metering splits every render into silent and sound-on scenes at different per-second rates: standard is 20 credits/sec silent, 30 with sound (+50%); pro tier runs 30 → 40 (+33%). The <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/3-0-omni-text-to-video" style="color:#f472b6;">Omni line</a> mirrors it (720p: 20 → 30; 1080p: 30 → 36) — then does something odd at the top: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">4K is 100 credits/sec with or without sound</strong>. The surcharge evaporates exactly where the clip is most expensive.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Our brief:</strong> 10 sec × 40 (pro with_sound) = <strong style="color:#f472b6;">400 credits list</strong> (~252 on Ultra monthly). The same clip silent would have been 300. You paid 100 credits — 25% of the clip — for the audio track.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The philosophy has one virtue and one vice. Virtue: if you render a third of your catalog silent (banners, loops with licensed music laid over later), you never pay for audio you mute in post. Vice: audio becomes a negotiation you forget you''re in — the toggle''s cost scales with duration, so the clips where sound matters most (longer narrative pieces) carry the biggest surcharge.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="camp-2">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Camp 2 — All-Inclusive: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan 3.0</a> and <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/happy-horse/v1-1-text-to-video" style="color:#34d399;">HappyHorse 1.1</a></h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Wan 3.0 bills one rate per resolution — 12 / 24 / 48 credits per second at 480p / 720p / 1080p — and the audio toggle does not move it. Sound on, sound off, same invoice. <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/happy-horse/v1-1-reference-to-video" style="color:#34d399;">HappyHorse 1.1</a> works the same way (36/sec at 720p, 45 at 1080p across its text, image, and reference modes): native audio is simply part of what the model is.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Our brief:</strong> Wan 10 sec × 48 = <strong style="color:#60a5fa;">480 credits list</strong> (~302 Ultra), sound included at zero marginal cost. HappyHorse lands at 450 (~284) for the same job. Both more expensive than Kling''s silent tier — and both cheaper than Kling pro-with-sound (400) only at the margin HappyHorse holds.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The all-inclusive camp changes your iteration math: since audio is free, you leave it on during drafts and get talking-blocking and ambience reviews for nothing. Teams on surcharge camps often draft silent and re-render with sound at the end — a workflow tax the invoice hides.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="camp-3">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Camp 3 — Flat and Unitemized: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/veo3/text-to-video" style="color:#818cf8;">Veo</a> and <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/sora/text-to-video" style="color:#60a5fa;">Sora 2</a></h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The flat-rate camp never tells you what audio costs because it never asks. Veo 3.1 Lite bills <strong style="color:#818cf8;">24 credits per clip</strong> and Fast 48, native audio and all; the full Veo 3 tier is 360 per generation. Sora 2 is the camp''s radical: <strong style="color:#60a5fa;">10 credits per generation</strong>, sound included, at its native durations.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Our brief:</strong> the 10-second sound-on clip costs 400–480 credits in the per-second camps and 24 in Veo Lite''s world. The honest asterisk: you don''t choose seconds on flat-billed models — you accept their fixed render lengths and styles, and paying the per-second price is precisely what buys duration sliders, resolution ladders, and reference controls.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Where the flat camp wins commercially: volume content with a platform-tolerant look. At Ultra monthly (63% of list), a Veo Lite clip is ~15 credits — cheaper than the audio surcharge on any single Kling clip.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="scorecard">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Same Brief, Five Ways</h2>
            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Setup</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">List credits</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Basic 82%</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Pro 73%</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Ultra 63%</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Kling 3.0 pro, silent</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">300</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">246</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">219</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">189</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Kling 3.0 pro, sound on</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">400</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">328</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">292</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">252</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Wan 3.0 1080p, sound on (surcharge-free)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">480</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">394</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">350</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">302</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">HappyHorse 1.1 1080p, sound native</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">450</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">369</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">329</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">284</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Veo 3.1 Lite, flat clip</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">24</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">20</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">18</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">15</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Sora 2, flat generation</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">10</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">8</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">7</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">6</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Add the escape hatch every camp offers: if the video model''s audio disappoints at any price, strip it with <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/audio-isolation" style="color:#a78bfa;">ElevenLabs Audio Isolation</a> (0.3 credits/sec) and rebuild sound from <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/sound-effect-v2" style="color:#a78bfa;">Sound Effect v2</a> (0.36/sec) plus a 20-credit <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/generate" style="color:#34d399;">Suno</a> bed — a full professional soundtrack for roughly the cost of one Kling audio surcharge on a ten-second clip.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="pitfalls">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Four Ways Audio Pricing Tricks You</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">The surcharge rides along with duration.</strong> Kling''s audio premium scales per second — on a 15-second Omni clip it''s the price of three extra Veo Lite generations, silently.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">"Audio free" is a resolution-tier claim.</strong> Wan''s toggle is free at every tier, but HappyHorse''s 1080p base rate (45/sec) still exceeds Kling''s silent 1080p (30/sec). Compare same-size totals, not toggle prices.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">4K erases the difference.</strong> At Kling''s 4K tier, sound costs nothing extra — the surcharge structure only matters below the flagship resolution.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Flat camp ≠ same deliverable.</strong> Veo Lite''s 24 credits buys its native length and look; quoting it against a controlled 1080p/10s render is comparing a single to a bespoke suit.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="action-checklist">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist</h2>
            <ol style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Route silent-by-design work to surcharge camps.</strong> Music-overlay edits and loop banners don''t need generated audio — Kling''s silent scenes profit you where all-inclusive camps overcharge.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Draft with sound on all-inclusive models.</strong> Wan and HappyHorse let you review speech and ambience in every pass at zero marginal cost.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Volume content with tolerant styles goes flat.</strong> Sora 2 at 10 and Veo Lite at 24 credits per clip beat every per-second rate at social scale.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Audit the 1080p boundary.</strong> It''s where Kling pro (40/sec with sound), HappyHorse (45), and Wan (48) nearly converge — model choice there should follow control features, not price.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Price the rebuild alternative before conceding surcharges.</strong> Isolate + SFX + Suno bed ≈ 10–15 credits of audio engineering on a clip that costs 300–480 for the picture.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section class="faq">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Which AI video model has the cheapest sound-on audio?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">In per-clip absolute terms, Sora 2 (10 credits flat, audio included) and Veo 3.1 Lite (24 flat). Among per-second models with duration control, HappyHorse 1.1 lands cheapest on our 10-second 1080p brief (450 list, ~284 on Ultra) because native audio is in its base rate, versus Kling''s 400-list surcharged quote only if you render silent.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Does Wan 3.0 really charge nothing for audio?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Correct — the audio toggle on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video-prime" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan 3.0 (and the 80/sec Prime tier)</a> does not change the per-second rate at any resolution. Sound on and sound off render identically priced. The fast Prime variant keeps the same rule.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: When does Kling''s audio surcharge disappear?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">At 4K: Kling 3.0 and the <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/3-0-omni-image-to-video" style="color:#f472b6;">Omni image-to-video line</a> both bill 100 credits/sec regardless of sound. Below 4K the surcharge is +50% at standard tier and +33% at pro — always per second, so it grows with duration.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Can I just replace bad generated audio instead of paying surcharges?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Yes, and it''s cheap: ElevenLabs Audio Isolation removes a track at 0.3 credits/sec, Sound Effect v2 foley-beds at 0.36/sec, and one Suno generation lays music for 20 credits. A fully rebuilt 10-second soundtrack runs about 26 credits — under a third of Kling''s 10-second audio surcharge at pro tier… except for voice, where the video model''s lip-synced native audio is worth the meter.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: How do plan discounts interact with the surcharge?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">They scale it like everything else — monthly Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63% apply to the with-sound rate, so Kling pro audio clips cost Ultra holders 252 credits versus the 200 silent quote. The surcharge camp stays a surcharge camp at any plan; discounts shrink the premium (from 100 to 63 credits on our brief) but never zero it.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="closing">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Pick a Philosophy, Not Just a Model</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Audio pricing is the cheapest kind of vendor decision to get right: the numbers are public, the brief is identical, and the crossover points are arithmetic. Surcharge when your output is mostly silent; all-inclusive when you iterate with sound on; flat when style tolerance meets volume.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The four camps are one toggle apart on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-0-video" style="color:#f472b6;">FuseAITools'' video shelf</a> — run your actual brief through each and let the invoice settle the philosophy debate.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- order-rejected-parameter-combinations-model-not-support (category=2)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'The Parameter Combinations That Get Your Order Refused: MODEL_IS_NOT_SUPPORT, the Three Legal Upscale Paths, and the Silent 2K Alpha Trap',
  2,
  'order-rejected-parameter-combinations-model-not-support',
  'Why orders get refused: three legal Grok upscale pairs, numImages mandatory 1–4, and the 2K transparent request that passes every gate and still bills.',
  'MODEL_IS_NOT_SUPPORT, AI video order rejected, numImages parameter, transparent background 2K, pricing row no-match, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>The Parameter Combinations That Get Your Order Refused: MODEL_IS_NOT_SUPPORT, the Three Legal Upscale Paths, and the Silent 2K Alpha Trap</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Why orders get refused: three legal Grok upscale pairs, numImages mandatory 1–4, and the 2K transparent request that passes every gate and still bills.">
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/news/order-rejected-parameter-combinations-model-not-support">
</head>
<body style="margin:0;padding:0;background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(circle at 15% 0%, rgba(248,113,113,0.07) 0%, transparent 45%),radial-gradient(circle at 85% 100%, rgba(251,191,36,0.05) 0%, transparent 45%),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318 0%, #0b0c0f 100%);color:#d1d5db;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="max-width:960px;margin:0 auto;padding:32px 20px 64px;">
        <header style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h1 itemprop="headline" style="font-size:2rem;line-height:1.25;color:#f9fafb;margin:0 0 16px;">The Parameter Combinations That Get Your Order Refused: MODEL_IS_NOT_SUPPORT, the Three Legal Upscale Paths, and the Silent 2K Alpha Trap</h1>
            <p style="color:#9ca3af;font-size:0.95rem;margin:0;">Published <time itemprop="datePublished" datetime="2026-11-12">November 12, 2026</time> · FuseAI Tools Editorial</p>
        </header>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Introduction: The Gate Is Not One Door, It Is Three</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Ask a team why orders fail and you will get one story. The real answer is three gates standing between your form and the vendor, and each one rejects for different reasons, at different costs, with different error text. On <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/upscale" style="color:#f87171;text-decoration:none;">FuseAI</a>: the <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">DTO whitelist</strong> (field-level: required fields, legal values, image counts), the <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">pricing matcher</strong> (combination-level: the request must hit exactly one row in the price table, or it dies as <code style="color:#f87171;background:#1f2937;padding:1px 6px;border-radius:4px;">MODEL_IS_NOT_SUPPORT</code>), and the <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">vendor itself</strong> — which only sees the orders the first two let through. Most rejection complaints filed as "the model is broken" are actually a pricing-row no-match, and the fix is not a bug report; it is knowing which combinations have rows.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Here is the part that changes budgets: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">six of the seven failure classes below cost zero credits</strong> because the gates refuse before anything is charged. The dangerous one is the eighth entry in the table — a request that passes every gate, bills normally, and delivers something subtly wrong. Read the refusals as free education, and the silent pass as the reason audits exist.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Refusal Taxonomy, With Real Rows</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Class A — the combination has no row.</strong> <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/upscale" style="color:#f87171;text-decoration:none;">Grok upscale</a> is the purest case: exactly three legal paths exist — 480→720 at 15/s of source seconds, 720→1080 at 30/s, and 480→1080 at 45/s. There is no 720→720 row (nothing changes) and no 1080→4K row (the ladder stops). Request one and the matcher finds nothing to bill against: no-match, <code>MODEL_IS_NOT_SUPPORT</code>, order gone, credits untouched. The same logic explains the 1080p refusal on Grok video 1.5: the vendor supports it, the price table does not have it, and a feature without a price is not a feature.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Class B — a mandatory key went missing.</strong> <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/qwen/image-edit" style="color:#f87171;text-decoration:none;">Qwen image edit</a> charges 9 credits per image with numImages required between 1 and 4 — but that mandatory status is dated October 20, 2026. Before it, an absent n resolved to <code>Integer.valueOf(null)</code> and the failure was a server error, not a clean refusal. <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/flux-3-klein-text-to-image" style="color:#f87171;text-decoration:none;">Flux 3 Klein</a> is stricter still: resolution and numImages are both <em>billing keys</em>, which means the pricing engine literally cannot compute a cost without them. Treat "the price depends on it" as "the order needs it."</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Class C — auto is the vendor''s default and the platform''s refusal.</strong> Layer decomposition on the Wan family accepts an <code>auto</code> size, and FuseAI rejects it on principle: with auto, the vendor picks the resolution after the fact, so no pricing row can be matched before submission and the cost is unknowable at the gate. Same story in reverse on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/3-0-omni-image-to-video" style="color:#f87171;text-decoration:none;">Kling Omni image-to-video</a>, where a single shot must pass <code>aspect_ratio=auto</code> — here auto is the <em>only</em> legal value, because the first frame owns the ratio. "Auto" is not one setting; it is a different contract per endpoint, and both directions of the mistake are common.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Class D — the input count is part of the row.</strong> Omni image-to-video wants exactly one image; v3-turbo image-to-video accepts at most one; GPT 2.5 flare edit takes up to sixteen; <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/qwen/image-to-image" style="color:#f87171;text-decoration:none;">Qwen 2.1 image-to-image</a> wants one to ten, with an optional mask that silently switches it into local-edit mode at the same price. Zero or two on the one-image endpoints, seventeen on the sixteen-cap endpoint: all refusals, all free, all avoidable by reading the input slot as a number and not a checkbox.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Rejection Ledger: Eight Requests, Seven Free Lessons</h2>
            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:20px;font-size:0.92rem;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:#1f2937;">
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:left;">Refused request</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:left;">Gate that caught it</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">Credits burned</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">If it had passed</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Grok upscale 720p → 720p</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;color:#9ca3af;">pricing matcher (no-match)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#34d399;">0</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">—</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Qwen image edit, n omitted</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;color:#9ca3af;">DTO @NotBlank</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#34d399;">0</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">n × 9</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Klein 4K batch of 5 (numImages 1–4)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;color:#9ca3af;">@AssertTrue combo check</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#34d399;">0</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">48 (4 × 12); a 5th would be 60</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Wan layer decomposition, size=auto</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;color:#9ca3af;">DTO whitelist</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#34d399;">0</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">unknown — the point</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Omni i2v, single shot, aspect 16:9</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;color:#9ca3af;">contract check (must be auto)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#34d399;">0</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">150 (5s @ 30/s)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Omni i2v with two reference frames</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;color:#9ca3af;">DTO (exactly 1)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#34d399;">0</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">150–225</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Nano Banana 2.1, resolution omitted</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;color:#9ca3af;">pricing matcher (billing key)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#34d399;">0</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">6 / 9 / 12 by tier</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;color:#f87171;">GPT Image 2.5, 2K + background=transparent</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;color:#f87171;">none — it passes</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#fbbf24;">18 billed</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">opaque 2K, no alpha</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;margin-top:16px;">The last row is the whole article in one line: <code>background</code> is only in effect at 1K on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image/v2-5-flare-text-to-image" style="color:#f87171;text-decoration:none;">GPT Image 2.5</a> — a parameter the pricing table does not read and the DTO does not police. Ask for transparency at 2K (18 list) and you get a legitimate, correctly-sized, completely opaque image — 13 at Pro''s 73% and 15 at Basic''s 82%, since the ledger is in list and the plans meter at Basic 82% / Pro 73% / Ultra 63%. It is not a bug; it is a contract clause nobody put on the button.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The October Tightening: When the Walls Moved</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Three of the refusals above have a birth date, and that is its own failure class for automation: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">a request that worked last month can legitimately fail this one</strong>. On October 19 the Wan-family quality rows tightened resolution from optional to mandatory (1K/2K only — other values are vendor-refused, and now platform-refused first). On October 20 the GPT Image 2.5 family tightened resolution to <code>@NotBlank</code>, Qwen''s n became mandatory with the 1–4 combo check, and Klein shipped with both keys required from day one. Nothing in this list is a regression; all of it is the gate learning to refuse on the platform''s terms instead of the vendor''s.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">For anyone running batch pipelines, the operational lesson is versioning: a template library is a set of assumptions about the gates, and the gates move. The cheap insurance is a canary order per model per week — at 6 credits (the debug-tier rate that <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/nano-banana/nano-banana-2-lite" style="color:#f87171;text-decoration:none;">Nano Banana 2 lite</a> charges for a whole generation) you learn within a week whether your assumptions are still true, instead of discovering it mid-batch when 200 of 240 orders fail the same new wall.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Pitfalls and One-Line Fixes</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">No-match reads like an outage; it is a price table.</strong> <code>MODEL_IS_NOT_SUPPORT</code> means "this combination has no row," not "this model is down." Check the combination against the ladder before filing anything.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Vendor support is not platform availability.</strong> Grok 1080p video and 720→720 upscale exist at the vendor level; without price rows they cannot be ordered. If a feature matters, its price row is what to watch for.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Billing-key fields fail loudly and cheaply.</strong> If resolution or numImages changes the price, omitting it must fail — the matcher cannot guess. The fix is never "make it default"; the fix is your form sending it.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Auto means "you choose" on some endpoints and "I refuse" on others.</strong> Kling i2v single-shot: auto is the only legal aspect value. Layer decomposition auto: the only illegal size. Read the contract per endpoint, never carry the habit.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Input counts are numbers with upper and lower bounds.</strong> Exactly-1, ≤1, ≤16, 1–10: four different sentences in four neighboring models. Put the sentence in the template note, not in memory.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">The silent pass is the one that costs.</strong> Every loud refusal saved credits. The 2K transparent request charged 18 and delivered an opaque file — the only line in the ledger where the money left and the requirement did not arrive. Feature-condition parameters (background at tier X, mode at resolution Y) belong on checklists.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;padding-left:24px;line-height:1.9;">
                <li>Before automating a new model, enumerate its legal parameter tuples from the price table — the rows are the contract, the form is not.</li>
                <li>Send every billing-key field explicitly (resolution, numImages, duration where metered); "the form has a default" is not the same sentence as "the matcher has a row."</li>
                <li>Pin transparent-background work to 1K, or verify alpha at 2K/4K with one test order before the batch, not after the invoice.</li>
                <li>Keep the error strings distinguishable in your logs: DTO refusal, pricing no-match, and vendor rejection are three different repairs.</li>
                <li>Run one 6-credit canary per pipeline model weekly — gates moved three times in October alone.</li>
                <li>For upscaling, plan the source resolution as if only three doors exist: 480→720, 720→1080, 480→1080. Everything else is a free lesson.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">FAQ</h2>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: What does MODEL_IS_NOT_SUPPORT actually mean?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">It is the pricing matcher saying "no row matches your combination." The model exists and is healthy; the specific tuple — a 720p-to-720p upscale, an omitted resolution on a tier-priced model — simply has nothing to bill against. These refusals are free, which makes them the cheapest information the platform gives you.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Why does Grok upscale reject my 720p video even though it is supported?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Only three source-to-target pairs have price rows: 480→720 at 15 per source-second, 720→1080 at 30, and 480→1080 at 45. A 720p source can only go to 1080; anything else finds no row and dies at the gate with no charge. If your plan needed 720→720, the request was always a no-op — the refusal is telling you the truth.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: My transparent background request worked but the PNG has no alpha — why?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Because background=transparent is only in effect at 1K on the GPT Image 2.5 family. At 2K the order passes every gate — the price row exists, the field is legal — and the transparency condition silently does not apply. This is the rare failure that bills: check alpha on one test frame at your delivery resolution before committing a batch.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Why is auto rejected as a size on layer decomposition when the vendor accepts it?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">With auto, the vendor picks the resolution after pricing must be locked, so no row can be matched in advance and the platform cannot tell you the cost before charging it. Refusing auto is the same principle that makes resolution a mandatory billing key on Klein and Nano Banana 2.1: if a field can change the price, the order must state it. Pick 1K or 2K explicitly.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Do rejected orders consume credits?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Gate refusals do not — DTO validation and pricing no-match both resolve before any charge, which is why this entire failure class is the cheapest to learn. The two things that do cost are the silent passes (valid order, wrong assumption — like alpha at 2K) and the pre-October behavior where missing mandatory fields surfaced as server errors rather than clean rejections. The 6-credit canary exists precisely to convert the first kind of lesson before it becomes a batch.</p></div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:0;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Closing: Free Lessons Are Still Lessons</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The gates are doing you a favor seven times out of eight: refusing loudly, charging nothing, and naming the exact tuple that has no home in the price table. The only expensive version of this story is the request that passes — because the platform''s job is to bill what it can price, and your job is to know what you are buying. Keep the three-door upscale map, the mandatory-key list, and the one silent-pass line somewhere your automation can see them, and spot-check the contract rows yourself inside <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/upscale" style="color:#f87171;text-decoration:none;">Grok upscale</a> and <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image/v2-5-flare-text-to-image" style="color:#f87171;text-decoration:none;">GPT Image 2.5 flare text-to-image</a> on FuseAI. A refusal costs seconds. The silent pass costs the batch.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- podcast-repurposing-suno-music-ad-reads-stt-clips-cost (category=3)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'One Episode, Three Clips: A Podcast Repurposing Month at 2,116 List Credits, Priced in Four Billing Units',
  3,
  'podcast-repurposing-suno-music-ad-reads-stt-clips-cost',
  'A podcast repurposing month priced in four billing units: Suno''s flat-per-op music, byte-billed ad reads, per-minute transcripts, per-second social clips.',
  'podcast intro music AI cost, Suno flat per operation pricing, sponsor ad read voiceover bytes, episode transcription cost per minute, podcast clip generation pricing, ElevenLabs STT clipping, Seedance mini video clips, audio content repurposing, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>One Episode, Three Clips: A Podcast Repurposing Month at 2,116 List Credits, Priced in Four Billing Units</title>
    <meta name="description" content="A podcast repurposing month priced in four billing units: Suno''s flat-per-op music, byte-billed ad reads, per-minute transcripts, per-second social clips.">
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/news/podcast-repurposing-suno-music-ad-reads-stt-clips-cost">
</head>
<body style="margin:0;padding:0;background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(circle at 15% 0%, rgba(167,139,250,0.07) 0%, transparent 45%),radial-gradient(circle at 85% 100%, rgba(52,211,153,0.05) 0%, transparent 45%),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318 0%, #0b0c0f 100%);color:#d1d5db;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="max-width:960px;margin:0 auto;padding:32px 20px 64px;">
        <header style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h1 itemprop="headline" style="font-size:2rem;line-height:1.25;color:#f9fafb;margin:0 0 16px;">One Episode, Three Clips: A Podcast Repurposing Month at 2,116 List Credits, Priced in Four Billing Units</h1>
            <p style="color:#9ca3af;font-size:0.95rem;margin:0;">Published <time itemprop="datePublished" datetime="2026-11-12">November 12, 2026</time> · FuseAI Tools Editorial</p>
        </header>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Introduction: The Show Bills in Four Currencies at Once</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">A podcast producer shipping six 30-minute episodes a month does not have one budget line — it has four, and they share no unit. Music is a <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">flat fee per operation</strong>, voice-over is <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">bytes</strong>, transcription is <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">rounded-up minutes</strong>, and social clips are <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">seconds</strong>. The whole repurposing month on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno" style="color:#a78bfa;text-decoration:none;">FuseAI</a> — theme beds, sponsor reads, six full transcripts, eighteen clips, six covers, and the audiogram backgrounds underneath them — lands at <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">2,116 list credits</strong>: 1,734 at Basic''s 82%, 1,544 at Pro''s 73%, 1,333 at Ultra''s 63%. No single meter could predict that total; you have to read four rate-card shapes.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The organizing idea is the repurposing ratio — <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">one episode in, three clips out</strong> — and it hides a cost most shows forget: to cut three clips you first transcribe the whole hour, so the per-minute transcript line (1,080, 51% of the month) is really the price of <em>finding</em> the moments worth clipping. The other 49% is what you ship: clips that render by the second on a 3-credit draft lane, ad reads billed by the byte, and music that costs the same whether you loop it twice or eight times.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Currency 1: Music Is a Flat Fee per Operation — Loop It Freely</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The intro theme, the outro, and the segment beds all come from <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/generate" style="color:#a78bfa;text-decoration:none;">Suno</a>, and Suno is the platform''s flattest biller: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">20 credits per operation, no duration dimension at all</strong>. Five operations for the show''s themes (generate + extends to reach full-length beds) = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">100 list credits</strong>; three more for segment transitions = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">60</strong>. The consequence is the opposite of every metered lane on this sheet: because the fee is per <em>render</em> and not per second, you can extend an intro to ninety seconds or cut six bed variants for the same 20 each, and you can loop the finished track through all six episodes without a new charge — the meter only moves when you ask for a <em>new</em> generation via <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/extend" style="color:#a78bfa;text-decoration:none;">extend</a> or the other Suno operations, never when you reuse one. Music is the one line where creativity is free at the margin.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Currency 2: Sponsor Reads Are Bytes — and a 90-Word Read Is One Block</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The midroll ads are voiced on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/turbo-2-5" style="color:#a78bfa;text-decoration:none;">ElevenLabs turbo</a>, billed <code>ceil(UTF-8 bytes / 1000) × 10</code>. A 30-second host read is about 90 words ≈ 600 bytes — under a thousand, so <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">one 10-credit block</strong>, and two reads per episode is 20: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">120 list credits</strong> for all six episodes'' sponsor inventory. The byte unit is the thing to watch here: a script that grows from 600 to 1,001 bytes does not cost 67% more, it costs <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">double</strong>, because the ceiling crosses into a second block. Sponsor copy is the one place a producer is tempted to pad ("and their partner this episode is..."), and padding is exactly how a 10-credit read becomes a 20-credit read. Keep ad scripts under a thousand bytes and the whole month''s voice-over is 120 credits.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Currency 3: Transcripts Are Minutes — the Rounding Tax that Buys the Clip Search</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">To pull three clips you need the whole episode searchable, and that is <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/speech-to-text" style="color:#a78bfa;text-decoration:none;">speech-to-text</a> at <code>ceil(seconds / 60) × 6</code>. A 30-minute episode bills 30 whole minutes = 180 credits; six of them = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">1,080 list credits</strong>, the largest block on the sheet. This is the line that funders misread as "utility" and then discover is half the budget — but the transcript is not overhead, it is the <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">clip-finding engine</strong>: searchable text is how you locate the quotable twenty seconds in an hour of talk, and the same transcript drops straight into show notes and the SEO page. The rounding tax is real (a 30:01 episode pays 31 minutes), so the discipline is to book clean halves; the 180-per-episode figure is the number to budget against the discovery it enables, not to minimize by skipping — a show that clips from gut-feel without transcripts just pays the search cost in producer hours instead.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Currency 4: Clips Are Seconds — and the Draft Lane Makes the Ratio Pencil Out</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The eighteen social clips (three per episode) are motion, and motion is the only lane here that bills per second — but on the cheapest meter available. Rendering on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedance/v2-mini" style="color:#a78bfa;text-decoration:none;">Seedance 2 mini</a> at 480p <em>with</em> a reference (the episode''s cover still driving the clip) is <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">3 credits per second</strong>, the with_video scene, so a 12-second clip is 36 and eighteen of them = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">648 list credits</strong>. That 3/s scene is the entire reason the one-in-three-clips ratio is affordable: the without-video rate is double at 6/s, and dropping to the with-reference draft lane is what turns eighteen renders from an unaffordable 1,296 into the month''s second-largest — but not the largest — line. The audiogram backgrounds behind the waveforms are the cheapest fixed asset on the platform, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/qwen/z-image" style="color:#a78bfa;text-decoration:none;">z-image</a> at a flat 3 apiece (18 = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">54</strong>), and the six episode covers render on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/nano-banana/nano-banana-2-1" style="color:#a78bfa;text-decoration:none;">Nano Banana 2.1</a> at 2K, 9 each = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">54</strong>. Stills and clips together, the visual output of the show is 756 credits — smaller than the transcripts that made them possible.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Month''s Invoice: Six Episodes, Four Meters, One Repurposing Engine</h2>
            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:20px;font-size:0.92rem;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:#1f2937;">
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:left;">Line</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:left;">Priced as</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">List</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">Basic 82%</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">Pro 73%</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">Ultra 63%</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Theme + outro beds, 5 ops</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Suno flat 20 × 5</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">100</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">82</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">73</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">63</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Segment transitions, 3 ops</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Suno flat 20 × 3</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">60</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">49</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">44</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">38</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Sponsor ad reads, 6 eps × 2</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Turbo ceil(bytes/1k) × 10</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">120</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">98</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">88</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">76</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Full-episode transcripts, 6 × 30 min</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">STT ceil(min) × 6</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#a78bfa;">1,080</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">886</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">788</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">680</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Social clips, 18 × 12s</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Seedance mini 480p with-ref 3/s</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#a78bfa;">648</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">531</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">473</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">408</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Episode covers, 6 at 2K</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Nano Banana 2.1 2K × 9</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">54</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">44</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">39</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">34</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Audiogram backgrounds, 18</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">z-image, flat 3</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">54</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">44</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">39</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">34</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Month total</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">—</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#f87171;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">2,116</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">1,734</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">1,544</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#34d399;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">1,333</strong></td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;margin-top:16px;">Discounts are the monthly plan rates: Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63%, applied per line and rounded, and the columns sum to the total. Sort the invoice by meter instead of by size and the whole budgeting lesson appears: per-<strong style="color:#f9fafb;">operation</strong> (music, 160) is flat and creativity-friendly; per-<strong style="color:#f9fafb;">byte</strong> (ad reads, 120) punishes padding at block boundaries; per-<strong style="color:#f9fafb;">minute</strong> (transcripts, 1,080) is the discovery tax that rounds up; per-<strong style="color:#f9fafb;">second</strong> (clips, 648) is the only line where shortening the deliverable is the main lever. A producer who budgets this month as "audio + video" will be wrong about both — the four units move independently, and only the per-minute and per-second lines reward trimming. The transcripts at 1,080 are the honest cost of finding the clips; the 648 of clips are the payoff; everything else is 388.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Pitfalls: Five Ways a Repurposing Month Overbills Itself</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Padding sponsor reads past a thousand bytes.</strong> Turbo voice bills per byte-block, and crossing 1,000 bytes doubles a 10-credit read to 20. Ad scripts are the place producers add words they don''t need — and pay double for them.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Clipping on the without-reference rate.</strong> Seedance mini with a reference still bills 3/s; without, it doubles to 6/s. Driving clips from the episode cover is not a stylistic choice — it is an 8%-of-the-month choice (1,296 vs 648 on this many clips).</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Booking episodes that don''t land on whole minutes.</strong> Transcripts round up every partial minute; six 30:20 episodes pay 31 minutes each for the sake of twenty seconds. Trim the recording to clean halves before the STT pass, not after.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Re-rendering music to "try another length."</strong> Suno is flat 20 per operation, so a longer intro is a new operation — but a longer <em>reused</em> bed is free. Generate once, extend deliberately, and loop the finished track across all six episodes without metering it.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Skipping transcripts and clipping by ear.</strong> It feels like saving 1,080, but it re-bills the same cost as producer hours hunting audio and loses the SEO show-notes page the transcript gives you for free. The transcript is a multi-product asset, not overhead.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist: Running the Repurposing Pipeline</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;padding-left:24px;line-height:1.9;">
                <li>Generate the theme and beds once on Suno''s flat-20 lane, then reuse and loop them freely — spend new operations only on genuinely new music.</li>
                <li>Cap every sponsor read under 1,000 UTF-8 bytes; write to the block boundary, and hand the host copy that fits in one 10-credit pass.</li>
                <li>Trim each recording to whole minutes before transcription, then let the single per-minute transcript feed clips, show notes, and the SEO page.</li>
                <li>Render social clips on the mini lane <em>with</em> the episode cover as reference (3/s), not without (6/s) — and keep them to 12 seconds.</li>
                <li>Build audiogram backgrounds on the flat-3 lane and covers on the flat-ish 9 lane; the show''s still assets are the cheapest line on the sheet.</li>
                <li>Budget in four currencies, not two: per-op, per-byte, per-minute, per-second each respond to a different lever, and only the last two reward trimming.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">FAQ</h2>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: What does it cost to run a six-episode podcast''s content month?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">2,116 list credits end to end — music beds, sponsor reads, six full transcripts, eighteen social clips, six covers, and the audiogram backgrounds: 1,734 at Basic 82%, 1,544 at Pro 73%, 1,333 at Ultra 63%. The transcripts are the biggest line at 1,080 because a whole episode must be searchable to find three clips.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Why is podcast music the cheapest creative lane?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Because Suno bills a flat 20 credits per operation with no duration dimension. Extending an intro to ninety seconds or generating six bed variants is 20 each, and looping a finished track through every episode costs nothing new — the meter only moves on a fresh generate, extend, or cover. Creativity is free at the margin on this lane.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: How do you keep AI sponsor reads from quietly doubling?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Watch the byte-block boundary. ElevenLabs turbo bills <code>ceil(bytes/1000) × 10</code>, so a 600-byte, 90-word read is one 10-credit block, but the same read padded past 1,000 bytes crosses into a second block and costs 20 — a doubling, not a bump. Write ad copy to fit one block and the whole month''s voice-over is 120 credits.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: What is the real cost of turning one episode into three clips?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">About 216 credits per episode here: 180 for the full transcript that lets you find the moments, plus about 36 per clip for eighteen 12-second renders on the mini draft lane with a reference image (3/s). The transcript, not the render, is the larger share — clip discovery is the hidden cost of the one-in-three ratio.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Which line rewards trimming, and which does not?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Two do, two don''t. Per-second clips and per-minute transcripts shrink when you shorten the deliverable — cut clip length, land recordings on whole minutes. But per-operation music (flat 20) and per-byte ad reads (under a block) do not reward trimming at all; you save on those by reusing renders and staying under block boundaries, not by making things shorter.</p></div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:0;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Closing: Read All Four Meters, or Budget the Wrong Show</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The repurposing engine works because the producer treated one month as four separate budgets: reuse music freely on the flat lane, write sponsor reads to the byte boundary, pay the per-minute transcript as the price of clip discovery, and render the clips on the 3-per-second draft scene instead of the 6 one. The invoice is that literacy priced — 2,116 list credits, half of it honestly spent finding the moments and a third of that turning them into clips. The lanes run inside <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/generate" style="color:#a78bfa;text-decoration:none;">Suno</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/speech-to-text" style="color:#a78bfa;text-decoration:none;">ElevenLabs speech-to-text</a>, and <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedance/v2-mini" style="color:#a78bfa;text-decoration:none;">Seedance 2 mini</a> on FuseAI — transcribe one real episode first and time how long clip-hunting takes without it; the 180-credit transcript is the cheapest producer-hours you will ever buy. Try <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/turbo-2-5" style="color:#a78bfa;text-decoration:none;">FuseAITools</a> for podcast ad reads and repurposed social clips.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- premium-tier-upgrade-4k-prime-upscale-worth-it (category=4)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'The Upgrade Ledger: What 4K, Prime Speed, and Upscale Passes Actually Cost — and Which Premium Tier Earns Its Delta',
  4,
  'premium-tier-upgrade-4k-prime-upscale-worth-it',
  '4K renders, Prime speed and upscale passes priced as real decisions — when each premium tier earns its delta, and when platforms delete it.',
  '4K AI video pricing, Wan Prime speed, Grok upscale cost, Kling Omni 4K credits, premium AI video tiers, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>The Upgrade Ledger: What 4K, Prime Speed, and Upscale Passes Actually Cost — and Which Premium Tier Earns Its Delta</title>
    <meta name="description" content="4K renders, Prime speed and upscale passes priced as real decisions — when each premium tier earns its delta, and when platforms delete it.">
</head>
<body>
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(ellipse 65% 50% at 50% 0%, rgba(96,165,250,.07), transparent),radial-gradient(ellipse 50% 40% at 100% 90%, rgba(244,114,182,.05), transparent),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318, #0b0c0f);color:#e5e7eb;padding:20px;border-radius:12px;">

        <section class="introduction">
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Every video family keeps a top drawer: the tier that costs roughly double and says nothing about why. On <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com" style="color:#60a5fa;">FuseAITools</a> three premium lanes cover almost all upgrade decisions — <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">resolution ladders</strong> (4K renders at 100 credits/sec and up), <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">speed lanes</strong> (<a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video-prime" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan 3.0 Prime</a> at 1.67× standard pricing), and <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">post-hoc upgrades</strong> (<a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/upscale" style="color:#a78bfa;">Grok upscale</a> at 15–45 credits/sec). The right answer for each isn''t a quality opinion; it''s a delta calculation against where the deliverable will actually be watched. This is that ledger, in list credits (100 = $1; monthly plans meter at Basic 82%, Pro 73%, <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Ultra 63%</strong>).</p>
        </section>

        <section class="ladders">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Resolution Ladder Is Exponential, Not Linear</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Start with the fact that makes 4K decisions cheap to argue: within <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan 3.0</a>, each rung doubles — 12 / 24 / 48 credits per second for 480p / 720p / 1080p. Budget-wise, resolution isn''t a staircase you climb evenly; it''s a doubling sequence where the last step costs as much as everything below it. <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-0-video" style="color:#f472b6;">Kling 3.0</a> runs the same shape harder: std-with-sound 30/sec, pro 40/sec, and <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">4K at 100/sec</strong> — 2.5× pro in one jump. An 8-second hero shot is 320 credits at pro and 800 at 4K; the +480 delta (302 metered on Ultra) is the exact question "does this shot ever get seen on a big screen?" has to answer.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">One quirk worth banking: Kling''s <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/3-0-omni-text-to-video" style="color:#f472b6;">Omni</a> line charges audio premiums at 720p and 1080p (30 → 36/sec) but bills 4K identically with or without audio at 100/sec — the audio surcharge quietly disappears at the top of the ladder.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="speed">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Prime Buys the Calendar, Not the Frame</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Prime is the upgrade most often mis-sold by intuition: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video-prime" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan 3.0 Prime</a> costs 20 / 40 / 80 per second — a flat 1.67× the standard line at identical output specs, because the contract difference is the generation lane''s speed, not its pixels. So the delta is never a quality argument; it''s a turnaround rate: 8-second 1080p shot, 384 standard, 640 Prime, +256 list credits (161 metered on Ultra) to compress the queue. That number clears a trend-jacking window, a same-day client notes cycle, or a live-event edit — and buys literally nothing for a video scheduled two weeks out. Price Prime like overtime labor, because that''s the product.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="upscale">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Upscale Pass: The Only Upgrade That Rides a Kept Draft</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The third lane is structurally different: Grok''s upscale bills against a video you already have — 480p→720p at 15/sec, 720p→1080p at 30/sec, 480p→1080p at 45/sec. Which reframes the comparison entirely: promoting a <em>kept</em> <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedance/v2-mini" style="color:#a78bfa;">8-second Mini draft</a> to 1080p delivery costs 360 credits flat, while rendering a fresh 1080p final costs 288–384 <em>before</em> the keep-rate multiplier makes it 461+ on real statistics. The upscale is the one premium that inherits your draft-lane work instead of replacing it — the graduation path from the 24-credit world into the delivery spec, capped at 1080p and worth using every time the platform''s ceiling is there anyway.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="invoice">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">One Month, Four Upgrade Decisions, Billed</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Baseline month from the production anchors: six 8-second Omni 1080p-with-audio shorts (1,728), one Kling pro hero (320), two same-day-capable Wan 1080p shots (768), three kept 480p drafts (288) — <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">3,104 list</strong>. Now take the upgrades selectively:</p>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Hero goes 4K</strong> (launch page, TV demo): +480 list.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Shorts stay 1080p</strong> (Shorts/Reels cap there): <strong style="color:#60a5fa;">+0, avoiding 3,072</strong> in Omni-4K deltas nobody will see.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Both client-feedback shots move to Prime</strong> (same-day promised): +512 list.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">The three kept drafts graduate by upscale</strong>, not re-render: +1,080 list — and −216 or better against re-rendering each at 1-in-2 odds.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Selective total: <strong style="color:#60a5fa;">5,176 list — 3,261 metered on Ultra, about $32.61</strong> for a month with a genuine 4K hero, two guaranteed-turnaround finals, and three delivered-at-1080p graduates. The across-the-board version — every deliverable maxed: six <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedance/v2" style="color:#a78bfa;">Seedance 4K reference renders</a> (15,552), 4K hero (800), Prime across (1,280), drafts re-rolled at 4K (2,400) — lands at <strong style="color:#60a5fa;">20,032 list, 3.9× the bill</strong>, and the entire difference is resolution the distribution stack deletes. Premium tiers are cheap exactly where their delta is visible, and only there.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="pitfalls">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Upgrade Floor</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Paying 4K for a 1080p ceiling.</strong> Vertical platforms re-encode at or below 1080p; the Omni 4K delta is 512 credits an 8-second short against a viewer who receives the same frames. Big-screen placement is the only honest ticket to that rung.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Prime mistaken for pro.</strong> Identical specs at 1.67× — anyone buying it "for quality" has bought a queue position and priced it as a model. Read the billing row: duration × quality, speed lane, no pixel difference.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Upselling the upscale beyond its tier.</strong> It tops out at 1080p and cannot invent detail the generation never committed; a soft 480p frame upscales to a soft 1080p frame, 45 credits/second of confident polish on an unpromised source.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">The surcharge you can have back.</strong> At Kling 4K, audio is free (100/sec either way) — teams who budget the 720p/1080p audio habit into 4K quotes are leaving a discount unclaimed on the one rung that waived it.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="action-checklist">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist</h2>
            <ol style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Name the viewer surface before the rung:</strong> TV/launch page justifies 4K; feeds, embeds and phone-first cut at 1080p and stop there.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Reserve Prime for dated commitments</strong> — if no one loses money when the render lands tomorrow, it should land tomorrow at standard price.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Graduate kept drafts through the upscale pass</strong> as the default promotion path; re-rendering at higher spec only when the composition itself must change.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Audit deltas, not totals</strong> — every premium decision should read as +480 / +256 / +360 against the baseline spec, metered through your plan.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Re-quote 4K work with audio included</strong> on Kling''s top rung; the free surcharge is a margin note, not a favor.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section class="faq">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">FAQ</h2>
            <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
                <h3 style="color:#f9fafb;" itemprop="name">Q: When is the 4K rung genuinely worth 2.5× pro?</h3>
                <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;" itemprop="text">When the deliverable will be seen large or cropped hard: launch-page hero films, trade-show loops, landscape masters with vertical crop headroom. A +480 delta on one 8-second shot that anchors a campaign is trivial; the same delta on six feed shorts is 3,072 credits of invisible pixels.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
                <h3 style="color:#f9fafb;" itemprop="name">Q: Does Wan Prime produce better video than standard?</h3>
                <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;" itemprop="text">No — the parameter contract is field-for-field identical; the input docs are the same model at the same specs. You pay 1.67× for the fast generation lane''s queue position, which is the whole product and the whole argument.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
                <h3 style="color:#f9fafb;" itemprop="name">Q: Can an upscale pass replace rendering at high quality?</h3>
                <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;" itemprop="text">For 1080p delivery of a composition you''ve already kept, it beats a fresh render cost-per-keep every time (360 flat for 8 seconds versus 461+ metered through take odds). It cannot reach 4K or improve source detail the generation never committed — for those, the native rung is the only real option.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
                <h3 style="color:#f9fafb;" itemprop="name">Q: What''s the cheapest path to a 1080p deliverable?</h3>
                <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;" itemprop="text">Draft at Mini 480p (3/sec), keep it, and graduate it with the 480p→1080p pass at 45/sec — 24 + 360 = 384 list worst-case for a hero-grade frame you were certain about, and usually less than a single failed native 1080p attempt at the top rung.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
                <h3 style="color:#f9fafb;" itemprop="name">Q: Do plan discounts change which upgrade pays?</h3>
                <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;" itemprop="text">No — Basic 82%, Pro 73% and Ultra 63% scale deltas and baselines alike, so 5,176 selective versus 20,032 blanket keeps its 3.9× shape at every tier. The plan decides the size of the bill; the ledger decides whether the line belongs on it.</p></div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="closing">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Closing: Premium Is a Placement Decision</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The three upgrade lanes fail in exactly one way — bought as qualities instead of deltas. 4K is a placement: pay it where a big screen exists. Prime is a date: pay it where a deadline exists. The upscale is a promotion: pay it where a kept draft exists. Held to those three sentences, the premium tier question stops being a debate about models and becomes what it always was — a one-line check against the deliverable''s destination, worth 480 credits at the outside, and 3,072 saved at the most.</p>
        </section>

    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- product-ad-video-shootout (category=4)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'The 10-Second Product Ad Shootout: Kling 3.0 vs Wan 3.0 vs Seedance 2.5 vs HappyHorse 1.1, Costed Clip by Clip',
  4,
  'product-ad-video-shootout-kling-wan-seedance-happyhorse',
  'Four AI video models quote the same 10-second 1080p ad from 400 to 2,500 credits — a head-to-head shootout on cost, control, audio, and who wins the brief.',
  'AI video comparison, Kling 3.0, Wan 3.0, Seedance 2.5, HappyHorse 1.1, product ad video, per second pricing, video cost shootout, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>The 10-Second Product Ad Shootout: Kling 3.0 vs Wan 3.0 vs Seedance 2.5 vs HappyHorse 1.1, Costed Clip by Clip</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Four AI video models quote the same 10-second 1080p ad from 400 to 2,500 credits — a head-to-head shootout on cost, control, audio, and who wins the brief.">
</head>
<body>
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(ellipse 65% 50% at 50% 0%, rgba(244,114,182,.06), transparent),radial-gradient(ellipse 50% 40% at 100% 90%, rgba(96,165,250,.06), transparent),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318, #0b0c0f);color:#e5e7eb;padding:20px;border-radius:12px;">

        <section class="introduction">
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Review articles rate AI video models on motion quality and then shrug — "it depends." But for the single most commercially important clip in the world, the 10-second product ad, most of the "it depends" is enumerable right now: price per second, resolution ladders, audio surcharges, and reference controls. Four per-second-billed models — <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-0-video" style="color:#f472b6;">Kling 3.0</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan 3.0</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedance/v2-5" style="color:#a78bfa;">Seedance 2.5</a>, and <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/happy-horse/v1-1-text-to-video" style="color:#34d399;">HappyHorse 1.1</a> — can render the identical brief today. We costed it.</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The brief: one hero product shot, camera orbit with soft dolly-out, label must stay legible, 10 seconds, 1080p, sound on. Every quote below is list price in credits (100 credits = $1); FuseAITools monthly plans meter credits at <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63%</strong>, so we show both.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="cost-table">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Round 1 — The Quote Sheet</h2>

            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Model</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">1080p per sec</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Audio treatment</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">10s clip (list)</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">10s clip (Ultra 63%)</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-0-video" style="color:#f472b6;">Kling 3.0</a> (pro, with sound)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">40 credits</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Surcharge: 30 → 40/sec (+33%)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">400</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~252</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan 3.0</a> (1080p)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">48 credits</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Free: audio toggle doesn''t change price</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">480</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~302</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/happy-horse/v1-1-text-to-video" style="color:#34d399;">HappyHorse 1.1</a> (1080p)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">45 credits</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Native audio in base price</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">450</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~284</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedance/v2-5" style="color:#a78bfa;">Seedance 2.5</a> (1080p, no ref video)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">250 credits</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Ref video cuts to 150/sec (−40%)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">2,500 / 1,500</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~1,575 / 945</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Winner, Round 1 — depends who''s asking.</strong> Cheapest honest 1080p-with-sound quote is Kling 3.0 (400); cheapest no-audio-needed variant is Kling std at 30/sec (300). Seedance 2.5 is 5–8× more expensive at this tier — that''s not a mistake, it''s a positioning statement, and Round 3 is where you find out what the premium buys.</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Two ladder notes before scoring: drop to 720p and Wan (24/sec) undercuts Kling''s Omni 1080p-with-audio rate (36/sec) while still including sound for free; and every per-second model here has a budget alter ego — <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-turbo-text-to-video" style="color:#f472b6;">Kling V3 Turbo</a> at 36/sec and <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video-prime" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan 3.0 Prime</a> at 80/sec bracket the "faster costs more" corridor.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="control-table">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Round 2 — Control Features for a Brand Locked to One Look</h2>

            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Control need</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Kling 3.0</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Wan 3.0</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Seedance 2.5</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">HappyHorse 1.1</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Duration ceiling</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">3–15s</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">2–30s ✓</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">4–30s ✓</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Per-mode, mid-range</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">First/last frame</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Via Omni line</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Built-in mode ✓</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Image-to-video ✓</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Image + reference modes ✓</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Multi-reference pack</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Omni reference (≤4 imgs + video) ✓</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">≤10 imgs + 5 video + 5 audio ✓✓</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Ref-video conditioning (price-cut mode)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Dedicated reference-to-video model ✓</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Choreographed motion transfer</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-0-motion-control" style="color:#f472b6;">Motion Control</a> (30–40/sec) ✓✓</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">File/link reference modes</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">—</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Video-edit lineage in family</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Winner, Round 2 — Wan 3.0.</strong> Five input modes behind one form (text, first/last frame, multi-reference, file, link) plus the longest ceiling in the group and free audio. For a brand that must keep one product looking identical across twenty takes, the ten-image reference pack is the decisive tool.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="what-the-premium-buys">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Round 3 — What Seedance''s 5–8× Premium Is For</h2>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">At 250 credits/sec (1080p), Seedance 2.5 costs what the others charge for a whole clip. Two structural facts explain the price and when it''s justified:</p>

            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#a78bfa;">Physics-first rendering.</strong> The Seedance line''s reputation is complex motion and material behavior — liquids, fabrics, impact. A pour shot or a fabric whiplash that costs Kling three re-renders may cost Seedance one.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#a78bfa;">The with-video discount inverts the comparison.</strong> Attach a reference video and the rate drops 40% (1080p: 250 → 150/sec). If your workflow already records phone-cam motion drafts, Seedance''s cheapest configuration is its intended one — and at 945 credits (Ultra) for the clip, it''s inside one hero-revision budget.</li>
            </ul>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Sanity check against the flat-rate world: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/veo3/text-to-video" style="color:#818cf8;">Veo</a> bills per clip, not per second — Veo 3.1 Lite is 24 credits flat and Fast is 48, roughly the price of <em>half a second</em> of Seedance 1080p. And <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/sora/text-to-video" style="color:#60a5fa;">Sora 2</a> sits at 10 credits per generation outright. If your ad can tolerate those render styles and native durations, the per-second premium models need to win on control, not just quality.</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Verdict, Round 3 — Seedance 2.5 for the money shot only.</strong> The rational stack is barbell: Wan/Kling carry the volume, Seedance carries the one hero shot where physics is the pitch.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="pitfalls">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Shootout Rules: Comparisons That Lie</h2>

            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">False comparison</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Why it''s wrong</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Fair version</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">"X is cheaper per second"</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Audio scene splits rates (Kling 30→40/sec) and Seedance''s cheaper tier requires uploading a ref video.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Quote same resolution + same sound state + same duration. Done above.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Flat vs per-second head-to-head</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Flat-clip models (Veo, Sora) bill one price regardless of clip seconds; the crossover point moves with every re-render.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Compare cost per <em>accepted</em> clip including drafts, not per generation.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">List price in the deck</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Plans meter credits at 82/73/63% monthly — Ultra''s 252-credit Kling clip isn''t a $4 clip on paper.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Bill to your discounted rate; it changes model ranking at the margin.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">One take per model</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Acceptance rate is a hidden price multiplier: a model needing 3 takes costs 3× its quote.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Track credits-per-approved-clip per model monthly; reroute accordingly.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </section>

        <section class="action-checklist">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist</h2>
            <ol style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Storyboard on the cheapest viable ladder first.</strong> Wan 3.0 at 480p (12/sec) drafts the shot list before anyone quotes 1080p.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Default volume clips to Kling std or Wan 1080p.</strong> 300–480 list credits; the discount plans pull both under ~310.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Budget one Seedance pass per campaign.</strong> The hero physics shot at 150/sec with a phone-cam reference video — not twenty.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Quote audio explicitly.</strong> Free on Wan, +33% on Kling — that reverses the two models'' ranking on sound-on briefs.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Measure credits-per-accepted-clip, not per-clip.</strong> It''s the only number that fairly compares flat and per-second billing.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section class="faq">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Which model is cheapest for a 10-second 1080p product ad with sound?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Kling 3.0 at the pro with-sound rate: 400 credits list (~252 on Ultra monthly). Wan 3.0 is 480 list (~302) but includes audio free — if your audio needs grow beyond the base scene, or you drop to 720p where Wan charges 24/sec, Wan often wins on total cost per accepted clip.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Why is Seedance 2.5 five times the price of the others?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">It targets the physics-heavy premium tier — 250 credits/sec at 1080p without a reference video, 150 with one, against a 30–48/sec field. You''re paying for first-take success rates on complex motion (liquids, cloth, impacts), which can make it cheaper per accepted hero shot than three retrys of a budget model.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Do flat-priced models like Veo or Sora belong in this comparison?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Yes, as a pricing-structure check: Veo 3.1 Lite bills 24 credits per clip and Sora 2 bills 10 per generation, versus per-second models at 300–480 for the same brief. They win on price for tolerable styles and standard durations; the per-second models win on resolution/duration control, references, and 30-second ceilings.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: How do plan discounts affect the ranking?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">They scale every per-second quote uniformly (monthly Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63%), so ordering holds — but they change the crossover with flat-rate models: an Ultra plan makes Kling''s 400-credit clip effectively 252, deepening the gap to Veo''s flat 24 for styles that fit.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Which single model handles the most ad jobs alone?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Wan 3.0: five input modes in one model, up to 10 reference images with 5 videos and 5 audios, 2–30 seconds, and free audio. For a one-model vendor policy it covers the widest slice of commercial briefs; HappyHorse 1.1''s dedicated reference-to-video model is the specialist alternative when references are the whole job.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="closing">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Final Score</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">No single winner exists — the shootout finds a division of labor: Kling 3.0 takes cheapest-with-sound, Wan 3.0 takes control and volume, HappyHorse 1.1 takes reference-native production at mid-price, and Seedance 2.5 takes the one hero shot where physics sells the product. A team that routes clips this way beats any team that married one model.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Run the same brief through all four on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-0-video" style="color:#f472b6;">FuseAITools'' video shelf</a> and let your acceptance rate, not the review scores, pick the vendor.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- real-estate-listing-virtual-staging-walkthrough-video-cost (category=2)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'Fifteen Listings, No Photoshoot: A Brokerage Staging-and-Walkthrough Month at 3,820 List Credits, Line by Line',
  2,
  'real-estate-listing-virtual-staging-walkthrough-video-cost',
  'A brokerage month of fifteen listings priced per line: staging edits at 24 credits, flat-per-clip walkthrough videos, and a 3-credit style-probe lane.',
  'virtual staging AI cost, listing photo editing credits, walkthrough video generation pricing, Hailuo image-to-video credits, fair housing marketing compliance, floor plan diagram AI cost, real estate video teaser billing, Zillow listing content batch, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Fifteen Listings, No Photoshoot: A Brokerage Staging-and-Walkthrough Month at 3,820 List Credits, Line by Line</title>
    <meta name="description" content="A brokerage month of fifteen listings priced per line: staging edits at 24 credits, flat-per-clip walkthrough videos, and a 3-credit style-probe lane.">
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/news/real-estate-listing-virtual-staging-walkthrough-video-cost">
</head>
<body style="margin:0;padding:0;background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(circle at 15% 0%, rgba(251,146,60,0.07) 0%, transparent 45%),radial-gradient(circle at 85% 100%, rgba(244,114,182,0.05) 0%, transparent 45%),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318 0%, #0b0c0f 100%);color:#d1d5db;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="max-width:960px;margin:0 auto;padding:32px 20px 64px;">
        <header style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h1 itemprop="headline" style="font-size:2rem;line-height:1.25;color:#f9fafb;margin:0 0 16px;">Fifteen Listings, No Photoshoot: A Brokerage Staging-and-Walkthrough Month at 3,820 List Credits, Line by Line</h1>
            <p style="color:#9ca3af;font-size:0.95rem;margin:0;">Published <time itemprop="datePublished" datetime="2026-11-12">November 12, 2026</time> · FuseAI Tools Editorial</p>
        </header>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Introduction: Fifteen Listings Is a Content Factory, Not a Photo Shoot</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">A residential brokerage''s listing pipeline has three product lines wearing one brand: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">staged versions of the real photos</strong> (empty rooms furnished, priced as edits because the room is the room), <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">compliance-shaped stills</strong> (neighborhood and amenity scenes with no people in them, floor-plan diagrams, disclosure badges), and <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">motion</strong> (walkthrough clips cut from the listing''s hero image, plus vertical teasers for the feeds). On <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/hailuo" style="color:#fb923c;text-decoration:none;">FuseAI</a> a fifteen-listing month — forty-five staging passes, forty scene images, fifteen diagrams, twenty-five corrections, thirty walkthrough clips across draft and final tiers, four teasers, narration, and a music bed — prices to <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">3,820 list credits</strong>: 3,133 at Basic''s 82%, 2,789 at Pro''s 73%, 2,407 at Ultra''s 63%.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The two structural lessons are worth more than the total. First, real estate motion does not have to bill by the second: the walkthrough lane this desk uses is <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">priced per clip by a duration × resolution menu</strong>, which turns "longer" and "sharper" into a visible trade instead of a meter. Second, the fair-housing and staging-disclosure rules are not just legal review — they are cost structure, and teams that follow them by editing approved images end up cheaper than teams that re-roll.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Stage 1: Virtual Staging Is an Image-to-Image Problem</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">A staged photo must be <em>the room</em> with furniture in it — same windows, same floor, same sightlines — which is exactly an <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/qwen/v3-pro-image-to-image" style="color:#fb923c;text-decoration:none;">image-to-image</a> job: the listing photo enters as input, the furniture enters as prompt. The Pro lane bills <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">12 credits at 1K, 24 at 2K</strong> (<code>resolution</code> is mandatory; MLS feed specs are why the finals run at 2K), so three staged rooms per listing across fifteen = forty-five finals = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">1,080 list credits</strong> — the largest single line of the month. Staging is priced per room, and this is the only line where the unit count is set by the building inventory rather than by taste.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Before any batch of forty-five runs, style exploration happens on the dumbest-cheapest lane on the platform: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/qwen/z-image" style="color:#fb923c;text-decoration:none;">z-image</a> at a <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">flat 3 credits</strong>. Thirty "does modern-scandinavian read in this lighting" probes is <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">90 list credits</strong> — and because every one of them is a throwaway by design, the 3-credit price is what makes thirty of them acceptable in the first place. The rehearsal row is the cheapest insurance on the sheet: discovering a style that photographs wrong at 3 credits each is a rounding error; discovering it inside a 24-credit staging batch is a re-run.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Stage 2: Compliance Still Exists — It Just Moved Into the Prompt Rules</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Marketing rules for housing are blunt: no people in listing or neighborhood imagery (no demographic signal), no claim-shaped text, and staged photos labeled as staged. The desk handles the first rule by generating neighborhood and amenity scenes — the park bench, the coffee shop counter, the school crosswalk — as <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">deliberately unpopulated</strong> shots on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/nano-banana/nano-banana-2-1" style="color:#fb923c;text-decoration:none;">Nano Banana 2.1</a> at 1K, 6 credits each: forty images across fifteen listings = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">240 list credits</strong>. Synthetic-by-construction beats redaction-by-review here: there is no bystander to blur and no release to chase, because nobody was ever in the frame.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The second rule''s home is the diagram lane: floor plans and site plans carry room labels, dimensions, and compass text at readable density, so they run on the <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/qwen/v3-pro-text-to-image" style="color:#fb923c;text-decoration:none;">Qwen 3 Pro text-to-image</a> tier at 2K (24) — fifteen diagrams = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">360 list credits</strong>. The disclosure badges themselves ("Virtual staging applied — property shown furnished") ride the corrections lane: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/image-2-0-segment-edit" style="color:#fb923c;text-decoration:none;">Grok Imagine 2.0 segment-edit</a> is a flat 6 to stamp text onto an approved staged photo or fix a mislabeled room, and twenty-five of those passes = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">150 list credits</strong>. A compliance badge that edits rather than re-rolls is a badge that never invalidates the staging review underneath it.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Stage 3: Walkthroughs on a Price Menu — the Flat-Per-Clip Lane</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Every listing''s hero photo becomes a six-second motion walkthrough on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/hailuo/image-to-video-standard" style="color:#fb923c;text-decoration:none;">Hailuo 2.3 image-to-video standard</a> — and this is the lane where the billing grammar changes. Hailuo does <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">not meter by the second</strong>: it prices a flat amount per clip by duration × resolution menu — 6s at 768p is 40, 6s at 1080p and 10s at 768p are both 60. Fifteen standard drafts = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">600 list credits</strong>; the five premium listings ship on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/hailuo/image-to-video-pro" style="color:#fb923c;text-decoration:none;">the pro tier</a> at 6s/1080p = 120 a clip = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">600 list credits</strong>. The menu makes the trade visible in a way a per-second clock never does: <em>a 10-second draft and a 6-second full-HD draft cost the same 60 credits</em> — longer versus sharper is a choice you can read off one row, not a meter that surprises you at the end of the render.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Two consequences the desk plans around. One: the input image is the product, so walkthroughs start from the <em>staged final</em>, not the empty-room original — motion inherits whatever the staging review approved, and the walkthrough cannot reintroduce pixels the disclosure process rejected. Two: the menu''s cliff is duration eligibility — only the listed combinations bill; the 1080p tier only exists at 6 seconds, so "make it longer" on premium listings is not a 120-credit decision, it is a different (10s/768p at 100) clip. On the per-second lanes elsewhere in the industry series, every extra second was money; here, the money is in reading the menu first.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Stage 4: Teasers, Narration, and a Music Bed You Already Know</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Feed teasers are the one lane where per-second billing is welcome, because vertical cuts need a specific aspect ratio: four 5-second 9:16 hooks on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-turbo-text-to-video" style="color:#fb923c;text-decoration:none;">Kling V3 Turbo</a> at 720p (27/s, aspect 9:16 legal, durations 3–15s) run 135 each = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">540 list credits</strong> — short by policy, because a teaser is charged per second and every second shows up. Narration for the five premium walkthroughs runs on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/turbo-2-5" style="color:#fb923c;text-decoration:none;">ElevenLabs turbo</a> at <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">10 credits per UTF-8 byte-thousandth</strong>: twelve tight scripts, each under a thousand bytes, one block each = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">120 list credits</strong>. The music bed is the platform''s flattest line: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/generate" style="color:#fb923c;text-decoration:none;">Suno</a> at 20 per operation — generate plus one extend for the loop = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">40 list credits</strong>. Audio is what the invoice looks like when pricing is predictable: three lines, 160 credits, zero surprises.</p>
        </section>

