        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Pitfalls: Five Ways a Listing Month Overspends</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Re-rolling a staged photo to fix a badge.</strong> A fresh generation re-decides the furniture that was already disclosed and approved. The disclosure stamp is a flat-6 masked edit; the re-roll is a 24-credit staging pass plus a re-review of the whole room.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Styling at 2K instead of probing at 3.</strong> Discovering that a style reads wrong inside a 24-credit batch is the expensive version of a cheap question. The z-image probe lane exists so the answer costs 3.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Treating the menu lane like a meter lane.</strong> Hailuo''s flat-per-clip pricing has an eligibility grid: 1080p exists only at 6 seconds; 10 seconds bills at 768p. "Twelve seconds in full HD" is not a pricier clip — it is not a clip at all, and the order should fail before it bills.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">People-shaped prompts in marketing scenes.</strong> A park scene with joggers is a demographic signal with a compliance conversation attached. The prompt template itself should say unpopulated — same deliverable, 6 credits, no argument.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Long teasers.</strong> Feed hooks bill per second on the turbo lane (27/s at 720p); a 10-second teaser is exactly twice a 5-second one and converts no better. Five seconds, one room, done.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist: Standing Up the Brokerage Content Desk</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;padding-left:24px;line-height:1.9;">
                <li>Stage on the image-to-image lane only — the room photo is input, furniture is prompt, and the windows stay where the seller left them.</li>
                <li>Run every style question through the 3-credit probe lane before it touches a 24-credit batch; thirty probes are 90 credits and zero re-runs.</li>
                <li>Write "no people, no claims" into the scene templates themselves, and stamp disclosures as edits to approved staged finals.</li>
                <li>Read the Hailuo menu before booking: drafts at 40 (6s/768p standard), premium finals at 120 (6s/1080p pro), and the longer-versus-sharper trade decided at 60.</li>
                <li>Generate walkthroughs from the disclosed staged image, never the raw empty room — motion inherits review status.</li>
                <li>Keep teasers five seconds per-second-billed, narration under a thousand bytes per block, and treat Suno as the flat 20 the invoice already assumes.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">FAQ</h2>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: What does it cost to produce listing content for fifteen properties?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">3,820 list credits for the whole month: forty-five staged rooms, thirty style probes, forty unpopulated scene images, fifteen diagrams, twenty-five edits, thirty walkthrough clips across draft and premium tiers, four teasers, narration, and a music bed — 3,133 at Basic 82%, 2,789 at Pro 73%, 2,407 at Ultra 63%. The staging line is the biggest at 1,080, because staging prices per room.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Is AI virtual staging cheaper than traditional staging?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">At listing volume, by roughly an order of magnitude per room: a staged final is a 24-credit image-to-image edit of the actual photo (1,080 credits for forty-five rooms this month), where physical staging costs a delivery-and-removal logistics conversation per room and traditional virtual staging services quote per image. The AI lane also keeps the room provably identical — corrections and disclosure badges run as flat-6 masked edits, not re-shoots.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: How are walkthrough videos billed differently across the platform?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Two grammars. Hailuo''s image-to-video lanes bill a flat amount per clip by a duration × resolution menu — 40 for 6s/768p standard, 60 for 6s/1080p or 10s/768p, 120 for 6s/1080p pro — so longer-versus-sharper is a visible trade on one row. The Kling turbo lane bills per second (27/s at 720p), where every added second is metered. The desk uses the menu for standard walkthroughs and the meter for five-second teasers, because shortness is the policy there.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: How do listing images stay fair-housing compliant?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">By construction instead of by review: neighborhood and amenity scenes generate unpopulated by prompt rule (no demographic signal to blur or litigate), staged rooms carry their disclosure as a stamped edit on the approved file, and walkthroughs render only from disclosed staged images. Compliance in this vertical is the property of the pipeline — templates, edit lanes, menus — not of a screenshot audit.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Is there a per-listing number to plan against?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Yes: divide the month by fifteen. A complete listing — three staged rooms (72), a walkthrough draft (40), a diagram (24), a couple of scene images (12) and one premium tier when it earns it (120) — lands around 150–270 list credits, meaning the marginal listing is roughly 4–7% of the month''s total while the fixed production system (probes, teasers, audio) amortizes across all of them.</p></div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:0;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Closing: The Stills Factory and the Video Factory Cost the Same — Plan Like It</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The brokerage ships fifteen listings a month because two decisions were made at intake: staging and badges move as edits to approved images, never as re-rolls, and motion books from a price menu whose rows say what longer-versus-sharper costs before anything renders. The invoice is those habits priced — 3,820 list credits, a 1,080 staging line that scales with inventory, a 1,200 walkthrough stack that scales with the menu, and a 90-credit probe row that keeps all of the above from being re-done. The lanes run inside <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/hailuo/image-to-video-standard" style="color:#fb923c;text-decoration:none;">Hailuo image-to-video</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/qwen/z-image" style="color:#fb923c;text-decoration:none;">z-image</a>, and their neighbors on FuseAI — run one style probe batch on your ugliest empty room first; 3 credits is the cheapest opinion your listing desk will ever get. Try <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/qwen/v3-pro-image-to-image" style="color:#fb923c;text-decoration:none;">FuseAITools</a> for virtual staging edits on real listing photos.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

