                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">58</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">50</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Month total</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">—</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#f87171;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">4,415</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">3,621</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">3,223</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#34d399;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">2,781</strong></td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;margin-top:16px;">Discounts are the monthly plan rates: Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63%, applied per line and rounded, and every column sums to its own total. Read the two lines worth walking to the marketing director with. First, video: the six 1080p hero-store finals (<strong style="color:#f9fafb;">2,160</strong>) plus five 720p bumpers (<strong style="color:#f9fafb;">675</strong>) plus ten 480p drafts (<strong style="color:#f9fafb;">480</strong>) = 3,315, or 75% of the entire kit — every still, cutout, edit, and audio line combined is 1,100. If you want to move this number, you move seconds and resolution on the Kling lane, not the photo count. Second, the draft-to-final ratio: one rehearsal clip is 48 credits, one shipped clip is 360 — a 7.5× gap that is the whole argument for rehearsing before you render, because a wrong camera move caught on the Grok lane is eighty times cheaper to fix than the same mistake caught in the store-final batch.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Pitfalls: Five Ways a Chain Kit Still Balloons</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Rendering every dish at 4K.</strong> Resolution is money on this lane: 40 heroes at 4K is 480 instead of the 360 a 2K render costs, and the extra pixels are deleted the moment the plate shows in a 240px app thumbnail. Pick resolution per deliverable, not per "quality" feeling.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Asking for a transparent PNG at 2K.</strong> The background flag on GPT 2.5 Flare only exists at 1K; request alpha higher up and the order can bill the 18-credit tier and hand back an opaque file. Transparency decides the working resolution — plan the composite pass around it.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Re-generating a hero plate to fix one garnish.</strong> A fresh roll re-rolls the whole approved dish. A masked edit is 6 credits and changes only the flagged region; on a forty-dish menu the reshoot reflex quietly doubles your stills line.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Shipping store videos at 1080p for every door.</strong> Per-second billing means a 10-second hero costs 360 on Turbo 1080p; the ambient store screen rarely needs it. Cut the volume of seconds first — shorter loops on the 720p lane at 27/s is the biggest single lever on the sheet.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Long, uncut promo scripts.</strong> Voice-over bills UTF-8 bytes, so a rambling announcement pays more blocks for the same message. Rehearse the read down to ~1,800 bytes; the VO line is meant to be a rounding error, not a line item.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist: Standing Up the Chain Content Kit</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;padding-left:24px;line-height:1.9;">
                <li>Lock one dish pyramid per deliverable: 1K social thumbs, 2K app heroes, 4K only for printed table tents. Price it before you shoot forty.</li>
                <li>Run packaging cutouts at 1K transparent, and composite the alpha sprites onto rendered backdrops as a separate step rather than fighting the resolution ceiling.</li>
                <li>Route every post-review dish change through the flat-6 edit lane; keep parent-file references so brand can see which pixels moved between approvals.</li>
                <li>Rehearse each store video on the Grok 480p draft lane (6/s), lock the camera move, then ship the approved shot on Kling Turbo — image-to-video from the exact dish still.</li>
                <li>Trim shipped seconds before you trim resolution: a 5-second 720p bumper is a fraction of a 10-second 1080p hero, and most in-store screens only ever show the bumper.</li>
                <li>Budget Suno as a flat 20 per operation and ElevenLabs in bytes, so the audio tail never surprises you after the video lines are set.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">FAQ</h2>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: What does it cost to produce a seasonal menu kit for a whole chain?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">One approved, localizable kit — forty dish stills across a resolution pyramid, twenty packaging cutouts, twenty-four corrections, ten draft plus six final store videos, five bumpers, music, and bilingual promo VO — prices to 4,415 list credits: 3,621 at Basic 82%, 3,223 at Pro 73%, 2,781 at Ultra 63%. It is a single content month that serves every door, not a per-store shoot budget multiplied by sixty.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Why is the video lane most of the bill?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Because video bills per second and per resolution. The six 1080p hero-store finals run 2,160 (36/s × 10s × 6), the 720p bumpers 675, and the 480p drafts 480 — together 3,315, or 75% of the month. Every image, edit, and audio line combined is 1,100. To cut the number, cut shipped seconds and drop in-store screens to the 720p lane before you touch the photo count.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: How do you keep dish photos consistent across forty items?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">By treating corrections as edits, not re-shoots. Draft on Nano Banana 2.1 at 1K, lock the approved plate, and run every garnish or styling change through the flat-6 edit lane so only the flagged region moves and the approved dish stays fixed. Consistency is a pipeline property — one pyramid, one edit lane — not a per-photo prompting gamble.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Can AI generate transparent-background packaging shots?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Yes, with a catch that decides your resolution. On GPT Image 2.5 Flare the <code>background=transparent</code> flag is a 1K-only capability, so the twenty-SKU cutout batch runs at 1K for 6 credits each (120 total) and delivers true alpha. Ask for transparent at 2K and the order can bill the 18-credit tier yet still return an opaque file — transparency sets the working resolution, not the other way around.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Is it cheaper to rehearse a store video before rendering the final?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">By roughly 7.5×. A rehearsal clip on Grok video 1.5 preview at 480p is 6/s, so one 8-second draft is 48 credits; the same move shipped on Kling V3 Turbo 1080p is 36/s, so a 10-second hero is 360. Discovering a bad camera move on the draft lane costs 48 to fix; discovering it in the final batch costs 360 — which is exactly why the draft line is on the sheet at all.</p></div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:0;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Closing: The Bill Scales by Seconds, Not by Doors</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The chain ships one menu kit to sixty stores because two decisions were made at template time rather than in the field: dish resolution is a per-deliverable price point, and store video gets rehearsed on the cheap lane before it gets rendered on the expensive one. The invoice is what that discipline looks like priced — 4,415 list credits a month, three-quarters of it honestly attributable to seconds of 1080p, and a photo library that would have cost a fraction if printed at the resolution the app actually shows. The lanes run inside <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/nano-banana/nano-banana-2-1" style="color:#fbbf24;text-decoration:none;">Nano Banana 2.1</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-turbo-image-to-video" style="color:#fbbf24;text-decoration:none;">Kling V3 Turbo</a>, and their neighbors on FuseAI — try the dish pyramid on one real menu before you commit forty; it is the cheapest way to find out your app only ever needed 2K.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- scene-parameter-misuse-six-symptoms (category=4)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'Six Symptoms of a Misused Scene Parameter: Three Vocabularies, a First-Match Classifier, and a Silent 4K Tier That Is Not Free',
  4,
  'scene-parameter-misuse-six-symptoms',
  'Scene misuse at real per-second prices: three naming vocabularies across Kling and Seedance, audio-on costing 50% more, and the reference-video repricing trap.',
  'AI video scene parameter, with_audio no_audio pricing, Kling Omni audio cost, Seedance reference video discount, scene dropdown meaning, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Six Symptoms of a Misused Scene Parameter: Three Vocabularies, a First-Match Classifier, and a Silent 4K Tier That Is Not Free</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Scene misuse at real per-second prices: three naming vocabularies across Kling and Seedance, audio-on costing 50% more, and the reference-video repricing trap.">
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/news/scene-parameter-misuse-six-symptoms">
</head>
<body style="margin:0;padding:0;background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(circle at 15% 0%, rgba(34,211,238,0.07) 0%, transparent 45%),radial-gradient(circle at 85% 100%, rgba(244,114,182,0.05) 0%, transparent 45%),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318 0%, #0b0c0f 100%);color:#d1d5db;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="max-width:960px;margin:0 auto;padding:32px 20px 64px;">
        <header style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h1 itemprop="headline" style="font-size:2rem;line-height:1.25;color:#f9fafb;margin:0 0 16px;">Six Symptoms of a Misused Scene Parameter: Three Vocabularies, a First-Match Classifier, and a Silent 4K Tier That Is Not Free</h1>
            <p style="color:#9ca3af;font-size:0.95rem;margin:0;">Published <time itemprop="datePublished" datetime="2026-11-12">November 12, 2026</time> · FuseAI Tools Editorial</p>
        </header>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Introduction: One Dropdown, Five Different Words For It</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Every video model on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling" style="color:#22d3ee;text-decoration:none;">FuseAI</a> that bills per second carries a hidden dimension behind the run button: the <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">scene</strong>. It is what separates the two rows that look identical in your history but cost 100 and 150 credits per second. Get it right and it is the cheapest lever in the stack — Seedance charges <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedance/v2" style="color:#22d3ee;text-decoration:none;">200/s for 4K with a reference video versus 324/s without one</a>. Get it wrong and it is a tax you pay on every single clip, permanently, because most people never learn which of the three vocabularies their model actually speaks.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">This is a failure-mode article, so everything below is written as a symptom: what you see in the invoice or the output, what actually caused it, and the fix. Six symptoms, one 30-second episode bill showing what each wrong turn costs, and the reason the pricing rows and the <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/pricing" style="color:#22d3ee;text-decoration:none;">subscription tiers</a> both bet on you not auditing this field.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Symptoms #1–#3: Three Vocabularies, One Clipboard</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Symptom #1: you carry a preset from one Kling model to another and the audio flag is ignored — or the order vanishes.</strong> The root cause is spelling. <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-0-video" style="color:#22d3ee;text-decoration:none;">Kling 3.0 video</a> speaks <code style="color:#22d3ee;background:#1f2937;padding:1px 6px;border-radius:4px;">with_sound</code> / <code style="color:#22d3ee;background:#1f2937;padding:1px 6px;border-radius:4px;">without_sound</code>. <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/3-0-omni-text-to-video" style="color:#22d3ee;text-decoration:none;">Kling 3.0 Omni</a> speaks <code style="color:#22d3ee;background:#1f2937;padding:1px 6px;border-radius:4px;">with_audio</code> / <code style="color:#22d3ee;background:#1f2937;padding:1px 6px;border-radius:4px;">no_audio</code>. Seedance speaks <code style="color:#22d3ee;background:#1f2937;padding:1px 6px;border-radius:4px;">with_video</code> / <code style="color:#22d3ee;background:#1f2937;padding:1px 6px;border-radius:4px;">without_video</code>. Three vocabularies across two vendors, and they are not interchangeable at any layer — the server matches the exact lowercase token, and nothing in your template library warns you that a preset built on Omni will silently miss on 3.0 video.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Symptom #2: you turn audio off and the price does not move, so you conclude audio is free.</strong> It is free exactly once: at the top of the ladder. Omni''s 4K rows price <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/3-0-omni-reference-to-video" style="color:#22d3ee;text-decoration:none;">100/s for no_audio, with_audio, and with_video alike</a> — the silent premium tier is not a discount, it is a flat rate that hides an included feature. The same "no move" behavior on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/3-0-omni-transformation" style="color:#22d3ee;text-decoration:none;">Omni transformation</a> means the opposite: that model has <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">no scene dimension at all</strong>, because with a video input the vendor does not generate audio, period. You cannot buy it and you cannot accidentally pay for it. Same invoice symptom, two completely different causes.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Symptom #3: you attach a brief reference video "for context" and every clip reprices.</strong> On Omni reference-to-video, the classifier runs first-match in a fixed order: <em>if a reference video is attached → with_video, regardless of your audio setting.</em> Your "keep audio on" checkbox stops mattering the moment the clip goes in, and at 1080p that is 45/s instead of the 36/s you were planning. On <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedance/v2-5" style="color:#22d3ee;text-decoration:none;">Seedance 2.5</a> the same classifier moves you the other way — 99/s down to 60/s — which is why teams that learned the discount on Seedance get burned when they carry the habit to Kling Omni.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Symptoms #4–#6: Money Leaks You Can See in the Invoice</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Symptom #4: ambient noise everywhere, nobody ordered it.</strong> Omni''s audio default is on at every resolution except 4K. At 720p that is 30/s instead of 20/s — a <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">50% scene premium</strong> on a track you will mute in post anyway. Five seconds of it costs you 150 credits; the same five seconds of deliberate silence cost 100.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Symptom #5: you budget the with_video discount, then generate from a still image and pay full text-to-video rate.</strong> Seedance''s cheaper scene is not a pricing tier you select — it is a property of your input. No reference video, no discount: 30/s at 480p, 66/s at 720p, 324/s at 4K. The discount is real (4K with a reference costs 200/s, a 38% cut), but it is conditional in a way the pricing table alone does not explain.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Symptom #6: a 12-second transformation bill looks wrong — and it is not.</strong> Omni transformation has no scene and no default duration; the front end must pass the actual source-video seconds, which become both the billing basis and the vendor submission. When someone sends a 12-second clip with the duration field set to the 5 they "usually use," the output can truncate while the cost still settles on output seconds. The row you should remember: transformation bills per second against duration with no classification to hide behind.</p>
            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:20px;font-size:0.92rem;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:#1f2937;">
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:left;">Wrong turn</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">Per-second swing</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">Per 10s clip (list)</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Audio left on, Omni 720p</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#fbbf24;">+10</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">+100 credits</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Audio left on, Omni 1080p</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#fbbf24;">+6</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">+60 credits</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Reference video repricing, Omni 1080p</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#f472b6;">+9</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">+90 credits</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Discount missed, Seedance 2 4K</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#f472b6;">+124</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">+1,240 credits</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Vocabulary mismatch (wrong scene token)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#22d3ee;">order fails</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">0 credits — free lesson</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Invoice: One 30-Second Episode, Six Scene Decisions</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">A 30-second narrated episode at 1080p, priced six ways with nothing else changed — same seconds, same resolution, only the scene path differs. The ladder runs from <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">900 to 1,350 list credits</strong>, a 50% spread decided entirely by a dropdown:</p>
            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:20px;font-size:0.92rem;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:#1f2937;">
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:left;">Scene path</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">List</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">Basic 82%</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">Pro 73%</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">Ultra 63%</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Kling 3.0 video, std, without_sound (20/s)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">600</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">492</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">438</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">378</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Kling 3.0 video, pro, without_sound (30/s)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">900</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">738</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">657</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">567</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Omni 1080p, no_audio (30/s)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">900</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">738</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">657</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">567</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Omni 1080p, with_audio (36/s) — the default</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#fbbf24;">1,080</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">886</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">788</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">680</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Omni 1080p reference-to-video, with_video (45/s)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#f472b6;">1,350</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">1,107</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">986</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">851</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Seedance 2.5 1080p, without_video (250/s) × 4s clip</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">1,000</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">820</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">730</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">630</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;margin-top:16px;">Discount rates are the monthly Basic 82% / Pro 73% / Ultra 63% plan rates. Read the last row against the fourth: a 4-second Seedance 2.5 clip without a reference (1,000 list) costs more than a full 30 seconds of silent Omni 1080p (900 list). The scene multiplier is the cheaper thing to fix. And note the two rows worth 900 — Omni no_audio at 30/s is the same list price as Kling 3.0 pro silent, which is exactly the kind of cross-model equivalence that presets blur when people copy at the dropdown instead of the invoice.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Pitfalls and One-Line Fixes</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Vocabulary drift.</strong> Keep a per-model note of which scene words it accepts — <code style="color:#22d3ee;background:#1f2937;padding:1px 6px;border-radius:4px;">sound</code> for 3.0 video, <code style="color:#22d3ee;background:#1f2937;padding:1px 6px;border-radius:4px;">audio</code> for Omni, <code style="color:#22d3ee;background:#1f2937;padding:1px 6px;border-radius:4px;">video</code> for Seedance. The good news: a mismatched token fails at the order gate at zero cost, so the vocabulary trap is loud, unlike the five quiet ones.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Default-on audio.</strong> If your edit suite adds its own track, set audio off explicitly at 720p and 1080p. That single click is the 900 vs. 1,080 line on the invoice above.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">"Audio is free at 4K" misread.</strong> Omni 4K is 100/s with or without sound — the flatness means audio carries no premium at the top tier, not that the tier is cheap. A silent 4K render is still 100/s, which at 5 seconds is 500 list for pixels most delivery specs do not even require.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Transformation has no audio and no default duration.</strong> Both of its hidden dimensions fail in the expensive direction: assume a scene pick exists and you will hunt an option that is not there; assume a duration fallback and you will truncate your own output.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Discount dependence.</strong> The with_video rows are cheaper by design (Seedance 2.5: 60/s vs. 99/s at 720p), but that is a conditional price. Budget it as a discount you must re-earn every run by attaching the reference, not as the model''s list price.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Classifier blindness.</strong> On Omni reference-to-video, audio settings stop mattering once a reference video is attached — with_video wins at 45/s. If you want to pay the with_audio price with a reference clip as input, that path does not exist; the reference is the more expensive truth.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;padding-left:24px;line-height:1.9;">
                <li>Write the model''s exact scene vocabulary into your preset notes: sound / audio / video — and never copy a preset across families.</li>
                <li>Before a batch, price your planned scene against the opposite scene at the same resolution; if you would mute the audio anyway, flip it to off (Omni 720p: 20 vs. 30 per second).</li>
                <li>Treat any reference-video attach as a repricing event: cheaper on Seedance, more expensive on Omni 1080p (45 vs. 36 per second).</li>
                <li>Do not render silent 4K to "save" — Omni 4K is flat at 100/s; if you pay 4K, take the audio with it.</li>
                <li>On transformation jobs, pass the exact source seconds; there is no default to catch you.</li>
                <li>Audit one invoice row per model per week against the per-second table; scene errors are the only multiplier that hides inside identical-looking history entries.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">FAQ</h2>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Why does my audio setting do nothing on Kling Omni reference-to-video?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">A: The scene classifier checks inputs before settings, in a fixed order: any attached reference video routes the order to with_video regardless of the audio flag. At 1080p that means 45 credits per second instead of the 36 you would pay for with_audio with no reference. Your checkbox is not broken — it was outranked.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Is Kling Omni 4K audio really free?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">The audio premium is free, the tier is not. All three Omni 4K scenes bill at 100 per second, so sound costs nothing extra at 4K — but 100 per second is still the price, and a silent 4K clip saves exactly zero credits over the version with audio. Take the audio.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Why does my Seedance 4K render cost 324 per second when a colleague paid 200?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Because 4K has two scenes on Seedance and the cheaper one is earned by input. Attaching a reference video drops 4K from 324 to 200 per second — a 38% cut that only applies when a real reference clip is in the request. Text- or image-driven 4K runs always pay the without_video line.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: My transformation output has no audio — did the order fail?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">No — Omni transformation never produces audio, by vendor design: video input suppresses audio generation, so the model is priced with no scene dimension at all. Nothing went wrong and nothing was wasted; you simply cannot buy sound on that endpoint. Plan to lay your own track in post.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: What happens if I pass the wrong scene name — will I be overcharged?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Scene vocabulary mistakes are the one failure mode that announces itself: pricing matches exact lowercase tokens, so <code>with_sound</code> on an Omni model hits no row and the order is refused at the gate with no charge. Silent overcharging comes from using the right word with the wrong intent — audio left on by default, references attached casually — not from spelling.</p></div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:0;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Closing: The Scene Is the Only Multiplier You Can Fix Without Changing the Render</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Resolution changes what you get. Models change what you get. The scene changes only what you pay — which is precisely why it should be the first field you audit when an invoice looks fat for the pixels you received. The gap between 900 and 1,350 list credits on an identical 30-second episode is not a quality story, a vendor story, or a keep-rate story. It is a dropdown story, and it repeats on every clip until someone reads the per-second row that history quietly keeps for anyone who asks. Compare the scene rows against your own export history inside <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/3-0-omni-text-to-video" style="color:#22d3ee;text-decoration:none;">Kling 3.0 Omni text-to-video</a> and <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedance/v2" style="color:#22d3ee;text-decoration:none;">Seedance 2</a> on FuseAI before your next batch — the 50% decision costs one click.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- seedream-5-layer-decomposition-industry-application (category=2)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'From Flat Image to Editable Layers: An Industry Application Guide with Seedream 5 Layer Decomposition for Design & Marketing Teams',
  2,
  'seedream-5-layer-decomposition-industry-application',
  'Seedream 5 Pro and Flash Layer Decomposition split one image into a base plate plus PNG layers — cost, size rules, and Pro vs Flash routing for design teams.',
  'Seedream 5, layer decomposition, image layer separation, design asset production, base plate, bounding box edit, marketing creative, PNG layers, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>From Flat Image to Editable Layers: An Industry Application Guide with Seedream 5 Layer Decomposition for Design & Marketing Teams</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Seedream 5 Pro and Flash Layer Decomposition split one image into a base plate plus PNG layers — cost, size rules, and Pro vs Flash routing for design teams.">
</head>
<body>
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(ellipse 65% 50% at 50% 0%, rgba(129,140,248,.07), transparent),radial-gradient(ellipse 50% 40% at 100% 90%, rgba(129,140,248,.05), transparent),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318, #0b0c0f);color:#e5e7eb;padding:20px;border-radius:12px;">

        <section class="introduction">
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">AI image generation solved "make the picture" but left the last mile unsolved: the moment a stakeholder says "keep everything, just move the product two inches left and swap the background." A flat raster — however beautiful — is not a design asset. It can''t be re-composed, re-sized for another placement, or re-themed for another market without a human reopening a layered file and rebuilding by hand.</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedream" style="color:#818cf8;">Seedream</a> 5 Layer Decomposition attacks exactly that gap. Feed it one finished image and it returns a base plate plus independently separated element layers, each as its own PNG — the raw material of an editable composition rather than a locked one. The capability ships in two tiers: the premium <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedream/5-pro-layer-decomposition" style="color:#818cf8;">Seedream 5 Pro Layer Decomposition</a> and the flat-rate <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedream/5-flash-layer-decomposition" style="color:#818cf8;">Seedream 5 Flash Layer Decomposition</a>.</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">This guide maps the workflow for design and marketing teams: what separation is actually for, the input and pricing constraints you must design around, and the routing rule between Pro and Flash.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="market-opportunity">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Why Layers Are the Missing Link in AI Creative Ops</h2>

            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Task</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Flat image workflow</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Layer-decomposed workflow</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Re-composite for a new placement</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Regenerate from scratch or manual cutout (30–90 min)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Rearrange existing PNG layers (minutes)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Swap one element''s color/asset</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Inpaint + risk bleeding edges</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Replace that single layer, base untouched</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Build a variant library</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">One regeneration per variant</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Recombine a shared layer pool</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The economic frame is simple: generation makes images cheap, but teams don''t bill for images — they bill for <em>changes</em>. Decomposition converts "make me an ad" output into "make fifty ads from this one" material, which is where production-scale creative budgets live.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="workflow">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Three Workflows and the Rules They Obey</h2>

            <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Workflow 1 — Decouple a Shot for Placement Variants</h3>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The canonical use: take one hero composition and separate it into background, product, and foreground props so each can be relocated for a story ad, a banner, and a square feed post. You can steer what gets separated by pointing at it — the prompt accepts normalized bounding-box coordinates in the form <code>&lt;bbox&gt;x1 y1 x2 y2&lt;/bbox&gt;</code> (values 0–1000), and can be omitted entirely to let the model auto-detect the main elements.</p>

            <div style="background:rgba(31,41,55,0.3);padding:16px;border-radius:8px;margin:16px 0;">
                <p style="color:#818cf8;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:8px;">Task: pull the watch off a lifestyle shot for banner reuse. Prompt: <code>&lt;bbox&gt;600 300 850 640&lt;/bbox&gt; isolate the watch as its own layer</code> — the frame the watch occupies becomes an explicit, controllable region.</p>
                <p style="color:#9ca3af;">Inputs are a single image (exactly one, png/jpeg, ≤30 MB). Output layers are always PNG; the base plate''s format (png/jpeg) is a separate switch that only affects the base.</p>
            </div>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Source stills can themselves originate from <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedream/5-pro-text-to-image" style="color:#818cf8;">Seedream 5 Pro Text-to-Image</a> or an uploaded photograph, closing the generate-then-decompose loop inside one family.</p>

            <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Workflow 2 — Pro vs Flash: Route by Resolution Need, Not by Quality Hope</h3>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Both tiers share the same absolute-price structure but differ sharply in ceiling and cost:</p>

            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Tier</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Size tiers (required)</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Credits (list)</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">At Ultra monthly (63%)</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Best for</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Flash</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">1K / 1.5K / 2K</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">6 (all sizes)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~3.8</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">High-volume catalog decomposition, drafts</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Pro</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">1K / 1.5K</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">12</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~7.6</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Standard-resolution finals</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Pro</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">2K</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">24</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~15.1</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Large-print / hi-res hero decomposition</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The routing rule is cost-first: Flash is a flat 6 credits regardless of size, so for anything you''d ever batch — a hundred SKU shots, a whole campaign''s worth of drafts — decompose on Flash, then re-run only the approved few on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedream/5-pro-layer-decomposition" style="color:#818cf8;">Pro</a> at 2K for finals. Pro earns its 2×–4× premium only when the separated layers must land at high resolution. Those are list prices; subscription plans meter credits at monthly Basic 82%, Pro 73%, and Ultra 63%, so the 24-credit Pro 2K pass drops to roughly 20 / 18 / 15 credits depending on plan.</p>

            <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Workflow 3 — Targeted Extraction for Asset Reuse</h3>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Not every job wants the whole image shattered. Because the bounding-box prompt can name a single region, decomposition doubles as an extraction tool: lift just the logo lockup, just the model, just the packaging, leaving a clean base plate behind. This is the cheapest asset-harvesting path on the platform — a handful of Flash extractions at 6 credits each replaces manual pen-tool cutout work measured in tens of minutes.</p>

            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#818cf8;">Aspect flexibility:</strong> inputs accept extreme ratios from 1:16 to 16:1, so tall banner art and wide panoramas decompose without pre-cropping.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#818cf8;">Pixel window:</strong> source total pixels must fall between 262,144 and 36,000,000 — most native renders qualify, oversized stock photos may need downscaling.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#818cf8;">Companion edits:</strong> once separated, individual layers can be re-imagined through <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedream/5-pro-image-to-image" style="color:#818cf8;">Seedream 5 Pro Image-to-Image</a> before recomposition.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="pitfalls">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Where Decomposition Projects Go Sideways</h2>

            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Risk</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">What happens</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Countermeasure</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Submitting an "auto" size</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">The vendor default is auto-follow-input, but both tiers reject it — a size tier (1K/1.5K/2K) must be chosen explicitly because cost can''t be predicted from "auto."</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Set the size tier deliberately; Flash makes the choice free (same 6 credits either way).</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Feeding a multi-image set</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Exactly one image is accepted; a stack or contact sheet returns meaningless separation.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Decompose one composition at a time; script batches as repeated single-image calls.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Undersized or oversized source</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Sources outside the 262k–36M pixel window fail; a tiny web image is as disqualifying as a huge scan.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Check pixel count in intake; upscale small assets before submitting.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Vague separation requests</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Left unguided, auto-detect may split elements differently than your layout needs.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Use explicit <code>&lt;bbox&gt;</code> regions when the split must match a known design grid.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Over-billing drafts</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Running every concept on Pro 2K (24 credits) to "see if it works" quadruples spend versus Flash (6) with zero approval-rate gain.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Flash for exploration, Pro only for the approved finals pass.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </section>

        <section class="action-checklist">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist</h2>
            <ol style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#818cf8;">Audit your rework load first.</strong> Decomposition pays off where teams repeatedly re-composite the same creative for new placements — quantify those hours before adopting.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#818cf8;">Standardize a size policy.</strong> 1K/1.5K for internal drafts on Flash, Pro 2K for print-scale finals.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#818cf8;">Intake-check each source.</strong> Verify single-image, ≤30 MB, pixel window 262k–36M before submission.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#818cf8;">Encode the split in coordinates.</strong> For known layouts, supply <code>&lt;bbox&gt;</code> regions so separated layers map cleanly to your template slots.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#818cf8;">Route by volume.</strong> Batch the catalog on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedream/5-flash-layer-decomposition" style="color:#818cf8;">Flash</a> (flat 6 credits), graduate winners to <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedream/5-pro-layer-decomposition" style="color:#818cf8;">Pro</a>.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#818cf8;">Store the layer pool.</strong> Keep base plates and element PNGs as reusable assets — the compounding value is in recombination, not one-off separation.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section class="faq">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: What exactly does Seedream 5 Layer Decomposition output?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">A base plate plus independently separated element layers from one input image. The element layers are always PNG (so transparency is preserved for recompositing); the base plate format is your choice of PNG or JPEG, defaulting to JPEG.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: When should I use Flash instead of Pro?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Whenever resolution isn''t the constraint. Flash bills a flat 6 credits at any of its 1K/1.5K/2K tiers, while Pro bills 12 credits at 1K/1.5K and 24 at 2K. For batches and drafts where you''re testing the split, Flash is four times cheaper at 2K; pay the Pro premium only for hi-res final deliverables.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Do I have to describe what to separate?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">No — the prompt is optional; leave it out and the model auto-detects the main elements. When you do write one, you can pin regions with normalized <code>&lt;bbox&gt;</code> coordinates (0–1000), and the size tier (1K/1.5K/2K) is always mandatory.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: What images can go in?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Exactly one png/jpeg up to 30 MB, with total pixels between 262,144 and 36,000,000 and aspect ratios from 1:16 to 16:1. Both an <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedream/5-flash-text-to-image" style="color:#818cf8;">AI-generated still</a> and a real photograph qualify, which is what makes the generate-then-decompose pipeline practical.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Is this a replacement for Photoshop cutout work?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">For separation and extraction, largely yes and far faster — it''s a 6-to-24-credit automated pass instead of manual pen-tool masking. Designers still own the recomposition and brand judgment; decomposition just removes the tedious isolation step that ate the hours.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="closing">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Real Shift</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Layer Decomposition''s contribution isn''t a fancier image — it''s making AI output editable. Once a generated composition arrives as separable layers, it enters the same recombination workflow designers already use, and "regenerate everything" loses its excuse-of-necessity. Cheap generation plus cheap separation is what finally makes large-scale variant production the default rather than the exception.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Try <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedream/5-pro-layer-decomposition" style="color:#818cf8;">Seedream 5 Pro Layer Decomposition on FuseAITools</a> for hi-res design assets — and <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedream/5-flash-layer-decomposition" style="color:#818cf8;">Flash</a> for the volume passes.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- social-media-agency-multi-client-calendar-cost (category=4)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'Six Clients, One Calendar: A Social Media Agency Month at 3,952 List Credits, Costed Per Client',
  4,
  'social-media-agency-multi-client-calendar-cost',
  'A six-client agency month at 3,952 list credits: shared canary probes, draft-then-final video, byte-billed voice-over, and per-client cost math.',
  'social media agency AI content cost, multi-client content calendar pricing, AI variation editing credits, canary probe batch pricing, draft final video workflow cost, per-client AI cost allocation, ElevenLabs voiceover byte pricing, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Six Clients, One Calendar: A Social Media Agency Month at 3,952 List Credits, Costed Per Client</title>
    <meta name="description" content="A six-client agency month at 3,952 list credits: shared canary probes, draft-then-final video, byte-billed voice-over, and per-client cost math.">
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/news/social-media-agency-multi-client-calendar-cost">
</head>
<body style="margin:0;padding:0;background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(circle at 15% 0%, rgba(192,132,252,0.07) 0%, transparent 45%),radial-gradient(circle at 85% 100%, rgba(52,211,153,0.05) 0%, transparent 45%),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318 0%, #0b0c0f 100%);color:#d1d5db;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="max-width:960px;margin:0 auto;padding:32px 20px 64px;">
        <header style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h1 itemprop="headline" style="font-size:2rem;line-height:1.25;color:#f9fafb;margin:0 0 16px;">Six Clients, One Calendar: A Social Media Agency Month at 3,952 List Credits, Costed Per Client</h1>
            <p style="color:#9ca3af;font-size:0.95rem;margin:0;">Published <time itemprop="datePublished" datetime="2026-11-12">November 12, 2026</time> · FuseAI Tools Editorial</p>
        </header>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Introduction: The Invoice Is Agency-Wide; the P&amp;L Runs Per Client</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">An agency running six social accounts does not have six budgets — it has one bill and six cost sheets, and the distance between them is where agency margin lives. The month on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/nano-banana" style="color:#c084fc;text-decoration:none;">FuseAI</a>: sixty posted finals, forty-eight platform variations, eighteen text-heavy cards, a shared probe pool, twenty-four transparent cutouts, twelve draft reels and six client-approved finals, twelve caption runs, thirty voice-over scripts, and eight music beds — <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">3,952 list credits</strong>: 3,240 at Basic''s 82%, 2,884 at Pro''s 73%, 2,489 at Ultra''s 63%. That divides to roughly <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">659 list credits per client</strong>, or about 415 each at Ultra.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The structural lesson is a scheduling one, not a pricing one: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">40% of the month is stills, 39% is motion, and 21% is the audio layer</strong>, and each of the three runs on a different weekly beat — stills approved midweek, drafts early-week, finals Thursday, audio on demand. Two of those lines are deliberately <em>not</em> charged per client: the probe pool and the bed library are agency-level buffers, the same insurance logic a game studio pays for its canary rows — except at agency volume one pool protects six retainers at once.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Stage 1: The Stills Engine — One Master Per Post, Every Platform From an Edit</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Posted finals run on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/nano-banana/nano-banana-2-1" style="color:#c084fc;text-decoration:none;">Nano Banana 2.1</a> at 2K, <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">9 credits a render</strong> (1K 6, 4K 12) — ten approved posts per client × six clients = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">540 list credits</strong>. Everything downstream of a master is an edit, not a re-generation: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/image-2-0-image-edit" style="color:#c084fc;text-decoration:none;">Grok Imagine 2.0 image-edit</a> bills a flat 6 to reframe a 1:1 master into 9:16 and 4:5, so the month''s forty-eight platform variations (eight per client) = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">288 list credits</strong>. The reason is brand risk more than price: a re-rolled "same" post is the one where the product got taller and the logo moved, and a client does not distinguish between drift and disrespect. Dense-text work — quotes, offer cards, three-line promos — earns the premium lane: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/qwen/v3-pro-text-to-image" style="color:#c084fc;text-decoration:none;">Qwen 3 Pro text-to-image</a> at 2K, <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">24 credits</strong> (<code>resolution</code> mandatory, 12 at 1K), three cards per client = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">432 list credits</strong>. Eleven percent of the month buys the eleven percent of the output where a typo is a screenshot.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Stage 2: The Shared Buffers — the Canary Pool and the Cutout Library</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Before any client batch runs, the agency runs <em>its own</em> batch: sixty concept probes on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/qwen/z-image" style="color:#c084fc;text-decoration:none;">z-image</a> at a flat <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">3 credits</strong> — ten per account, throwaways by design, <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">180 list credits</strong> (4.6% of the month). This is the line no client invoice contains and every client benefits from: hook formats, color directions, and composition bets get killed here for the price of twenty posted finals, instead of being tested on the 9-credit lane where a loser still bills. Probes on the posted-final lane would cost 540 for the same sixty ideas — the pool exists to make being wrong cheap.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The second buffer is the asset library: twenty-four transparent product cutouts for the two e-commerce accounts on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image/v2-5-flare-text-to-image" style="color:#c084fc;text-decoration:none;">GPT Image 2.5 Flare</a> with <code>background=transparent</code> — and the clause the whole series keeps quoting: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">alpha exists only at 1K</strong>, 6 credits, while a 2K order can bill 18 and hand back an opaque PNG. <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">144 list credits</strong> buys cutouts that every seasonal re-scene and promo card reuses all month; billed per-campaign instead of once per product, the same visual inventory would double.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Stage 3: Motion on a Weekly Beat — Draft Monday, Final Thursday, Buffer Never Per-Client</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Every account ships one hero video a month, and the calendar gives it two passes. Concepts rehearse on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedance/v2-mini" style="color:#c084fc;text-decoration:none;">Seedance 2 mini</a> at 480p without a reference — <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">6 credits per second</strong>, so an 8-second draft is 48 — two drafts per client = twelve = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">576 list credits</strong>. The winning concept ships as a 6-second <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-turbo-image-to-video" style="color:#c084fc;text-decoration:none;">Kling V3 Turbo image-to-video</a> final at 720p (27/s = 162 a clip), driven from the approved Stage 1 still so the motion matches the brand asset: six finals = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">972 list credits</strong>. Draft spend is 15% of the month — that is the insurance premium, and the cap is the discipline: two drafts per client is a process, five is a habit, and habits at 48 a render are how an account goes unprofitable without any single mistake.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Two scheduling details carry real money. Multi-shot orders bill <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Σ shot-seconds</strong>, so a "three-cut teaser" is 6 seconds priced as 6 seconds, not three clips — storyboards get cut to total duration, not shot count. And i2v takes <code>image_urls</code> capped at one with no aspect flag (the still sets the frame), which is exactly why Stage 1 exists: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">a finished still is a free video direction sheet</strong>. The whole motion stack, drafts included, is 1,548 credits — 39% of the month, with drafts and finals split roughly 1:1.7.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Stage 4: The Audio Layer and the Per-Client Cost Sheet</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Promo voice-over on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/turbo-2-5" style="color:#c084fc;text-decoration:none;">ElevenLabs turbo</a> bills <code>ceil(UTF-8 bytes / 1000) × 10</code>: thirty short ad scripts, each written to stay under one block, = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">300 list credits</strong>. Captions and quote-pulls come from <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/speech-to-text" style="color:#c084fc;text-decoration:none;">speech-to-text</a> at <code>ceil(seconds / 60) × 6</code> — twelve repurposing runs of client long-form (webinar clips, founder interviews), five billed minutes each = 30 a run = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">360 list credits</strong>. Music is the flattest biller on the platform: eight <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/generate" style="color:#c084fc;text-decoration:none;">Suno</a> operations (generate plus extends) at 20 = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">160 list credits</strong>, built as one agency-wide bed library the six accounts share — one client-specific bed per account would be six generates before a single extend.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Now the sheet an account manager actually needs: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">per-client cost = posted lines ÷ 6 + your share of the buffers</strong>. The eight client-attributable lines run 3,612 credits — 602 per average client at list. The 340-credit remainder (probe pool plus bed library) is agency overhead that <em>lowers</em> each client''s true cost rather than hiding it: without the pool, the same sixty ideas test on the finals lane at +360, and without the library, the same beds regenerate at roughly double the Suno operations. A tier decision shows up here too: the identical calendar costs a client 602 at list, 494 at Basic, 439 at Pro, <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">379 at Ultra</strong> — the same deliverables, and the margin delta is the retainer conversation.</p>
        </section>

