        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Pitfalls: Five Ways an Agency Month Bleeds Margin</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Variations by re-generation.</strong> Reframing a master costs a flat 6 as an edit; re-rolling "the same post in vertical" costs the same order of magnitude but re-rolls the product, the logo, and the brand. Agencies get billed twice for this: once in credits, once in the client email asking why the bottle looks different.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Testing concepts on the finals lane.</strong> Sixty ideas probed at 9 a render is 540 instead of 180 — a 360-credit penalty for skipping the canary pool, which is more than double the entire music budget.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Letting the draft pool run free.</strong> Drafts at 48 are cheap individually and ruinous in bulk: two per client is a process at 576; five per client is 1,440 — more than the entire finals line, spent on videos nobody posts.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Transcribing whole client webinars.</strong> STT bills every rounded-up minute; pulling twenty clips from a 60-minute recording bills 360 in one pass when 200 seconds of source would do. Cut the source before you cut the transcript.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Padding voice-over scripts past 1,000 bytes.</strong> A second block doubles the read. Thirty client scripts each tipping over the boundary is +300 credits of pure copy bloat — nearly seventy percent of the text-card line.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist: Running Six Accounts Without Six Budgets</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;padding-left:24px;line-height:1.9;">
                <li>Approve one master per posted asset, then produce every platform ratio as a flat-6 edit — never as a re-generation.</li>
                <li>Run the probe pool agency-wide on the flat-3 lane; kill ideas cheaply before any client batch touches the premium renderers.</li>
                <li>Build the transparent-cutout library once per product at 1K — alpha only exists there, and reuse makes it the cheapest asset on the sheet.</li>
                <li>Cap drafts at two per client per month, schedule finals off approved stills, and storyboard multi-shot cuts to total seconds, not shot count.</li>
                <li>Write every voice-over script under 1,000 bytes, trim long-form source before transcription, and share one Suno bed library across accounts.</li>
                <li>Divide the invoice weekly, not monthly: 602 list per average client is the line that decides whether the retainer still works at your tier.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">FAQ</h2>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: What does an AI content month cost for a six-client social agency?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">3,952 list credits for sixty posted finals, forty-eight variations, eighteen text cards, cutouts, twelve draft reels, six video finals, captions, voice-over, and a shared music library: 3,240 at Basic 82%, 2,884 at Pro 73%, 2,489 at Ultra 63%. Client-attributable lines work out to about 602 credits per account at list.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Why should agencies keep a probe pool no client pays for?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Because testing has to happen somewhere, and the flat-3 canary lane makes being wrong cost a third of what posting costs. Sixty concept probes across six accounts cost 180 credits; the same sixty tried on the finals lane cost 540. The pool is a shared buffer: every client inherits the killed ideas'' lesson without inheriting their invoice.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: How do you keep platform variations from drifting off-brand?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Never re-generate a variation. One approved master per post goes through an edit lane — a flat 6 per reframe to 9:16 or 4:5 — so the product, logo, and layout are inherited, not re-rolled. The month''s forty-eight variations billed 288 and matched their masters pixel-for-pixel, which is the client-facing promise a re-generation always eventually breaks.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: What is the right draft-to-final ratio for client video?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Two to one, priced honestly. At 48 credits a draft and 162 a 6-second 720p final, two drafts per client put draft spend at 15% of the entire month — insurance against a failed concept. Above about two drafts per client, the testing budget approaches the production budget, and the calendar, not the rate card, is what you should fix.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: How much does the plan tier change per-client cost?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">The identical calendar costs an average client about 602 credits at list, 494 at Basic''s 82%, 439 at Pro''s 73%, and 379 at Ultra''s 63% — a 37% swing between list and Ultra on pure platform pricing. Same deliverables, same buffers; the tier is what turns into retainer margin or price competitiveness.</p></div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:0;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Closing: One Bill, Six Sheets, Zero Surprises</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The agency model works at these numbers because the month is engineered before it is rendered: masters approved once and edited into every ratio, a canary pool eating the cost of being wrong, drafts capped at two per client, cutouts and music built as shared libraries, and a per-client sheet divided every week instead of discovered at month-end. The invoice — 3,952 list credits, roughly 602 per account — is what happens when six retainers run on three weekly beats and two buffers the client never sees. The lanes sit inside <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedance/v2-mini" style="color:#c084fc;text-decoration:none;">Seedance 2 mini</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-turbo-image-to-video" style="color:#c084fc;text-decoration:none;">Kling V3 Turbo</a>, and <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/qwen/z-image" style="color:#c084fc;text-decoration:none;">z-image</a> on FuseAI. Price one real client''s month against this sheet before signing the next retainer; the 602-credit line is the one your pricing needs to clear. Try <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/nano-banana/nano-banana-2-1" style="color:#c084fc;text-decoration:none;">FuseAITools</a> for multi-client social content and per-account cost control.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- subscription-vs-credit-topup-breakeven-ladder (category=4)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'The 990 / 2,290 / 3,417 Ladder: The Exact Credits Where Basic, Pro, and Ultra Start Beating Raw Top-ups',
  4,
  'subscription-vs-credit-topup-breakeven-ladder',
  'Raw credits vs Basic, Pro, Ultra monthly: the exact list-credit crossovers (990 / 2,290 / 3,417) where each plan starts winning, priced in real deliverables.',
  'AI credits subscription vs pay as you go, Basic plan discount, Pro plan pricing, Ultra plan cost, credit breakeven math, FuseAI pricing, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>The 990 / 2,290 / 3,417 Ladder: The Exact Credits Where Basic, Pro, and Ultra Start Beating Raw Top-ups</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Raw credits vs Basic, Pro, Ultra monthly: the exact list-credit crossovers (990 / 2,290 / 3,417) where each plan starts winning, priced in real deliverables.">
</head>
<body>
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(ellipse 65% 50% at 50% 0%, rgba(52,211,153,.07), transparent),radial-gradient(ellipse 50% 40% at 100% 90%, rgba(34,211,238,.05), transparent),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318, #0b0c0f);color:#e5e7eb;padding:20px;border-radius:12px;">

        <section class="introduction">
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">"Is the subscription worth it?" is one question wearing a costume: it''s really three. At what monthly volume does each plan''s fee stop being a liability — and past the first answer, which of the three wins? On <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com" style="color:#34d399;">FuseAITools</a> the raw credits baseline is simple — 100 credits per dollar, no fee — and the monthly plans on the <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/pricing" style="color:#34d399;">pricing page</a> each bring a fee, a credit grant, and a consumption meter: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Basic $9.9 with 1,188 credits metering at 82%, Pro $16.8 with 2,184 at 73%, Ultra $19.9 with 2,786 at 63%</strong>. Every question reduces to one curve per plan, and the curves cross at numbers you can memorize.</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Throughout, usage is measured in <em>list credits per month</em> — the sticker price of the work before any metering, because that''s the only volume figure that''s plan-independent.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="one-plan">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Crossover #1: Any Plan vs Raw Credits — the Fee Always Loses Until...</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Take Basic. Under 1,449 list credits a month (where 82% metering keeps you inside the 1,188 grant), the plan costs a flat $9.9 while raw buying costs list credits ÷ 100. $9.9 = 990 raw credits, so <strong style="color:#34d399;">Basic starts beating raw at exactly 990 list credits a month</strong> — about five finished faceless episodes or forty 8-second Mini beats. Past the grant ceiling the comparison only improves: the meter''s 18-point discount (100 − 82) grows faster than nothing, so raw never re-wins. The same algebra gives <strong style="color:#34d399;">Pro its entry at 1,680 list</strong> and <strong style="color:#34d399;">Ultra at 1,990</strong> — the fee expressed in the work it has to cover.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The easy but wrong mental model: "a plan pays off when the discount alone covers the fee." At 82% metering, the discount on 990 list credits is $1.78 — nowhere near $9.9. What actually carries the crossover is the grant; the metering discount is the plan''s second engine, and it''s what makes bigger volumes increasingly absurd to buy raw.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="two-plans">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Crossover #2: The Plans Beat Each Other — and Nobody Wins the Middle</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The staircase, solved pairwise: Basic and Pro cost the same when Pro''s larger grant plus deeper meter absorb the $6.9 fee difference — that''s at <strong style="color:#34d399;">2,290 list credits</strong> (Basic $16.80 = Pro $16.80). Pro and Ultra cross at <strong style="color:#34d399;">3,417</strong>, where Ultra''s flat $19.9 inside its grant meets Pro beginning to bill overage. Below 990, pay as you go; 990 to 2,290, Basic; 2,290 to 3,417, Pro; above 3,417, Ultra — and once past the last rung, the gap compounds: at 10,000 list, Ultra saves $44.96 over raw; at the 18,336-credit pyramid month, it saves $75.80.</p>
            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Monthly list credits</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Raw top-up</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Basic</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Pro</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Ultra</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Winner</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">500 (2 episodes)</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#34d399;">$5.00</strong></td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">$9.90</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">$16.80</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">$19.90</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Raw</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">1,000 (5 episodes)</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">$10.00</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#34d399;">$9.90</strong></td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">$16.80</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">$19.90</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Basic</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">2,000 (10 episodes)</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">$20.00</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#34d399;">$14.42</strong></td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">$16.80</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">$19.90</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Basic</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">3,000 (15 episodes)</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">$30.00</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">$22.62</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#34d399;">$16.86</strong></td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">$19.90</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Pro</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">4,000 (one flagship week + channel)</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">$40.00</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">$30.82</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">$24.16</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#34d399;">$19.90</strong></td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Ultra</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">10,000 ($100 of work)</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">$100.00</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">$80.02</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">$67.96</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#34d399;">$55.04</strong></td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Ultra</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">18,336 (the 60-video pyramid)</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">$183.36</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">$148.38</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">$128.81</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#34d399;">$107.56</strong></td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Ultra</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Plan cost = fee + (list × meter − grant) ÷ 100 when overage applies, fee alone inside the grant. Two shapes worth noticing: below the first rung the staircase doesn''t exist at all — small users genuinely should pay as they go — and past the last rung the columns never swap again. The ladder is one-way.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="weekly">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Fifth Wallet: Weekly Plans as Burst Cover</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Weekly plans (Basic $3.9 / 429 credits at 89%, Pro $5.9 / 708 at 79%, Ultra $7.9 / 1,027 at 68%) run a lighter meter but a quarter of the fee window — and that trade is precisely shaped like a client burst. A single 3,400-list week — a launch video, a localization sprint, a music package — hits Ultra monthly logic hard (the fee only amortizes over four such weeks). On Ultra weekly the same week costs $7.90 flat with grant covering 1,027 ÷ 0.68 ≈ 1,510 list and the rest topping up inside the week''s budget. The strategy falls out of the table: <strong style="color:#34d399;">steady months are monthly-shaped; spiky months are four weekly decisions</strong>, and anyone whose usage alternates 500 and 4,000 credits by week is paying the monthly fee eleven times they didn''t have to.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="pitfalls">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Where the Ladder Gets Misread</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">List-credit self-estimates are always low.</strong> The table''s rungs are in list credits — pre-failure takes included. If your honest keep-rate is one-in-two, a "2,000 list month" of delivered work is a 4,000 list month of billed work, and the right rung moved with it.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Grants don''t roll, fees do.</strong> The crossover math assumes the grant is fully consumed each cycle. A Basic month of 800 list credits is still a $9.9 month — raw would have cost $8 — the plan only wins if the work actually arrives.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">The Pro rung is real but narrow.</strong> 2,290 to 3,417 is only 1,100 list credits wide; creators who skip Pro because "I''ll just go straight to Ultra" at 2,500 list pay $3.10 a month for optionality they''re not using. Use the table, not the intuition.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Annual changes the math again.</strong> Yearly plans meter deeper still (Basic 76%, Pro 68%, Ultra 59%) with twelve-month grants — an Ultra-year $199.9 is $16.66 a month at 59% — which drags every crossover down and is the correct rung for anyone whose usage is already a habit, not a plan.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="action-checklist">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist</h2>
            <ol style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Audit last month in list credits</strong> — metered spend ÷ your current meter, takes included. That number is the only input the ladder needs.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Read the rung:</strong> &lt;990 raw · 990–2,290 Basic · 2,290–3,417 Pro · &gt;3,417 Ultra · spiky weeks go weekly.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Re-cross at plan-change volume</strong> — one new client or channel season moves you a rung; the ladder doesn''t care that you subscribed before it did.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">If volume is stable above ~6,000 list for a year, test the annual meter</strong> (Ultra 59%) before anything else — it''s the deepest single number on the page.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Keep this table bookmarked against quote-season</strong>: pricing a client month at list, then billing it at plan, is exactly the margin the crossover rows quantify.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section class="faq">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: What''s the cheapest way to buy FuseAITools credits?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Per dollar, always the deepest plan meter — but per actual usage, the cheapest wallet is the one whose fee your volume covers. Below about 990 list credits a month that''s a raw $1-per-100-credits top-up; above 3,417 it''s Ultra monthly at 63% metering with its 2,786-credit grant, and above habit-level volume, Ultra yearly at 59%.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Is Pro or Ultra better for a mid-size creator?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Pro wins from 2,290 to 3,417 list credits a month — 15-ish lean episodes or a channel plus occasional client work. Ultra wins past 3,417 and the gap only widens: at a 10,000-credit month, Ultra saves $12.92 over Pro, $44.96 over raw. Below Pro''s entry point, neither is worth a fee at all.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Do plan discounts apply to granted credits?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The meter applies to consumption, not acquisition — every credit your account spends bills at plan rate (Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63% monthly), whether it entered as a grant or a purchase. That''s the compounding lever behind the crossover numbers: the discount makes the grant bigger in list-credit terms than its face value.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: How do weekly plans change the calculus?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">They''re the burst wallet: Ultra weekly costs $7.9 and meters at 68% (vs 63% monthly) with a 1,027-credit grant — the right shape when a heavy week is followed by a light one. A month of 500/4,000/500/4,000-list weeks is four weekly decisions instead of a monthly fee amortized over a quarter of its useful life.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Give me the crossover numbers as finished videos, not abstractions.</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">At the lean episode''s 202 list credits: raw''s ceiling is ~5 episodes (990), Basic''s rung ends at ~11 (2,290), Pro''s at ~17 (3,417). If you publish more than seventeen finished videos a month, Ultra is cheaper than every other wallet on this page — before counting that its meter turns $100 into the work of a raw-$171 month.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="closing">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">One Number Decides the Whole Wallet Question</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Everything here collapses to a habit: measure the month in list credits, read the rung. Nine hundred ninety, two thousand two hundred ninety, three thousand four hundred seventeen — a ladder as legible as the <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedance/v2-mini" style="color:#a78bfa;">3-credit draft second</a> and the <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/3-0-omni-text-to-video" style="color:#f472b6;">36-credit hero second</a> that compose it. In deliverable units, that ladder is the <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video" style="color:#a78bfa;">24-credit second of 720p Wan 3.0</a>, the <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/text-to-video" style="color:#f472b6;">6-credit second of 480p Grok</a>, and the <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/flux-3-klein-text-to-image" style="color:#34d399;">3-credit Klein batch file</a>. The fee-versus-meter question never needed an opinion; it needed your volume, and now it has where to put it. Raw under 990 · Basic under 2,290 · Pro under 3,417 · <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/pricing" style="color:#34d399;">Ultra above</a> — and yearly when the habit is a year old.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- suno-music-marketing-pipeline-six-stations (category=3)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'Six Music Stations, One Flat 20-Credit Price: The Suno Pipeline That Scores a Whole Marketing Campaign for Under 200 Credits',
  3,
  'suno-music-marketing-pipeline-six-stations-flat-pricing',
  'Six Suno stations, 20 flat credits each on FuseAITools: generate, extend, cover, expansion, vocals, accompaniment — a campaign''s sound under 200 credits.',
  'Suno pricing, AI music marketing, Suno extend cover, music licensing campaign cost, ElevenLabs audio isolation, brand theme song, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Six Music Stations, One Flat 20-Credit Price: The Suno Pipeline That Scores a Whole Marketing Campaign for Under 200 Credits</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Six Suno stations, 20 flat credits each on FuseAITools: generate, extend, cover, expansion, vocals, accompaniment — a campaign''s sound under 200 credits.">
</head>
<body>
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(ellipse 65% 50% at 50% 0%, rgba(52,211,153,.07), transparent),radial-gradient(ellipse 50% 40% at 100% 90%, rgba(167,139,250,.05), transparent),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318, #0b0c0f);color:#e5e7eb;padding:20px;border-radius:12px;">

        <section class="introduction">
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Most AI pricing hides strategy in plain sight and nobody reads it. Suno''s is the clearest example on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com" style="color:#34d399;">FuseAITools</a>: every operation in the music studio — <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/generate" style="color:#34d399;">generation</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/extend" style="color:#34d399;">extension</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/upload-extend" style="color:#34d399;">audio expansion</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/upload-cover" style="color:#34d399;">covers</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/add-vocals" style="color:#34d399;">vocal tracks</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/add-instrumental" style="color:#34d399;">accompaniment</a> — bills exactly 20 credits per attempt. No duration dimension, no tier ladder. A campaign built on all six stations is therefore priced like an à la carte menu where every dish costs the same — which means the only question is how many dishes a smart buyer orders.</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">List credits (100 = $1); monthly plans meter at <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63%</strong> — a 20-credit operation lands at 16 / 15 / 13 depending on plan.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="why-flat-matters">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">What Flat-Per-Operation Pricing Actually Buys</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Three consequences fall out of the flat structure, and each one is a workflow lever:</p>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#34d399;">Length is free.</strong> Extending a hook into a full track costs the same 20 as a 30-second sketch — so generate short, test the hook cheaply, and extend only winners. Duration-based billing would charge you to be ambitious; this one doesn''t.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#34d399;">Variants are the cheapest marketing line item there is.</strong> A cover of your theme in another genre is 20 credits. A karaoke version (accompaniment) is 20. A cappella (vocals) is 20. Every format your channels demand is one operation away at identical cost.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#34d399;">Uploads join the same economy.</strong> Audio expansion and upload-cover take <em>your</em> existing tracks into the same 20-credit station — a jingle from last year''s campaign becomes this year''s extended mix without a licensing conversation.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="campaign">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Campaign: One Brand Theme, Eight Deliverables</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Brief: a coffee brand''s autumn campaign needs a theme track plus platform variants. Station by station, attempts counted honestly:</p>
            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Station</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Campaign job</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Attempts</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">List</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Ultra 63%</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Generate</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Theme hook candidates</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">4</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">80</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">50</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Extend</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Winner → full track (×2 passes)</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">2</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">40</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">25</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Upload-cover</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Acoustic variant for in-store play</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">2</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">40</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">25</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Add-instrumental</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Karaoke bed for UGC contest</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">1</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">20</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">13</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Add-vocals</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Voice-forward social mix</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">1</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">20</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">13</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Upload-extend</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Last year''s jingle → autumn loop</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">1</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">20</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">13</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Suno subtotal</strong></td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">8 deliverables</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">11 ops</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">220</strong></td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~139</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">ElevenLabs support</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">See below</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">—</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~31</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~20</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Campaign sound total</strong></td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Full audio identity</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">—</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">~251</strong></td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">~159</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The ElevenLabs support row: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/speech-to-text" style="color:#a78bfa;">Speech to Text</a> (6 credits/minute) transcripts the vocal mixes for timed lyric captions; <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/audio-isolation" style="color:#a78bfa;">Audio Isolation</a> (0.3 credits/sec) splits a finished track into voice and bed so the in-store and social versions can diverge further; and a 90-second voice tag on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/turbo-2-5" style="color:#a78bfa;">Turbo 2.5</a> is ~2 credits. A campaign''s whole audio post-production: thirty credits.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="picture">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Bonus Lap: The Music Video You Can Now Afford</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">With audio identity under 260 credits, visuals inherit the budget question — and the per-second shelf answers it: a 10-second teaser on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/kling/v3-turbo-text-to-video" style="color:#f472b6;">Kling V3 Turbo</a> at 720p (27/sec) is 270 credits, or the same shot drafted on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/grok/video-1-5-preview" style="color:#60a5fa;">Grok 1.5 Preview</a> at 480p for 6/sec (60 credits, then upscaled only if the cut earns it). The full teaser-plus-track campaign still lands under 600 list credits — a jingle package that stock-music licensing alone used to price above.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="pitfalls">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Flat Pricing''s Blind Spots</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Flat per operation ≠ flat per result.</strong> Eleven operations at 20 is 220; eleven <em>failed</em> operations is 220 with nothing to ship. Cap attempts per station (our 4-generate budget is a real budget) before the menu pricing lulls you into sampling everything.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Extension direction matters.</strong> Extend continues <em>your generated track</em>; upload-extend continues <em>an uploaded audio file</em>. Ordering the wrong one is a 20-credit detour, not an error message.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Isolation is a scalpel, not a rights manager.</strong> Audio Isolation splits stems from any file you upload — capability is not license. Use it on your own generations and cleared uploads.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Platform duration formats arrive sideways.</strong> Need a 15-second cut for shorts and a 3-minute full? The flat structure says generate → extend to full → let the edit decide the short — not three generations of three lengths.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="action-checklist">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist</h2>
            <ol style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Hook first, budget after:</strong> spend four generates on the catchiest 30 seconds, not one on a full song.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Extend only the winner, twice:</strong> hook → verse pass → final-chorus pass gets a full track for 40.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Order variants from the master, never re-generate:</strong> cover, instrumental, and vocals stations all start from the approved track.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Mine the archive with upload-extend and upload-cover</strong> — last campaign''s audio is this campaign''s cheap input.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Finish with isolation + STT captions</strong> (~31 credits) and bill the whole campaign at plan rate: ~159 on Ultra monthly (63%).</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section class="faq">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Does every Suno operation really cost the same?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">On FuseAITools, yes: generate, extend, audio expansion (upload-extend), cover (upload-cover), add vocals, and add instrumental are each 20 credits per attempt, regardless of track length. Plan holders pay 16 / 15 / 13 monthly (Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63%). Six different operations at one price is the studio''s entire pricing model.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: How do you make a full-length song cheaply?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Hook-and-extend: generate short candidates (4 × 20 = 80), pick the winner, extend it to full length (2 × 20 = 40) — a finished track for 120 list credits. Generating a full song outright risks the same 120 on a composition that never lands.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: What''s the difference between extend and audio expansion?</h3>
                <p style="padding:0;color:#d1d5db;margin-bottom:6px;">Extend continues a track you generated inside the studio. Audio expansion (upload-extend) takes an uploaded audio file — an old jingle, a live recording — and continues that instead. Both are 20 credits; the distinction is whose tape you''re threading.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Can the campaign use one track across every channel?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">That''s what the variant stations are for: cover (20) re-styles the track for another channel''s mood, add-instrumental (20) yields the karaoke/bed version, add-vocals (20) the voice-forward mix, and ElevenLabs Audio Isolation (0.3/sec) splits stems for further edits. One composition, eight deliverables, 220 credits of operations.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: How much does the complete campaign audio cost?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">About 251 list credits end to end: 220 across eleven Suno operations plus ~31 for isolation, transcription, and a voice tag. That''s roughly $2.50 of list-priced sound identity — 159 credits (~$1.59) on an Ultra monthly plan — for a brand theme with platform variants.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="closing">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Read the Pricing, Then Run the Menu</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Flat-per-operation pricing is an invitation disguised as a price list: variants are as cheap as drafts, extensions as cheap as sketches, and the archive as playable as the present. The campaigns losing the music game are the ones paying licensing for what six stations would produce for the price of a coffee — the autumn track above scored, mixed, captioned, and stemmed for under 260 credits.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Start at <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/generate" style="color:#34d399;">generate</a>, keep the attempt budgets honest, and let the flat menu do the rest.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- transparent-cutout-rework-post-mortem-alpha-defaults (category=2)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'The Transparent-Cutout Rework Post-Mortem: JPG Defaults, a 2K Transparency Nobody Validates, and the 5.6× Bill of Doing It Twice',
  2,
  'transparent-cutout-rework-post-mortem-alpha-defaults',
  'A cutout rework in five stages: a default JPG that drops alpha, 2K transparency no backend validates, and the 66-credit path that did it right.',
  'transparent background PNG alpha, nano banana default jpg, GPT image 2K transparent, flux klein output format, cutout rework cost, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>The Transparent-Cutout Rework Post-Mortem: JPG Defaults, a 2K Transparency Nobody Validates, and the 5.6× Bill of Doing It Twice</title>
    <meta name="description" content="A cutout rework in five stages: a default JPG that drops alpha, 2K transparency no backend validates, and the 66-credit path that did it right.">
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/news/transparent-cutout-rework-post-mortem-alpha-defaults">
</head>
<body style="margin:0;padding:0;background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(circle at 15% 0%, rgba(192,132,252,0.07) 0%, transparent 45%),radial-gradient(circle at 85% 100%, rgba(251,191,36,0.05) 0%, transparent 45%),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318 0%, #0b0c0f 100%);color:#d1d5db;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="max-width:960px;margin:0 auto;padding:32px 20px 64px;">
        <header style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h1 itemprop="headline" style="font-size:2rem;line-height:1.25;color:#f9fafb;margin:0 0 16px;">The Transparent-Cutout Rework Post-Mortem: JPG Defaults, a 2K Transparency Nobody Validates, and the 5.6× Bill of Doing It Twice</h1>
            <p style="color:#9ca3af;font-size:0.95rem;margin:0;">Published <time itemprop="datePublished" datetime="2026-11-12">November 12, 2026</time> · FuseAI Tools Editorial</p>
        </header>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Introduction: The Prompt Said Transparent. Three Defaults Disagreed.</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">A sticker pack is the simplest job in the image catalog: 60 assets, transparent background, one style. The batch ran on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/nano-banana/nano-banana-2-1" style="color:#c084fc;text-decoration:none;">Nano Banana 2.1</a> at 1K, and it consumed <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">948 list credits across five stages</strong> to deliver what a 66-credit first pass would have covered: 60 usable transparent PNGs. The gap is not a keep-rate story, a prompt-quality story, or a model-choice story. It is three separate default values in three neighboring models, plus one resolution combination that no backend on the platform validates.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">This is a post-mortem, written the way failure reviews should be: stage by stage, credit by credit, ending with the one-line check that would have caught every stage before the first invoice. The delivery-spec side of this story — the platform rejection at upload — is a different article; this one is about the half before upload, where the file is made wrong and the tools are technically behaving exactly as documented.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Stage 1: The JPG Default That Swallows Alpha</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Nano Banana 2.1''s <code style="color:#c084fc;background:#1f2937;padding:1px 6px;border-radius:4px;">output_format</code> accepts png or jpg and <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">defaults to jpg</strong> when the field is absent. JPG has no alpha channel — not "weak alpha," none. A prompt reading "isolated object, transparent background" on a default-jpg request produces a perfectly rendered object on a perfectly <em>opaque</em> background, at 6 list credits (1K) to 12 (4K) an image, and the history thumbnail looks fine. The failure only surfaces when the file lands on a colored canvas.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The trap is asymmetric across models, and that is what makes it travel: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/flux-3-klein-text-to-image" style="color:#c084fc;text-decoration:none;">Flux 3 Klein</a> defaults <code>output_format</code> to <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">png</strong>. A team that built its habit on Klein — where omitting the format still yields alpha-capable files — carried the omission to Nano Banana and got 60 JPGs. Two neighboring models, opposite defaults, identical-looking forms. The defaults are documented per model; the muscle memory is per team.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Stages 2–4: The 2K Clause, the Upscale Trap, the Batch Multiplier</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Stage 2: "just redo it at 2K" bought transparency that cannot exist.</strong> On GPT Image 2.5, <code>background=transparent</code> is a 1K-only capability at the vendor level — and the backend deliberately does not police the combination; it passes the request through and lets the vendor decide. The documented escape hatch is real, but the failure mode is the expensive kind: depending on vendor behavior you get a clean rejection (free, the good outcome) or a billed 2K image with an opaque background (18 credits, the outcome this team got). At 24 credits, the 4K version of the same mistake is the worst per-asset price in the family.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Stage 3: the rescue that multiplied the batch.</strong> Facing 60 flat JPGs, the natural instinct is to upscale — but Klein''s 4K is a <em>generation</em> tier (12 credits per image), not a rescue service, and requesting numImages=4 "to get options" bills four copies of every attempt: a single 4K asset with four variants is 48 list (12 × 4), and scaled across even a tenth of the pack the same instinct turns the 60-credit job into a 480-credit one. Batch pricing multiplies intentions; a draft-quality default paired with a batch ceiling of 4 is the single fastest way to inflate any per-asset price by the number of options nobody asked for.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Stage 4: the aspect ratio that only exists at 1K.</strong> When the pack finally got re-rendered at correct settings, one template still carried 27:16 — a legal GPT Image 2.5 aspect that is <em>only available at 1K</em>, one of four such values (27:16, 16:27, 9:8, 8:9) in its 13-value whitelist. Combined with the stage-2 mistake in the same request — 2K + 27:16 + transparent — the order left the building with three conditions stacked, and nothing on the form warned that the resolution field silently invalidates the other two.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Rework Ledger: Five Stages, Fourteen Times the Necessary Bill</h2>
            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:20px;font-size:0.92rem;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:#1f2937;">
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:left;">Stage</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:left;">What ran</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">List</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">Ultra 63%</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:left;">Delivered</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">1. First pass</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">60 × NB2.1 1K, format omitted → JPG</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">360</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">227</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">0 usable (no alpha)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">2. The "fix"</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">10 × GPT 2.5 2K + transparent spot batch</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#fbbf24;">180</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">113</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">0 usable (opaque at 2K)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">3. The rescue</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Klein 4K, numImages=4 (one asset)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#fbbf24;">48</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">30</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">1 oversized opaque→alpha guess</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">4. The correction</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">60 × NB2.1 1K with png stated</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">360</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">227</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;color:#34d399;">60 usable</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Total as run</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">—</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#f472b6;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">948</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">597</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">—</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Path once-right</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">60 × NB2.1 1K png (+1 GPT 1K transparent style probe)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#34d399;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">66</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">42</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;color:#34d399;">60 usable</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;margin-top:16px;">Discount rates are the monthly Basic 82% / Pro 73% / Ultra 63% plan rates. The headline number is the ratio: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">948 list spent, 66 necessary — a 14× rework tax</strong> on the simplest job in the catalog, and every dollar of it traces to a field left at its default or a combination left unvalidated. Note stage 2 is the only line where the platform behaved as documented and still billed for nothing: background=transparent at 2K passes the DTO, passes pricing, and delivers an opaque file.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Pitfalls and One-Line Fixes</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">State the format; never inherit the habit.</strong> NB2.1 defaults to jpg, Klein to png, GPT Image 2.5 writes png when transparent is requested. Three models, three defaults, one delivery requirement: put <code>output_format</code> in every cutout template.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Transparent means 1K on GPT Image 2.5 — plan the resolution around the alpha, not the other way.</strong> If alpha is required, the working resolution is decided for you. If 2K/4K is also required, transparency is someone else''s job: matte it in post or pick a lane where the spec allows both — GPT Image 2.5''s sibling <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image/v2-5-sunburst-text-to-image" style="color:#c084fc;text-decoration:none;">Sunburst text-to-image</a> tier is the one to probe before committing the pack, not after.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Check the channel, not the thumbnail.</strong> A JPG of a white object on a white background is invisible in a contact sheet. One alpha-channel inspection per batch, on one file, before the other 59 render — the check costs seconds and kills the stage-1 line entirely.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Batch counts are multipliers on mistakes.</strong> numImages=4 on a wrong-settings asset is four wrong-settings assets; the draft-before-batch rule from the reroll economics work exists precisely because the keep denominator multiplies whatever you get wrong first.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Stacked conditions fail as a unit.</strong> 27:16 is legal, 1K is legal, transparent is legal — the triple is legal only at 1K. The backend does not validate combinations on this family, so combination checks belong in your template review, not in the order gate.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">The free lesson exists — take it.</strong> A <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/nano-banana/nano-banana-2-lite" style="color:#c084fc;text-decoration:none;">Nano Banana 2 lite</a> run is a flat 6 credits with no resolution key: the cheapest place to verify style and format behavior before committing the batch''s real budget (6-credit canaries, as the rejection-taxonomy article argues, are the platform''s gift to people who plan).</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;padding-left:24px;line-height:1.9;">
                <li>Write "format: png" into every transparent-asset template, regardless of which model the template runs on.</li>
                <li>Before any cutout batch, open one output in an editor that shows channels — confirm alpha, then start the remaining run.</li>
                <li>Pin transparent GPT Image 2.5 work to 1K; if the delivery needs 2K, decide the matte workflow before generating, not after invoicing.</li>
                <li>Keep one style probe per vendor family in the batch budget at draft tier; six credits is the cheapest rework insurance in the catalog.</li>
                <li>Audit aspect ratios against their resolution windows — the 1K-only values are invisible until the combination fails.</li>
                <li>Price batches as count × unit × variants; a 4-image draft on a 60-asset job is a 240-image invoice, and the form will not say so.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">FAQ</h2>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: I asked for a transparent background and the PNG is still opaque. Did the model ignore my prompt?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Most likely the prompt was never the transparency control. On Nano Banana 2.1 the output defaults to JPG — a format with no alpha channel at all — so the request did what it documented and left the field unhelpfully. Transparency is a <em>format</em> property plus, on GPT Image 2.5, a <em>background parameter</em>; prose in the prompt contributes style, not channels. State png explicitly and treat background=transparent as the switch it is.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Why did my 2K transparent request get billed instead of refused?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Because background=transparent is a 1K capability at the vendor level, and the backend passes the combination through without validating resolution pairs — the vendor gets to decide. When it accepts the order you get a legitimately billed, legitimately rendered <em>opaque</em> 2K image. Refusal would have been free; the architecture chose vendor judgment over platform policing, and the invoice is where you find out which way it went.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Can I fix a batch of JPGs by upscaling them?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">No — upscaling changes pixels, not channels. A JPG has no alpha to inherit, so any "upscaled cutout" still needs a matte pass. And on Klein, 4K is a generation tier priced per image (12 list) with numImages multiplying it up to four at a time: the rescue route costs more than the redo and delivers less certainty. Regenerate with the format stated; that is the 60-credit path, not the 300-credit one.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Why do two models I use default to different formats?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Because each family''s default follows its vendor''s convention: Flux 3 Klein ships png-default (alpha-capable by luck), Nano Banana 2.1 ships jpg-default (smaller, lossy, alpha-free). Nothing in either form hints at the other. This is the silent-pass failure class in its purest image-side form: identical-looking requests, opposite outputs, and the only defense is an explicit format field in every template.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: What is the cheapest way to verify all of this before a 60-asset batch?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Two probe orders: one at the draft tier you intend to use, one at the family where transparency lives (GPT Image 2.5 at 1K, background transparent, 6 credits). Inspect channels, confirm the aspect/resolution pair, then release the batch. Against the 948 this post-mortem traces, the 12-credit rehearsal is the highest-return line item in the entire job — and it is the same canary logic the rejection article prices at 6.</p></div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:0;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Closing: Defaults Are Someone Else''s Requirements</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Every credit in the 882-credit gap between this job and the right version of it was spent satisfying a default: a format nobody stated, a resolution nobody paired with the transparency clause, a batch count nobody multiplied out. The image families on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image/v2-5-flare-text-to-image" style="color:#c084fc;text-decoration:none;">FuseAI</a> document all of these — the post-mortem is not a complaint about the contracts but about the reading habits they assume. Before the next asset run, state the format, probe the alpha, and check the resolution pairs against the 1K-only list yourself inside <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/nano-banana/nano-banana-2-1" style="color:#c084fc;text-decoration:none;">Nano Banana 2.1</a> and <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/flux-3-klein-text-to-image" style="color:#c084fc;text-decoration:none;">Flux 3 Klein text-to-image</a> — because in cutout work, the cheapest file is the one that arrives with its channels already correct.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- transparent-image-asset-shootout-gpt-image-flux-klein-nano-banana (category=2)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'Transparent Image Assets Compared: GPT Image 2.5 vs Flux 3 Klein vs Nano Banana 2.1 on One Production Brief, Priced Round by Round',
  2,
  'transparent-image-asset-shootout-gpt-image-flux-klein-nano-banana',
  'GPT Image 2.5, Flux 3 Klein and Nano Banana 2.1 on one transparent-asset brief: background switches, 4K batch math, a 252-credit invoice.',
  'transparent background AI image, GPT Image 2.5 pricing, Flux 3 Klein, Nano Banana 2.1, PNG alpha AI generation, product sticker assets, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Transparent Image Assets Compared: GPT Image 2.5 vs Flux 3 Klein vs Nano Banana 2.1 on One Production Brief, Priced Round by Round</title>
    <meta name="description" content="GPT Image 2.5, Flux 3 Klein and Nano Banana 2.1 on one transparent-asset brief: background switches, 4K batch math, a 252-credit invoice.">
</head>
<body>
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(ellipse 65% 50% at 50% 0%, rgba(192,132,252,.07), transparent),radial-gradient(ellipse 50% 40% at 100% 90%, rgba(52,211,153,.05), transparent),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318, #0b0c0f);color:#e5e7eb;padding:20px;border-radius:12px;">

        <section class="introduction">
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Every merch designer, sticker seller, and listing optimizer eventually runs the same shopping trip: a sheet of cut-out assets on transparent backgrounds, plus a handful of full-bleed hero images. <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com" style="color:#c084fc;">FuseAITools</a> carries three families happy to take that order — <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image/v2-5-sunburst-text-to-image" style="color:#c084fc;">GPT Image 2.5</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/flux-3-klein-text-to-image" style="color:#34d399;">Flux 3 Klein</a>, and <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/nano-banana/nano-banana-2-1" style="color:#fbbf24;">Nano Banana 2.1</a> — and they answer it in three completely different ways, only one of which is a literal switch labeled "transparent." This shootout prices the confusion out of the record: same brief, three quotes, one invoice.</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">List credits (100 = $1); monthly plans meter credits at <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63%</strong>. The brief: 20 transparent-background sticker designs at 1K, four 4K hero renders, six 2K lifestyle scenes, plus cleanup passes.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="round-1">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Round 1 — The Transparency Question: One Real Switch, Two Format Tricks</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The uncomfortable fact most comparison articles skip: only GPT Image 2.5 exposes an explicit <code style="color:#c084fc;">background</code> parameter (transparent / opaque / auto) across all four of its v2.5 models — flare and sunburst, text-to-image and <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image/v2-5-sunburst-image-to-image" style="color:#c084fc;">image-to-image</a>. One catch, and it''s a big one: the vendor only honors transparency at 1K. Request it at 2K or 4K and the backend doesn''t block you — the vendor refuses the job. At 1K the switch costs nothing extra: still 6 credits a generation.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Flux 3 Klein answers differently: its output format defaults to PNG (png/jpeg/webp selectable) — a container that <em>can</em> carry alpha, which is not the same promise as "will" carry alpha. Nano Banana 2.1 goes further the other way: its output format defaults to <strong style="color:#fbbf24;">jpg</strong>, the one format that structurally cannot be transparent; opting into png is a per-call parameter. Same word "PNG asset," three different realities — and any workflow that assumes them equivalent is one delivery cycle from a refund.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="round-2">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Round 2 — The Quote Sheet: 1K Singles vs 4K Batch Math</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Per-unit prices on this brief, from the live billing tables: GPT Image 2.5 bills <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image/v2-5-flare-text-to-image" style="color:#c084fc;">absolute by size</a> — 6 at 1K, 18 at 2K, 24 at 4K. Nano Banana 2.1 sits almost on top of it — 6 / 9 / 12 — the cheapest 2K renders in the comparison. Flux 3 Klein wins Round 2 for one reason nobody quotes: it''s the only batch-priced family of the three. Its per-image rates (3 at 1K, 6 at 2K, 12 at 4K) multiply by numImages (1–4) in a single operation — a four-up 4K hero sheet is 12 × 4 = 48 credits, while the other two need four separate 24-credit calls for the same sheet at GPT''s 4K rate (96) or price 4K at Nano Banana''s 12 with less resolution authority behind it.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Prompt ceilings split the same three ways: GPT Image 2.5 and Nano Banana 2.1 carry 20,000-character scene descriptions — full brand-spec text per asset — while Klein''s prompt tops out at 5,000 characters. For style-sheet-driven production with long inherited prompts, that is a real workflow constraint, not a spec-sheet footnote.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="round-3">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Round 3 — The Rescue Station: Where Alpha Actually Gets Fixed</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Production reality: some assets arrive with backgrounds regardless of who made them. The repair shop on this platform is embarrassingly cheap. <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedream/5-flash-layer-decomposition" style="color:#22d3ee;">Seedream 5 Flash layer decomposition</a> (6 credits flat at any size) splits a finished image into editable layers, and GPT Image 2.5 image-to-image with <code style="color:#c084fc;">background=transparent</code> at 1K (6 credits) re-renders an existing design as a clean cutout — the same 6 that generated it. Klein''s own <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/flux-3-klein-edit-image" style="color:#34d399;">edit model</a> bills identically to its generator (3/6/12 per image × batch), the cheapest rescue at 1K if you trust the prompt. The honest takeaway: transparency is a two-step skill anywhere — decide the alpha layer costs six credits, not a vendor lawsuit.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="invoice">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Asset-Pack Invoice, Divided Three Ways</h2>
            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Line (model chosen for its strength)</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Job</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">List</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Basic 82%</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Pro 73%</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Ultra 63%</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">GPT Image 2.5 Sunburst t2i, 1K, background=transparent ×20</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">sticker sheet</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">120</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">98</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">88</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">76</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Flux 3 Klein t2i, 4K, numImages=4 ×1 batch</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">hero sheet</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">48</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">39</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">35</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">30</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Nano Banana 2.1, 2K, output_format=png ×6</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">lifestyle scenes</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">54</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">44</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">39</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">34</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Rescue: i2i transparent ×3 (6 each) + Flash layer decompositions ×2 (6 each)</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">cutout fixes</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">30</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">25</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">22</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">19</td></tr>
                    <tr><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Full asset pack</strong></td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">30 deliverables</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">252</strong></td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">207</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">184</td><td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">159</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Read the assignment, not just the total: each family got the line it can actually guarantee. <strong style="color:#c084fc;">Explicit alpha → GPT Image 2.5</strong> at 1K. <strong style="color:#34d399;">Four-up 4K cheap → Klein''s batch multiplier</strong>. <strong style="color:#fbbf24;">Mid-res volume at the lowest 2K price → Nano Banana 2.1</strong>, with the png flag consciously set. The 252 list credit pack — 159 on Ultra monthly — buys the whole shop''s week of assets, and the line assignment is what keeps re-rolls out of the invoice.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="pitfalls">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Four Comparisons That Lie</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">"PNG means transparent."</strong> PNG is a container; alpha is a promise. Nano Banana 2.1 defaults to jpg — file extension assumptions produce opaque deliverables silently. Klein defaults to PNG but exposes no background parameter. Check what each model actually controls.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">"Transparent at 4K, please." </strong>GPT Image 2.5 honors transparency at 1K only, and the backend forwards your request without argument — the vendor rejects it downstream. Budget alpha work at 1K and composite up, or pay the failed-job lesson.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">"Klein is 3 credits."</strong> That''s 3 <em>per image</em> at 1K, and numImages is a required billing key — a 4-up batch at 4K bills 48. The multiplier cuts both ways: cheapest sheet of four in the comparison, but never quote the unit price as the order price.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">"Any model can take my 8,000-character brand prompt."</strong> Klein tops out at 5,000 characters; GPT Image 2.5 and Nano Banana 2.1 take 20,000. Teams porting long style-sheet prompts between families hit this in week one.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="action-checklist">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist</h2>
            <ol style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Route every alpha-dependent asset to GPT Image 2.5 at 1K</strong> with background=transparent set explicitly — the only generation flag in this matchup that means it.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Give Klein the big-format batch lines</strong> — 4K heroes as numImages=4 sheets at 48 a pop, with a 5,000-character prompt rewrite pass.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Set output_format=png on every Nano Banana 2.1 call</strong> and treat the jpg default as a production bug waiting to ship.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Bank a 30-credit rescue buffer</strong> per pack: six-credit i2i cutouts and Flash decompositions are cheaper than any re-roll debate.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Bill the pack at your plan rate before quoting clients</strong> — 252 list becomes 207 / 184 / 159 on Basic / Pro / Ultra monthly, and margin lives in that number.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section class="faq">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Which AI image model has a real transparent-background switch?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">On FuseAITools, only GPT Image 2.5 (flare and sunburst, both directions) exposes a background parameter with a transparent option — and only at 1K, where it costs the standard 6 credits. Flux 3 Klein and Nano Banana 2.1 control file format (Klein defaults png, Nano Banana 2.1 defaults jpg) but offer no alpha guarantee at generation time.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Is batch generation actually cheaper for large formats?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Only on Flux 3 Klein, and yes dramatically: 12 credits per image at 4K × four images is one 48-credit operation. The same four 4K renders on GPT Image 2.5 are four × 24 = 96 credits. Klein is the only model of the three whose per-call price scales with output count instead of duplicating calls.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: How do I fix an asset that arrived with an unwanted background?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Two six-credit moves: GPT Image 2.5 image-to-image with background=transparent re-renders the design as a cutout (1K), or <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/seedream/5-flash-layer-decomposition" style="color:#22d3ee;">Seedream 5 Flash layer decomposition</a> breaks the image into editable layers at any size. Klein''s edit model is 3 at 1K if you want to stay in-family. Cleanup belongs in every asset budget at roughly 10% of generation cost.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: What does a full product-asset pack cost end to end?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">This brief — 20 transparent stickers, a four-up 4K hero sheet, six 2K scenes, five rescue passes — invoices 252 credits list, 30 deliverables at an average of 8.4 credits each. On plans: about 207 (Basic 82%), 184 (Pro 73%), 159 (Ultra 63%) monthly.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Which model should a sticker shop standardize on if it picks one?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">GPT Image 2.5, honestly, because sticker work is alpha work and it''s the only explicit switch — 6 credits a tile at 1K, with the 20,000-character prompt room for brand specs. What you give up is 4K authority and batch pricing; shops that grow into poster and banner format graduate to a two-family shop (Klein for large-format batches) around the point where monthly volume exceeds ~40 large renders.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="closing">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Stop Asking Which Model Is Best — Ask Which Promise It Makes</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The transparent-asset market''s dirty secret is that three plausible answers to one brief mean "guaranteed alpha," "probably alpha," and "you didn''t ask." The production workflow is just assignment: <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/gpt-image/v2-5-sunburst-text-to-image" style="color:#c084fc;">the switch</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/flux-kontext/flux-3-klein-text-to-image" style="color:#34d399;">the batch</a>, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/nano-banana/nano-banana-2-1" style="color:#fbbf24;">the volume price</a>, and six credits of cleanup insurance wherever alpha came out wrong. 252 list credits, 159 on Ultra, thirty deliverables — the shootout''s real verdict is that the invoice is built, not won.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- voice-billing-traps-three-times-expected-invoice (category=3)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'Why Your Voice Invoice Is Triple What You Expected: The UTF-8 Byte Trap, the 1,000-Cliff, and Six Flat Operations in Disguise',
  3,
  'voice-billing-traps-three-times-expected-invoice',
  'Audio billing traps with exact math: ElevenLabs charges UTF-8 bytes so Chinese costs 3×, 1,001 characters bills as two blocks, Suno prices per operation.',
  'ElevenLabs per character pricing, UTF-8 byte billing Chinese TTS cost, STT per minute rounding, Suno flat credits, audio isolation per second, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Why Your Voice Invoice Is Triple What You Expected: The UTF-8 Byte Trap, the 1,000-Cliff, and Six Flat Operations in Disguise</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Audio billing traps with exact math: ElevenLabs charges UTF-8 bytes so Chinese costs 3×, 1,001 characters bills as two blocks, Suno prices per operation.">
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/news/voice-billing-traps-three-times-expected-invoice">
</head>
<body style="margin:0;padding:0;background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(circle at 15% 0%, rgba(167,139,250,0.07) 0%, transparent 45%),radial-gradient(circle at 85% 100%, rgba(52,211,153,0.05) 0%, transparent 45%),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318 0%, #0b0c0f 100%);color:#d1d5db;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="max-width:960px;margin:0 auto;padding:32px 20px 64px;">
        <header style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h1 itemprop="headline" style="font-size:2rem;line-height:1.25;color:#f9fafb;margin:0 0 16px;">Why Your Voice Invoice Is Triple What You Expected: The UTF-8 Byte Trap, the 1,000-Cliff, and Six Flat Operations in Disguise</h1>
            <p style="color:#9ca3af;font-size:0.95rem;margin:0;">Published <time itemprop="datePublished" datetime="2026-11-12">November 12, 2026</time> · FuseAI Tools Editorial</p>
        </header>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Introduction: Audio Is the Only Modality That Bills in Three Different Units</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Text generation bills tokens. Video bills seconds. Audio — on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs" style="color:#a78bfa;text-decoration:none;">FuseAI</a> — bills <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">bytes, minutes, seconds, and operations</strong> in the same product line, sometimes in the same workflow. That is not a rounding story; it is why voice invoices surprise people by factors, not percentages. The single largest multiplier in the entire catalog is invisible in the UI: ElevenLabs character pricing reads the <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">UTF-8 byte length</strong> of your script, which means the same sentence in Chinese costs three times what it costs in English — not because Chinese is penalized, but because CJK glyphs occupy three bytes each where Latin letters occupy one.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Seven traps below, each with the arithmetic written out, because every one of them is a division or a ceiling function away from a predictable budget. Then a two-column invoice: the same 10-minute episode in English versus Chinese, from 405 to 645 list credits — and most of that gap is one language switch nobody approved.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Traps #1–#4: Bytes, Ceilings, and Minutes</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Trap #1: the 3× CJK multiplier.</strong> <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/turbo-2-5" style="color:#a78bfa;text-decoration:none;">ElevenLabs TTS</a> is CHARACTER-priced: <code style="color:#a78bfa;background:#1f2937;padding:1px 6px;border-radius:4px;">ceil(bytes / 1000) × rate</code>, where the turbo lane runs 10 credits per thousand bytes and multilingual runs 20. A 400-character English script is 400 bytes — one thousandth-bill. The same content in Chinese is roughly 400 characters × 3 bytes = 1,200 bytes — two thousandths-bill. Your character counter says the workload was identical. The byte length says it cost <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">2× on turbo, 3× on a like-for-like character basis</strong>, and there is no UI field that shows you bytes.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Trap #2: the 1,000 cliff and the one-character crime.</strong> The ceiling function means 1 byte over a thousand bills as two thousand. A 999-byte script and a 1,001-byte script differ by 10 credits on turbo (10 vs. 20 list) — a 100% jump for one character. Multilingual makes it worse: the cliff step is 20 credits, and a 1,001-byte non-English script starts at 40 list. Long scripts should be composed to land <em>under</em> the cliff per request, not split after the invoice proves the point.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Trap #3: STT bills the minute you touched.</strong> <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/speech-to-text" style="color:#a78bfa;text-decoration:none;">Speech-to-text</a> is <code>ceil(seconds / 60) × 6</code>. A 61-second interview clip costs 12 — double what the first minute cost. A 20-minute raw recording is 20 minutes of billing at 120 list — but slice the same recording into 20 clips of 61 seconds and the ceilings stack to 240. The minute is the unit; fewer, fuller requests beat many tidy clips.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Trap #4: the silent 2× between two TTS lanes.</strong> Turbo (10/1k bytes) and multilingual (20/1k bytes) differ by a clean factor of two on the same byte count. Multilingual is the right call for many languages and accents; it is never the right call for a script that turbo voices handle, because the lane you did not evaluate for is the lane you paid double for. Every trap so far is 2× or 3× — which is exactly how "triple the expected invoice" gets assembled from pieces.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Traps #5–#7: Per-Second Ceilings and the Flat-Operation Illusion</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Trap #5: per-second ceilings at sub-credit rates.</strong> <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/audio-isolation" style="color:#a78bfa;text-decoration:none;">Audio isolation</a> bills <code>ceil(seconds) × 0.30</code> and <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/sound-effect-v2" style="color:#a78bfa;text-decoration:none;">sound effects</a> <code>ceil(seconds) × 0.36</code>. The rates look trivial — until you notice two things: anything over zero seconds rounds up to a full second (a 0.2s impact stinger bills a whole 0.36), and these are the only per-second audio lanes where the discount plan math matters most, because list credits below 1 round visibly. Thirty stingers of 2.5 seconds is 3 × 20 × 0.36 = 21.6 list credits nobody itemizes; a full episode''s isolation pass at 600 seconds is 180 list by itself.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Trap #6: six operations, one price, zero information about output.</strong> <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/generate" style="color:#34d399;text-decoration:none;">Suno</a> charges a flat 20 credits for every operation on the sheet: generate, extend, upload-cover, upload-extend, add-instrumental, add-vocals. A 30-second generate costs the same 20 as a 4-minute one — great until you learn the trap inverts by workflow: building a 4-minute track through <em>one generate plus three extends</em> costs 80, because extends are priced per operation, not per delivered second. The flat rate is a per-track bargain and a per-assembly penalty at the same time. (The keep-rate version of this math — extends pulled on tracks you discard — is the same 1-in-4 tax the video reroll economics article documents, at 20 credits a roll.)</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Trap #7: audio in the wrong wallet.</strong> Under the monthly plans (Basic 82% / Pro 73% / Ultra 63%), your grant buys list-credit equivalent, and the effective rate depends on which lane you spend it in — the same reason a $100 top-up wallet stretches to 10,000 list credits raw and 17,137 at Ultra. When audio sits in a shared pool with video, an "audio-only" month can consume its grant at turbo''s 10/1k rate while a multilingual-heavy month burns it at 20/1k on identical scripts. The lane decides how fast your subscription evaporates; the script decides which lane you actually needed.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Invoice: Same 10-Minute Episode, Two Languages</h2>
            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:20px;font-size:0.92rem;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:#1f2937;">
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:left;">Audio line</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">English list</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">Chinese list</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">Ultra 63% (EN / CN)</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">TTS turbo — 9,000 EN bytes / 3,000 chars × 3 CN bytes</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">90</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#fbbf24;">270</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">57 / 170</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">STT review pass — 600s = ceil(10 min) × 6</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">60</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">60</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">38 / 38</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Isolation — 600s × 0.30</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">180</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">180</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">113 / 113</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Sound effects — 20 cues × 2s × 0.36</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">15</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">15</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">9 / 9</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Music bed — 1 generate + 1 add-instrumental (flat 20 × 2)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">40</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">40</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">25 / 25</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Total</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">405</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;color:#fbbf24;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">645</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">255 / 406</strong></td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;margin-top:16px;">The TTS line is the entire story: identical script length, 3× the bill, purely from bytes. The other four lines are language-blind, which is why the episode total moves 59% and not 200% — and why the "triple" complaint is almost always about <em>one</em> line item, not the invoice. If you localize, budget the localization as its own TTS purchase, not a rounding note: at Chinese volumes, turbo (10/1k bytes) against multilingual (20/1k) is a 270 vs. 540 decision on the same 9,000 CJK bytes.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Pitfalls and One-Line Fixes</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Count bytes before you count characters.</strong> Paste your script through a byte-length check (UTF-8) before batch submission; for CJK, divide your expected bill by nothing — triple it.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Compose to stay under the 1,000-byte cliff.</strong> Trim to 999, or split at natural paragraph boundaries below the thousand — never let the ceiling function add a whole 10-or-20-credit step for one stray byte.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Batch STT into minute-fulls.</strong> 61-second uploads pay two minutes each; concatenating a batch to exact multiples of 60 before transcription is free money.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Evaluate turbo first, multilingual second.</strong> The 2× lane gap should be a deliberate quality purchase, not a default. If a turbo voice covers the language, the multilingual premium bought nothing.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Assemble music in one generation, not five operations.</strong> Suno''s flat 20 makes extends feel free; three extends to finish a track cost 60 — more than the generate that should have carried the length.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Read sub-credit rates as volume rates.</strong> 0.30/s isolation sounds free until the 600-second episode passes; 0.36 stingers sound free until 30 of them. Per-second audio lanes are the ones where plan discounts (the 63% Ultra rate) actually change the dollar math.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;padding-left:24px;line-height:1.9;">
                <li>Put UTF-8 byte counts into your TTS cost spreadsheet next to character counts — one column, permanent fix for trap #1.</li>
                <li>Set a 950-byte soft ceiling per request in automation; pad-down beats ceil-up every single time.</li>
                <li>Pre-concatenate STT inputs to whole minutes; charge the minute you fill, not the clip you own.</li>
                <li>Keep a lane decision note per language: turbo for Latin-heavy, multilingual only when the voice list requires it.</li>
                <li>Audit Suno per finished track, not per operation — the operation log hides the assembly tax.</li>
                <li>Run one isolation pass per episode, not per re-export; at 600s, a second pass is a 180-credit repeat.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">FAQ</h2>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Why does a Chinese script cost three times the English version of the same sentence?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Because ElevenLabs TTS bills the UTF-8 byte length divided into 1,000-byte blocks, and CJK glyphs encode as three bytes each while Latin letters take one. A 400-character English script is one billable block; a 400-character Chinese script is 1,200 bytes — two blocks. Nothing is being charged per language; the byte is the unit, and CJK simply carries more bytes per meaning.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: I went over by one character and my bill doubled. Is that real?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Yes, and one byte is enough. Pricing is ceil(bytes/1000), so the 1,001st byte opens a full second block — 10 list credits on turbo, 20 on multilingual. The practical rule is to keep scripts under 950 bytes of their own language encoding, or split at paragraph boundaries before the cliff, never after the invoice.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Why did a 61-second clip cost the same as two minutes of audio?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Speech-to-text bills ceil(seconds/60) × 6: any second into a new minute opens a whole minute at 6 list credits. A 61-second file is two minutes of billing. For batches, concatenate clips to exact minute multiples before upload — the rounding is per request, so fewer requests with fuller minutes is strictly cheaper.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Is Suno really the same price for a 30-second clip and a 4-minute song?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Per operation, yes — all six Suno operations (generate, extend, upload-cover, upload-extend, add-instrumental, add-vocals) are a flat 20 credits regardless of output length. That makes single-generate tracks the best per-second value in the catalog and extend-assembled tracks the worst: one generate plus three extends is 80 credits for the same final length.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Do the monthly plan discounts apply to audio lanes?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">They apply to list credits across the whole account, so audio at Basic 82%, Pro 73%, and Ultra 63% behaves exactly like video in the wallet math — a 270-list Chinese TTS line lands at 170 credits on Ultra. What the discount cannot do is fix lane choice: turbo versus multilingual is still a 2× inside whichever wallet you pay from.</p></div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:0;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Closing: The Unit Is the Product</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Every surprise in this article is a unit mismatch: you counted characters where the biller counted bytes, clips where the biller counted minutes, and operations where the biller counted requests. The fix is not cheaper models — it is knowing which physical quantity each lane invoices, because in audio that answer changes every three rows. Before your next voice batch, run one script through the byte counter, price the same content in both TTS lanes, and check your last STT upload log for 61-second files — inside <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/elevenlabs/turbo-2-5" style="color:#a78bfa;text-decoration:none;">ElevenLabs turbo 2.5</a> and <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/suno/generate" style="color:#34d399;text-decoration:none;">Suno generate</a> on FuseAI the numbers are all on the form; only the arithmetic was ever hidden.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- wan-3-0-five-input-mode-production-guide (category=4)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  'Wan 3.0 and Wan 3.0 Prime: One Endpoint, Five Ways Into Video — A Production-Mode Guide',
  4,
  'wan-3-0-five-input-mode-production-guide',
  'Wan 3.0 unifies text, frame, reference, file and link inputs in one per-second-priced video lane — how Wan 3.0 and Prime differ in cost and workflow.',
  'Wan 3.0, Wan 3.0 Prime, unified video model, first last frame video, reference to video, per second pricing, Tongyi Wanxiang, AI video workflow, video production cost, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Wan 3.0 and Wan 3.0 Prime: One Endpoint, Five Ways Into Video — A Production-Mode Guide</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Wan 3.0 unifies text, frame, reference, file and link inputs in one per-second-priced video lane — how Wan 3.0 and Prime differ in cost and workflow.">
</head>
<body>
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(ellipse 65% 50% at 50% 0%, rgba(96,165,250,.07), transparent),radial-gradient(ellipse 50% 40% at 100% 90%, rgba(96,165,250,.05), transparent),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318, #0b0c0f);color:#e5e7eb;padding:20px;border-radius:12px;">

        <section class="introduction">
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Most AI video stacks force you to make a decision before you have an idea: text-to-video or image-to-video? Reference-driven or frame-driven? Every choice comes with its own model, its own parameter vocabulary, and its own pricing sheet. Teams shipping dozens of clips a week end up maintaining five pipelines where they only wanted one product.</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan</a> 3.0 removes that pre-decision. The <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan 3.0</a> model exposes five mutually exclusive input modes — plain text, first/last frame, multi-reference (up to 10 images, 5 videos, 5 audio tracks), single file, and single link — through one form, one pricing logic, and one 2–30 second duration slider. Its sibling <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video-prime" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan 3.0 Prime</a> accepts the exact same inputs at a higher per-second rate, trading cost for turnaround.</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">This guide is not a feature tour. It is a mode-by-mode production walkthrough: when each input path wins, what a finished clip actually costs, and how the 3.0 family relates to the earlier <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v2-7-r2v" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan 2.7 R2V</a> and <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v2-7-image-to-video" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan 2.7 Image-to-Video</a> lines that still run today.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="pipeline-consolidation">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Why One Endpoint Beats Five Pipelines</h2>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Under the old model-per-mode layout, switching from "animate this keyframe pair" to "keep this brand character consistent across shots" meant jumping tools, re-learning parameters, and re-estimating budgets. The consolidation in Wan 3.0 has three practical consequences:</p>

            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#60a5fa;">Budget predictability.</strong> All five modes bill identically: a flat per-second rate scaled by resolution. You estimate a clip by multiplying seconds × rate — the input mode never changes the unit price.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#60a5fa;">Iteration inside one workspace.</strong> A shot can start as a text concept, get re-shot with a first/last frame pair, then re-shot again with reference material — all without leaving the same tool tab.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#60a5fa;">A clean standard-vs-speed decision.</strong> Standard and Prime share the identical contract, so "pay more to render sooner" becomes a per-job toggle instead of a workflow fork.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">The Price Sheet, Reckoned Per Clip</h3>

            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Resolution</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Wan 3.0 (per sec)</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Wan 3.0 Prime (per sec)</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">10-sec clip: 3.0</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">10-sec clip: Prime</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">480p</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">12 credits</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">20 credits</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">120 credits</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">200 credits</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">720p</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">24 credits</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">40 credits</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">240 credits</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">400 credits</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">1080p (default)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">48 credits</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">80 credits</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">480 credits</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">800 credits</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Prime costs roughly 67% more per second. The two versions also share the same aspect ratio set (adaptive, 16:9, 4:3, 1:1, 3:4, 9:16) and the same audio switch — and notably, enabling audio does not change the unit price in either version.</p>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Those are list prices. Subscription plans bill credits at a discount — on monthly plans, <strong style="color:#60a5fa;">Basic pays 82%, Pro 73%, and Ultra 63%</strong> of the face amount. The 480-credit standard 1080p clip above lands at roughly 394 / 350 / 302 credits depending on plan, and the 800-credit Prime render at 656 / 584 / 504. Long-form teams effectively negotiate with themselves at the Ultra tier: a 30-second 1080p final costs 907 credits there instead of the headline 1,440.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="workflow">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Four Ways to Actually Use the Five Modes</h2>

            <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Mode 1 — Text-Only Concepting: Cheap, Fast, Throwaway</h3>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">With no media attached, the prompt becomes mandatory, and this is the cheapest way to scout a concept because you can run it at 480p for as little as 24 credits (a 2-second micro-test).</p>

            <div style="background:rgba(31,41,55,0.3);padding:16px;border-radius:8px;margin:16px 0;">
                <p style="color:#60a5fa;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:8px;">"Slow push-in on a rain-soaked Tokyo alley at night, neon reflections pooling on asphalt, a vending light flickering over an empty bench, documentary handheld feel, 8 seconds, 9:16"</p>
                <p style="color:#9ca3af;">Explicit camera motion + one lighting anchor + duration in the prompt. Text mode is where you find out if the shot exists before you spend 1080p money on it.</p>
            </div>

            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Practitioners migrating from <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v2-7-text-to-video" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan 2.7 Text-to-Video</a> will find the prompt discipline similar, but with a longer duration ceiling — 30 seconds instead of the 2.7-era defaults around 5–10.</p>

            <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Mode 2 — First/Last Frame: Animating Between Two Known States</h3>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The frame mode takes a start image and an optional end image (the last frame always requires a first frame — the pairing is validated before submission). This is the deterministic storytelling mode: you know exactly what the clip begins and ends with, and the model interpolates the journey.</p>

            <div style="background:rgba(31,41,55,0.3);padding:16px;border-radius:8px;margin:16px 0;">
                <p style="color:#60a5fa;font-style:italic;margin-bottom:8px;">First frame: product sealed on a pedestal. Last frame: same product open, packaging fanned out. Prompt: "camera orbits 90 degrees, lid lifts, tissue paper unfolds smoothly, studio softbox lighting."</p>
                <p style="color:#9ca3af;">Frames can themselves originate from <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/2-7-image" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan 2.7 Image</a> generations, closing the image-to-video loop inside one family.</p>
            </div>

            <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Mode 3 — Multi-Reference: Brand Consistency Across Shots</h3>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The reference mode is the family''s most differentiated capability: up to 10 images, 5 videos, and 5 audio tracks passed together, which is how you lock a character, a product look, and a soundtrack vibe simultaneously. It supersedes the single-purpose <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v2-7-r2v" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan 2.7 R2V</a> reference workflow by absorbing it into the same unified form.</p>

            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#60a5fa;">Character shoots:</strong> 3–5 turnaround images of the same figure, one motion-reference video, prompt describes only the new scene.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#60a5fa;">Music-reactive cuts:</strong> attach an audio reference so rhythm and pacing anchor the edit — audio is supported where the 2.7 line had no equivalent.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong style="color:#60a5fa;">Exclusivity rule:</strong> reference inputs and first/last-frame inputs cannot be mixed in one request. Decide which kind of control you need.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Modes 4 &amp; 5 — File and Link: Feed It a Document or a Page</h3>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Two single-item modes accept one file or one URL as the creative source — a storyboard PDF, a landing page, a mood board link. Use them when the "brief" is the input: the model extracts visual direction from the artifact itself rather than from your typing. File and link are mutually exclusive, and both are capped at one item per request.</p>

            <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Choosing Standard vs Prime</h3>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Because the contracts are identical, the decision reduces to queue economics: client-facing revisions and deadline-driven renders go to <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video-prime" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan 3.0 Prime</a>; internal drafts, volume variants, and mode-exploration passes go to standard. A common budget split is 80% standard drafts, 20% Prime finals.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="pitfalls">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Where the Unified Form Can Still Bite You</h2>

            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:#d1d5db;font-size:14px;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:rgba(55,65,81,0.4);">
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Risk</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">What Happens</th>
                        <th style="padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;">Countermeasure</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">"Smart" duration doesn''t exist</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Per-second billing requires a fixed length; the slider runs 2–30s and every request is billed at its exact seconds — no auto-length option.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Storyboard in seconds first; treat 5s (the default) as a unit test and 15–30s as finals.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">30s at 1080p is a 1,440-credit commitment (907 at the Ultra 63% plan discount, 1,051 at Pro 73%)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">48 credits/sec × 30s is the family''s single most expensive legal request; a bad prompt at that length is a four-figure loss.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Never send an untested prompt above 720p/10s. Draft at 480p (12 credits/sec), then re-render.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Mixing frame + reference inputs</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">The two control strategies are rejected together at validation; a last frame without a first frame fails the same way.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Pick one input mode per shot. Re-plan a "frame + reference" idea as: frames from mode 2, consistency via mode 3 on the next pass.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Assuming audio adds cost</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Surprise in the other direction: the audio toggle changes output, not price — some teams avoid it reflexively and lose free value.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Default audio on for social cuts; turn off only when you''re laying your own track.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Over-referencing</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Ten images plus five videos plus five audios is the ceiling, not the recipe; crowded references dilute each other''s signal.</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">Start with 2–4 references; add only what visibly pulls the render.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </section>

        <section class="action-checklist">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist</h2>
            <ol style="color:#d1d5db;">
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#60a5fa;">Scout in text mode at 480p.</strong> Two-to-five second probes at 24–60 credits to validate the shot concept.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#60a5fa;">Lock endpoints early.</strong> If the clip must start/end on known visuals, generate those stills now — via <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/2-7-image-pro" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan 2.7 Image Pro</a> or your own library — so mode 2 becomes available.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#60a5fa;">Build a reference pack per brand.</strong> Collect the approved turnarounds, motion clips, and audio signatures once; reuse them across every mode-3 request.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#60a5fa;">Set a per-clip credit ceiling.</strong> 720p × 10s × standard = 240 credits list price (176 on Ultra monthly) is a sane final-render cap; anything above requires a passing 480p draft first.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#60a5fa;">Reserve Prime for deadline lanes.</strong> Route client revisions and same-day finals to <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video-prime" style="color:#60a5fa;">Prime</a>; keep bulk iteration on standard.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#60a5fa;">Log the mode you used.</strong> Tag each accepted clip with its input mode — after a month you''ll know which of the five doors actually ships your work.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section class="faq">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Does Wan 3.0 pricing depend on which input mode I use?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">No. All five modes — text, first/last frame, multi-reference, file, link — bill at the same per-second rate: 12, 24, or 48 credits per second for 480p, 720p, and 1080p on the standard model, and 20/40/80 credits on Prime. Plan discounts apply on top: monthly Basic, Pro, and Ultra subscribers pay 82%, 73%, and 63% of those amounts. Duration is fixed between 2 and 30 seconds, and resolution defaults to 1080p, so always confirm before sending.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: What is the difference between Wan 3.0 and Wan 3.0 Prime?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The input contract is field-for-field identical; Prime is the premium tier at roughly 67% higher per-second cost (80 vs 48 credits at 1080p). Generate on the same form, and switch to Prime when turnaround speed matters more than marginal cost — typically final client-facing renders.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Can I combine a first/last frame pair with reference images?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">No — frame inputs and reference inputs are mutually exclusive and rejected at submission. You can, however, re-create the composition in two passes: render with references first, then feed a still from that render as the first frame of a follow-up clip.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: How much does audio cost in Wan 3.0?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Nothing extra. The audio switch changes whether the output carries generated sound, but the per-second price is driven solely by resolution. That makes native audio a free option to test on social cuts before paying for a separate music tool.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:16px;">
                <h3 style="color:#f3f4f6;">Q: Should I still use Wan 2.7 models at all?</h3>
                <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Yes, for two cases: 2.7 remains the cheaper option for short standard-format clips, and its separate per-mode tabs suit teams whose pipeline is already organized by input type. Wan 3.0 wins when you want reference-heavy control (audio references in particular), longer durations up to 30s, or one unified estimate sheet across a mixed workload.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="closing">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Real Shift</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The interesting part of Wan 3.0 isn''t any single mode — it''s that "how do I feed the model?" stops being an architectural question. Once five input styles share one price sheet and one form, the planning conversation moves upstream, to storyboards, reference packs, and duration budgets, where it always should have been.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Try <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan 3.0 on FuseAITools</a> for unified multi-mode video production — and switch to <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video-prime" style="color:#60a5fa;">Wan 3.0 Prime</a> when the deadline says so.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
</body>
</html>'
);

-- wan-version-history-per-second-vs-flat-pricing (category=4)
INSERT INTO news (title, category, path, description, keyword, content) VALUES (
  '24 a Second Through Three Generations: How Wan''s Per-Second Pricing Flat-Matched the Old Menu — Then Beat It',
  4,
  'wan-version-history-per-second-vs-flat-pricing',
  'Wan video pricing across three generations: the 2.6 flat menu''s hidden long-clip discount, 2.7''s exact per-second conversion, and 3.0''s new floors.',
  'Wan AI video pricing history, flat menu vs per-second billing, Wan 3.0 video credits, per-second video generation cost, long duration video discount, Wan prime speed tier pricing, video rate card comparison, FuseAI Tools',
  '<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>24 a Second Through Three Generations: How Wan''s Per-Second Pricing Flat-Matched the Old Menu — Then Beat It</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Wan video pricing across three generations: the 2.6 flat menu''s hidden long-clip discount, 2.7''s exact per-second conversion, and 3.0''s new floors.">
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/news/wan-version-history-per-second-vs-flat-pricing">
</head>
<body style="margin:0;padding:0;background:#0b0c0f;background-image:radial-gradient(circle at 15% 0%, rgba(56,189,248,0.07) 0%, transparent 45%),radial-gradient(circle at 85% 100%, rgba(163,230,53,0.05) 0%, transparent 45%),linear-gradient(180deg, #151318 0%, #0b0c0f 100%);color:#d1d5db;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
    <article class="ai-model-comparison" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article" style="max-width:960px;margin:0 auto;padding:32px 20px 64px;">
        <header style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h1 itemprop="headline" style="font-size:2rem;line-height:1.25;color:#f9fafb;margin:0 0 16px;">24 a Second Through Three Generations: How Wan''s Per-Second Pricing Flat-Matched the Old Menu — Then Beat It</h1>
            <p style="color:#9ca3af;font-size:0.95rem;margin:0;">Published <time itemprop="datePublished" datetime="2026-11-12">November 12, 2026</time> · FuseAI Tools Editorial</p>
        </header>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Introduction: Rate Cards Retire Quietly and Rewrite Loudly</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The first rule of pricing archaeology: generations die silently. The earliest Wan video lanes no longer hold a line on <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan" style="color:#38bdf8;text-decoration:none;">FuseAI</a>''s live rate card — what survives, in order, is the <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">2.6 flat menu</strong>, the <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">2.7 per-second conversion</strong>, and <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">3.0''s two-tier family</strong> (standard and the flat-double prime), plus 3.0''s unified-entry design that folded five input modes into one priced lane. Three generations, one continuous argument about a single question: <em>should a video''s price come from a menu of durations, or from the seconds themselves?</em></p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">The 2.6-to-2.7 step is the cleanest case study the platform has to offer, because the conversion was arithmetic-transparent: every per-second rate carried over <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">to the digit</strong> — and one number did not carry over at all. The flat menu had been quietly discounting long renders, per-second billing taxes them. This article is the story of that discount: where it hid, how the conversion erased it, and what 3.0 built in its place. All figures are list credits; the standing monthly-plan discounts — Basic 82%, Pro 73%, Ultra 63% — apply per lane, so the reference render used throughout, a 15-second 1080p clip on 3.0, bills 720 list, 590 at Basic, 526 at Pro, 454 at Ultra.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Act I: The Flat Menu — and the Discount Hidden in Its Last Row</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/text-to-video" style="color:#38bdf8;text-decoration:none;">Wan 2.6 text-to-video</a> (and its <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/image-to-video" style="color:#38bdf8;text-decoration:none;">image-to-video</a> and <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/video-to-video" style="color:#38bdf8;text-decoration:none;">video-to-video</a> twins) priced by absolute per-clip menus on a duration × quality grid. At 720p the menu was honest to the point of boring: 5 seconds = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">120</strong>, 10 = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">240</strong>, 15 = <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">360</strong> — exactly 24 credits a second at every row. Do the division on the 1080p column and the shape changes: 180, 360, and then <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">480 for 15 seconds</strong>. Five seconds and ten divide to 36/s; the long row divides to <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">32/s</strong>. Nobody announced it, but the menu was doing what airline pricing does — rewarding the commitment to a longer render with a lower unit rate, one tier down, where the arithmetic only shows up if you go looking.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Why does a hidden unit-rate discount matter? Because it is invisible to exactly the two decisions it should inform: choosing duration ("15 seconds is 480, fine") and choosing generation ("the 36-a-second 1080p lane"). It is visible only in the division nobody performs. Menus price packages; they do not price seconds — and every downstream generation in this story is a reaction to that gap.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Act II: The Flat-Match — 2.7 Converted the Rate, and Deleted the Fifth Row</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v2-7-text-to-video" style="color:#38bdf8;text-decoration:none;">Wan 2.7 text-to-video</a> flipped the billing mode: once.credits now stores a <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">per-second rate</strong> (duration=1 rule × actual seconds), and the menu rows — all of them, on all four 2.7 lanes including <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v2-7-r2v" style="color:#38bdf8;text-decoration:none;">r2v</a> — collapse into two numbers: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">24/s at 720p, 36/s at 1080p</strong>. Read those against Act I and the conversion strategy is explicit: the per-second rates are the old menu''s <em>short-row</em> unit rates, kept digit for digit. Nothing about a 5- or 10-second render changed by a single credit; 2.7''s whole innovation was legibility. You could finally quote a Wan render by multiplying, and the price of "one second more" became a constant.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">But constants cut both ways, and the 15-second 1080p row is where this one lands: at 36/s, fifteen seconds bill <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">540</strong> — sixty credits more than the old menu''s 480. The discount didn''t expire; <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">it couldn''t survive the unit change</strong>. A flat menu can embed any curve it likes between rows; a per-second rate is, by definition, a straight line. Transparency and concession were the same lever pulled one way, and teams repricing their long-form renders from muscle memory got the short end of the trade — a 12.5% tax that only exists if you were previously collecting an unadvertised subsidy that only existed if you divided.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Act III: 3.0 Moves the Premium — New Floor, New Ceiling, No Duration Game</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Added on 2026-10-14, <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video" style="color:#38bdf8;text-decoration:none;">Wan 3.0 video</a> kept the per-second line and re-cut where it bends: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">12/s at 480p, 24 at 720p, 48 at 1080p</strong>. The 720p rate is now three generations unchanged — 24 credits a second is the steadiest number in Wan''s history, the lane where flat-menu and per-second pricing agreed to stop arguing. The movement is at the edges: a new <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">480p floor at half the 720p rate</strong> (a 5-second draft costs 60 — the same number 2.6 charged for 720p, two rungs lower), and the 1080p ceiling lifted from 36 to 48. Read together: the duration discount of the menu era is gone for good, and the quality premium now does all the pricing work. Duration stopped being a deal and became a meter; resolution is where the negotiation happens.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Two design decisions complete the generation. The audio switch <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">does not move the per-second rate at all</strong> — the opposite of Kling''s scene-priced sound, and a quiet statement that in this family, sound is a feature, not a tier. And 3.0 is a unified entry: one priced lane serving text, first/last-frame, reference, file, and link inputs, with duration a continuous 2–30-second choice — no menu rows to round to, and no smart-duration -1 escape hatch: you pick seconds, you pay 24/48 of them per unit, per second. The sibling <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video-prime" style="color:#38bdf8;text-decoration:none;">3.0 prime</a> ships the identical contract at <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">exactly double the rate</strong> (20/40/80) — a speed tier, priced as one: same inputs, same resolutions, 2× credits, the cleanest premium structure on the card because it requires no arithmetic to audit.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">The Generations Side by Side: Six Renders, Three Eras</h2>
            <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:20px;font-size:0.92rem;">
                <thead>
                    <tr style="background:#1f2937;">
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:left;">Render (list credits)</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:left;">Billing shape</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">2.6 menu</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">2.7 /s</th>
                        <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #374151;color:#f9fafb;text-align:right;">3.0 /s</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">5s at 720p</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">the anchor row</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">120</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">120</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">120</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">10s at 720p</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">linear × 2</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">240</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">240</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">240</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">5s at 1080p</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">quality premium</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">180</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">180</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">240</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">15s at 720p</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">long row, cheap tier</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">360</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">360</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">360</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">15s at 1080p</strong></td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">the erased discount</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">480</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">540</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">720</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">5s at 480p (3.0''s new floor)</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;">draft economics</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">—</td>
                        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937;text-align:right;">—</td>
