                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;margin-top:16px;">The table''s argument is row five. Four of six renders held flat across an entire conversion — the per-second era honored the menu''s short rows exactly — and the one that didn''t is the one the menu was subsidizing invisibly: <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">480 → 540 → 720</strong> for fifteen seconds of 1080p, a <strong style="color:#f9fafb;">50% climb</strong> while 720p never moved. And row six is the counterpoint: the era that taxed the long premium also minted the floor, and the cheapest five Wan seconds on the card — 60 credits at 480p — now costs exactly what the flat menu charged for five seconds of 720p. The average of those two moves is the honest summary of the whole history: quality got dearer, drafts got cheaper, and the meter itself stopped lying in either direction.</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Pitfalls: Five Ways Old Wan Habits Misprice New Wan</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Carrying the long-render discount forward.</strong> 15 seconds of 1080p at 480 was a menu-era number. It became 540 the day per-second billing arrived, and 720 at 3.0 — sheets that still assume "long rows are cheaper per second" overpay invisibly in the other direction, by up to half.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Buying prime for quality.</strong> 3.0 prime bills exactly 2× standard (20/40/80) on an identical input contract — the same resolutions, the same durations. That doubling buys speed, not pixels; paying it to get "more 1080p" is the most expensive category error on this family''s card.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Planning an audio surcharge.</strong> Wan 3.0''s rate is audio-indifferent — the switch does not move the per-second price. Teams porting budget logic from sound-priced families (Kling''s +10/s at 720p) allocate a premium that does not exist here, then wonder why the invoice reads light.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Quoting the 480p floor at 720p rates.</strong> The 12/s lane is new with 3.0; nothing below 720p existed in the 2.x menus. Drafts quoted at 24/s against a 3.0 render are budgeted double — and the 5-second, 60-credit draft is the number most legacy sheets never learned to spend.</p>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;"><strong style="color:#f9fafb;">Leaving duration to "smart."</strong> 3.0 rejects the vendor''s -1 auto-duration; seconds are a manual 2–30 choice and every one of them bills. The menu era''s implicit rounding (you could only pick 5, 10, 15) has been replaced by an obligation to decide — a 17-second cut bills 17, not "the 15 row."</p>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Action Checklist: Pricing Against a Rate Card''s History</h2>
            <ul style="color:#d1d5db;padding-left:24px;line-height:1.9;">
                <li>Divide every flat menu by its rows before abandoning it — the unit rates expose which durations were subsidized, and that is where conversions will bite.</li>
                <li>After any menu-to-meter conversion, re-quote your longest and highest-quality render first; it is the cell where hidden discounts go to die.</li>
                <li>Treat the unchanged middle (720p at 24/s for three generations) as your planning anchor; edges move, the anchor is the platform telling you its floor price for competence.</li>
                <li>Route drafts to the 480p floor (60 for five seconds) and reserve the 1080p premium for locked cuts — quality-per-render, not quantity-of-renders, is where 3.0 concentrates cost.</li>
                <li>Read ×2 lanes as speed, not quality — prime''s whole contract is "the same frame, faster," and paying it for the wrong reason is a self-inflicted double.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">FAQ</h2>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: What does "per-second pricing flat-matching absolute pricing" actually mean in numbers?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Exact arithmetic, not a slogan. Wan 2.6''s flat menu billed 120/240/360 at 720p and 180/360 at 1080p for 5/10-second clips; when 2.7 converted to per-second billing, the stored rates were the menu''s own divided unit prices — 24/s and 36/s — so every one of those renders cost the same to the credit. "Flat-matched" means the conversion changed the meter''s unit without changing the meter''s reading, for every row except the discounted one.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: What happened to the 15-second discount?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">It was erased by the change of unit. The 2.6 menu''s 15-second 1080p row cost 480 — 32 per second, four below the 36/s of its own shorter rows. A per-second rate is a straight line and cannot carry that kind of curve, so 2.7 billed the same render at 540, and 3.0''s higher-quality rate took it to 720. Long premium renders climbed 50% across the history while short and mid renders never moved.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Is Wan 3.0 prime a better model or just faster?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Faster, and priced as exactly that. Prime holds the identical input contract — same 480p/720p/1080p grid, same 2–30-second range, same audio-indifferent rules — at precisely double standard''s rates (20/40/80 versus 12/24/48). There is no resolution or duration where prime buys you a better pixel; it buys queue position. If your calendar, not your client, is the constraint, it is the correct 2×; otherwise it is the most avoidable one on the card.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: Why did 1080p get more expensive while 720p held still?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">Because 3.0 re-concentrated the pricing logic on resolution after duration stopped carrying discounts. 720p held 24/s from the menu era through three generations — a deliberate anchor — while the premium tier moved from 36 to 48 and a new 480p floor opened at 12. The rate card''s message: the era of paying less for committing longer is over; the era of choosing your resolution per render, draft to delivery, is the one replacing it.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div itemscope itemprop="hasPart" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
                <h3 itemprop="name" style="color:#f9fafb;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 8px;">Q: What is the cheapest Wan render the platform has ever listed?</h3>
                <div itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p style="color:#d1d5db;margin:0;">The shortest second times the lowest floor: a 2-second 480p clip on 3.0 bills 24 list credits (2 × 12/s). Structurally, though, the number that matters is 60 — five seconds at the 480p floor — because it matches what the 2.6 menu charged for five seconds of 720p. The draft lane now costs what the standard lane cost two generations ago: the real tell of how fast this family''s floor has fallen.</p></div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section itemprop="articleSection" style="margin-bottom:0;">
            <h2 style="color:#f9fafb;border-bottom-color:#374151;">Closing: The Meter Doesn''t Lie — But Menus Could Hide</h2>
            <p style="color:#d1d5db;">Three generations, one lesson: flat menus and per-second meters are not different prices, they are different <em>theologies of rounding</em> — the menu hides its opinions between rows, the meter states every one, and converting from one to the other is where the hidden opinions get repriced. Wan''s conversion said 24 and 36 out loud, took back 32, and 3.0 re-drew the whole curve as 12/24/48 with a 2× speed lane over it. The working plan reads the history forward: anchor on 720p''s three-generation 24, draft at the 480p floor, treat 1080p as the premium decision it now officially is, and re-verify every legacy number against the lane it now lives in — because the cheapest credit on this card''s menu era is somebody''s current overpayment. The whole family bills live on FuseAI — the <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v2-7-image-to-video" style="color:#38bdf8;text-decoration:none;">2.7 lanes</a> for exact menu-era continuity, the <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video" style="color:#38bdf8;text-decoration:none;">3.0 unified entry</a> for the five-input meter, and <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video-prime" style="color:#38bdf8;text-decoration:none;">prime</a> when the deadline needs the 2×. Try <a href="https://www.fuseaitools.com/home/wan/v3-0-video" style="color:#38bdf8;text-decoration:none;">FuseAITools</a> for Wan video generation with transparent per-second pricing.</p>
